// Content for Break the Cycle (node g65, Parent Layer, Chapter 8): NEW v2 build to GDD 65 (mechanic-embodying),
// the EMOTIONAL CORE of the Parent Layer and the deepest Unlearn->Relearn beat in the app: we parent the way we
// were parented, until we choose not to. FIRMLY NON-SHAMING: it busts the PRACTICE, never the parent, and treats
// inherited patterns with compassion (most parents using harsh methods were raised that way). 84 researched,
// India-grounded scenarios across how-you-were-raised → discipline-differently → the-repair → calm-yourself →
// heal-your-wounds → tools-and-help, each encoded as one of the shared v2 play actions (strike-rewrite / branch /
// reflect / role-play / sort / match / spot): no binary "tap the right card". Lead: strike-rewrite (bust the
// myth), branch (your move), reflect. Six modes: how you were raised (gently surface inherited patterns, keep the
// good, leave the harmful; the inheritance isn't destiny and questioning it isn't betrayal), discipline
// differently (positive, firm AND kind discipline that works; busts "a slap never hurt me", "fear equals respect",
// "positive means permissive", "shaming motivates"; physical punishment is harmful and ineffective), the repair
// (reconnecting after you snap or get it wrong: a clean apology that owns it without blaming the child; repair
// beats perfection), calm yourself (self-regulation, the gap between feeling and acting, knowing your triggers,
// stepping away safely to keep everyone safe when anger surges), heal your own wounds (unhealed pain resurfaces in
// parenting: tend it and seek support; NOT therapy), tools & help (a calm-down-before-you-react and repair
// toolkit, and routing). Care-sensitive and child-safety aware: promotes non-violent, evidence-based positive
// discipline and NEVER endorses hitting or shaming; routes a parent's deeper wounds to support (Tele-MANAS 14416)
// and, where patterns risk real harm to a child, to Be the Safe Adult (g69) and help: with warmth and never
// shame. Builds on Mind Matters (g38) and Bounce (g39) and the Unlearn->Relearn core. India: corporal punishment
// and shaming are still widely normalised and most parents using them were raised that way, so the node is
// compassionate and honest that change is hard, offering positive-discipline alternatives that actually work.
// gameId "break-the-cycle".
import type { V2GameConfig, Scenario } from "./v2-schema";

const SCENARIOS: Scenario[] = [
  {"id":"bc-001","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"You parent the way you were parented, and there's nothing you can do about it.\"","myth":{"un":"You parent the way you were parented, and there's nothing you can do about it.","re":"We tend to repeat what we knew, but the inheritance isn't destiny; new patterns are genuinely learnable.","why":"Awareness is the first step to choosing differently."},"relearn":"You tend to parent as you were parented, but it isn't destiny; you can learn and choose new patterns.","persona":"Suresh","source":"not destiny"},
  {"id":"bc-002","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: noticing a pattern you inherited isn't blaming anyone; it's the start of choosing what to keep and what to leave. Sit right?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Sits right","Keep some, leave some","Start of change"],"affirm":"Noticing inherited patterns, without blame, lets you keep the good ones and set down the ones that cause harm.","relearn":"Noticing what you inherited lets you choose what to keep and what to leave, without blame.","persona":"any","source":"keep or leave"},
  {"id":"bc-003","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: an inherited pattern worth keeping, or one worth gently leaving behind?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Warmth, stories and showing up"},{"id":"b","text":"Hitting when angry"},{"id":"c","text":"Strong family bonds"},{"id":"d","text":"Shaming and harsh words"},{"id":"e","text":"Eating and praying together"},{"id":"f","text":"Ruling the house by fear"}],"bins":[{"id":"keep","label":"Worth keeping","valence":"pos"},{"id":"leave","label":"Worth leaving","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"keep","b":"leave","c":"keep","d":"leave","e":"keep","f":"leave"},"relearn":"Warmth, bonds and showing up are worth keeping; hitting and shaming are patterns worth gently leaving behind.","persona":"any","source":"keep vs leave","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-004","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If I question how my parents raised me, I'm betraying or disrespecting them.\"","myth":{"un":"If I question how my parents raised me, I'm betraying or disrespecting them.","re":"You can love and honour your parents and still choose to do some things differently; that's growth, not betrayal.","why":"Doing better isn't a verdict on them."},"relearn":"You can honour your parents and still parent differently; choosing new ways is growth, not betrayal.","persona":"Suresh","source":"not betrayal"},
  {"id":"bc-005","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"interrupt the pattern","hook":"When your son breaks a glass, you catch yourself about to shout what your father always shouted.","steps":[{"prompt":"The words are right on your tongue. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Pause, and ask if this is the father you want to be","then":"The words stay unsaid. You take a breath and see a scared boy, not a naughty one.","best":true},{"text":"Let the words out. It's how you learned","then":"Your son freezes, then starts picking up glass with shaking hands."},{"text":"Say nothing and walk off in a huff","then":"Your son is left alone with the broken glass, unsure what's coming."},{"text":"Bite it back, then tell him he's always so careless","then":"The shout is gone, but his face still falls at 'always'."}],"why":"That pause is where you get to choose, instead of running on your father's script."},{"prompt":"A moment later, your son is still by the broken glass. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Check he's not hurt, then clean up together","then":"He relaxes and fetches the dustpan. 'Sorry, Papa,' he says quietly.","best":true},{"text":"Tell him to clean it up himself as punishment","then":"He kneels by the sharp pieces, blinking back tears."},{"text":"Lecture him about how glasses cost money","then":"He nods at the floor, and you can see he's stopped listening."},{"text":"Clean it all up yourself in silence","then":"He watches from the doorway, unsure if you're still angry."}],"why":"Staying calm and putting his safety first shows him mistakes can be fixed, not feared."},{"prompt":"That night, the moment with the glass keeps coming back to you. What do you do with it?","options":[{"text":"Notice what set it off, to catch it next time","then":"You spot it: a tiring day and a mess. Next time, you'll know the feeling.","best":true},{"text":"Decide it's just how you are","then":"The thought feels heavy, and tomorrow looks like more of the same."},{"text":"Promise yourself you'll never feel angry again","then":"The promise feels good tonight. It won't survive the next tiring day."},{"text":"Push it out of mind. It's done now","then":"You sleep fine. The same script is waiting at the next spill."}],"why":"Knowing your triggers lets you catch the old reflex before it acts."}],"debrief":"When an old harsh pattern rises, pause and ask if it's the parent you want to be, rather than autopilot.","relearn":"Pause and choose when an inherited pattern rises; autopilot just passes it on."},
  {"id":"bc-006","type":"match","hook":"Match each inherited message to the truer one to raise your child on.","pairs":[{"left":"'Children should be seen, not heard'","right":"Your voice and feelings matter"},{"left":"'Fear keeps kids in line'","right":"Respect grows from trust, not fear"},{"left":"'Affection spoils them'","right":"Warmth makes children stronger"},{"left":"'Crying is weakness'","right":"Feelings are safe to show here"},{"left":"'A slap is just discipline'","right":"Calm limits teach better than pain"}],"relearn":"Each harsh inherited message has a truer one, voice matters, respect from trust, warmth strengthens, to pass on instead.","persona":"any","source":"truer messages","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-007","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: most parents who were raised harshly aren't bad people; they're carrying what they were handed. Compassion for that?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Compassion","They carried it too","I get it"],"affirm":"Most parents who use harsh methods are repeating what they were handed; understanding that, without excusing harm, is where change starts.","relearn":"Most who parent harshly are carrying what they were handed; compassion for that is where change begins.","persona":"Suresh","source":"compassion"},
  {"id":"bc-008","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two unhealthy ways of handling your own upbringing.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Keeping the good, gently leaving the harmful","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Repeating it all unquestioned","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Choosing your own patterns with care","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Insisting nothing about it can change","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Honouring your parents while doing things differently","trick":false}],"why":"\"Repeating it all unquestioned\" and \"nothing can change\" pass on the harm; keeping the good and choosing your own way is healthy.","relearn":"Handle your upbringing by keeping the good and choosing your own patterns, not repeating it unquestioned.","persona":"any","source":"healthy handling","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-009","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"feel then fuel","hook":"Realising how harshly you were raised brings up guilt and grief, and it sits heavy for days.","steps":[{"prompt":"One evening, the sadness hits hard while the kids watch TV. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Let yourself feel it, gently, without judging it","then":"Tears come and go. Afterwards you feel lighter, and oddly clearer.","best":true},{"text":"Bury it and carry on as before","then":"The feeling goes quiet. Your short temper at dinner doesn't."},{"text":"Tell yourself real men don't dwell on this","then":"You straighten up. The grief goes underground, still heavy."},{"text":"Distract yourself with work until it fades","then":"It fades by bedtime and returns, heavier, a few days later."}],"why":"Letting grief be felt, with compassion, keeps it from leaking out in other ways."},{"prompt":"Next morning, your son asks why you seemed different last night. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say an old memory made you sad, and you're okay","then":"He nods and hugs you. 'I get sad about memories too,' he says.","best":true},{"text":"Say it's nothing for children to worry about","then":"He goes quiet, the way you used to when adults shut you out."},{"text":"Tell him it's because he was noisy","then":"He looks confused, then guilty, and apologises for nothing."},{"text":"Tell him about the beatings you got as a boy","then":"His eyes go wide, and he looks frightened for you."}],"why":"Simple honesty shows him feelings are okay, without handing him your heavy load."},{"prompt":"A week later, the heaviness has eased a little. What do you do with it now?","options":[{"text":"Let it fuel one change, like no shouting at bedtime","then":"The first calm bedtime feels strange. By Friday, your son is asking for stories again.","best":true},{"text":"Set it aside. Feeling it once was enough","then":"Life goes back to normal, and so do the old habits."},{"text":"Keep going over every wrong your parents did","then":"The anger grows, and there's less room for what you'd change."},{"text":"Vow to become a completely different father overnight","then":"Two days in, you're exhausted and snapping again."}],"why":"Grief turned into one small change becomes motivation, not shame."}],"debrief":"Let the grief of how you were raised be felt with compassion and turned into motivation, not buried.","relearn":"Feel the grief with compassion and let it fuel change, rather than burying it and repeating the past."},
  {"id":"bc-010","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you get to decide what stops with you, and that decision is a gift to your child. Feel the weight?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Feel it","Stops with me","A gift"],"affirm":"Deciding what harmful patterns stop with you is a profound gift to your child and the generations after.","relearn":"You decide what stops with you, and that decision is a gift to your child.","persona":"any","source":"stops with me"},
  {"id":"bc-011","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A slap never hurt me, and hitting is just normal discipline.\"","myth":{"un":"A slap never hurt me, and hitting is just normal discipline.","re":"Evidence is clear that physical punishment harms children and doesn't work; it raises aggression and problems, not good behaviour.","why":"Common doesn't mean harmless or effective."},"relearn":"Physical punishment harms children and doesn't improve behaviour; it's common, but neither safe nor effective.","persona":"Suresh","source":"hitting harms"},
  {"id":"bc-012","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Fear equals respect; a child who fears you will behave.\"","myth":{"un":"Fear equals respect; a child who fears you will behave.","re":"Fear gets short-term compliance but teaches hiding and resentment; real respect grows from trust and clear, kind limits.","why":"Fear and respect are not the same thing."},"relearn":"Fear isn't respect; it teaches hiding and resentment, while real respect grows from trust and kind, firm limits.","persona":"Suresh","source":"fear isn't respect"},
  {"id":"bc-013","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Positive discipline means being permissive and having no rules.\"","myth":{"un":"Positive discipline means being permissive and having no rules.","re":"Positive discipline is firm and kind: clear boundaries and consequences held with warmth, not harshness, work better.","why":"Firm plus kind beats harsh or soft."},"relearn":"Positive discipline is firm and kind, clear limits held with warmth, not permissiveness and not harshness.","persona":"Vikram","source":"firm and kind"},
  {"id":"bc-014","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: positive, effective discipline, or fear-based punishment?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A calm, clear consequence"},{"id":"b","text":"Hitting or slapping"},{"id":"c","text":"Naming the limit and the reason"},{"id":"d","text":"Shaming or humiliating"},{"id":"e","text":"Staying warm while holding the rule"},{"id":"f","text":"Threatening to abandon them"}],"bins":[{"id":"positive","label":"Positive discipline","valence":"pos"},{"id":"fear","label":"Fear-based","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"positive","b":"fear","c":"positive","d":"fear","e":"positive","f":"fear"},"relearn":"Calm consequences, explained limits and warmth are positive discipline; hitting, shaming and abandonment threats are fear-based and harmful.","persona":"any","source":"positive vs fear"},
  {"id":"bc-015","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"calm consequence","hook":"Your son Rohan scribbles on the wall you just painted, and your hand itches to smack him.","steps":[{"prompt":"He sees your face and freezes. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Pause, breathe, and choose a calm, firm consequence","then":"Your breath slows. Rohan watches you, waiting to see what comes.","best":true},{"text":"Yell until he understands how bad this is","then":"He bursts into tears, too scared to hear a word."},{"text":"Say nothing and scrub it off yourself","then":"You scrub, but the crayon won't budge. Rohan wanders off, unsure what he did wrong."},{"text":"Give him one smack like the ones you got as a child","then":"Rohan cries and runs off. The scribbles stay, and so does your guilt."}],"why":"Pausing before you act lets you discipline without harm."},{"prompt":"The crayon marks are still on the wall. What consequence do you choose?","options":[{"text":"Have him scrub it with you and lose the crayons for a day","then":"Rohan scrubs hard, tongue out in concentration. The wall is mostly clean.","best":true},{"text":"Tell the whole family at dinner so he feels properly ashamed","then":"Everyone laughs. Rohan stares at his plate, face burning."},{"text":"No consequence at all. He's too young to understand","then":"Rohan runs off happily, and an hour later he's eyeing the other wall."},{"text":"Ban TV and outdoor play for the whole of next week","then":"He sulks all evening, already unsure what the ban is even for."}],"why":"A short, related consequence teaches him far more than a big punishment or shame."},{"prompt":"That night, your spouse says a smack is the only thing that really works. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say nothing. It's not worth an argument tonight","then":"Your spouse keeps suggesting a smack every time Rohan misbehaves."},{"text":"Tell them they have no idea what you're going through","then":"It turns into an argument, and nothing is decided."},{"text":"Say smacking harms him and doesn't teach him anything","then":"Your spouse pauses. 'So what does work, then?'","best":true},{"text":"Agree, and say maybe next time you'll just smack him","then":"The idea sits there, ready for the next bad day."}],"why":"Smacking harms and doesn't teach. Saying so helps your spouse choose the calm way too."}],"debrief":"When the urge to smack rises, use a firm, calm consequence instead; it works better and breaks the cycle.","relearn":"Pause and use a calm consequence rather than smacking; it disciplines without harm and ends the cycle."},
  {"id":"bc-016","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"match","hook":"Match each misbehaviour to a positive-discipline response.","pairs":[{"left":"A tantrum","right":"Stay calm, name the feeling, hold the limit"},{"left":"Breaking a rule","right":"A clear, related consequence, explained"},{"left":"Hitting a sibling","right":"Stop it firmly, teach the better choice"},{"left":"Lying about something","right":"Make truth safe, set a fair consequence"},{"left":"Refusing to cooperate","right":"Offer a small choice within the limit"}],"relearn":"Tantrums, broken rules, hitting, lying and refusing each have a firm, kind, teaching response, no fear or pain required.","persona":"Vikram","source":"positive responses"},
  {"id":"bc-017","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Shaming a child, calling them stupid or bad, motivates them to improve.\"","myth":{"un":"Shaming a child, calling them stupid or bad, motivates them to improve.","re":"Shaming damages a child's self-worth and bond with you; it discourages, it doesn't motivate.","why":"Shame wounds; it doesn't teach."},"relearn":"Shaming a child damages their self-worth and your bond; it discourages rather than motivates.","persona":"Suresh","source":"shame doesn't teach"},
  {"id":"bc-018","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: discipline means to teach, not to punish or frighten. Does that reframe land?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["It lands","Teach not frighten","Reframed"],"affirm":"Discipline at its root means to teach; the goal is a child who learns, not one who fears.","relearn":"Discipline means to teach, not to punish or frighten; that reframe changes everything.","persona":"any","source":"teach not frighten"},
  {"id":"bc-019","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two 'discipline' moves that harm instead of building good behaviour.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Clear, calm, consistent limits","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Hitting and yelling to instil fear","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Warmth alongside firm boundaries","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Shaming the child until they comply","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Explaining the reason behind the rule","trick":false}],"why":"Hitting-to-instil-fear and shaming wound a child and don't build behaviour; calm limits, warmth and explained reasons do.","relearn":"Hitting to instil fear and shaming don't build behaviour; clear calm limits, warmth and explained reasons do.","persona":"any","source":"builds behaviour"},
  {"id":"bc-020","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"hold the line","hook":"Your family insists you must beat your son Ishaan, or he'll be 'spoiled'.","steps":[{"prompt":"At Sunday lunch, your uncle says it again in front of everyone. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Give Ishaan a quick smack to show you're not soft","then":"Ishaan's eyes fill with tears in front of the whole family."},{"text":"Say kindly but firmly that you don't hit, and won't","then":"Your uncle scoffs, but the table moves on. Ishaan glances at you, relieved.","best":true},{"text":"Tell him his generation damaged enough children","then":"The table goes silent. Your aunt leaves the room in a huff."},{"text":"Agree to keep the peace, and change the subject","then":"Your uncle nods approvingly. Ishaan looks at you, worried."}],"why":"Holding your line kindly protects your child without starting a war."},{"prompt":"Later, your mother takes you aside and says only a firm hand keeps children in line. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say it's your choice and walk off before she replies","then":"She's left more worried than before, and says so to the whole family."},{"text":"Tell her you don't need her advice any more","then":"She looks stung and barely speaks to you for the rest of the day."},{"text":"Promise her you'll be stricter from now on","then":"She smiles, and you feel the pressure settle on you."},{"text":"Say you'll be firm without hitting: clear rules, calmly held","then":"She listens, frowning. 'Firm without hitting? Hmm.' It's a start.","best":true}],"why":"Firm and kind isn't soft. Explaining that shows your family you haven't dropped the limits."},{"prompt":"That evening, Ishaan breaks his cousin's toy on purpose. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Smack him, since the whole family is watching you now","then":"Ishaan cries. The relatives nod, and you feel sick inside."},{"text":"Let it go, so the relatives don't all pile on him","then":"The cousin cries. Your uncle rolls his eyes. 'Spoiled.'"},{"text":"Shout at him so the family sees you're strict","then":"Ishaan flinches. The family watches, and so does his cousin."},{"text":"Calmly have him apologise and give one of his own toys","then":"Ishaan grumbles but hands over his car. His cousin cheers up.","best":true}],"why":"A calm, related consequence shows that discipline without hitting actually works."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, the family still pushes for a firm hand. What helps you parent without hitting from now on?","options":[{"text":"Give in quietly, since you can't fight them all","then":"The old cycle quietly restarts, one smack at a time."},{"text":"Avoid family gatherings so no one can comment","then":"You miss your cousins, and the pressure waits for Diwali."},{"text":"Keep your calm way at home, but smack when family's around","then":"Ishaan learns to dread family visits more than anything."},{"text":"Ask your spouse or someone you trust to back you up","then":"The next time your uncle starts, they speak up before you do.","best":true}],"why":"Someone backing you up makes it far easier to stay non-violent under pressure."}],"debrief":"Hold your non-violent discipline line against family pressure; protecting your child matters more than the old norm.","relearn":"Hold firm on non-violence despite pressure; your child's safety outranks 'spoiling' fears."},
  {"id":"bc-021","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the parents kids feel safest with are firm and warm, not frightening. Worth aiming for?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Worth it","Firm and warm","Aiming there"],"affirm":"Children feel safest and behave best with parents who are both firm and warm, not frightening.","relearn":"The parents kids feel safest with are firm and warm, not frightening; that's the aim.","persona":"any","source":"firm and warm"},
  {"id":"bc-022","cat":"the-repair","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If I lost my temper with my child, I've ruined everything and I'm a bad parent.\"","myth":{"un":"If I lost my temper with my child, I've ruined everything and I'm a bad parent.","re":"Every parent slips; what matters most is the repair afterward, reconnecting and making it right, which children remember.","why":"Repair, not perfection, builds secure kids."},"relearn":"Slipping doesn't make you a bad parent; the repair afterward is what counts and what children remember.","persona":"Divya","source":"repair not perfection"},
  {"id":"bc-023","cat":"the-repair","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"go back and repair","hook":"You snapped and yelled at your son over his homework, and now you feel awful.","steps":[{"prompt":"He's gone quiet in his room. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Wait till you're both calm, then go and own it","then":"An hour later you knock. He looks up warily, but lets you in.","best":true},{"text":"Pretend it didn't happen, and call him for dinner","then":"He comes to dinner and eats silently, not meeting your eyes."},{"text":"Rush in right away, still upset, to explain","then":"Your voice is still tight, and he shrinks back against the wall."},{"text":"Send him a laddoo through his sister","then":"He eats the laddoo, but doesn't come out of his room."}],"why":"Repair lands best once you're both calm, and it starts with you going to him."},{"prompt":"Later, you sit beside your son. He won't look at you. What do you say first?","options":[{"text":"Say sorry for yelling, and that it wasn't okay","then":"He glances up. 'You were really angry,' he says quietly.","best":true},{"text":"Explain how tired you were after the shop","then":"He nods, but he's still waiting for a sorry."},{"text":"Ask why he made you so angry","then":"He shrugs and stares at his homework."},{"text":"Say sorry, but remind him to finish his homework","then":"He hears the 'but' and goes quiet again."}],"why":"Owning it plainly, without excuses, is what tells him the fault was yours, not his."},{"prompt":"Your son says, 'I thought you hated me.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you love him, and it wasn't his fault","then":"He leans into you, and his shoulders finally drop.","best":true},{"text":"Say he's being dramatic","then":"His face closes, and he pulls away."},{"text":"Say you'd never hate him, so he shouldn't think that","then":"He nods, but looks as if he's done something wrong again."},{"text":"Promise it will never happen again","then":"He brightens, and you wonder how you'll keep that promise."}],"why":"Saying it wasn't his fault lifts the blame he's been carrying."},{"prompt":"Next day, he's stuck on homework again, and you feel the old frustration rise. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Breathe, and sit with him through the hard part","then":"It's slow, but he finishes the sum and grins at you.","best":true},{"text":"Tell him you'll leave if he doesn't focus","then":"He tenses up, and the mistakes get worse."},{"text":"Do the homework for him to avoid a fight","then":"It's done fast, but he hasn't learned a thing."},{"text":"Stay quiet and just watch him struggle","then":"He keeps glancing at you, unsure if you're angry."}],"why":"A calmer next time is what makes the repair real. That's how trust rebuilds."},{"prompt":"At the weekend, your wife says apologising to children makes fathers look weak. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say owning a mistake earns his respect, not loses it","then":"She thinks about it. 'My father never once said sorry,' she says quietly.","best":true},{"text":"Agree, and say you won't make a habit of it","then":"She nods. You decide to keep the next sorry to yourself."},{"text":"Tell her she doesn't understand children","then":"She bristles, and the talk turns into a squabble."},{"text":"Say it was a one-off, since he was really upset","then":"She nods. Next time, the sorry feels harder to say."}],"why":"Apologising to a child models respect and strengthens your bond, and your authority."}],"debrief":"After you snap, go back, own it and reconnect when calm; repair is what your child remembers and learns.","relearn":"Repair after a snap by owning it and reconnecting; pretending it didn't happen leaves the hurt unhealed."},
  {"id":"bc-024","cat":"the-repair","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"clean apology","hook":"You lost your temper and shouted at your daughter Pooja when she spilled ink on the shop ledger.","setup":"Now she sits on the back step, not looking at you.","steps":[{"prompt":"Pooja whispers: \"Are you still angry with me, Papa?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm sorry I shouted. That wasn't okay, and it wasn't your fault.\"","then":"Pooja looks up, eyes wet, and edges a little closer.","best":true},{"text":"\"I only shouted because you made me so angry.\"","then":"She hugs her knees tighter. \"Sorry, Papa. I'm always bad.\""},{"text":"\"No, no. Come, let's go and buy some jalebi.\"","then":"She takes your hand, still quiet. The shouting goes unmentioned for now."},{"text":"\"I'm sorry, beta, but you must be more careful with the ledger.\"","then":"Pooja nods. \"Sorry, Papa,\" she says, and the apology becomes hers."}],"why":"A clean sorry owns what you did and tells her plainly that it wasn't her fault."},{"prompt":"Pooja says: \"But the ledger is ruined because of me.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes, and now it will take me hours to rewrite all of it.\"","then":"Her face crumples. She hears that the shouting was earned."},{"text":"\"Forget the ledger. It doesn't matter at all.\"","then":"She frowns. She saw how upset you were and doesn't quite believe you."},{"text":"\"Next time, keep the ink far from the books.\"","then":"She nods seriously. The fear in her eyes hasn't gone."},{"text":"\"The ink was an accident. The shouting was my mistake.\"","then":"\"So you're not angry with me?\" she asks, starting to smile.","best":true}],"why":"Separating her accident from your reaction lets her set down blame that isn't hers."},{"prompt":"Pooja asks: \"Will you shout like that again?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Never again. I promise you that with my whole heart.\"","then":"She beams. You feel the weight of a promise that's hard to keep."},{"text":"\"Only if you make me that angry again.\"","then":"Pooja goes still, already planning how to be perfect."},{"text":"\"I'm working on it. If I slip, I'll come and say sorry.\"","then":"Pooja thinks, then nods. \"Okay. I'll tell you if you get loud.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Grown-ups shout sometimes. That's just life.\"","then":"She shrugs, as if loud anger is simply how homes are."}],"why":"An honest promise to keep trying, and to repair when you slip, is one you can keep."}],"relearn":"A clean apology, owning it without blaming the child, repairs the bond and models accountability."},
  {"id":"bc-025","cat":"the-repair","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Apologising to a child makes you lose authority.\"","myth":{"un":"Apologising to a child makes you lose authority.","re":"Apologising models honesty and respect and actually strengthens your bond and your authority, not weakens it.","why":"Owning a mistake earns respect."},"relearn":"Apologising to your child models respect and strengthens your bond and authority; it doesn't weaken them.","persona":"Vikram","source":"apology builds authority"},
  {"id":"bc-026","cat":"the-repair","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"parts of repair","relearn":"Owning it, removing blame, reassuring safety, keeping the limit and reconnecting are the parts of a repair that restore a child's security.","hook":"Match each part of a good repair to what it gives the child.","pairs":[{"left":"Owning what you did","right":"Honesty modelled"},{"left":"Saying it's not their fault","right":"Their security restored"},{"left":"Reconnecting warmly","right":"The bond made whole again"},{"left":"Reassuring they're safe","right":"Their calm returns"},{"left":"Keeping the fair limit","right":"Boundaries still hold kindly"}]},
  {"id":"bc-027","cat":"the-repair","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: kids don't need perfect parents; they need parents who come back and make it right. Resonate?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Resonates","Come back","Not perfect"],"affirm":"Children don't need perfection; they need parents who repair, who come back and make a rupture right.","relearn":"Kids don't need perfect parents, just ones who come back and make it right.","persona":"any","source":"come back"},
  {"id":"bc-028","cat":"the-repair","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"healthy repair","relearn":"A healthy repair owns it and reconnects warmly; blaming the child or buying them off instead of apologising is not repair.","hook":"Spot the two moves that look like repair but aren't.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"A genuine apology and reconnection","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Blaming the child for your reaction","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Reassuring them they're loved","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Buying a treat instead of saying sorry","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Owning the slip without excuses","trick":false}],"why":"Blaming the child for your reaction and buying them off instead of apologising both skip the ownership a real repair needs."},
  {"id":"bc-029","cat":"the-repair","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"never too late","hook":"Two days ago you snapped at your crying baby. It still weighs on you, and it feels too late to fix.","steps":[{"prompt":"This morning, she's babbling on her mat. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Hold her close and talk to her softly","then":"She grabs your finger and gurgles. Something in your chest loosens.","best":true},{"text":"Leave it. She's too little to remember","then":"You keep your distance, and the guilt sits with you all day."},{"text":"Busy yourself with chores and let her play","then":"She plays alone, and the space between you feels bigger."},{"text":"Wait for a perfect calm day to reconnect","then":"The perfect day doesn't come, and the weight stays."}],"why":"It's almost never too late to reconnect. Warmth offered now still reaches her."},{"prompt":"That evening, your husband Karan finds you in tears about it. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell him, and ask for help on hard evenings","then":"Karan hugs you. 'I'll take her after seven,' he says.","best":true},{"text":"Say you're a terrible mother","then":"Karan tries to reassure you, but the words don't reach you."},{"text":"Say it's nothing, just tiredness","then":"He nods and goes back to his phone, and you stay alone with it."},{"text":"Tell him, but insist you'll cope alone","then":"He offers help. You refuse, and the next hard evening is all yours."}],"why":"Sharing the weight and asking for support makes repair, and the next evening, easier."},{"prompt":"A week later, an aunt remarks that babies never forget a harsh voice, and the guilt returns. What do you tell yourself?","options":[{"text":"Repair counts, and I keep coming back to her","then":"The guilt eases. You pick her up and she nuzzles into your neck.","best":true},{"text":"I've already ruined it. It's too late","then":"The thought spirals, and you hold her stiffly all afternoon."},{"text":"I must never slip again, not even once","then":"The pressure makes every cry feel like a test you might fail."},{"text":"It was one moment. I'll just forget it","then":"The guilt fades a little, but so does the chance to learn from it."}],"why":"Children don't need a perfect parent. They need one who keeps coming back to repair."}],"debrief":"It's almost never too late to repair; reconnect whenever you can, not only in the perfect moment.","relearn":"Reach for repair even later; reconnection restores trust whenever it's offered."},
  {"id":"bc-030","cat":"the-repair","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: every repair teaches your child that love survives conflict, a priceless lesson. Worth it?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Worth it","Love survives conflict","Priceless"],"affirm":"Each repair teaches a child that love and connection survive conflict, one of the most valuable lessons there is.","relearn":"Every repair teaches your child that love survives conflict, a priceless thing to learn.","persona":"any","source":"love survives"},
  {"id":"bc-031","cat":"the-repair","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"true vs false repair","relearn":"A clean sorry and warm reconnection truly repair; a blaming 'sorry but', ignoring it, a bribe or pressuring don't heal.","hook":"Sort: a true repair, or a false one that doesn't heal?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'm sorry, that wasn't okay\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"Sorry, but you made me do it\""},{"id":"c","text":"Warm reconnection after"},{"id":"d","text":"Acting like nothing happened"},{"id":"e","text":"\"It wasn't your fault, I love you\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"Forgive me right now, say it\""}],"bins":[{"id":"true","label":"True repair","valence":"pos"},{"id":"false","label":"Doesn't heal","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"true","b":"false","c":"true","d":"false","e":"true","f":"false"}},
  {"id":"bc-032","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If I'm furious, I can't help how I react to my child.\"","myth":{"un":"If I'm furious, I can't help how I react to my child.","re":"Strong feelings are valid, but there's a gap between feeling and acting, and you can learn to use it to choose your response.","why":"You can feel the anger and still choose the action."},"relearn":"Anger is valid, but there's a gap between feeling and acting; you can learn to pause and choose your response.","persona":"Suresh","source":"feeling vs acting"},
  {"id":"bc-033","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"step away to calm","hook":"Rohan knocks your phone into a bucket of water, and the anger surging up scares you.","steps":[{"prompt":"He's standing right in front of you, frozen. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Grab his arm and demand to know why he did that","then":"He winces at your grip and goes silent. You hear your father in your own voice."},{"text":"Stay and force out a calm voice while you're still boiling","then":"Your words come out tight and clipped. Rohan stares at the floor, bracing."},{"text":"Scold him hard right now, while the lesson is fresh","then":"Your voice fills the house. Rohan bursts into tears and runs off."},{"text":"Check he's safe, then step into the next room to breathe","then":"By the window, you take slow breaths. After a few minutes your hands stop shaking.","best":true}],"why":"Stepping away while he's safe gives the surge time to pass before you act on it."},{"prompt":"An hour later you're calmer, but the phone is still dead. What do you do about it?","options":[{"text":"Ban his cricket for a month so he never forgets","then":"He storms off. You can tell he'll remember the ban far more than the phone."},{"text":"Let it go completely. He's had enough of a fright today","then":"Rohan looks relieved, and a bit puzzled about what he did wrong."},{"text":"Leave it to your spouse to handle, so you stay out of it","then":"Your spouse talks to him. Rohan still watches you warily at dinner."},{"text":"Tell Rohan calmly what went wrong and have him help dry it","then":"Rohan fetches a towel and dries it off himself. He says sorry without being asked.","best":true}],"why":"A calm talk and a way to help make it right teach him more than fear or silence."},{"prompt":"That night you lie awake, thinking about how big the anger felt. What do you do with that?","options":[{"text":"Plan what you'll do next time it surges: step away first","then":"You picture the next room, the window, the breaths. It feels doable.","best":true},{"text":"Decide you're simply short-tempered, just like your father","then":"The thought sits heavy. It feels like there's nothing to be done."},{"text":"Put it out of your mind. It all worked out in the end","then":"You drift off. The surge is forgotten until the next time."},{"text":"Promise yourself you'll never feel that angry again","then":"The promise feels strong tonight. You're not sure how you'll keep it."}],"why":"A simple plan you can repeat turns stepping away into a habit, not luck."},{"prompt":"A week later, Rohan spills tea on your papers, and the heat rises again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Clench your jaw and say nothing at all","then":"The silence is heavy. Rohan tiptoes around you until bedtime."},{"text":"Say 'I need a minute' and go breathe by the window","then":"When you come back, you can talk about the tea without shaking.","best":true},{"text":"Count to three under your breath, then let him have it","then":"The count helps for a moment. Then the shout comes anyway."},{"text":"Walk out of the house for the rest of the evening","then":"You cool down outside. Rohan spends the evening wondering if you're still cross."}],"why":"Naming your pause out loud keeps him safe and shows him how to handle big anger."}],"debrief":"When anger surges toward your child, if it's safe, step away to calm down before responding.","relearn":"Step away to calm down when anger surges; protecting your child in that moment comes first."},
  {"id":"bc-034","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"match","hook":"Match each calming tool to the moment it helps.","pairs":[{"left":"A few slow breaths","right":"The first rush of anger"},{"left":"Stepping into another room","right":"When you feel close to losing it"},{"left":"Naming your feeling silently","right":"Catching the surge early"},{"left":"Cool water on your face","right":"When heat is flushing your skin"},{"left":"Asking someone to tag in","right":"When you've nothing left to give"}],"relearn":"Slow breaths, a short step away, naming the feeling, cool water and a hand-off each help you ride out a surge without harm.","persona":"any","source":"calming tools"},
  {"id":"bc-035","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Walking away from my child in anger means I'm a failure.\"","myth":{"un":"Walking away from my child in anger means I'm a failure.","re":"Stepping away to keep everyone safe is a responsible, strong move, not a failure; you can return calm.","why":"A safe pause is strength, not surrender."},"relearn":"Stepping away to stay safe and calm isn't failure; it's a responsible, strong move, and you return calmer.","persona":"Suresh","source":"stepping away is strong"},
  {"id":"bc-036","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a healthy way to handle your own anger, or one that risks harm?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Breathing and a short pause"},{"id":"b","text":"Taking it out on the child"},{"id":"c","text":"Stepping away to cool down"},{"id":"d","text":"Bottling it until you explode"},{"id":"e","text":"Asking another adult to step in"},{"id":"f","text":"Grabbing or shaking in frustration"}],"bins":[{"id":"healthy","label":"Healthy regulation","valence":"pos"},{"id":"risk","label":"Risks harm","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"healthy","b":"risk","c":"healthy","d":"risk","e":"healthy","f":"risk"},"relearn":"Breathing, stepping away and asking another adult in regulate anger safely; taking it out on the child, bottling it or grabbing risk harm.","persona":"any","source":"regulate vs risk"},
  {"id":"bc-037","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"know your triggers","hook":"For the third evening running, you've snapped at the kids during homework hour.","steps":[{"prompt":"On Sunday you get a quiet moment to think it over. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask your spouse to take over homework from now on","then":"Your spouse agrees, a little tired. You still flare up during the school rush."},{"text":"Decide to just try harder to be patient this week","then":"You feel determined, with no idea yet what you'll do differently."},{"text":"Blame the kids for being extra difficult lately","then":"The thought is comforting for a moment. Nothing about the evenings changes."},{"text":"Write down the moments that keep setting you off","then":"The list is short: homework hour, the school rush, and the TV at bedtime.","best":true}],"why":"Naming your flashpoints is the first step to meeting them ready instead of ambushed."},{"prompt":"Monday evening comes, and the familiar tension starts to build. How do you prepare?","options":[{"text":"Warn the kids up front that you're in no mood for nonsense","then":"The kids go stiff and quiet. The evening is slow and tense."},{"text":"Plan one calm move first: water, three slow breaths","then":"When the first eraser flies, you're already holding the glass. You breathe.","best":true},{"text":"Tell yourself firmly that tonight you will not shout","then":"The resolve holds for ten minutes. Then the old snap slips out."},{"text":"Keep the TV on so everyone stays quiet","then":"It's quiet, but nothing gets done. The fight just moves to bedtime."}],"why":"A calm move planned in advance is there for you when the trigger hits."},{"prompt":"By the weekend you notice the school rush is still a flashpoint. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Accept that mornings will always be a shouting match","then":"The shouting carries on. The kids start dreading breakfast."},{"text":"Shift one thing: lay out bags and clothes the night before","then":"The next morning is still busy, but nobody gets shouted at by the door.","best":true},{"text":"Wake the kids an hour early and hurry them through it","then":"Everyone is groggy and cranky. The rush is longer, not calmer."},{"text":"Give up on planning, since it didn't fix everything","then":"Without a plan, the next week feels just like the last one."}],"why":"Each trigger you plan for is progress. Change comes one flashpoint at a time."}],"debrief":"Learn the situations that trigger your temper and plan a calm response in advance.","relearn":"Spot your recurring triggers and plan a calm response, rather than being ambushed again and again."},
  {"id":"bc-038","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the moment you notice you're escalating is the moment you get your power back. Make sense?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Makes sense","I get my power back","Notice and choose"],"affirm":"Noticing that you're escalating is the moment you regain the power to choose a different response.","relearn":"The moment you notice you're escalating is the moment you can choose differently.","persona":"any","source":"notice to choose"},
  {"id":"bc-039","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two red flags: the moves that make a heated moment more dangerous.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Pausing and breathing before reacting","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Reacting instantly from the anger","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Stepping away until you're calm","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Letting the shouting keep building","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Lowering your voice on purpose","trick":false}],"why":"Reacting instantly and letting the shouting build escalate the danger; pause, breath, distance and a soft voice keep it safe.","relearn":"Reacting straight from anger or letting a shout build makes a moment dangerous; pause, breath and distance keep it safe.","persona":"any","source":"keep it safe"},
  {"id":"bc-040","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"gentle hand-off","hook":"Your partner Neha is shouting at your son Ayaan over spilt juice, and her voice keeps climbing.","setup":"You walk into the kitchen.","steps":[{"prompt":"Neha says: \"He never listens! I've told him a hundred times!\"","options":[{"text":"\"You're right. He never listens. Ayaan, say sorry now!\"","then":"Ayaan sobs louder. Neha folds her arms. \"Finally, someone backs me up.\""},{"text":"\"Let's both deal with him together, right now.\"","then":"Neha keeps going, louder with you beside her. \"Tell him!\""},{"text":"\"Take a breather. I've got this one. We'll talk later.\"","then":"Neha exhales. \"Okay. Thanks,\" she says, and steps out.","best":true},{"text":"\"What is wrong with you? Calm down!\"","then":"Neha turns on you. \"Don't you tell me to calm down!\""}],"why":"A calm hand-off gives her a way out of the surge without blame."},{"prompt":"Ayaan whispers: \"I didn't mean to spill it.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I know. Accidents happen. Let's clean it up together.\"","then":"Ayaan nods and grabs a cloth. \"Can I do the floor bit?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Go to your room while we sort this out.\"","then":"He trudges off. \"Nobody listens to me either,\" he mutters."},{"text":"\"It's fine. I'll clean it. Go and play.\"","then":"\"Okay!\" He runs off, and the cloth stays where it was."},{"text":"\"You should be more careful. Look how upset Amma is.\"","then":"Ayaan's lip wobbles. \"I'm always the bad one.\""}],"why":"Calm and practical helps him learn from an accident without shame."},{"prompt":"Later, Neha sighs: \"I hate how I sound when I get like that.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I get it. Want a signal, so either of us can tag out?\"","then":"Neha smiles tiredly. \"Yes. Maybe a tap on the shoulder?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Then just try harder not to shout.\"","then":"\"I do try,\" she snaps. \"Every single day.\""},{"text":"\"Honestly, you do get scary sometimes.\"","then":"Her face closes. \"Great. So I'm the monster now.\""},{"text":"\"Don't worry. It's nothing. Kids forget.\"","then":"She nods, unconvinced. \"It doesn't feel like nothing.\""}],"why":"A shared tag-out signal is a plan you can both use, without blame."},{"prompt":"Neha asks: \"And what if it's you losing it next time?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then just leave me to handle it my way.\"","then":"She frowns. \"So I just stand there and watch you lose it?\""},{"text":"\"Then you step in and send me out for a breather.\"","then":"Neha laughs. \"Deal. We back each other up.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's not plan for fights. It'll only jinx it.\"","then":"She shrugs. \"Okay. Then we're guessing every time.\""},{"text":"\"That won't happen. I don't lose my temper.\"","then":"Neha raises an eyebrow. \"Really? What about last Sunday?\""}],"why":"Co-parents who back each other up both ways can break the cycle together."}],"relearn":"A calm hand-off, like 'Take a breather. I've got this,' defuses the moment far better than shouting at your partner too."},
  {"id":"bc-041","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: regulating yourself first is how you teach your child to regulate themselves. Worth practising?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Worth it","I model it","Practising"],"affirm":"When you regulate your own emotions, you model and teach your child how to regulate theirs.","relearn":"Regulating yourself first is how you teach your child to regulate themselves.","persona":"any","source":"model regulation"},
  {"id":"bc-042","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"My own childhood is in the past and has nothing to do with how I parent.\"","myth":{"un":"My own childhood is in the past and has nothing to do with how I parent.","re":"Unhealed wounds often resurface in parenting, in our triggers and reactions; tending them helps the cycle stop with you.","why":"The past leaks into the present until it's tended."},"relearn":"Unhealed childhood wounds often resurface in parenting; tending them helps the harmful cycle stop with you.","persona":"Suresh","source":"past leaks in"},
  {"id":"bc-043","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: healing your own wounds isn't self-indulgence; it's how you protect your child from inheriting them. Resonate?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Resonates","Protects them","Worth tending"],"affirm":"Tending your own old wounds protects your child from inheriting them; it's a gift to them, not self-indulgence.","relearn":"Healing your own wounds protects your child from inheriting them; it's for them as much as you.","persona":"Suresh","source":"protect from inheriting"},
  {"id":"bc-044","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"heal vs running","hook":"Sort: helps you heal and parent better, or keeps the wound running?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Acknowledging what hurt you"},{"id":"b","text":"Insisting you're completely fine"},{"id":"c","text":"Seeking support for your past"},{"id":"d","text":"Numbing and never looking"},{"id":"e","text":"Letting yourself grieve it"},{"id":"f","text":"Snapping at your kids instead"}],"bins":[{"id":"heal","label":"Helps you heal","valence":"pos"},{"id":"running","label":"Keeps it running","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"heal","b":"running","c":"heal","d":"running","e":"heal","f":"running"},"relearn":"Acknowledging the hurt, seeking support and grieving help you heal; insisting you're fine, numbing and snapping keep it running.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-045","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Getting help for my own past means I'm weak or broken.\"","myth":{"un":"Getting help for my own past means I'm weak or broken.","re":"Reaching for support to process your past is a strong, loving act, for yourself and for your child.","why":"Tending wounds is strength, not brokenness."},"relearn":"Getting help to process your past isn't weakness; it's a strong, loving act for you and your child.","persona":"Suresh","source":"help is strength"},
  {"id":"bc-046","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"tend the root","hook":"You realise some of your sharpest reactions to your kids come from pain you've never dealt with.","steps":[{"prompt":"One evening, a small thing sets you off, and you catch yourself. What do you do with that?","options":[{"text":"Take it seriously: this is old pain, not just a bad day","then":"It's uncomfortable to admit. But something in you feels clearer.","best":true},{"text":"Push it down and hope it stays buried","then":"It goes quiet for a while, then leaks out at the next small thing."},{"text":"Decide it's just who you are and can't change","then":"The thought feels heavy, and you stop trying to change."},{"text":"Put it down to a long week and move on","then":"It helps tonight. Tomorrow a small thing sets you off again."}],"why":"Noticing where the pain comes from is the first step. The old hurt was never your fault."},{"prompt":"Your friend Priya notices you seem low and asks what's up. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Joke that your father raised a tough kid","then":"She laughs, but her eyes say she's not convinced."},{"text":"Say you're fine, just a bit tired from the long week","then":"She nods. The weight stays where it was."},{"text":"Ask her how to stop being angry, but not why you are","then":"She gives you tips. They help for a day or two."},{"text":"Tell her honestly about the old pain and your reactions","then":"Priya listens. 'You're worried about it. That means you're already changing.'","best":true}],"why":"Speaking about it to someone you trust takes some of the weight off."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later, the old pain still rises most evenings. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Call Tele-MANAS 14416 to talk it through with a counsellor","then":"The counsellor listens without judging. You hang up lighter, with a next step.","best":true},{"text":"Work it off at the gym, and never talk about it","then":"You feel strong at the gym, and still snap at home."},{"text":"Keep going alone. Needing help would mean you're weak","then":"The weight grows. The snaps at home get sharper."},{"text":"Read up on childhood wounds online late into the night","then":"You learn a lot, but feel more alone with it."}],"why":"Reaching for support to heal is caring and brave, never shameful."},{"prompt":"At home, your son spills milk, and you feel the old heat rise. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Snap at him, then feel guilty all night","then":"His face falls. You lie awake replaying it."},{"text":"Tell yourself you should be healed by now","then":"The frustration adds to the heat, and your voice sharpens."},{"text":"Notice the old pain, breathe, and answer him gently","then":"The heat passes. 'It's just milk,' you say, and hand him a cloth.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing and walk away without explaining","then":"He watches you go, confused and a little scared."}],"why":"Tending old wounds gives you room to answer your child from the present."}],"debrief":"When old pain drives your reactions, gently seek support to work through it rather than pushing it down.","relearn":"Seek support for unhealed pain behind your reactions; burying it keeps it surfacing in your parenting."},
  {"id":"bc-047","type":"match","persona":"Suresh","source":"steps of healing","hook":"Match each step of healing to what it makes possible.","pairs":[{"left":"Naming what happened to you","right":"Understanding your triggers"},{"left":"Grieving and processing it","right":"Less of it leaking onto your child"},{"left":"Getting support (e.g. Tele-MANAS 14416)","right":"A real path through it"},{"left":"Being kind to your younger self","right":"The old self-blame easing"},{"left":"Choosing differently now","right":"The cycle stopping with you"}],"relearn":"Naming, processing, getting support and self-kindness are the steps that turn an old wound into something that stops with you.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-048","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you can give your child the childhood you didn't have, and healing is how. Feel the hope in that?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Feel it","The hope","I can"],"affirm":"By tending your own wounds, you can give your child a childhood different from, and better than, your own.","relearn":"You can give your child the childhood you didn't have, and healing yourself is how.","persona":"Suresh","source":"the childhood you didn't have"},
  {"id":"bc-049","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"This game can heal my childhood trauma on its own.\"","myth":{"un":"This game can heal my childhood trauma on its own.","re":"This offers compassion and signposting, not therapy; deeper wounds deserve real professional support.","why":"Support tools point to care; they don't replace it."},"relearn":"This game supports and signposts but isn't therapy; deeper wounds deserve professional support.","persona":"any","source":"not therapy"},
  {"id":"bc-050","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"stop with you","hook":"Spot the two moves that keep the cycle running instead of stopping it.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Tending your own old wounds","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Insisting the past doesn't matter","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Reaching for support when it's heavy","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Taking the old pain out on your kids","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Naming what happened, kindly","trick":false}],"why":"Denying the past and taking pain out on your kids keep the cycle alive; tending wounds, support and naming stop it with you.","relearn":"The cycle stops with you when you tend your wounds, seek support and name the past, not when you deny it or lash out.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-051","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: every wound you heal in yourself is one your child never has to carry. Sit right?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Sits right","One less to carry","For them"],"affirm":"Each wound you heal in yourself is one your child won't have to carry or pass on further.","relearn":"Every wound you heal in yourself is one your child never has to carry.","persona":"any","source":"one less to carry"},
  {"id":"bc-052","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"ready plan","hook":"Evenings after closing the shop are when your temper runs shortest with your son Manoj.","steps":[{"prompt":"You want something to hold on to when the anger rises. What do you set up?","options":[{"text":"Nothing. You'll just try harder to hold it in","then":"You start the week determined. By Wednesday evening, the anger wins again."},{"text":"A plan: pause, breathe, step away if needed, repair after","then":"Writing it down, you feel steadier. It's short enough to remember when tired.","best":true},{"text":"A rule that Manoj keeps out of your way after seven","then":"Manoj keeps away, confused, and your temper finds other targets."},{"text":"A promise to yourself not to shout this week","then":"The promise feels strong on Monday, but gives you nothing to do when anger comes."}],"why":"A ready plan gives you a path to follow when willpower runs out mid-surge."},{"prompt":"On Thursday, Manoj knocks over a rice sack just as you're closing. What do you do first?","options":[{"text":"Stop and take three slow breaths before speaking","then":"The first rush passes. You can hear yourself think again.","best":true},{"text":"Tell him off quickly, before the anger builds","then":"Your voice comes out louder than you meant, and Manoj flinches."},{"text":"Sweep it up in silence, jaw clenched","then":"Manoj hovers, unsure, as the silence grows heavy."},{"text":"Send him home at once so you don't explode","then":"He leaves in tears, sure he's in deep trouble."}],"why":"Pausing to breathe first catches the surge before it turns into words you regret."},{"prompt":"At dinner, Manoj is unusually quiet. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Leave it. Nothing bad really happened this time","then":"Manoj eats in silence, unsure where he stands."},{"text":"Tell him he's lucky you're trying a new plan","then":"Manoj nods, confused. The plan now sounds like a warning."},{"text":"Buy him a treat tomorrow and say nothing","then":"He enjoys it, but still wonders whether you're cross."},{"text":"Talk about the rice and own any sharpness","then":"Manoj brightens. \"I thought you'd be really angry,\" he admits.","best":true}],"why":"Repair is the last part of the plan. Talking it through reconnects you, whatever happened."}],"debrief":"Ready a simple plan for heated moments: pause, breathe, step away if needed, repair after.","relearn":"Have a concrete pause-breathe-step-away-repair plan ready; willpower alone tends to fail mid-surge."},
  {"id":"bc-053","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each tool to what it does.","pairs":[{"left":"The calm-down-before-you-react step","right":"Stops a harmful reaction"},{"left":"The repair routine","right":"Heals a rupture after"},{"left":"Support routes (Tele-MANAS 14416)","right":"Help for your own past"},{"left":"Knowing your own triggers","right":"Catches the old reflex early"},{"left":"A ready pause-plan","right":"A path in the heat of the moment"}],"relearn":"The calm-down step, the repair routine, support routes, knowing triggers and a ready plan are the core tools.","persona":"any","source":"the toolkit"},
  {"id":"bc-054","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Wanting to change but slipping sometimes means change is impossible for me.\"","myth":{"un":"Wanting to change but slipping sometimes means change is impossible for me.","re":"Change is gradual and slips are part of it; each pause, each repair, each calmer day is real progress.","why":"Progress isn't all-or-nothing."},"relearn":"Slipping while changing doesn't mean change is impossible; each pause and repair is real progress.","persona":"Suresh","source":"progress not perfection"},
  {"id":"bc-055","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a green-flag move for breaking the cycle, or one to rethink?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Pausing before reacting"},{"id":"b","text":"Hitting when overwhelmed"},{"id":"c","text":"Repairing after a slip"},{"id":"d","text":"Shaming to control"},{"id":"e","text":"Reaching for support"},{"id":"f","text":"Threatening to keep them in line"}],"bins":[{"id":"green","label":"Green-flag move","valence":"pos"},{"id":"rethink","label":"Rethink it","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"green","b":"rethink","c":"green","d":"rethink","e":"green","f":"rethink"},"relearn":"Pausing, repairing and reaching for support are green-flag moves; hitting, shaming and threatening need a rethink.","persona":"any","source":"green or rethink"},
  {"id":"bc-056","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"reach for help now","hook":"Last night your anger boiled over and you grabbed your son Deepak's arm hard. This morning there's a bruise.","steps":[{"prompt":"You feel sick with fear about what your anger could do next. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Stay silent and hope it never happens again","then":"The fear sits heavy in your chest, and nothing around you changes."},{"text":"Reach out for help today, to keep Deepak safe","then":"Deciding to say it out loud is frightening, but the weight starts to shift.","best":true},{"text":"Swear to yourself it will never happen again","then":"The promise feels solid this morning. You still have no plan for tonight."},{"text":"Tell yourself one bruise isn't that serious","then":"The worry quietens for a while, and the risk stays exactly where it was."}],"why":"Reaching for help when you fear harming your child is brave and caring, never shameful."},{"prompt":"Deepak is quiet at breakfast and keeps his sleeve pulled down. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say sorry, ask to see his arm, say it wasn't his fault","then":"Deepak lets you look, and some of the stiffness leaves his shoulders.","best":true},{"text":"Say sorry, then ask him to keep it between you","then":"Deepak nods, relieved by the sorry but uneasy about the secret."},{"text":"Act normal so that he forgets about it","then":"He watches you carefully all through breakfast."},{"text":"Tell him to behave so it doesn't happen again","then":"Deepak nods fast, believing the bruise was his fault."}],"why":"Owning it and saying it wasn't his fault, with no secrets, helps him feel safe with you again."},{"prompt":"By afternoon, the fear still hasn't eased. Where do you turn?","options":[{"text":"Ask your father what he would do in your place","then":"He says boys need a firm hand, and the old script starts to sound normal."},{"text":"Look up anger tips online and keep it to yourself","then":"You find some breathing tips, but no one knows you're struggling."},{"text":"Nobody. This is shameful and must stay private","then":"The shame keeps you quiet, and tonight feels as risky as last night."},{"text":"Call Tele-MANAS 14416 and tell them about last night","then":"The person on the line listens without judging and helps you plan for tonight.","best":true}],"why":"A trained listener helps you plan. Deepak has been hurt, so Childline 1098 can help keep him safe too."},{"prompt":"That night, Deepak tests you again and the anger surges fast. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Stay put and force yourself to keep calm","then":"You grip the table, barely holding on, as Deepak watches your face."},{"text":"Warn him loudly not to push you tonight","then":"Deepak freezes, and the room feels as tense as last night."},{"text":"Make sure he's safe, step away, and breathe it out","then":"In the next room, your hands stop shaking. Deepak is safe.","best":true},{"text":"Give him whatever he wants to avoid a blow-up","then":"The moment passes, and he learns that pushing gets him his way."}],"why":"Stepping away while he's safe is the caring act. If he's ever in danger, call Childline 1098 or 112."}],"debrief":"If you fear your anger could harm your child, or it has, reach out for help now (see Be the Safe Adult).","relearn":"When anger risks real harm to your child, reach for help now; don't carry it alone."},
  {"id":"bc-057","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: needing help to parent gently isn't shameful; it's the most caring thing you can do for your child. Hear that?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["I hear it","Most caring","Not shameful"],"affirm":"Reaching for help to parent more gently is a caring, courageous act, never a shameful one.","relearn":"Needing help to parent gently isn't shameful; it's one of the most caring things you can do.","persona":"any","source":"not shameful"},
  {"id":"bc-058","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match a parent's need to where it routes.","pairs":[{"left":"Help regulating anger and discipline","right":"Positive-parenting support"},{"left":"Processing your own past","right":"A counsellor or Tele-MANAS 14416"},{"left":"A child at risk of harm","right":"Be the Safe Adult (g69)"},{"left":"A child in immediate danger","right":"Childline 1098"},{"left":"A life-threatening emergency","right":"Emergency 112"}],"relearn":"Anger help, support for your past, and child-safety routing each have a clear place to turn.","persona":"Suresh","source":"where it routes"},
  {"id":"bc-059","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two ideas that aren't a sustainable way to break the cycle.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Small, consistent changes and self-compassion","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Expecting to be perfect overnight","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Pausing, repairing, healing over time","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Quitting the moment you slip","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Counting each calmer day as progress","trick":false}],"why":"Expecting overnight perfection and quitting at a slip aren't sustainable; small consistent changes, self-compassion and counting calmer days are.","relearn":"Break the cycle through small consistent changes and self-compassion over time, not overnight perfection.","persona":"any","source":"sustainable change"},
  {"id":"bc-060","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: notice the pattern, discipline with warmth, repair your slips, heal your wounds, and reach for help. The cycle ends with you. Ready?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Ready","It ends with me","Let's go"],"affirm":"Noticing patterns, disciplining warmly, repairing, healing and reaching for help are how the harmful cycle ends with you.","relearn":"Notice the pattern, discipline warmly, repair, heal, and reach for help, and the cycle ends with you.","persona":"any","source":"ends with me"},
  {"id":"bc-061","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"invite change together","hook":"Your wife Pallavi grew up with harsh discipline and threatens a slap when your daughter Ria talks back.","setup":"After Ria is asleep, you bring it up.","steps":[{"prompt":"Pallavi says: \"What? That's how my mother raised me, and I'm fine.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You're just like your mother. You'll never change.\"","then":"Pallavi's face hardens. \"Easy for you to say. She's never rude to you.\""},{"text":"\"Mine too. But let's choose differently for Ria, together.\"","then":"Pallavi pauses. \"Differently how? She doesn't listen otherwise.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Fine. Handle discipline your way. I'll stay out of it.\"","then":"Pallavi shrugs, then hesitates, as if she hoped for more from you."},{"text":"\"I think it's wrong, and it has to stop right now.\"","then":"Pallavi hears your point, but also a verdict. \"So I'm the bad parent now?\""}],"why":"Naming your shared upbringing invites her to change with you instead of putting her on trial."},{"prompt":"Pallavi asks: \"So what do I do when she's rude to me?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Stay calm, name it, and give a clear consequence. I'll back you.\"","then":"Pallavi considers it. \"And you won't undo it when she cries to you?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Let her be. She'll grow out of it on her own.\"","then":"Pallavi rolls her eyes. \"That's why she's rude.\""},{"text":"\"Leave it to me. I'll handle her from now on.\"","then":"Pallavi feels pushed aside. \"So I'm not trusted with my own daughter?\""},{"text":"\"A slap now and then is fine, just not every time.\"","then":"Pallavi nods, and the threats stay part of the house."}],"why":"Firm and kind gives her a real alternative, and backing each other up makes it work."},{"prompt":"Pallavi asks: \"And what if I lose it anyway, like my mother did?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You won't. You just have to be stronger than that.\"","then":"Pallavi looks unconvinced. \"I've told myself that for years.\""},{"text":"\"Then Ria will learn to fear you, the way you did.\"","then":"Pallavi's eyes fill, and she stops talking."},{"text":"\"We'll deal with that when it happens.\"","then":"Pallavi nods, uneasy, with no plan for the hard moments."},{"text":"\"Then tap me in, and we go back and repair with Ria.\"","then":"Pallavi's shoulders ease. \"Okay. A signal, then. You'll step in?\"","best":true}],"why":"Planning for slips, and for the repair after, makes change realistic instead of all-or-nothing."},{"prompt":"A week later, Pallavi tells you: \"I nearly threatened her today, but I stopped.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Nearly? So you still wanted to.\"","then":"Her smile vanishes. \"Forget I told you.\""},{"text":"\"Good. Now make sure it never happens again.\"","then":"Pallavi nods, the small win already feeling like a test."},{"text":"\"That's huge. How did you manage to stop?\"","then":"Pallavi smiles. \"I remembered what we said, and took a breath.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I've honestly never felt like threatening her.\"","then":"Pallavi goes quiet, feeling judged by the comparison."}],"why":"Noticing her progress warmly keeps her going. Change comes in small steps."}],"relearn":"Inviting change together, with compassion for your shared upbringing, motivates a co-parent far more than condemnation."},
  {"id":"bc-062","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"calm conviction","hook":"A relative says you're 'soft' for not raising your kids the strict way you were raised.","setup":"Your uncle Ramesh says it loudly, over chai at your shop.","steps":[{"prompt":"Ramesh Chacha says: \"In our day, one look from your father and we behaved.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I loved my childhood. Some things I'm doing differently.\"","then":"He raises an eyebrow. \"Differently? Like what?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. Maybe I should be harder on them.\"","then":"He nods. \"Children need to fear a little.\""},{"text":"\"And we were all scared stiff of him. Some childhood.\"","then":"He frowns. \"Scared? That's respect, not fear.\""},{"text":"\"Times are different now, Chacha. That's all.\"","then":"He laughs. \"Children are children in any time.\""}],"why":"Owning what you keep and what you change holds your path without attacking your parents."},{"prompt":"Ramesh Chacha asks: \"So what do you do when they misbehave? Nothing?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Clear rules, held kindly and firmly. I stick to them.\"","then":"He considers this. \"Firm, at least. That's something.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Mostly nothing. They grow out of it.\"","then":"He snorts. \"That's exactly what I mean. Soft.\""},{"text":"\"I don't hit them, if that's what you're asking.\"","then":"He bristles. \"Nobody said hit. I said discipline.\""},{"text":"\"Whatever works in the moment. It depends.\"","then":"He shakes his head. \"So no plan at all.\""}],"why":"Firm and kind isn't soft. Clear limits held with warmth are real discipline."},{"prompt":"Ramesh Chacha says: \"Well, don't blame me when they don't respect you.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'd rather they trust me than fear me, Chacha.\"","then":"He grunts, but he's quieter now. \"We'll see, beta.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. I'll bring back the strictness.\"","then":"He smiles, satisfied. \"That's my boy.\""},{"text":"\"Your kids barely talk to you. I don't want that.\"","then":"His face falls, and he looks away. \"That's not fair.\""},{"text":"\"Let's not talk about this again, please.\"","then":"He shrugs. \"Fine. But I'll be watching.\""}],"why":"Respect grows from trust, not fear. Saying so calmly keeps your line without an attack."}],"relearn":"Owning what you keep and what you change, calmly, holds your new path without attacking your own parents."},
  {"id":"bc-063","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a compassionate truth about inherited parenting, or a shaming one?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"'Most harsh parents were raised that way'"},{"id":"b","text":"'Anyone who slips is a monster'"},{"id":"c","text":"'You can choose differently now'"},{"id":"d","text":"'You're doomed to repeat it'"},{"id":"e","text":"'Noticing the pattern is real progress'"},{"id":"f","text":"'One bad day means you've failed'"}],"bins":[{"id":"compassion","label":"Compassionate truth","valence":"pos"},{"id":"shame","label":"Shaming","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"compassion","b":"shame","c":"compassion","d":"shame","e":"compassion","f":"shame"},"relearn":"'Most were raised that way' and 'you can choose now' are compassionate; 'monster' and 'doomed' are shaming and false.","persona":"any","source":"compassion vs shame","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-064","type":"match","hook":"Match each thing to keep or leave to the reason.","pairs":[{"left":"Keep: family closeness","right":"It nourished you"},{"left":"Leave: hitting","right":"It harms and doesn't work"},{"left":"Keep: hard work","right":"A value worth passing on"},{"left":"Leave: shaming","right":"It wounds a child's self-worth"},{"left":"Keep: showing up","right":"It built your sense of safety"}],"relearn":"Sorting your inheritance, keep the closeness and values, leave the harm, lets you pass on the best of it.","persona":"any","source":"keep-leave reasons","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-065","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two mindsets that keep the old pattern running.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"'I can learn new ways'","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"'This is just who I am, unchangeable'","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"'I choose what stops with me'","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"'It's in my blood, nothing to be done'","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"'I can keep the good and leave the rest'","trick":false}],"why":"'This is just who I am' and 'it's in my blood' keep the old pattern running; 'I can learn' and 'I choose what stops with me' help you change.","relearn":"Parenting differently starts with 'I can learn' and 'I choose what stops with me', not 'this is just who I am'.","persona":"Suresh","source":"growth mindset","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-066","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"calm in public","hook":"Your son Kiaan is melting down in a busy mall, and you feel the old urge to grab and shout.","setup":"People are starting to stare.","steps":[{"prompt":"Kiaan screams: \"I want the toy car! I want it NOW!\"","options":[{"text":"\"Everyone in the shop is staring at you. Aren't you ashamed?\"","then":"He hides his face. \"Don't look at me!\" he wails."},{"text":"\"Okay, okay. Take the car. Just stop crying.\"","then":"He grabs it and goes quiet, for now."},{"text":"\"Stop it right now, or you'll get it when we get home.\"","then":"He screams louder. \"No! I want it!\""},{"text":"\"You're upset. Let's breathe. We're still not buying it.\"","then":"Kiaan sobs, but breathes with you. \"But I really want it.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming the feeling while holding the limit calms a meltdown without threats."},{"prompt":"Kiaan spots a robot at the next shop and wails: \"I need that one!\"","options":[{"text":"\"Fine. But this is the very last time.\"","then":"He grins through his tears. \"Yay!\""},{"text":"\"You really want it. Let's add it to your birthday list.\"","then":"He sniffs. \"Can we write it now?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Stop being so greedy.\"","then":"He cries harder. \"I'm not!\""},{"text":"\"No! You already have more toys than you could ever play with!\"","then":"\"I do NOT!\" he yells, and the argument grows."}],"why":"Acknowledging the wish without giving in helps him cope with the no."},{"prompt":"A stranger nearby says: \"In my day, one slap would fix that.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Sorry, sorry. We're leaving.\"","then":"\"Good,\" she says. Kiaan wails as you rush for the exit."},{"text":"\"Thanks, but I've got this. We do it differently.\"","then":"\"Suit yourself,\" the stranger says, and walks on. Kiaan takes your hand.","best":true},{"text":"\"Mind your own business!\"","then":"\"How rude!\" she huffs, and Kiaan starts crying again."},{"text":"\"You're right. Maybe that's what he needs right now.\"","then":"\"Good,\" she says. Kiaan looks up at you, frightened."}],"why":"You don't owe strangers a show. Calmly holding your approach protects your child."}],"relearn":"Naming the feeling while holding the limit calms a meltdown and disciplines without threats or fear."},
  {"id":"bc-067","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: teaches the child a better choice, or just instils fear?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Explaining why and what to do instead"},{"id":"b","text":"A slap to make them stop"},{"id":"c","text":"A calm, related consequence"},{"id":"d","text":"Yelling threats"},{"id":"e","text":"Offering a small choice within the limit"},{"id":"f","text":"Mocking until they comply"}],"bins":[{"id":"teaches","label":"Teaches a better choice","valence":"pos"},{"id":"fear","label":"Just instils fear","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"teaches","b":"fear","c":"teaches","d":"fear","e":"teaches","f":"fear"},"relearn":"Explaining, calm consequences and a small choice teach a better choice; slaps, yelled threats and mockery only instil fear.","persona":"any","source":"teaches vs fear"},
  {"id":"bc-068","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"why no hitting","hook":"Your daughter Riya says her friend Sonu gets hit at home for bad marks.","setup":"She looks up from her homework.","steps":[{"prompt":"Riya asks: \"Why don't you hit me, like Sonu's papa does?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Don't give me ideas. Behave, or else.\"","then":"Riya goes quiet for a moment, then tries again, softer."},{"text":"\"Every family is different. Now finish your homework.\"","then":"\"Okay,\" she says, picking up her pencil, still curious."},{"text":"\"Because hurting you is wrong. You can learn without it.\"","then":"Riya smiles. \"Sonu says it doesn't even help him learn.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Because you're a good girl. Sonu must be naughty.\"","then":"Riya frowns. \"So if I'm naughty, you'll hit me too?\""}],"why":"Telling her you'll never hurt her builds trust and models non-violence."},{"prompt":"Riya asks: \"Did your papa hit you?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's not talk about your grandfather like that, beta.\"","then":"\"Okay,\" she says, but the question stays in her eyes."},{"text":"\"That's none of your business.\"","then":"Her face closes. \"Sorry,\" she mumbles."},{"text":"\"Yes, and it made me who I am today.\"","then":"She looks puzzled. \"So hitting is good?\""},{"text":"\"Sometimes. He was raised that way. I chose differently.\"","then":"Riya thinks. \"I'm glad you chose differently.\"","best":true}],"why":"Honest words about the past, without blame, show her change is possible."},{"prompt":"Riya asks: \"Should I tell Sonu's papa not to hit him?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Sonu must deserve it sometimes.\"","then":"Riya goes quiet. \"Would I deserve it too?\""},{"text":"\"It's their family's business, beta. You stay out of it, okay?\"","then":"Riya frowns. \"But Sonu's my friend.\""},{"text":"\"I'm glad you told me. It's not Sonu's fault. I'll get help.\"","then":"Riya nods, relieved. \"Maybe his teacher could help too?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Yes, go and tell him tomorrow.\"","then":"Riya looks scared. \"What if he gets angry at me?\""}],"why":"It's never a child's fault. A grown-up can get help, like Childline 1098."}],"relearn":"Telling a child you'll never hurt them, and don't need to, builds trust and models non-violence directly."},
  {"id":"bc-069","cat":"the-repair","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"reopen and repair","hook":"Hours after you snapped at Anvi over spilled juice, she is still quiet and playing alone.","setup":"You sit down near her on the floor.","steps":[{"prompt":"Anvi says, without looking up: \"I'm playing by myself.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Are you still sulking about that? Let it go.\"","then":"Anvi turns her back and keeps playing alone."},{"text":"\"I was wrong to shout earlier. Can we start over?\"","then":"She glances at you. \"You were really loud,\" she says.","best":true},{"text":"\"Okay. Can I play with you, then?\"","then":"She hands you a doll without a word. The earlier moment stays between you."},{"text":"\"I only got cross because juice went everywhere.\"","then":"\"I didn't mean to,\" she mumbles, hunching over her toys."}],"why":"Naming the earlier shout and owning it reopens the door her quiet has closed."},{"prompt":"Anvi says softly: \"You looked like you didn't like me.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I was angry, but I love you. Always.\"","then":"She climbs into your lap without a word.","best":true},{"text":"\"That's silly. Of course I like you.\"","then":"She goes quiet again, her feeling brushed aside."},{"text":"\"You shouldn't think such things about Mumma.\"","then":"She looks down, now worried she said something wrong."},{"text":"\"My mother used to shout at me too, you know.\"","then":"She looks puzzled, unsure what to do with your story."}],"why":"Separating your anger from your love answers the fear underneath her words."},{"prompt":"Anvi asks: \"Will you tell Papa I spilled the juice?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Only if you spill something again.\"","then":"She nods solemnly and holds her cup with both hands at dinner."},{"text":"\"Let's not tell Papa about any of it.\"","then":"She nods, but now a secret about the shouting sits with her."},{"text":"\"No need. A juice spill doesn't matter.\"","then":"She seems relieved, but your shouting is quietly left out of the story."},{"text":"\"I'll tell him it was an accident, and that I shouted.\"","then":"Anvi's face clears. \"Okay. Then we can all have dinner.\"","best":true}],"why":"Owning your part out loud, even to others, shows her the accident was never the problem."}],"relearn":"Reopening with ownership and warmth repairs the rupture; dismissing the child's hurt deepens it."},
  {"id":"bc-070","cat":"the-repair","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"safe vs edge","relearn":"A sincere apology, warmth and reassurance help a child feel safe; ignoring it, blaming them or a cold spell keep them on edge.","hook":"Sort: helps a child feel safe again after a rupture, or keeps them on edge?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A sincere apology"},{"id":"b","text":"Acting like nothing happened"},{"id":"c","text":"Extra warmth and reassurance"},{"id":"d","text":"Making them apologise for your outburst"},{"id":"e","text":"\"You're safe, I'm sorry I scared you\""},{"id":"f","text":"Going cold and silent for days"}],"bins":[{"id":"safe","label":"Helps them feel safe","valence":"pos"},{"id":"edge","label":"Keeps them on edge","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"safe","b":"edge","c":"safe","d":"edge","e":"safe","f":"edge"}},
  {"id":"bc-071","cat":"the-repair","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"slips don't erase","hook":"After weeks of doing well, you slipped and shouted at your kids again, and you feel like giving up.","steps":[{"prompt":"That night, the old voice says all your effort was wasted. What do you tell yourself?","options":[{"text":"One slip doesn't erase weeks of progress","then":"The voice loses a little of its grip. You remember the calm evenings.","best":true},{"text":"It was all pointless. I'm back where I started","then":"You feel the fight drain out of you."},{"text":"The kids pushed me. It wasn't my slip","then":"The guilt eases, and so does any sense that you need to repair."},{"text":"I'll go twice as hard from tomorrow to make up for it","then":"You set yourself a punishing goal, and dread the first mistake."}],"why":"A slip after progress isn't failure. The calmer weeks still count."},{"prompt":"Next morning, your kids are quieter than usual at breakfast. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say sorry for shouting, and it wasn't their fault","then":"Your daughter smiles shyly. Your son says, 'We knew you were tired.'","best":true},{"text":"Act cheerful, as if nothing happened","then":"They smile back carefully, still watching your mood."},{"text":"Tell them they'd better behave today","then":"They nod, eyes down, and the kitchen stays tense."},{"text":"Say sorry, then add that they did make it very hard","then":"They nod, and your son looks down at his plate."}],"why":"Repairing quickly, without blame, reconnects you and shows them slips can be mended."},{"prompt":"A week on, you notice you still judge yourself for that evening. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Forgive yourself, and keep going with what works","then":"The weight lifts a little, and the calm evenings start to return.","best":true},{"text":"Keep reminding yourself so it never happens again","then":"The memory keeps stinging, and you feel tense with the kids."},{"text":"Decide you're just not cut out for this","then":"You stop trying the new ways, and the old ones creep back."},{"text":"Push the feeling away and stay busy","then":"The feeling fades, but the lesson from that night never lands."}],"why":"Self-forgiveness keeps you on the path. Slips are part of change, not the end of it."}],"debrief":"A slip after progress isn't failure; repair, forgive yourself and keep going.","relearn":"Treat a slip as a stumble, not the end; repair, self-forgive and continue rather than giving up."},
  {"id":"bc-072","cat":"the-repair","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"what makes repair land","relearn":"A repair lands when it owns the behaviour, frees the child of blame, restores the bond, keeps the limit and invites their feelings.","hook":"Match each repair phrase to what makes it land.","pairs":[{"left":"\"I was wrong to do that\"","right":"It owns the behaviour"},{"left":"\"It wasn't your fault\"","right":"It frees the child of blame"},{"left":"\"I love you, always\"","right":"It restores the bond"},{"left":"\"The rule still stands, though\"","right":"It keeps the limit firm"},{"left":"\"Tell me how that felt\"","right":"It invites their feelings"}]},
  {"id":"bc-073","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"narrate safe exit","hook":"After a long day, the kids' fight over the TV remote has you close to exploding.","setup":"Both kids turn to you at once.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your son Rohan yells: \"She took it! Tell her to give it back!\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm too angry. I'll cool off in the other room and come back.\"","then":"Rohan blinks. \"Okay,\" he says, and the shouting drops a notch.","best":true},{"text":"\"That's it. I've had it with both of you!\"","then":"Pinky starts to cry. \"We're sorry!\" Rohan whispers, scared."},{"text":"\"Give it here. No TV for anyone, ever again!\"","then":"\"That's not fair!\" Rohan wails, and the fight turns on you."},{"text":"\"Sort it out yourselves. I'm not dealing with this.\"","then":"The fight gets louder. \"Pinky's pulling my hair!\" Rohan shouts."}],"why":"Naming your anger and a calm exit keeps everyone safe and shows them how it's done."},{"prompt":"A little later, Pinky asks quietly: \"Are you still angry with us?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No, no. Everything's fine. Just forget it.\"","then":"She frowns. \"You don't look fine.\""},{"text":"\"Ask your brother. He's the one who started it.\"","then":"\"I did not!\" Rohan shouts from the sofa, and it starts again."},{"text":"\"A bit. But I'm calm now. Let's sort out the remote.\"","then":"Pinky nods. \"Can we take turns? I'll go first.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Yes. And it's your fault for fighting.\"","then":"Her eyes fill up. \"Sorry,\" she whispers."}],"why":"Being honest and calm about your feelings teaches them feelings can be handled."},{"prompt":"At bedtime, Rohan asks: \"Do you ever get really, really angry?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Never. Grown-ups don't get angry like kids do.\"","then":"He frowns. \"But you shouted last week.\""},{"text":"\"Yes. I'm learning to stop and calm down first.\"","then":"He thinks about it. \"Can I do that too, when I'm mad?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Sometimes. That's just how our family is.\"","then":"He shrugs. \"So I'll get angry like that too?\""},{"text":"\"Only when you two misbehave.\"","then":"He looks guilty. \"So it's my fault?\""}],"why":"Telling him how you handle anger teaches him he can do it too."}],"relearn":"Narrating a calm exit models emotional honesty and keeps everyone safe, unlike erupting at the children."},
  {"id":"bc-074","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each one: a real self-regulation skill, or a way of feeding the anger?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Slow breathing"},{"id":"b","text":"Replaying what enraged you"},{"id":"c","text":"A brief, safe time-out for yourself"},{"id":"d","text":"Clenching and pushing on"},{"id":"e","text":"Naming the feeling to settle it"},{"id":"f","text":"Muttering how unfair it all is"}],"bins":[{"id":"regulate","label":"Self-regulation","valence":"pos"},{"id":"feed","label":"Feeds the anger","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"regulate","b":"feed","c":"regulate","d":"feed","e":"regulate","f":"feed"},"relearn":"Breathing, a brief self time-out and naming the feeling regulate anger; replaying the trigger, clenching on and muttering feed it.","persona":"any","source":"regulate vs feed"},
  {"id":"bc-075","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"reduce the load","hook":"You notice you snap most on days you've barely slept and done everything for baby Myra alone.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner Karan asks how he can help this week. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you're managing, since he's got his own work stress","then":"He nods and goes back to his laptop. Nothing changes."},{"text":"Ask him to take two night feeds a week so you can sleep","then":"Karan agrees. On Thursday you sleep five hours straight for the first time in weeks.","best":true},{"text":"Tell him to help whenever he sees you're struggling","then":"He says of course. But he rarely notices, and you rarely ask."},{"text":"List everything he never does around the house","then":"He gets defensive, and the evening ends in a sulk on both sides."}],"why":"A specific ask shares the load in a way someone can actually say yes to."},{"prompt":"Your mother-in-law says new parents shouldn't need afternoon naps. How do you respond?","options":[{"text":"Agree politely, then nap in secret when she's out","then":"You get the rest, but you tense up every time you hear her key."},{"text":"Say rest makes you a calmer parent, and keep your nap","then":"She purses her lips but lets it go. You wake with more patience for the evening.","best":true},{"text":"Skip the nap so she won't think badly of you","then":"She approves. By evening you're frayed and snapping at every cry."},{"text":"Snap back that she has no idea how hard it is","then":"She goes quiet and hurt, and the house feels tense all evening."}],"why":"Rest isn't selfish. It refills the patience Myra needs from you."},{"prompt":"A few weeks on, the load is creeping back up. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Feel guilty that you can't manage it all like others","then":"The guilt adds to the load, and your fuse gets shorter."},{"text":"Push through on willpower until things calm down","then":"It works for a few days. Then one sleepless night, and the snaps are back."},{"text":"Wait until you snap again, then sort it out","then":"The snap comes on Friday. You sort it out afterwards, shaken."},{"text":"Drop a chore, like ironing, and guard your rest time","then":"The week is still full, but you have a little left in the tank when Myra fusses.","best":true}],"why":"Tending your tiredness prevents snaps. Willpower alone runs out when you're empty."}],"debrief":"Notice that exhaustion and overwhelm fuel your snaps; reduce the load and rest to widen your patience.","relearn":"Tend your exhaustion and load to prevent snaps; willpower alone runs out when you're depleted."},
  {"id":"bc-076","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two red flags: responses to your warning signs that make things worse.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Pausing the moment your jaw clenches","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Telling yourself 'I can't stop now'","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Stepping back when your voice rises","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Pushing harder because you've started","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Breathing when your heart pounds","trick":false}],"why":"'I can't stop' and 'push harder' override the warning signs; pausing, stepping back and breathing heed them.","relearn":"Heed early-warning signs by pausing, stepping back and breathing; 'I can't stop' and 'push harder' override them.","persona":"Suresh","source":"early warning"},
  {"id":"bc-077","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"role-play","persona":"Priya","source":"support a friend","hook":"Your friend Kavita admits she's scared she'll repeat her own harsh upbringing with her son.","setup":"You're on a park bench while the kids play.","steps":[{"prompt":"Kavita says: \"What if I turn into my mother? I already hear her in my voice.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You worrying about it means you're already changing it.\"","then":"Kavita looks up. \"I never thought of it like that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"We all turn into our parents eventually.\"","then":"Kavita sighs. \"So there's no point trying, then.\""},{"text":"\"You'd never do that. You're a much gentler person than her.\"","then":"She shakes her head. \"You don't see me at home, though.\""},{"text":"\"Just don't do what she did. It's simple.\"","then":"Kavita frowns. \"It's not that simple when you're this tired.\""}],"why":"Her worry shows she's already noticing the pattern, which is where change starts."},{"prompt":"Kavita says: \"But yesterday I screamed at him, just like she used to.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Every parent slips. Going back to him after is what counts.\"","then":"She nods slowly. \"I did hug him later. I didn't say sorry, though.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Once is fine. Don't worry about it.\"","then":"Kavita nods, but her shoulders stay tight. \"It wasn't once.\""},{"text":"\"Honestly, screaming is a bit much, Kavita.\"","then":"She goes red. \"I know. I shouldn't have told you.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe let your husband handle him when you're tired.\"","then":"She shrugs. \"He's never home by then,\" she says quietly."}],"why":"Normalising the slip and pointing to repair keeps her from spiralling in guilt."},{"prompt":"Kavita asks: \"Is there anyone I could actually talk to about all this?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Tele-MANAS 14416 helps with exactly this.\"","then":"Kavita saves the number. \"I'll call tomorrow, before I lose my nerve.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You don't need that. Just talk to me.\"","then":"Kavita smiles. \"Thanks. I just don't know where to start with it.\""},{"text":"\"Counselling is for serious problems, not this.\"","then":"Kavita's face drops. \"Oh. I suppose I'm overreacting.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe ask your mother how she coped back then.\"","then":"Kavita laughs shortly. \"She'd say she did nothing wrong.\""}],"why":"Pointing her to real support, like Tele-MANAS 14416, gives her more than reassurance alone."}],"relearn":"Affirming that their very worry means they're changing, plus pointing to help, supports a friend far more than fatalism."},
  {"id":"bc-078","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"stops vs passes","hook":"Sort: helps the cycle stop with you, or keeps passing it on?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Tending your own wounds"},{"id":"b","text":"Pretending the past didn't shape you"},{"id":"c","text":"Seeking support for old pain"},{"id":"d","text":"Taking your pain out on your kids"},{"id":"e","text":"Repairing after you're triggered"},{"id":"f","text":"Expecting your child to soothe you"}],"bins":[{"id":"stops","label":"Stops with you","valence":"pos"},{"id":"passes","label":"Passes it on","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"stops","b":"passes","c":"stops","d":"passes","e":"stops","f":"passes"},"relearn":"Tending wounds, seeking support and repairing stop the cycle; denying the past, lashing out and leaning on your child pass it on.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-079","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"acknowledge kindly","hook":"You want to admit to yourself, kindly, that you were hurt as a child.","setup":"On the walk home one evening, an old voice in your head answers back.","steps":[{"prompt":"The old voice says: \"It was nothing. You turned out fine.\"","options":[{"text":"\"What happened to me wasn't okay, and it wasn't my fault.\"","then":"The voice falters. \"But everyone was raised like that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"True. I'm being dramatic about it.\"","then":"The voice settles in. \"Exactly. Now stop dwelling on it.\""},{"text":"\"My father ruined my whole life.\"","then":"The voice sneers. \"So you'll blame him forever, then?\""},{"text":"\"Maybe it hurt, but plenty of people had it far worse than me.\"","then":"The voice agrees quickly. \"So it doesn't count.\""}],"why":"Saying it wasn't okay, and wasn't your fault, is the kindness that lets you stop passing it on."},{"prompt":"The old voice says: \"Dwelling on it just makes you weak.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Looking at it kindly is how I stop passing it on.\"","then":"The voice has less to say. \"And what will you do with it?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. I'll forget it again.\"","then":"The voice goes quiet, satisfied. \"That's the way.\""},{"text":"\"A real man doesn't dwell. I'll toughen up.\"","then":"The voice approves. \"Just like your father taught you.\""},{"text":"\"I'll look at it someday, when I have time.\"","then":"The voice relaxes. \"Someday. Sure.\""}],"why":"Tending the hurt is strength. It stops it leaking into how you treat your own children."},{"prompt":"The old voice says: \"Whatever you do now, your father can't undo any of it.\"","options":[{"text":"\"True. But I can choose something better for my son.\"","then":"The voice is quieter now. \"Something better. Maybe you can.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Then there's nothing to be done about any of it.\"","then":"The voice agrees. \"Exactly. Leave it where it was.\""},{"text":"\"He owes me an apology, and I'll demand one.\"","then":"The voice scoffs. \"And when he refuses, what then?\""},{"text":"\"I'll make sure my son never has a hard day.\"","then":"The voice mutters. \"Nobody can promise that.\""}],"why":"You can't change the past, but seeing it kindly frees you to choose differently now."}],"relearn":"Acknowledging your own hurt with compassion, rather than dismissing it, is what lets you stop passing it on."},
  {"id":"bc-080","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"not alone","hook":"Thinking about your childhood has started bringing up more pain than you can handle alone.","steps":[{"prompt":"One night, a memory of your father's anger keeps you awake. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Get up and scroll until you're too tired to think","then":"You fall asleep at three, the memory waiting for tomorrow."},{"text":"Decide the past is past and there's no point","then":"The memory doesn't agree. It's back the next night."},{"text":"Lie still and force yourself to push through it alone","then":"You lie awake until dawn, exhausted and alone."},{"text":"Tell your spouse what's been coming up for you","then":"Your spouse holds your hand. 'I didn't know it was that bad.'","best":true}],"why":"Sharing the weight with someone close means you don't carry it alone."},{"prompt":"The memories keep coming during the day too. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Handle it entirely alone, the way you always have","then":"The weight gets heavier, and you start snapping at home."},{"text":"Wait a few more months to see if it fades","then":"It doesn't fade. It goes quiet, then comes back louder."},{"text":"Call Tele-MANAS 14416 and talk to a counsellor","then":"The counsellor listens and helps you find a next step.","best":true},{"text":"Confront your parents tonight and demand answers","then":"Your mother cries. Your father shouts. You feel worse, and no less alone."}],"why":"What happened to you wasn't your fault, and you don't have to carry it alone."},{"prompt":"A week later you feel raw, and part of you wants to stop looking back. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Keep facing it gently, with support, at your own pace","then":"Some days are heavy, but each one feels a little more manageable.","best":true},{"text":"Stop, since it's only making things worse","then":"The raw feeling fades, and the old reflexes stay."},{"text":"Tell yourself you turned out fine, so it didn't matter","then":"The thought soothes for a while, but your temper tells a different story."},{"text":"Go all in and dig up every memory at once","then":"It floods you, and you can barely get through the day."}],"why":"Healing goes at your own pace. Slow, supported steps are real progress."},{"prompt":"Your son asks why you've seemed sad lately. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you're fine, and quickly change the subject to school","then":"He nods, but keeps watching your face."},{"text":"Say it's because he's been naughty lately","then":"He goes quiet, and tries hard to be invisible."},{"text":"Tell him everything your father did to you","then":"His eyes go wide. He seems frightened for days."},{"text":"Say you're sorting out old sad feelings, and it's not his fault","then":"He hugs you. 'I hope you feel better soon.'","best":true}],"why":"A simple, honest answer without blame keeps him from feeling responsible."},{"prompt":"Months later, a relative says people who dwell on their childhood are just weak. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Agree, and say you should have just toughed it out","then":"The relative nods. You feel the old silence closing in."},{"text":"Snap that your family is the reason you need it","then":"The relative storms off, and the family gossip starts."},{"text":"Laugh it off and say you're not one of those people","then":"The relative laughs too, and the family silence stays."},{"text":"Say healing takes courage, and support makes it easier","then":"The relative goes quiet. Later, they ask how to find a counsellor.","best":true}],"why":"Speaking of support as normal helps the cycle stop with you."}],"debrief":"When your past brings up more than you can handle alone, reach for a counsellor or helpline, like Tele-MANAS 14416.","relearn":"Get support to process heavy past pain; you don't have to walk through it entirely alone."},
  {"id":"bc-081","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"steady co-parent","hook":"Your wife Shalini yelled at your son Om over homework, and now she's in the kitchen, spiralling.","setup":"You find her standing at the sink.","steps":[{"prompt":"Shalini says: \"I'm a terrible mother. I sounded exactly like my father.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Great. Now you've scarred him for life.\"","then":"Shalini's face crumples, and she turns back to the sink."},{"text":"\"You're not terrible. He was being really difficult.\"","then":"Shalini nods, half convinced. \"He was.\" Then she glances toward Om's room."},{"text":"\"Don't cry. It's honestly not such a big deal.\"","then":"Shalini goes quiet. It still feels like a big deal to her."},{"text":"\"You slipped. It happens to all of us. You can repair it.\"","then":"Shalini wipes her eyes. \"How? He looked so scared.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming the slip without blame steadies her enough to think about repair."},{"prompt":"Shalini asks: \"What do I even say to him?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Tell him you only yelled because he wasn't trying.\"","then":"Shalini frowns. \"That sounds like my father too.\""},{"text":"\"Just give him a chocolate. He'll forget about it.\"","then":"Shalini hesitates, unsure a treat is what he needs."},{"text":"\"That you're sorry you yelled, and it wasn't his fault.\"","then":"Shalini nods. \"Short and honest. I can do that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Nothing yet. Wait till you feel less guilty.\"","then":"Shalini sighs. \"That could take all night.\""}],"why":"A short, clean sorry that owns it is what heals the rupture."},{"prompt":"Shalini says: \"Will you come with me? I don't trust myself not to cry.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'll just talk to him for you. It's easier.\"","then":"Shalini agrees, relieved, and Om never hears it from her."},{"text":"\"I'll sit with you both. You do the talking.\"","then":"Shalini squeezes your hand, and you walk to Om's room together.","best":true},{"text":"\"You need to do this alone, or it won't count.\"","then":"Shalini's shoulders sag, and she stays at the sink a while longer."},{"text":"\"Crying in front of him will only upset him more.\"","then":"Shalini wipes her face hard, trying not to feel anything."}],"why":"Backing her up while she leads the repair lets Om hear it from the one who slipped."}],"relearn":"Steadying a guilt-spiralling co-parent toward repair helps the child far more than piling on blame."},
  {"id":"bc-082","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"shared family rule","hook":"After your son Nikhil hit his sister today, you want a family rule: no one hits when angry, adults too.","setup":"At dinner, you raise it with everyone.","steps":[{"prompt":"Nikhil mutters: \"But you hit me when you're angry.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You're right. So the rule is for all of us, me too.\"","then":"Nikhil looks up, startled. \"Even you?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Do as I say, not as I do.\"","then":"Nikhil stares at his plate. The rule already sounds unfair."},{"text":"\"That's different. I'm your father.\"","then":"Nikhil shrugs. \"So hitting is for grown-ups.\""},{"text":"\"Let's not talk about me. This is about your sister.\"","then":"Nikhil scowls. He hears that the rule is only for him."}],"why":"Including yourself makes the rule fair, and shows the self-control you want them to learn."},{"prompt":"Your daughter Pinky asks: \"So what do we do instead when we're really angry?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Just don't get angry. It's simple.\"","then":"Pinky frowns. \"But I do get angry.\""},{"text":"\"Come and tell me, and I'll punish whoever started it.\"","then":"Pinky grins at Nikhil, already planning to tell on him."},{"text":"\"Shout it out. Just don't use your hands.\"","then":"Nikhil grins. Dinner gets a lot louder after that."},{"text":"\"We take a breath, walk away, and talk once we're calm.\"","then":"Pinky nods. \"Like counting to ten?\"","best":true}],"why":"Giving everyone something to do instead makes the rule usable in the heat of the moment."},{"prompt":"Your wife asks quietly: \"And if you slip, what then?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I won't slip. I'm the adult here.\"","then":"Your wife raises an eyebrow, and the children look doubtful."},{"text":"\"We'll see. It depends how bad they are.\"","then":"Nikhil's face falls. The old rule is back already."},{"text":"\"Then I apologise, and you can all remind me of the rule.\"","then":"The children exchange a look, surprised they're allowed to.","best":true},{"text":"\"That's between you and me, not the children.\"","then":"She nods, but the children never learn what happens when a grown-up slips."}],"why":"Owning that you might slip, and how you'll repair, keeps the rule honest for everyone."},{"prompt":"A week later, Nikhil says: \"Papa, you raised your hand at me yesterday.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I didn't actually hit you this time, so that doesn't count.\"","then":"Nikhil looks unsure whether the rule means anything at all."},{"text":"\"You're right. I broke our rule. It wasn't your fault. I'm sorry.\"","then":"Nikhil nods slowly. \"You stopped, though. I saw.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Don't answer back. I'm still your father.\"","then":"Nikhil goes quiet, and the rule quietly dies."},{"text":"\"You were being impossible yesterday, beta. You know that.\"","then":"Nikhil looks down. He hears that it was his fault after all."}],"why":"Owning it, and saying it wasn't his fault, keeps the rule real. If the urge feels close, call Tele-MANAS 14416."}],"relearn":"A shared no-hitting rule that applies to adults too models the very self-control you want your kids to learn."},
  {"id":"bc-083","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a realistic plan for changing how you parent, or a setup to fail?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"One small change at a time"},{"id":"b","text":"Becoming a perfect parent overnight"},{"id":"c","text":"Self-compassion when you slip"},{"id":"d","text":"Quitting at the first relapse"},{"id":"e","text":"Counting your calmer days"},{"id":"f","text":"Shaming yourself for every mistake"}],"bins":[{"id":"realistic","label":"Realistic plan","valence":"pos"},{"id":"fail","label":"Setup to fail","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"realistic","b":"fail","c":"realistic","d":"fail","e":"realistic","f":"fail"},"relearn":"One change at a time with self-compassion is realistic; perfectionism, quitting and self-shaming set you up to fail.","persona":"any","source":"realistic plan"},
  {"id":"bc-084","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match a hard moment to the tool for it.","pairs":[{"left":"Anger surging","right":"Pause, breathe, step away safely"},{"left":"After a slip","right":"Repair and self-forgive"},{"left":"Fear of real harm to your child","right":"Reach for help now (Be the Safe Adult)"},{"left":"Your own past resurfacing","right":"Tele-MANAS 14416"},{"left":"A hard day ahead","right":"Ready your pause-plan"}],"relearn":"Each hard moment has a tool: calm yourself, repair after, route your past, and reach for help fast.","persona":"Suresh","source":"moment to tool"},
  {"id":"bc-900","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"My temper is just my nature. Short fuse runs in our family, nothing to be done.\"","myth":{"un":"My temper is just my nature; a short fuse runs in our family, nothing to be done.","re":"A short fuse is a learned habit, not a fixed fate; the pause between feeling and acting can be trained like any skill.","why":"Habits are learnable and so is the pause."},"relearn":"A short temper is a habit you can retrain, not a fixed fate; the pause before acting can be practised.","persona":"Suresh","source":"temper is trainable"},
  {"id":"bc-901","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If I stay quiet instead of shouting, my child wins and I look weak.\"","myth":{"un":"If I stay quiet instead of shouting, my child wins and I look weak.","re":"A calm pause isn't losing; it's you staying in charge of yourself so you can hold the limit without harm.","why":"Self-control is authority, not surrender."},"relearn":"Staying calm instead of shouting isn't losing or weakness; it keeps you in charge so you hold the limit safely.","persona":"Suresh","source":"calm isn't losing"},
  {"id":"bc-902","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"The anger comes so fast there's no moment to catch it.\"","myth":{"un":"The anger comes so fast there's no moment to catch it.","re":"The first rush of fury usually peaks and fades within a couple of minutes; riding out that wave gives you the moment back.","why":"The surge passes if you don't feed it."},"relearn":"The first rush of anger peaks and eases within minutes; waiting it out gives you back the moment to choose.","persona":"any","source":"the wave passes"},
  {"id":"bc-903","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Counting to ten is silly advice that never works for real anger.\"","myth":{"un":"Counting to ten is silly advice that never works for real anger.","re":"A slow count buys a few seconds for the first surge to drop, just enough to unclench and choose your next words.","why":"A few seconds is often all the gap you need."},"relearn":"A slow count isn't silly; it buys seconds for the surge to drop so you can unclench and choose your words.","persona":"any","source":"count buys a gap"},
  {"id":"bc-904","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Showing my child I'm angry but staying calm will confuse them.\"","myth":{"un":"Letting my child see I'm angry but staying calm will confuse them.","re":"Naming your anger calmly teaches them feelings are okay and can be handled; it's one of the best lessons you can model.","why":"Calmly named anger is a lesson, not confusion."},"relearn":"Showing anger while staying calm teaches your child that feelings are okay and manageable; it's a gift, not confusion.","persona":"any","source":"named anger teaches"},
  {"id":"bc-905","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Stepping out of the room when I'm angry means I'm abandoning my child.\"","myth":{"un":"Stepping out of the room when I'm angry means I'm abandoning my child.","re":"A short, safe pause where the child is fine is care, not abandonment; you leave to come back calmer, not to disappear.","why":"You step away to protect, then return."},"relearn":"A brief safe step away isn't abandonment; with the child safe, you leave to return calmer, which protects them.","persona":"Suresh","source":"pause isn't abandonment"},
  {"id":"bc-906","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"I never get angry at work, so my temper at home is my kids' fault.\"","myth":{"un":"I never get angry at work, so my temper at home is my kids' fault.","re":"Holding it together all day often means the leftover stress spills at home; the trigger is your load, not your child.","why":"Spilled stress isn't the child's doing."},"relearn":"Calm at work but short at home usually means spilled stress, not your child's fault; the load is the trigger to tend.","persona":"Vikram","source":"spilled stress"},
  {"id":"bc-906b","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"name it early","hook":"Your daughter Pinky whines for the tenth time while you cook, and your patience wears thin.","steps":[{"prompt":"She tugs at your sleeve again as the dal boils over. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Hand her your phone so she'll finally stop asking","then":"She goes quiet with the phone, but only until the game gets boring."},{"text":"Take a deep breath but say nothing to her about it","then":"The breath helps a little, but Pinky keeps tugging, not knowing you're near the edge."},{"text":"Tell her to shut up and get out of the kitchen right now","then":"Pinky's face crumples. She leaves the kitchen in tears."},{"text":"Say 'I'm getting frustrated. Give me a minute,' and breathe","then":"Pinky pauses, surprised. Your breath slows, and so does your pulse.","best":true}],"why":"Naming your frustration out loud catches it early and shows her what's happening."},{"prompt":"A few minutes later, Pinky asks again when dinner will be ready. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell her 'ten minutes' and give her a small job to help","then":"She counts out spoons proudly. The whining stops, and you finish in peace.","best":true},{"text":"Tell her to stop whining or there'll be no dinner","then":"She sniffles quietly, and dinner starts in a sulk."},{"text":"Give her biscuits to keep her busy until then","then":"She's quiet now, and not hungry at dinner."},{"text":"Ignore her until the food is on the table","then":"She whines louder, and you feel the heat rising again."}],"why":"A clear answer and a small job meet her need, which calms you both."},{"prompt":"After dinner you think back to the moment in the kitchen. What do you take from it?","options":[{"text":"Learn your early signs, like a tight jaw and fast breath","then":"The next evening, your jaw tightens at the fifth whine. You notice it straight away.","best":true},{"text":"Decide you're failing because you got so frustrated","then":"The guilt sits heavy all night and makes tomorrow's fuse shorter."},{"text":"Think it's fine, since at least nobody got hit","then":"The bar stays low. Tomorrow the frustration builds the same way."},{"text":"Decide cooking with the kids around is just impossible","then":"You start cooking only once they're asleep, and you're drained by nine."}],"why":"Knowing your early signs lets you catch the surge before it takes over."}],"debrief":"When patience thins, name the feeling out loud and breathe before you act.","relearn":"Name your rising frustration and breathe rather than snapping; catching it early keeps you in control."},
  {"id":"bc-907","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"lay baby down safely","hook":"At 3am, baby Myra won't stop crying, and you feel a frightening urge to shake her.","steps":[{"prompt":"The urge is getting stronger, and your arms are tense. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Keep holding her and rock faster, willing her to stop","then":"She cries harder and the urge grows. Frightened, you lay her in the cot and back out."},{"text":"Keep holding her and tell yourself to just get a grip","then":"Gritting your teeth doesn't slow the urge. Trembling, you set her in the cot and step out."},{"text":"Lay Myra on her back in the cot and step out to breathe","then":"Myra keeps crying in the cot, but she's safe. Outside the door, you gulp air.","best":true},{"text":"Carry her down the hall to wake your partner","then":"The walk feels long with the urge rising. Your partner takes her, and you step out."}],"why":"Putting her down safely and stepping away protects you both while the urge passes."},{"prompt":"Out of the room, your heart is pounding, and you can still hear Myra crying. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Breathe slowly for a few minutes, then go back steadier","then":"After a few minutes your chest loosens. When you pick Myra up again, your arms are soft.","best":true},{"text":"Go straight back in, since a crying baby shouldn't be left","then":"You pick her up again, but your arms are still tight and the urge comes back."},{"text":"Put in earphones and wait until she cries herself out","then":"The crying stops after a long while. You feel numb and far away."},{"text":"Sit on the floor and cry, sure you're a terrible parent","then":"The tears come hard. The urge fades, but the shame stays with you."}],"why":"A few minutes of crying in a safe cot is fine. Going back calm is what keeps her safe."},{"prompt":"In the morning, the memory of that urge frightens you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Search online at night for whether you're a bad parent","then":"The results scare you more. You close the phone feeling worse and alone."},{"text":"Keep it to yourself. Nobody should know you felt that","then":"The fear sits alone with you, and by evening you're dreading the dark."},{"text":"Tell your partner, but laugh it off as just tiredness","then":"Your partner laughs too. The real fear stays unspoken."},{"text":"Tell your partner and call Tele-MANAS 14416 for support","then":"Your partner holds your hand. The counsellor listens and helps you plan for the nights.","best":true}],"why":"The urge doesn't make you a bad parent. Reaching for support keeps you and Myra safe."},{"prompt":"The next night, Myra wakes crying at 3am again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Push through, whatever the urge does, because she needs you","then":"You get through the feed, rigid and frightened of yourself."},{"text":"Go to her, and if the urge rises, put her down and step out","then":"The urge flickers once. You put her down, breathe at the door, and go back softer.","best":true},{"text":"Stay in bed and let her cry until she settles","then":"The crying goes on and on. You lie there, tense and wide awake."},{"text":"Go to her, hoping the urge won't come back","then":"It comes back, and you're caught off guard. You set her down, shaken."}],"why":"A plan made in daylight is far easier to follow at 3am than a decision made in the moment."}],"debrief":"When a frightening urge surges, lay the baby down safely and step away to breathe, then reach for support.","relearn":"If a scary urge surges, put the baby safely down and step away; a safe pause protects you both."},
  {"id":"bc-908","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"separate past from present","hook":"Your son Aarav talks back at dinner, the very thing your father punished you for, and rage floods up.","steps":[{"prompt":"Everyone at the table goes still, waiting for you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Laugh it off so no one sees how shaken you are","then":"The table relaxes, but your hands shake underneath it."},{"text":"Notice it's the old wound, breathe, then answer Aarav","then":"Your breath steadies. Across the table, he's just a cheeky eleven-year-old again.","best":true},{"text":"Tell him he'll get what you got at his age","then":"Aarav goes pale and silent. The meal ends in a heavy hush."},{"text":"Breathe, then give him the lecture your father gave you","then":"The words come out in your father's voice. Aarav rolls his eyes and tunes out."}],"why":"Seeing the old wound for what it is lets you answer the child in front of you, not your past."},{"prompt":"Later, Aarav mutters that he only said what he thought. How do you respond?","options":[{"text":"Tell him children should be seen and not heard","then":"He shuts down and leaves. It feels exactly like your own childhood."},{"text":"Agree he can say whatever he likes, however he likes","then":"He grins, and before bed he talks back to his grandmother the same way."},{"text":"Say his view matters, but ask him to say it politely","then":"Aarav blinks, then tries again, more politely. You actually hear his point.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing and let it drop to keep the peace","then":"He shrugs and walks off. The rudeness and his point both go unanswered."}],"why":"Firm and kind means his voice counts and the limit on rudeness holds."},{"prompt":"That night, the memory of your father's anger keeps coming back. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Name to yourself what happened back then and how it felt","then":"It hurts to name it. But you see why talking back hits you so hard.","best":true},{"text":"Push it away. That's all in the past now","then":"It fades for tonight, then surfaces the next time Aarav argues."},{"text":"Call your father and demand to know why he did it","then":"Your father gets defensive, the call ends badly, and the ache is worse."},{"text":"Tell yourself you turned out fine, so it didn't matter","then":"The thought settles you a little. The trigger stays just where it was."}],"why":"Naming the old hurt helps you understand your trigger, so it has less hold on you."},{"prompt":"A few days later, Aarav argues back again and the rage stirs. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Think 'this is Aarav, not the past,' and breathe first","then":"The rage passes through you, and you answer his point in your own voice.","best":true},{"text":"Breathe, then end the talk and never bring it up again","then":"You stay calm, but Aarav learns his side is never heard."},{"text":"Hold it in until it's too much, then let it out","then":"You manage for a while, then snap over something small."},{"text":"Give in to whatever he wants so the feeling goes away","then":"Aarav gets his way. You feel oddly powerless and resentful."}],"why":"Each time you separate past from present, the old reflex loosens its grip a little."}],"debrief":"When a child's act triggers an old wound, notice it's the past and respond to the child here.","relearn":"When rage from an old wound floods up, name it as the past and respond to your child, not the ghost."},
  {"id":"bc-909","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"soften the voice","hook":"Your daughter Anaya spilled paint on the new sofa and stands before you, scared, as a shout builds.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your first words are already coming out loud. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Go completely silent and stare at the sofa","then":"The silence frightens her more than a shout. She starts to cry."},{"text":"Drop and slow your voice on purpose, mid-sentence","then":"Your voice comes out lower, and your chest loosens. Anaya's shoulders drop a little.","best":true},{"text":"Finish the sentence loud, then calm down after","then":"The last words land hard. Anaya flinches before you manage to calm down."},{"text":"Keep your voice loud so she knows it's serious","then":"Anaya bursts into tears, and your anger climbs with the noise."}],"why":"Slowing your voice cools you both and tells her she is safe."},{"prompt":"Anaya whispers, 'It was an accident.' What do you do now?","options":[{"text":"Say accidents still get punished in this house","then":"Anaya nods, scared. She hides the next spill under a cushion."},{"text":"Tell her it's no big deal and clean it all up yourself","then":"She runs off to play while you scrub alone. Nothing about taking care gets said."},{"text":"Tell her she's always careless and needs to learn","then":"Her face falls. 'I'm always bad,' she mutters."},{"text":"Say you believe her, and get cloths to clean up together","then":"She fetches a bucket, sniffling less. The stain fades a little with every rub.","best":true}],"why":"Believing her and fixing it together teaches care without fear or shame."},{"prompt":"That evening, your spouse says a good shout is the only thing kids listen to. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Snap that they're the reason the kids are so loud","then":"It turns into a row about who's the better parent."},{"text":"Say nothing, since it's not worth an argument","then":"Your spouse takes the silence as agreement and keeps shouting."},{"text":"Agree to save shouting for when it's really serious","then":"Soon everything starts to feel serious, and the shouting creeps back."},{"text":"Say a calmer voice works better for you and for the kids","then":"Your spouse raises an eyebrow, then says, 'Show me how it works.'","best":true}],"why":"Sharing what works brings your spouse along, so the calmer voice becomes the family's."}],"debrief":"When a shout builds, deliberately drop and slow your voice; it cools you and reassures your child.","relearn":"Deliberately lower and slow your voice when a shout builds; softening cools you and steadies your child."},
  {"id":"bc-910","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"reset when alone","hook":"You're alone at dinnertime with three tired, squabbling kids, and you're at your limit.","steps":[{"prompt":"The youngest throws a cup, and the other two start shrieking. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Shout at all three to be quiet this minute","then":"Three faces freeze. The youngest starts to wail."},{"text":"Lock yourself in the bathroom until everyone calms down","then":"You get quiet, but a crash from the kitchen sends you running back."},{"text":"Push past your limit and hope you don't blow up","then":"You grip the table edge. A minute later you're yelling anyway."},{"text":"Make the room safe, then take ten breaths at the doorway","then":"From the doorway you can still see them. By breath ten, your jaw unclenches.","best":true}],"why":"A safe room and a few breaths at the doorway let you reset without leaving them."},{"prompt":"The shrieking settles into a sulky squabble over who got more rice. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Give the loudest one extra to end it fast","then":"It ends this fight, but now the other two start shouting for extra too."},{"text":"Say calmly, 'Everyone gets a fair share,' and serve it out","then":"They grumble, then eat. The squabble fizzles out.","best":true},{"text":"Let them fight it out while you clear up","then":"It gets louder, and the youngest ends up in tears."},{"text":"Take every plate away until they learn to stop fighting","then":"Now all three are hungry and wailing, and your patience thins again."}],"why":"A calm, fair limit settles a squabble better than a threat or a prize for the loudest."},{"prompt":"After bedtime you sit alone, wrung out. What do you do about tomorrow?","options":[{"text":"Tell yourself real parents don't need breaks","then":"You go to bed tense, already dreading the morning."},{"text":"Decide that tomorrow you'll just try harder","then":"The resolve lasts until the first spilled cup."},{"text":"Plan your reset for tomorrow and line up one bit of help","then":"Your sister agrees to come over on Thursday. Tomorrow feels less like a wall.","best":true},{"text":"Scroll your phone till 1am just to feel like yourself again","then":"You feel better for an hour, and wake up exhausted."}],"why":"Planning your reset and asking for help makes tomorrow easier than willpower alone."}],"debrief":"With no one to tag in, make the space safe and breathe at the doorway; you can still reset.","relearn":"Alone and at your limit, make the space safe and breathe at the doorway; you can reset without a tag-in."},
  {"id":"bc-911","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"stop mid-shout","hook":"You're already shouting at your son Kabir over a broken toy, and part of you wants to keep going.","steps":[{"prompt":"Kabir is crying, and the words keep coming. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Stop mid-sentence and say, 'I need a moment'","then":"The room goes quiet. Kabir looks up, surprised you stopped.","best":true},{"text":"Finish what you started, since you've begun","then":"By the end, Kabir is sobbing, and you feel sick."},{"text":"Switch to a cold, quiet lecture instead","then":"Your voice is low, but the words sting. Kabir shrinks."},{"text":"Stop shouting, but keep glaring at him","then":"The glare says it all. Kabir can't stop crying."}],"why":"You can stop even mid-shout. 'I need a moment' cuts it off before it peaks."},{"prompt":"A few minutes later, you're calmer and Kabir is sitting on the stairs. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Leave it. Bringing it up will only upset him again","then":"He wipes his eyes and avoids you until dinner."},{"text":"Buy him a new toy to make up for it","then":"He brightens at the toy, and the shouting never gets talked about."},{"text":"Say sorry for shouting, and that it wasn't his fault","then":"Kabir leans into you. 'I didn't mean to break it,' he whispers.","best":true},{"text":"Say sorry, but add that he made you angry","then":"Kabir nods, eyes on the floor. 'Sorry I made you angry.'"}],"why":"A clean apology owns what you did without putting the blame back on him."},{"prompt":"That night, you wonder if one bout of shouting has ruined things with Kabir. What do you tell yourself?","options":[{"text":"It will never, ever happen again, full stop","then":"It feels strong tonight. You haven't planned how to keep it."},{"text":"Every parent slips. The repair is what he'll remember","then":"You feel steadier. Tomorrow you'll try to pause earlier.","best":true},{"text":"Kids forget these things by the morning","then":"You sleep easily. The next outburst comes a bit sooner."},{"text":"You've ruined it, just like your father did","then":"The guilt keeps you up and makes you short with everyone in the morning."}],"why":"One slip doesn't ruin everything. Repair, not perfection, is what builds trust."}],"debrief":"Even mid-shout you can stop; say 'I need a moment' and step back rather than peaking.","relearn":"You can stop even mid-shout; 'I need a moment' cuts the escalation off instead of riding it out."},
  {"id":"bc-912","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"buffer after stress","hook":"Your boss has just ended a tense call, and you can hear the kids fighting in the next room.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your daughter Tara calls out for you to come and settle it. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Go straight in and sort it out while you're wound up","then":"Your tone is sharp. Tara bursts into tears over something small."},{"text":"Stay at the laptop and pretend you didn't hear","then":"The fight grows louder until someone gets pushed."},{"text":"Call 'two minutes,' splash your face, breathe, then go","then":"By the time you walk in, the call feels further away. Your voice comes out even.","best":true},{"text":"Shout from your desk for them to shut up","then":"The fighting stops, then turns into sulking in the next room."}],"why":"A short buffer lets you drop the call's stress instead of carrying it to them."},{"prompt":"In the kids' room, both insist the other one started it. How do you handle it?","options":[{"text":"Tell them you've had a terrible day, so stop it","then":"They go quiet and guilty, carrying your bad day."},{"text":"Punish both so no one feels picked on","then":"Both feel wronged and sulk the rest of the evening."},{"text":"Side with the younger one, who's crying","then":"The older one storms off, sure it's unfair."},{"text":"Listen to each in turn, then ask what would be fair","then":"They grumble, then agree to take turns with the tablet.","best":true}],"why":"Hearing both sides calmly teaches them to solve it, not to fear your mood."},{"prompt":"Next week, another tough call ends just as the kids get home from school. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask the kids to stay out of your way till you're ready","then":"They hover at your door, confused, and feel shut out."},{"text":"Skip any pause. You should be able to switch off by now","then":"The stress walks in with you. You snap at the first muddy shoe."},{"text":"Vent to the kids about your boss over snacks","then":"The kids listen wide-eyed, and your stress becomes theirs."},{"text":"Take two minutes to breathe before you greet them","then":"It's quick. You greet the kids with a smile you actually mean.","best":true}],"why":"A short pause after every tough call becomes a habit that shields them from your stress."}],"debrief":"When a stressor primes your temper, take a short buffer to reset before facing the kids.","relearn":"Build a short reset buffer after a known stressor so its tension doesn't spill onto your children."},
  {"id":"bc-913","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"don't hit back","hook":"Your toddler Chhotu smacks you across the face, and your first instinct is to hit back.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your hand is already rising. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Give him a light smack so he learns how it feels too","then":"Chhotu looks shocked, then hits you again, harder."},{"text":"Laugh it off, because he's far too little to know better","then":"Chhotu giggles and swings at you again."},{"text":"Hold his hands, breathe, and say calmly, 'We don't hit'","then":"Chhotu squirms, then stops. You feel your hand relax around his.","best":true},{"text":"Shout 'No!' right in his face","then":"He startles and bursts into tears, then hits the cushion."}],"why":"Holding his hands teaches the limit without doing the thing you're asking him not to do."},{"prompt":"A minute later you take the TV remote away, and Chhotu goes red-faced and furious. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Put him in his room alone until he stops","then":"He howls behind the door, and it takes much longer to settle."},{"text":"Distract him with a biscuit so he forgets all about it","then":"He calms down, and learns that a tantrum ends with a biscuit."},{"text":"Name it: 'You're angry. You can stamp your feet, not hit'","then":"Chhotu stamps hard, then flops into your lap, spent.","best":true},{"text":"Tell him good boys don't get angry","then":"He sulks, and you can see him swallowing the anger."}],"why":"Naming the feeling and offering a safe outlet teaches him what to do with anger."},{"prompt":"Your mother, watching, says, 'A slap never hurt you. Give him a proper one.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Give him a small tap on the hand, just to keep her happy","then":"Your mother nods, satisfied. Chhotu looks at you, confused and hurt."},{"text":"Nod along and quickly change the subject to dinner","then":"She takes your silence as agreement and brings it up again tomorrow."},{"text":"Snap that her slaps are why you're like this","then":"Your mother goes quiet and hurt. The evening turns cold."},{"text":"Say gently that you're teaching him differently","then":"She shakes her head, but says no more. Chhotu is already playing again.","best":true}],"why":"You can respect her and still choose non-violent discipline for your child."},{"prompt":"Two days later, Chhotu hits you again when you take away the scissors. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Hold his hands and say calmly, 'We don't hit'","then":"He struggles, then stops, and soon he's reaching for his blocks instead.","best":true},{"text":"Give up on the calm way, since it clearly doesn't work","then":"You slip back into shouting, and the hitting gets more frequent."},{"text":"Give the scissors back to avoid another fight","then":"He stops crying, and you spend the afternoon worried about his fingers."},{"text":"Put the scissors away and ignore the hit","then":"He hits you again the next time he's cross."}],"why":"Toddlers learn by repetition. Staying calm and consistent is what makes the lesson stick."}],"debrief":"When your child hits you, regulate first, hold their hands and name the limit calmly.","relearn":"Breathe and hold the limit calmly when your child hits you; hitting back only models that bigger people hit."},
  {"id":"bc-914","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"pause when stuck","hook":"On the drive home, the kids are screaming in the back seat, and your temper is rising.","steps":[{"prompt":"You can't walk away, and the traffic is heavy. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Go quiet and breathe slowly until the surge drops","then":"Your grip on the wheel loosens. The kids notice the quiet and lower their voices.","best":true},{"text":"Turn around and shout at them while driving","then":"The car swerves slightly, and everyone falls silent in fright."},{"text":"Speed up to get home and end it faster","then":"You brake hard at a signal, and your heart pounds even more."},{"text":"Turn the radio up loud to drown them out","then":"The noise doubles, and your temper with it."}],"why":"When you can't leave, going quiet and breathing gives you a safe pause right where you are."},{"prompt":"At the next signal, a fight breaks out and the screaming peaks. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Threaten to leave them by the road if they don't stop","then":"The kids go silent, frightened. Your youngest asks if you really would."},{"text":"Keep driving and say you'll deal with it at home","then":"The noise carries on, and so does the heat in your chest."},{"text":"Hand your phone back so they'll be quiet","then":"They fight over the phone instead, louder than before."},{"text":"Pull over somewhere safe, stop the car, and settle things","then":"Parked, you turn around calmly and tell them you can't drive safely with this noise.","best":true}],"why":"Pulling over safely stops the danger and gives you room to calm down."},{"prompt":"At home, you think about the drive. What do you plan for next time?","options":[{"text":"Warn them next time that you'll punish them hard","then":"They're tense and silent the whole next drive, and so are you."},{"text":"Drive without the kids whenever you possibly can","then":"It works once. On the next family drive, the heat is back."},{"text":"A halfway stop, plus snacks and a game for the ride","then":"On Sunday's drive, the halfway chai stop breaks the tension before it builds.","best":true},{"text":"Hope the next drive will just go better","then":"The next drive goes just like this one."}],"why":"Planning ahead for a known flashpoint makes the next drive calmer for everyone."}],"debrief":"When you can't walk away, find another safe pause: pull over, or go quiet and breathe until the surge passes.","relearn":"When you can't step away, pull over or go quiet and breathe; a safe pause is possible even in the car."},
  {"id":"bc-915","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"match","hook":"Match each rising body signal to the quick calming move that meets it.","pairs":[{"left":"Jaw and fists clenching","right":"Unclench and drop your shoulders"},{"left":"Voice climbing louder","right":"Deliberately lower and slow it"},{"left":"Breath going shallow and fast","right":"One long, slow exhale"},{"left":"Heat rushing to your face","right":"Splash cool water or step outside"},{"left":"Thoughts racing 'I can't take this'","right":"Name it: 'I'm flooded, I need a minute'"}],"relearn":"Each early signal, like clenching, a rising voice, shallow breath, heat or racing thoughts, has a quick move to meet it.","persona":"any","source":"signal to move"},
  {"id":"bc-916","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"match","hook":"Match each trigger you can plan for to a calmer response prepared in advance.","pairs":[{"left":"The chaotic homework hour","right":"Lower the bar and take breaks together"},{"left":"Mornings running late","right":"Lay clothes out the night before"},{"left":"Whining for the same thing","right":"Decide your calm answer ahead of time"},{"left":"Sibling fights at dinner","right":"Separate them early, before it peaks"},{"left":"Bedtime stalling","right":"A fixed, gentle routine you both know"}],"relearn":"Naming your flashpoints lets you plan a calmer response in advance, so the trigger doesn't ambush you.","persona":"any","source":"plan for triggers"},
  {"id":"bc-917","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"match","hook":"Match each calming idea to what it's really doing for you.","pairs":[{"left":"A slow exhale","right":"Signals your body it's safe to settle"},{"left":"Stepping to another room","right":"Puts distance between feeling and acting"},{"left":"Naming the feeling","right":"Turns the surge into something you can hold"},{"left":"Cool water on your face","right":"Physically interrupts the heat of anger"},{"left":"Knowing your trigger","right":"Lets you catch the reflex before it fires"}],"relearn":"Breathing, distance, naming, cool water and knowing your trigger each work on the surge in a different way.","persona":"any","source":"why each tool works"},
  {"id":"bc-918","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each move: it widens the gap between feeling and acting, or it shrinks it.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Taking one slow breath first"},{"id":"b","text":"Shouting the instant you feel it"},{"id":"c","text":"Stepping to the next room"},{"id":"d","text":"Acting on the urge straight away"},{"id":"e","text":"Naming 'I'm angry' to yourself"},{"id":"f","text":"Replaying what enraged you"}],"bins":[{"id":"widens","label":"Widens the gap","valence":"pos"},{"id":"shrinks","label":"Shrinks the gap","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"widens","b":"shrinks","c":"widens","d":"shrinks","e":"widens","f":"shrinks"},"relearn":"Breathing, stepping away and naming the feeling widen the gap before you act; reacting instantly and stewing shrink it.","persona":"any","source":"widen vs shrink"},
  {"id":"bc-919","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each thought: does it help you pause, or does it pour fuel on the anger?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"'This will pass if I wait'"},{"id":"b","text":"'He's doing this on purpose to provoke me'"},{"id":"c","text":"'I can handle this calmly'"},{"id":"d","text":"'I've had enough, I'll show him'"},{"id":"e","text":"'He's a tired child, not my enemy'"},{"id":"f","text":"'A real parent wouldn't take this'"}],"bins":[{"id":"cools","label":"Helps you pause","valence":"pos"},{"id":"fuels","label":"Fuels the anger","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"cools","b":"fuels","c":"cools","d":"fuels","e":"cools","f":"fuels"},"relearn":"Thoughts like 'this will pass' and 'he's a tired child' help you pause; 'he's provoking me' and 'I'll show him' fuel the anger.","persona":"Suresh","source":"cooling vs fuelling thoughts"},
  {"id":"bc-920","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each habit: it keeps your fuse longer, or it burns it shorter.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Getting enough sleep when you can"},{"id":"b","text":"Skipping meals and running on empty"},{"id":"c","text":"Asking for help before you're drained"},{"id":"d","text":"Bottling stress until it bursts"},{"id":"e","text":"Short breaks during a hard day"},{"id":"f","text":"Drinking to take the edge off"}],"bins":[{"id":"longer","label":"Lengthens your fuse","valence":"pos"},{"id":"shorter","label":"Shortens your fuse","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"longer","b":"shorter","c":"longer","d":"shorter","e":"longer","f":"shorter"},"relearn":"Sleep, asking for help and small breaks lengthen your patience; skipping meals, bottling stress and drinking shorten it.","persona":"any","source":"lengthen vs shorten fuse"},
  {"id":"bc-921","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each one: a real early-warning sign of your anger, or just a passing thing?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Your chest tightening"},{"id":"b","text":"A song stuck in your head"},{"id":"c","text":"Your voice starting to rise"},{"id":"d","text":"Feeling a bit hungry earlier"},{"id":"e","text":"Heat flushing your neck"},{"id":"f","text":"A light drizzle outside"}],"bins":[{"id":"warning","label":"Anger warning sign","valence":"uhoh"},{"id":"unrelated","label":"Unrelated","valence":"neutral"}],"key":{"a":"warning","b":"unrelated","c":"warning","d":"unrelated","e":"warning","f":"unrelated"},"relearn":"A tight chest, a rising voice and a hot neck are your anger's early-warning signs; a stuck song or the weather aren't.","persona":"Suresh","source":"warning vs noise"},
  {"id":"bc-922","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: feeling furious doesn't make you a bad parent. What you do with the fury is the part you get to choose. Sit right?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Sits right","Feeling isn't acting","I get to choose"],"affirm":"Anger itself isn't wrong or bad; it's a feeling. The choice you make in the gap after it is what's yours to own.","relearn":"Feeling furious doesn't make you a bad parent; what you do with the fury is the part you choose.","persona":"any","source":"feeling isn't the failure"},
  {"id":"bc-923","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: every time you pause instead of erupting, you're showing your child what self-control looks like. Worth practising?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Worth it","They're watching","Practising"],"affirm":"Children learn to handle big feelings mostly by watching you handle yours; each pause you take is a quiet lesson.","relearn":"Each time you pause instead of erupting, you show your child what self-control looks like.","persona":"any","source":"they learn by watching"},
  {"id":"bc-924","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the calmest parents aren't the ones who never feel anger. They're the ones who've practised the pause. Land?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["It lands","Practised the pause","Not never-angry"],"affirm":"Calm parents still feel fury; they've just rehearsed the pause so often that reaching for it has become the reflex.","relearn":"Calm parents still feel anger; they've simply practised the pause until it became their reflex.","persona":"Suresh","source":"pause is practised"},
  {"id":"bc-925","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: noticing your trigger before it fires is a skill that grows every time you use it. Believe that?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["I believe it","It grows","Catch it earlier"],"affirm":"The more you notice your own triggers, the earlier you catch them next time; this awareness genuinely strengthens with use.","relearn":"Noticing your trigger before it fires is a skill that grows sharper every time you practise it.","persona":"any","source":"awareness grows"},
  {"id":"bc-926","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two red flags: the moves that make a heated moment more dangerous.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Putting space between you before you speak","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Getting in the child's face to shout","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Taking a slow breath to steady yourself","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Grabbing them hard to make them listen","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Lowering your voice on purpose","trick":false}],"why":"Getting in a child's face and grabbing them hard escalate and frighten; space, breath and a low voice keep it safe.","relearn":"Crowding and grabbing a child in anger raise the danger; space, breath and a softer voice keep it safe.","persona":"any","source":"red flags in the heat"},
  {"id":"bc-927","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two red-flag thoughts: the ones that feed your anger instead of cooling it.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"'This surge will pass if I wait'","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"'He's provoking me on purpose'","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"'I can choose my next move'","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"'I have every right to lose it'","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"'He's a tired kid, not my enemy'","trick":false}],"why":"'He's provoking me' and 'I have a right to lose it' justify erupting; the others remind you it passes and you can choose.","relearn":"Thoughts that cast your child as an enemy or justify losing it feed the anger; cooling ones remind you it passes.","persona":"Suresh","source":"red-flag thoughts"},
  {"id":"bc-928","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two red flags: the 'calming' tricks that actually do harm.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Stepping out for a few slow breaths","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"A drink to 'take the edge off'","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Splashing cool water on your face","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Smashing things near the kids","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Naming the feeling to yourself","trick":false}],"why":"Drinking to cope and smashing things frighten and risk harm; breath, cool water and naming the feeling actually calm.","relearn":"Drinking to cope or smashing things aren't calming; they frighten and risk harm. Breath, cool water and naming do.","persona":"any","source":"fake calm vs real calm"},
  {"id":"bc-928b","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two red flags: the lines that pretend to be a pause but are threats.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"'I need a minute, I'll be right back'","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"'Wait till I get my hands on you'","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"'Let's both take a breath'","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"'You'll regret this when I'm done'","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"'I'm too angry to talk well now'","trick":false}],"why":"'Wait till I get my hands on you' and 'you'll regret this' are threats dressed as a pause; the rest are honest exits.","relearn":"A real pause is honest ('I need a minute'); a threat dressed as one ('wait till I get you') only frightens.","persona":"Suresh","source":"pause vs threat"},
  {"id":"bc-929","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"honest step-out","hook":"You're flooding with anger at your twelve-year-old, Meera, and need to step out without scaring her.","setup":"She's glaring at you across the room.","steps":[{"prompt":"Meera shouts: \"You never let me do anything I want!\"","options":[{"text":"\"Fine. Do whatever you want.\"","then":"\"Seriously?\" She stares, unsure if you mean it, and stomps off."},{"text":"\"Get out of my sight before I do something I regret.\"","then":"Meera goes white. \"Fine!\" She locks herself in her room."},{"text":"\"I'm very angry, so I'm taking a few minutes. Then we'll talk.\"","then":"Meera crosses her arms, but nods. \"Fine. Five minutes.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I can't even deal with you right now. Just leave me alone.\"","then":"Her face falls. \"Nobody can,\" she mutters."}],"why":"Saying why you're stepping out, and that you'll come back, keeps her safe and reassured."},{"prompt":"Later, Meera mutters: \"I just wanted to go to Riya's party.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Okay, go. I don't want another fight.\"","then":"She pauses, suspicious. \"Wait. Are you just saying that?\""},{"text":"\"I hear you. Tell me about it, and I'll explain my worry.\"","then":"She sits down. \"It's at her house. Her mum will be there.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"When I was your age, I never talked back like this.\"","then":"\"Good for you,\" she mutters, and shuts down."},{"text":"\"The answer is no, and that's final.\"","then":"\"You never even listen,\" she says. \"I'm asking again later.\""}],"why":"Listening first shows her feelings are safe with you, even when the answer may be no."},{"prompt":"Before bed, Meera asks: \"Riya's mum can drop me home. So can I go?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No. You shouted at me, so no party.\"","then":"\"That's so unfair!\" She slams her door."},{"text":"\"Yes, and stay as late as you like.\"","then":"\"Really?\" She's delighted, and you spend the night watching the clock."},{"text":"\"Maybe. We'll see how you behave for the rest of the week.\"","then":"She groans. \"That means no.\""},{"text":"\"Yes, till eight, and text me when you leave.\"","then":"Meera grins. \"Deal. Eight sharp.\"","best":true}],"why":"A clear yes with a sensible limit is firm and kind at once."}],"relearn":"Calmly telling a child you need a few minutes to settle keeps them safe and reassured, unlike a frightening threat."},
  {"id":"bc-930","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"ask to tag in","hook":"Bedtime has dragged on for an hour with your son out of bed again, and you're near your limit.","setup":"Your spouse Rahul looks up from the dishes.","steps":[{"prompt":"Rahul asks: \"Everything okay in there?\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's fine. I'll manage it myself, like always.\"","then":"\"Okay,\" he says, and turns back to the sink."},{"text":"\"I'm at my limit. Can you take over for ten minutes?\"","then":"\"Sure,\" Rahul says, drying his hands. \"Go and breathe.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Why am I the only one dealing with this? I give up.\"","then":"Rahul stiffens. \"I've been doing the dishes. That's not nothing.\""},{"text":"\"You try it, since you think it's so easy!\"","then":"\"I never said that,\" Rahul says, hurt, and doesn't move."}],"why":"Asking plainly for a tag-in is a strong step that keeps the moment safe."},{"prompt":"Later, once your son is asleep, Rahul asks: \"Are you okay? You looked rattled.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I was close to shouting. Can we plan how to share bedtimes?\"","then":"Rahul nods. \"Let's alternate nights, and have a signal if one of us is struggling.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm just short-tempered. Nothing to be done.\"","then":"Rahul shrugs. \"Maybe it runs in your family,\" he says, half joking."},{"text":"\"I'm fine. It's just him being difficult.\"","then":"\"Okay,\" he says. Tomorrow's bedtime looks exactly the same."},{"text":"\"You should've stepped in without me asking.\"","then":"\"I'm not a mind reader,\" he says, and goes quiet."}],"why":"Sharing what really happened lets you plan together instead of struggling alone."},{"prompt":"A few nights later, Rahul says: \"I've got a late work call, so bedtime's all yours.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Okay. If I get close, I'll step out and breathe first.\"","then":"Rahul nods. \"And I'm free by nine if you need me.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Again? I can't face another night of this.\"","then":"Rahul sighs. \"I really can't skip this call.\""},{"text":"\"Fine. But if he acts up, he's getting a smack.\"","then":"Rahul frowns. \"Please don't. That's not who we want to be.\""},{"text":"\"Fine. I'll just grit my teeth and get through it.\"","then":"\"Great,\" he says, relieved, as your jaw tightens."}],"why":"Having your own calm-down plan ready means you're not relying on a tag-in every time."}],"relearn":"Asking your partner to tag in while you cool down is a strong, planned move that keeps the moment safe."},
  {"id":"bc-931","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"self-coaching script","hook":"After nearly shouting at your son Dev over his shoes, you're trying to talk yourself steady.","setup":"It's the school rush, and you're already late.","steps":[{"prompt":"The old voice in your head says: \"He's doing it on purpose to test you.\"","options":[{"text":"\"He is testing me. Time to show him who's boss.\"","then":"Your fists clench. The voice cheers: \"That's how Papa did it.\""},{"text":"\"Don't think about it. Just push it all down.\"","then":"It goes quiet for a moment, then comes back: \"He's still doing it.\""},{"text":"\"I can't take this. I'm going to explode any second.\"","then":"Your heart races. The voice gets louder: \"See? You can't cope.\""},{"text":"\"Breathe. This passes. He's a child. I'm the calm one.\"","then":"Your breath slows. The voice grumbles: \"Fine. But he's still dawdling.\"","best":true}],"why":"A steadying inner script pulls you back from the edge. Panic talk pushes you over it."},{"prompt":"Dev mumbles: \"I can't find my other shoe.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Wear your old ones. I'm not searching for anything.\"","then":"\"They pinch!\" Dev wails, and the rush gets longer."},{"text":"\"How many times do I have to tell you?\"","then":"Dev shrinks. \"Sorry,\" he whispers, still in one shoe."},{"text":"\"Okay. Let's look together. Try under the bed?\"","then":"Dev dives under the bed. \"Found it!\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You'd lose your head if it wasn't attached!\"","then":"Dev's face falls. \"I'm trying,\" he says, moving even slower."}],"why":"Once you've steadied yourself, helping calmly gets you out the door faster."},{"prompt":"On the way out, the voice whispers: \"You nearly lost it. You're just like your father.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Who cares? He deserved a shout anyway.\"","then":"The voice agrees: \"Next time, don't hold back.\""},{"text":"\"Stop it. I'll never, ever feel that way again.\"","then":"The voice laughs: \"We'll see about that tomorrow.\""},{"text":"\"You're right. I'm hopeless at this, and always will be.\"","then":"The voice settles in: \"Told you.\" The day starts heavy."},{"text":"\"I nearly did, and I didn't. I'm learning something new.\"","then":"The voice goes quiet. At the gate, Dev turns and waves: \"Bye! Love you!\"","best":true}],"why":"Noticing your progress kindly keeps you going. The inheritance isn't destiny."}],"relearn":"A steadying inner script, like 'Breathe. This will pass. I'm the calm one,' pulls you back from the edge better than panic talk."},
  {"id":"bc-932","cat":"calm-yourself","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"hold against shout louder","hook":"Your mother is visiting, and the kids are ignoring your call to come and eat.","setup":"She watches you ask a second time.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother says: \"You're too soft. Just shout louder and they'll listen.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You're right. I'll just yell harder next time.\"","then":"She nods, satisfied. \"Good. They need to know who's in charge.\""},{"text":"\"And look how well all that shouting worked out on me, Amma.\"","then":"Her face tightens. \"I did my best with you,\" she says quietly."},{"text":"\"Okay, okay. Chiku! Mini! Come here NOW!\"","then":"The kids come running, wide-eyed. \"See?\" your mother says, smiling."},{"text":"\"I know that's how we did it. I'm learning to stay calm instead.\"","then":"She raises an eyebrow. \"Calm? With these two? Let's see.\"","best":true}],"why":"You can honour how she raised you and still choose your own way."},{"prompt":"Chiku dawdles at the sink, and your mother says: \"Go on, shout. It's all they ever hear.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Fine. You deal with him, then.\"","then":"She bellows his name. \"There,\" she says, as Chiku flinches."},{"text":"\"Watch. I'll go to him and say it once, calmly.\"","then":"Chiku grumbles, but starts scrubbing. Your mother mutters, \"Hmm. Lucky this time.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. CHIKU! Hands. Now!\"","then":"Chiku jumps and scrubs fast, eyes down. \"Good,\" says your mother."},{"text":"\"Can you please stop? You're making this so much worse.\"","then":"\"I'm only trying to help,\" she says, hurt."}],"why":"Showing calm firmness in action convinces more than arguing about it."},{"prompt":"After dinner, your mother says: \"We shouted at you, and you turned out fine.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I love how you raised me. I just want to change this part.\"","then":"Her face softens. \"Well. You always were stubborn.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's not talk about it.\"","then":"She shrugs, but she isn't done. \"One more thing before I go.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe. But times have changed, Amma. Nobody does that now.\"","then":"She bristles. \"So we were bad parents?\""},{"text":"\"You're right. Maybe I'm overthinking it.\"","then":"\"Exactly,\" she says, relieved, and you feel yourself sliding back."}],"why":"Keeping the good and leaving the harm isn't betrayal. It's a choice you're free to make."},{"prompt":"As she leaves, your mother asks: \"And if they really misbehave? Then what?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then I stay firm, calmly. Clear rules, no shouting or slaps.\"","then":"She nods slowly. \"Firm but calm. Hmm. Show me next visit.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Then honestly, maybe a shout or two is okay now and then.\"","then":"\"See? You'll come round,\" she says, smiling."},{"text":"\"Then I'll send them to stay with you!\"","then":"She laughs. \"Any time!\" The real question goes unanswered."},{"text":"\"Then I'll let it go. They're only kids.\"","then":"She frowns. \"That's exactly what worries me.\""}],"why":"Firm and kind means clear limits held calmly, not shouting and not letting things slide."}],"relearn":"Kindly holding your calm approach against 'just shout louder' honours your parent without inheriting the harsh script."},
  {"id":"bc-990","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Sparing the rod spoils the child. That's just wisdom.\"","myth":{"un":"Sparing the rod spoils the child. That's just wisdom.","re":"Children disciplined without hitting turn out at least as well behaved; hitting raises aggression, not character.","why":"The saying is tradition, not evidence."},"relearn":"You don't need a rod; non-violent discipline raises better-behaved children, and hitting only adds aggression.","persona":"Suresh","source":"no rod needed"},
  {"id":"bc-991","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A quick smack is the fastest way to make a child stop.\"","myth":{"un":"A quick smack is the fastest way to make a child stop.","re":"A smack stops the moment but teaches nothing; the child learns to hide it and to use force when angry.","why":"Fast isn't the same as effective."},"relearn":"A smack only buys silence now; it teaches hiding and that force is how the strong get their way.","persona":"Suresh","source":"fast not effective"},
  {"id":"bc-992","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"I turned out fine after being hit, so it clearly does no harm.\"","myth":{"un":"I turned out fine after being hit, so it clearly does no harm.","re":"Turning out okay despite being hit isn't proof it helped; many carry quiet costs, and better ways exist now.","why":"Surviving harm isn't the same as benefiting."},"relearn":"'I turned out fine' isn't proof hitting helped; you turned out fine despite it, and gentler ways work better.","persona":"Suresh","source":"despite not because"},
  {"id":"bc-993","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If I'm gentle, my child will walk all over me and never listen.\"","myth":{"un":"If I'm gentle, my child will walk all over me and never listen.","re":"Gentle isn't weak: firm-and-kind parents hold clear limits warmly, and their children listen more, not less.","why":"Warmth and firmness work together."},"relearn":"Gentle doesn't mean a pushover; holding limits warmly earns more cooperation than harshness ever does.","persona":"Vikram","source":"gentle not weak"},
  {"id":"bc-994","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Yelling isn't hitting, so shouting them down is harmless discipline.\"","myth":{"un":"Yelling isn't hitting, so shouting them down is harmless discipline.","re":"Harsh, frequent yelling also frightens and wounds children and works no better than a calm, clear limit.","why":"Verbal harshness harms too."},"relearn":"Constant shouting frightens a child like hitting does; a calm, clear limit teaches far more than volume.","persona":"Suresh","source":"yelling wounds too"},
  {"id":"bc-995","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Threats like 'I'll leave you here' keep a child in line.\"","myth":{"un":"Threats like 'I'll leave you here' keep a child in line.","re":"Abandonment threats scare a child into obeying but quietly damage their sense of safety with you.","why":"Fear of losing you isn't security."},"relearn":"Threatening to leave or abandon a child controls through fear and chips away at the safety they need from you.","persona":"Suresh","source":"no abandonment threats"},
  {"id":"bc-996","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Comparing my child to others ('why can't you be like him?') pushes them to do better.\"","myth":{"un":"Comparing my child to others ('why can't you be like him?') pushes them to do better.","re":"Constant comparison teaches a child they're not enough; it breeds resentment and shame, not motivation.","why":"Comparison wounds self-worth."},"relearn":"Comparing your child to others doesn't motivate; it tells them they're not enough and feeds shame.","persona":"Suresh","source":"no comparing"},
  {"id":"bc-997","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Children only respect a strict parent; a soft one gets no respect.\"","myth":{"un":"Children only respect a strict parent; a soft one gets no respect.","re":"Children respect a parent who is fair, consistent and warm; harshness earns fear, which isn't respect.","why":"Respect grows from trust, not fear."},"relearn":"Real respect comes from fairness, consistency and warmth, not strictness; harshness only buys fear.","persona":"Suresh","source":"respect from fairness"},
  {"id":"bc-998","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Praising good behaviour spoils a child; only correcting the bad works.\"","myth":{"un":"Praising good behaviour spoils a child; only correcting the bad works.","re":"Noticing and naming good behaviour teaches more than punishment; children repeat what gets warm attention.","why":"What you notice, you grow."},"relearn":"Praise doesn't spoil; naming the good behaviour you want teaches a child more than only catching the bad.","persona":"Vikram","source":"catch the good"},
  {"id":"bc-999","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A consequence has to hurt or it won't teach the lesson.\"","myth":{"un":"A consequence has to hurt or it won't teach the lesson.","re":"A consequence teaches when it's clear, related and explained, not when it hurts; pain teaches fear, not the lesson.","why":"Clarity teaches; pain only frightens."},"relearn":"A consequence works when it's clear, related and explained, not when it hurts; pain teaches fear, not the lesson.","persona":"any","source":"clear not painful"},
  {"id":"bc-1000","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"My child is being defiant on purpose just to provoke me.\"","myth":{"un":"My child is being defiant on purpose just to provoke me.","re":"Most 'defiance' is a child overwhelmed or testing limits, not plotting against you; reading it that way calms your response.","why":"Behaviour is communication, not attack."},"relearn":"A child's tough behaviour is usually overwhelm or limit-testing, not a plot against you; seeing that softens how you respond.","persona":"Suresh","source":"not out to get you"},
  {"id":"bc-1001","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Rules only work if I enforce them harshly and inconsistently scares them.\"","myth":{"un":"Rules only work if I enforce them harshly, and being unpredictable keeps them on their toes.","re":"Children follow rules best when limits are calm, clear and consistent; unpredictability breeds anxiety, not behaviour.","why":"Consistency teaches; unpredictability frightens."},"relearn":"Calm, clear, consistent limits work better than harsh, unpredictable ones, which just leave a child anxious.","persona":"any","source":"consistent not harsh"},
  {"id":"bc-1002","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Discipline is about punishing the wrong, full stop.\"","myth":{"un":"Discipline is about punishing the wrong, full stop.","re":"Discipline at its root means to teach; the aim is a child who understands and chooses better, not one who's been punished.","why":"To discipline is to teach, not to punish."},"relearn":"Discipline means to teach, not to punish; the goal is a child who learns the better choice, not one who fears.","persona":"any","source":"discipline is teaching"},
  {"id":"bc-1003","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Letting a child feel a hard feeling without punishing is just being soft.\"","myth":{"un":"Letting a child feel a hard feeling without punishing is just being soft.","re":"You can hold the limit AND let the feeling be felt; naming the feeling while keeping the rule is firm and kind, not soft.","why":"You hold the line and the feeling both."},"relearn":"Allowing the feeling while holding the limit isn't soft; naming the feeling and keeping the rule is firm and kind.","persona":"Vikram","source":"feeling plus limit"},
  {"id":"bc-1004","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If hitting is off the table, I'm left with no way to discipline at all.\"","myth":{"un":"If hitting is off the table, I'm left with no way to discipline at all.","re":"Without hitting you still have many tools: clear limits, related consequences, naming feelings, and warmth that works better.","why":"Non-violent discipline is full of tools."},"relearn":"Taking hitting off the table leaves you a full toolkit: clear limits, related consequences, and warmth that works.","persona":"Suresh","source":"a full toolkit"},
  {"id":"bc-1005","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each line: firm-and-kind discipline, or fear-based control?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"You broke it, so let's clean it up together.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"Hit you back so you know how it feels.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"I won't let you hit; we'll find words instead.\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"Wait till your father gets home.\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"The rule stands, and I'm right here with you.\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"You're useless, why can't you behave?\""}],"bins":[{"id":"firm","label":"Firm & kind","valence":"pos"},{"id":"fear","label":"Fear-based","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"firm","b":"fear","c":"firm","d":"fear","e":"firm","f":"fear"},"relearn":"Repairing together, blocking harm calmly and holding a limit warmly are firm-and-kind; hitting back, threats and insults are fear-based.","persona":"any","source":"firm vs fear lines"},
  {"id":"bc-1006","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: an effective consequence, or just a punishment that doesn't teach?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Spilt juice → help mop it up"},{"id":"b","text":"A whole week with no meals"},{"id":"c","text":"Skipped homework → no screen till it's done"},{"id":"d","text":"A hard slap for talking back"},{"id":"e","text":"Threw a toy in anger → toy rests for the day"},{"id":"f","text":"Locking the child outside alone"}],"bins":[{"id":"teaches","label":"Teaches the lesson","valence":"pos"},{"id":"harms","label":"Just punishes / harms","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"teaches","b":"harms","c":"teaches","d":"harms","e":"teaches","f":"harms"},"relearn":"Clear, related consequences (clean the spill, finish homework, the toy rests) teach; starving, slapping or locking out only harm.","persona":"any","source":"consequence vs punishment"},
  {"id":"bc-1007","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each phrase: builds your child up, or shames them down?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"That was unkind; let's make it right.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"You're so stupid, you never learn.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"You're better than that choice, try again.\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"Look how you've embarrassed me in front of everyone.\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"Mistakes happen; what can we do next time?\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"No wonder nobody likes you.\""}],"bins":[{"id":"builds","label":"Builds them up","valence":"pos"},{"id":"shames","label":"Shames them down","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"builds","b":"shames","c":"builds","d":"shames","e":"builds","f":"shames"},"relearn":"Naming the act and offering a redo builds a child; calling them stupid, unloved or an embarrassment shames and discourages.","persona":"any","source":"build vs shame phrases"},
  {"id":"bc-1008","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a way to set a limit that teaches, or one that only frightens?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Explaining the reason behind the rule"},{"id":"b","text":"Glaring and raising a hand to threaten"},{"id":"c","text":"Offering a small choice within the limit"},{"id":"d","text":"Mocking the child until they comply"},{"id":"e","text":"Staying calm and repeating it once"},{"id":"f","text":"Silent cold-shouldering for hours"}],"bins":[{"id":"teaches","label":"Teaches the limit","valence":"pos"},{"id":"frightens","label":"Only frightens","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"teaches","b":"frightens","c":"teaches","d":"frightens","e":"teaches","f":"frightens"},"relearn":"Explaining the reason, offering a choice and calm repetition teach a limit; threatening, mocking and cold-shouldering only frighten.","persona":"any","source":"teach vs frighten limit"},
  {"id":"bc-1009","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: discipline that respects the child, or that humiliates them?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A quiet word aside, not in front of others"},{"id":"b","text":"Scolding them loudly in front of guests"},{"id":"c","text":"Letting them save face while holding the rule"},{"id":"d","text":"Telling relatives how badly they behaved"},{"id":"e","text":"Asking what happened before deciding"},{"id":"f","text":"Calling them a name in front of friends"}],"bins":[{"id":"respects","label":"Respects the child","valence":"pos"},{"id":"humiliates","label":"Humiliates","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"respects","b":"humiliates","c":"respects","d":"humiliates","e":"respects","f":"humiliates"},"relearn":"A quiet word, saving face and hearing them out respect a child; public scolding and name-calling humiliate.","persona":"any","source":"respect vs humiliate"},
  {"id":"bc-1010","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each belief: true about positive discipline, or a myth to leave behind?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Firm-and-kind holds clear limits"},{"id":"b","text":"Being kind means no rules at all"},{"id":"c","text":"Children learn better without fear"},{"id":"d","text":"You must choose: strict or spoiled"},{"id":"e","text":"Warmth and boundaries go together"},{"id":"f","text":"Only pain makes a lesson stick"}],"bins":[{"id":"true","label":"True","valence":"pos"},{"id":"myth","label":"Myth to leave","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"true","b":"myth","c":"true","d":"myth","e":"true","f":"myth"},"relearn":"Firm limits, learning without fear and warmth-with-boundaries are true; 'kind means no rules', 'strict or spoiled' and 'only pain teaches' are myths.","persona":"any","source":"true vs myth"},
  {"id":"bc-1011","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each response to a meltdown: helps the child settle, or pours fuel on it?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Lowering your voice, getting to their level"},{"id":"b","text":"Shouting louder than they are"},{"id":"c","text":"Naming the feeling: 'you're really angry'"},{"id":"d","text":"Threatening a beating if they don't stop"},{"id":"e","text":"Holding the limit calmly while they cry"},{"id":"f","text":"Mocking the crying to shame them quiet"}],"bins":[{"id":"settles","label":"Helps them settle","valence":"pos"},{"id":"fuels","label":"Pours fuel on it","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"settles","b":"fuels","c":"settles","d":"fuels","e":"settles","f":"fuels"},"relearn":"A low voice, naming the feeling and a calm limit help a child settle; shouting, threats and mockery escalate it.","persona":"any","source":"settle vs fuel"},
  {"id":"bc-1012","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"lie discipline","hook":"Your daughter Tanvi said she'd finished her homework. A note from her teacher says she hadn't.","steps":[{"prompt":"Tanvi walks in and sees the note in your hand. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Lecture her about honesty for twenty minutes","then":"She nods at every pause and hears none of it."},{"text":"Calmly name what happened and ask why lying felt safer","then":"Tanvi looks at the floor. 'You'd have been angry,' she mumbles.","best":true},{"text":"Slap her once and call her a liar, so she remembers","then":"Tanvi cries and runs out. She says nothing about the homework."},{"text":"Pretend you didn't see the note, to avoid a scene","then":"She relaxes, but she saw you read it. The note hangs between you all evening."}],"why":"Asking calmly why she lied shows you want the truth, not someone to punish."},{"prompt":"That night, Tanvi says she was scared you'd shout. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say she should be scared, after what she did","then":"She nods, eyes down. The fear stays."},{"text":"Tell her she's making excuses now, on top of lying","then":"She clams up completely."},{"text":"Promise you'll never, ever be angry about anything she does","then":"She looks doubtful. She's heard promises like that before."},{"text":"Say the truth will always be safe with you, even when it's hard","then":"Tanvi's shoulders drop. 'The maths was too hard,' she admits.","best":true}],"why":"When the truth is safe, children stop needing to hide."},{"prompt":"The homework still isn't done. What consequence do you choose?","options":[{"text":"No consequence at all, since she's been through enough","then":"She's relieved, and the homework slips again the next week."},{"text":"Do the homework for her so she's not in trouble","then":"The teacher is happy. Tanvi still can't do the sums."},{"text":"Ban TV for a whole month so she never lies again","then":"Tanvi sulks, and the maths stays confusing."},{"text":"She finishes it before TV tonight, with help if needed","then":"Tanvi groans but sits down. With a bit of help, she's done by eight.","best":true}],"why":"A fair, related consequence teaches responsibility without fear."},{"prompt":"A week later, Tanvi confesses she broke your reading glasses. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say it's fine, and hide how annoyed you are","then":"She's relieved, but your tight voice gives you away."},{"text":"Ask why she's always breaking things","then":"Her face falls. 'I'm always the problem,' she mutters."},{"text":"Thank her for telling you, then talk about fixing it","then":"She lets out a breath. 'I'll be more careful,' she says, and means it.","best":true},{"text":"Scold her properly for being so careless with your things","then":"She looks crushed. Next time, she might not tell you."}],"why":"Welcoming a confession keeps her telling you the truth next time."}],"debrief":"Name the lie calmly, set a fair consequence and make truth safe; hitting just teaches hiding.","relearn":"Discipline a lie by naming it and making honesty safe; hitting only teaches better lying."},
  {"id":"bc-1013","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"sibling hitting","hook":"Your son Arnav hits his little sister Kavya, and the old reflex says to hit him back.","steps":[{"prompt":"Kavya is wailing, and Arnav's hand is raised again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Stop his hand firmly, then comfort Kavya first","then":"Arnav's arm drops. Kavya buries her face in your shoulder.","best":true},{"text":"Scold Arnav first, then check on Kavya","then":"Arnav sulks. Kavya cries alone until you get to her."},{"text":"Smack Arnav once so he learns how it feels","then":"Arnav howls. Now both children are crying, and one is furious."},{"text":"Tell Kavya to hit him back so she learns to stand up","then":"Kavya swings wildly. Arnav laughs and pushes her over."}],"why":"Stopping the hit and caring for the hurt child first shows who needs you most."},{"prompt":"Once Kavya calms down, Arnav says, 'She broke my car!' What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say that's no excuse, and send him out of the room","then":"Arnav stomps off, sure nobody cares about his car."},{"text":"Agree she deserved it, since she broke his car first","then":"Arnav grins. Kavya learns her brother's hits are allowed."},{"text":"Make him say sorry, then move on","then":"He mutters 'sorry' and stomps off, still angry."},{"text":"Say that's upsetting, and teach him to use words next time","then":"Arnav frowns, then says, 'I'm angry you broke it.' Kavya nods.","best":true}],"why":"Naming his feeling and teaching words gives him something to do instead of hitting."},{"prompt":"The next day, Arnav raises his hand at Kavya again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Keep them in separate rooms for the rest of the day","then":"The house is quiet, but they never practise sorting it out."},{"text":"Smack him this time, since talking clearly didn't work","then":"He cries, then hits Kavya later when you're not looking."},{"text":"Take his side, since she probably started it","then":"Kavya wails that it's unfair, and she's right."},{"text":"Step in calmly, say 'use words, not hands,' and stay close","then":"Arnav stops himself, glares, and says, 'Give it back!' instead.","best":true}],"why":"Calm repetition builds the new habit. Hitting back just proves the bigger person wins."}],"debrief":"Block the hitting, tend the hurt child, teach words; hitting back just proves force wins.","relearn":"When your child hits, stop it, comfort the sibling, teach words; hitting back proves force wins."},
  {"id":"bc-1014","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"relative pressure","hook":"At your cousin's wedding, your son Bunty knocks over a tray of sweets, and relatives start muttering.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your uncle says loudly, 'Give him a good thrashing.' What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say warmly that you'll handle it, and take Bunty aside","then":"Your uncle huffs. In a quiet corner, Bunty looks up at you, scared.","best":true},{"text":"Tell your uncle to mind his own business","then":"Your uncle turns red. Now the whole table is watching."},{"text":"Smack Bunty right there to satisfy the relatives","then":"Bunty bursts into tears. A few relatives nod, and your stomach knots."},{"text":"Pretend not to hear, and keep chatting with your cousins","then":"Your uncle repeats himself, louder. Bunty hides behind your chair."}],"why":"Holding your line warmly and handling it privately protects your child and the peace."},{"prompt":"Bunty whispers, 'I didn't mean to.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell him it doesn't matter at all and send him to play","then":"He runs off, and the mess is left for the caterers."},{"text":"Say you believe him, and he can help clean up","then":"Bunty nods and picks up a napkin to help.","best":true},{"text":"Tell him to stop crying in front of everyone","then":"He swallows his tears and goes silent."},{"text":"Tell him he's embarrassed the whole family today","then":"Bunty shrinks and won't look at anyone."}],"why":"Believing him and helping him make amends teaches responsibility without shame."},{"prompt":"Your aunt tells the others you're raising a spoilt child. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Argue it out with her, right there in front of everyone","then":"Heads turn. The bride's mother looks upset."},{"text":"Smile, say 'He's learning,' and get back to the celebration","then":"She sniffs, but the music starts and the moment passes.","best":true},{"text":"Leave the wedding early to avoid the comments","then":"Bunty is disappointed, and the talk carries on without you."},{"text":"Smack Bunty after all, just so they stop talking","then":"Bunty cries again, and can't understand what he did this time."}],"why":"A short, calm reply holds your line without turning it into a public fight."},{"prompt":"On the drive home, your spouse says they felt embarrassed too. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say they should have backed you up in there","then":"Your spouse goes quiet. The drive home is tense."},{"text":"Say you felt it too, and agree how you'll both handle it","then":"Your spouse nods. 'Calm, firm, and together. Even at weddings.'","best":true},{"text":"Change the subject, since the wedding's over now anyway","then":"The embarrassment stays unspoken, ready for next time."},{"text":"Agree that maybe a smack would have been easier","then":"The idea hangs in the car, waiting for the next function."}],"why":"Agreeing on your approach together makes it easier to hold under family pressure."},{"prompt":"At home, Bunty asks why his uncle wanted you to hit him. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say some grown-ups were raised that way, but you won't hit him","then":"Bunty thinks, then hugs you. 'I'm glad you don't.'","best":true},{"text":"Tell him not to worry his head about grown-up talk","then":"Bunty nods, but lies awake wondering."},{"text":"Say his uncle is just a bad, old-fashioned man who's wrong","then":"Bunty looks confused. He likes his uncle."},{"text":"Say he'll deserve one if he ever does that again","then":"Bunty goes quiet, worried about his next mistake."}],"why":"Honest words that don't blame his uncle teach him he's safe with you."}],"debrief":"Hold your non-violent line under pressure and handle it calmly; their approval isn't worth his safety.","relearn":"Pressed to 'thrash' your child, hold your line and stay calm; their approval isn't worth his safety."},
  {"id":"bc-1015","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"toddler limit","hook":"Your toddler Mishti keeps throwing food on the floor, grinning at you each time.","steps":[{"prompt":"Another fistful of rice hits the floor, and your patience is gone. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say calmly, 'Food stays on the plate,' and end the meal","then":"Mishti protests loudly, then gets down to play.","best":true},{"text":"Slap her little hand every single time she throws some","then":"She startles and cries, then throws the next handful anyway."},{"text":"Laugh along with her, since she's only little","then":"She squeals and throws more, looking to you for the laugh."},{"text":"Take the plate and feed her yourself, spoon by spoon","then":"She eats a bit, then spits out the next spoonful, grinning."}],"why":"A clear, calm limit teaches her far better than fear."},{"prompt":"An hour later, Mishti is hungry and whining for food. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell her it's her own fault she's hungry","then":"She doesn't understand, and cries harder."},{"text":"Offer her a small snack at the table, calmly","then":"She sits and eats every bite of her banana.","best":true},{"text":"Refuse any food till dinner, to teach her","then":"She wails until dinner, and you're both worn out."},{"text":"Give her biscuits on the sofa so she stops","then":"Crumbs everywhere, and she learns the sofa is for snacks."}],"why":"Food at the table, calmly offered, keeps the limit without punishing hunger."},{"prompt":"At dinner, she picks up a fistful of dal and grins at you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Move everything out of reach and feed her yourself","then":"Dinner is tidy, but she never gets the chance to learn."},{"text":"Say once, calmly, 'Food stays on the plate,' and wait","then":"Mishti hesitates, then puts the dal in her mouth.","best":true},{"text":"Warn her she'll get a smack if she throws it","then":"She freezes, scared, and won't eat anything."},{"text":"Grab her wrist before she can throw it","then":"She cries out, and dinner becomes a battle."}],"why":"Toddlers learn limits through calm repetition, not fear."}],"debrief":"Name the rule calmly and end the meal; a toddler learns the limit far better than from a slap.","relearn":"Meet thrown food with a calm limit and ending the meal; a slap only frightens a toddler."},
  {"id":"bc-1016","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"exam marks","hook":"Your son Nikhil brings home a poor maths mark, and you feel the old urge to punish him hard.","steps":[{"prompt":"He hands you the paper, eyes on the floor. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say marks don't matter and sign it quickly","then":"Nikhil relaxes, and puts the paper away without a word."},{"text":"Sit with him and ask what felt tough","then":"Nikhil sits beside you. 'The fractions didn't make sense,' he says.","best":true},{"text":"Beat him the way you were beaten for marks","then":"He cries, and learns to fear showing you his papers."},{"text":"Tell him no play until his marks improve","then":"Nikhil nods silently and goes to his room."}],"why":"Curiosity about what was hard opens the door to fixing it."},{"prompt":"With Nikhil in earshot, your sister-in-law calls to ask about his maths mark. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say he found it hard, and you're working on it together","then":"She says her son struggled too. Nikhil, listening, looks less ashamed.","best":true},{"text":"Complain loudly that he's lazy and just useless at maths","then":"Nikhil hears every word from the next room."},{"text":"Laugh and say he's just not a maths person","then":"Nikhil hears, and starts to believe it."},{"text":"Say he got full marks, to dodge the comparison","then":"She's impressed. Nikhil hears, and feels worse."}],"why":"Speaking about him with respect protects his confidence, even when marks are low."},{"prompt":"That evening, you sit down to plan how he'll catch up. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Sign him up for three tuitions straight away","then":"He's exhausted and has no time left to play."},{"text":"Plan twenty minutes of fractions with him each day","then":"Nikhil groans, then says, 'Only twenty?' and agrees.","best":true},{"text":"Tell him to figure it out by himself","then":"He stares at the book, still stuck."},{"text":"Make him study for three hours every night","then":"He sits for hours, learning little and hating maths."}],"why":"A small, steady plan beats pressure. Support helps a child try harder."},{"prompt":"The next test comes back only a little better. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell him a little better is still not good enough","then":"His face drops. He stops trying so hard."},{"text":"Give up on the plan, since it hasn't really worked","then":"The fractions fade, and next time is worse."},{"text":"Notice the effort and the improvement, and keep going","then":"Nikhil beams. 'Can we do decimals next?'","best":true},{"text":"Compare him with his cousin who topped the class","then":"He goes quiet and avoids his cousin at the next function."}],"why":"Noticing effort keeps him trying. Fear only teaches a child to hide."}],"debrief":"Meet a poor mark with curiosity and a plan, not a beating; support raises effort, fear raises hiding.","relearn":"Answer a poor mark with support and a plan, not a beating; fear makes a child hide, support helps them try."},
  {"id":"bc-1017","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"screen limit","hook":"Your daughter Zoya has ignored three reminders to get off the phone, and you're about to snatch it.","steps":[{"prompt":"She doesn't even look up. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Snatch it out of her hands and tell her exactly what you think","then":"Zoya screams, and the evening turns into a shouting match."},{"text":"Give a calm final warning: 'Two minutes, or no phone tomorrow'","then":"Zoya sighs dramatically, but glances at the clock.","best":true},{"text":"Give up for tonight and let her keep scrolling in peace","then":"She scrolls on till eleven, and you seethe quietly."},{"text":"Remind her a fourth time, a bit louder","then":"She mumbles 'yes, yes' and keeps scrolling."}],"why":"A calm, clear final warning makes the limit real without a fight."},{"prompt":"The next evening, you give a two-minute warning. When it's up, Zoya is still scrolling. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Give one more warning, and another after that","then":"She learns your warnings don't mean much."},{"text":"Lecture her about phones ruining her brain","then":"She rolls her eyes and tunes you out."},{"text":"Take it and say she'll get it back when she learns respect","then":"She asks when that will be, and you don't know either."},{"text":"Follow through quietly: take the phone and keep it overnight","then":"Zoya protests, then stomps off. The phone sits on your shelf.","best":true}],"why":"Quiet follow-through makes limits feel predictable, not scary."},{"prompt":"In the morning, Zoya says the rule is unfair. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Warn her she'll lose the phone for a month if she argues","then":"She goes silent, but hides the phone under her pillow."},{"text":"Listen, then agree a clear phone time together","then":"She suggests nine. You say eight-thirty. You settle on quarter to nine.","best":true},{"text":"Say it's your house and your rules, end of discussion","then":"She stomps off, and the resentment builds."},{"text":"Drop the rule to keep her happy","then":"She's happy, and back on the phone past midnight."}],"why":"Letting her have a say makes the limit fair, and she's more likely to keep it."}],"debrief":"A calm final warning plus quiet follow-through holds the limit better than snatching or caving.","relearn":"Hold a screen limit with a calm warning and quiet follow-through, not snatching or caving in."},
  {"id":"bc-1018","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"broken window","hook":"Your son Farhan broke a neighbour's window playing cricket, and now he's terrified of you.","steps":[{"prompt":"He's hiding behind the door, shaking. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Drag him out and thrash him in front of the neighbour","then":"Farhan screams and cries. The neighbour looks away, uncomfortable."},{"text":"Shout from the doorway that he's in big trouble","then":"He shrinks further behind the door."},{"text":"Stay calm, and tell him you'll sort it out together","then":"Farhan peeks out, tear-streaked. 'Are you really not angry?'","best":true},{"text":"Tell him it's no big deal and you'll pay quietly","then":"He's relieved, and runs back out to play."}],"why":"Staying calm makes it safe for him to face what he did."},{"prompt":"Mr Joseph next door still hasn't had an apology for his window. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Avoid Mr Joseph and hope he forgets","then":"Mr Joseph knocks that evening, cross that nobody came."},{"text":"Go with Farhan so he can apologise himself","then":"Farhan mumbles 'sorry.' Mr Joseph softens. 'Thank you for coming.'","best":true},{"text":"Go alone and apologise on his behalf","then":"Mr Joseph accepts. Farhan watches from the window, off the hook."},{"text":"Send Farhan over alone, so he learns to face it","then":"Farhan stands frozen at the gate and can't get a word out."}],"why":"Standing beside him while he apologises teaches accountability without fear."},{"prompt":"The glass will cost a fair bit to replace. What do you do about the money?","options":[{"text":"Make him pay it all, even if it takes a year","then":"Farhan despairs, and stops asking for anything."},{"text":"Stop his cricket for the whole summer instead","then":"He storms off. The ban is all he can think about, not the window."},{"text":"Pay, and have Farhan chip in some pocket money","then":"Farhan solemnly hands over the coins from his piggy bank.","best":true},{"text":"Pay it yourself and never mention it","then":"Farhan never quite learns what it cost."}],"why":"A fair share of the cost teaches responsibility without crushing him."},{"prompt":"At dinner, your brother says a good thrashing is the only way kids learn. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Agree, and say maybe next time you'll give him one","then":"Farhan hears, and stops eating."},{"text":"Say fear teaches hiding, and you want him to own up instead","then":"Your brother shrugs. Farhan looks up from his plate, listening.","best":true},{"text":"Tell your brother he's still stuck in the dark ages","then":"Your brother bristles, and dinner turns frosty."},{"text":"Say nothing, and let him have his say","then":"Farhan wonders if you secretly agree."}],"why":"Fear teaches a child to hide mistakes. Calm accountability teaches them to own up."}],"debrief":"Have them own it and help make it right while you stay calm; accountability grows without fear.","relearn":"After a broken window, have your child help make it right calmly; it teaches accountability without fear."},
  {"id":"bc-1019","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"whining no","hook":"You're exhausted, and your son Aditya keeps whining for sweets before dinner.","steps":[{"prompt":"He asks for the fifth time, louder now. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Snap and threaten to take away his toys","then":"He cries loudly, and the whining turns into wailing."},{"text":"Give in so you can have some peace","then":"He munches one happily, and within minutes he wants another."},{"text":"Say once, 'No, and I know that's hard,' then stop arguing","then":"Aditya stamps his foot, then goes off to sulk with his toys.","best":true},{"text":"Explain again, in detail, why sweets before dinner are bad","then":"He argues every point, and the whining gets louder."}],"why":"A kind, firm 'no' held once stops the back and forth."},{"prompt":"Ten minutes later, he's back, whining for sweets again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Calmly say 'Not before dinner,' and offer a game instead","then":"He hesitates, then grabs the carrom board. The whining fades.","best":true},{"text":"Ignore him completely until he gives up","then":"He whines a little longer, then trails off, looking hurt and shut out."},{"text":"Give in. You're far too tired for this fight","then":"He learns that whining long enough gets a yes."},{"text":"Slap his hand away the moment he reaches for the tin","then":"He cries and knocks the tin off the shelf."}],"why":"Holding the no warmly shows him the answer won't change with whining."},{"prompt":"The next evening, as Aditya whines again, your mother-in-law says, 'Just give the child a sweet.' What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Hide the sweet tin so she has nothing to give him","then":"Aditya and his grandmother hunt for it together. The rule never gets said out loud."},{"text":"Give in to avoid an argument with her","then":"Aditya grins. He knows who to go to next time."},{"text":"Tell her not to interfere with your parenting","then":"She's hurt, and the evening turns tense."},{"text":"Thank her, and say sweets come after dinner today","then":"She shrugs. 'Your rules.' Aditya sighs and waits.","best":true}],"why":"Holding your limit politely keeps it consistent, even under family pressure."}],"debrief":"A warm, firm 'no' held once without re-arguing settles whining better than a slap or giving in.","relearn":"Hold a 'no' warmly and stop negotiating. A slap teaches hitting, and caving teaches that whining works."},
  {"id":"bc-1020","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"match","hook":"Match each misbehaviour to a firm-and-kind response that teaches.","pairs":[{"left":"Refusing to share a toy","right":"Set a turn-taking timer, stay close"},{"left":"Drawing on the wall","right":"Hand them a cloth to clean it together"},{"left":"Yelling 'I hate you'","right":"Name the anger, hold the limit anyway"},{"left":"Dawdling at bedtime","right":"A calm routine and one clear choice"},{"left":"Grabbing in a shop","right":"Quietly leave, try again another day"}],"relearn":"Each misbehaviour has a calm teaching response: a timer, a clean-up, naming the feeling, a routine, or a quiet exit.","persona":"Vikram","source":"misbehaviour to response"},
  {"id":"bc-1021","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"match","hook":"Match each fear-based old move to the firm-and-kind move that replaces it.","pairs":[{"left":"A slap to stop them","right":"A calm, clear limit"},{"left":"'Shut up or else'","right":"'I'm listening; the rule still stands'"},{"left":"Shaming in public","right":"A quiet word aside"},{"left":"'You're so bad'","right":"'That choice wasn't okay'"},{"left":"Threatening to abandon them","right":"'I'm staying right here with you'"}],"relearn":"Every fear-based reflex has a firm-and-kind swap: a calm limit, real listening, a private word, naming the act, and staying close.","persona":"any","source":"swap the reflex"},
  {"id":"bc-1022","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"match","hook":"Match each part of a calm consequence to why it works.","pairs":[{"left":"It's related to what happened","right":"The lesson makes sense to the child"},{"left":"It's explained, not just imposed","right":"They understand, not just obey"},{"left":"It's delivered calmly","right":"No fear clouds the learning"},{"left":"It ends and is forgiven","right":"They aren't shamed long-term"},{"left":"It's consistent next time","right":"They learn the limit is real"}],"relearn":"A consequence works when it's related, explained, calm, time-limited and consistent. That's what makes a child learn, not fear.","persona":"any","source":"why consequences work"},
  {"id":"bc-1023","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"match","hook":"Match each child's underlying need to the discipline that meets it.","pairs":[{"left":"Overtired and melting down","right":"Comfort first, rules after rest"},{"left":"Testing if the limit is real","right":"Hold it calmly and consistently"},{"left":"Big feeling with no words","right":"Name the feeling for them"},{"left":"Wants some control","right":"Offer a small choice within the limit"},{"left":"Feeling unseen","right":"Warm attention before correcting"}],"relearn":"Behind misbehaviour is a need, like rest, a real limit, words for a feeling, some control, or attention, and meeting it teaches best.","persona":"any","source":"need to discipline"},
  {"id":"bc-1024","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"match","hook":"Match each common myth to the truth that replaces it.","pairs":[{"left":"'A slap never hurt anyone'","right":"Hitting harms and doesn't work"},{"left":"'Fear makes them respect you'","right":"Respect grows from trust"},{"left":"'Gentle means spoiled'","right":"Firm-and-kind holds the limit"},{"left":"'Shame makes them improve'","right":"Shame discourages and wounds"},{"left":"'Discipline means punishment'","right":"Discipline means to teach"}],"relearn":"Each discipline myth has a truth: hitting harms, respect is from trust, gentle still holds limits, shame wounds, discipline teaches.","persona":"any","source":"myth to truth"},
  {"id":"bc-1025","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the word 'discipline' shares a root with 'disciple', a learner, not a target. Does that shift how it feels?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["It shifts it","A learner, not a target","Teaching, not punishing"],"affirm":"Discipline and disciple share a root meaning 'to teach a learner'; the goal is a child who understands, not one who's been punished.","relearn":"Discipline means teaching a learner, not punishing a target; that root changes the whole aim.","persona":"any","source":"disciple root"},
  {"id":"bc-1026","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: picture the parent your child would feel safest telling a mistake to. That parent is firm AND warm. Worth becoming?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Worth it","Safe to tell","Firm and warm"],"affirm":"Children confide in parents who are firm and warm, not frightening; being safe to tell the truth to is the aim.","relearn":"Aim to be the parent your child feels safest telling a mistake to, firm and warm, never frightening.","persona":"any","source":"safe to tell"},
  {"id":"bc-1027","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: when you stayed calm and held a limit instead of snapping, what did you notice in your child afterward?","prompt":"What did you notice?","options":["They settled sooner","They came back closer","They listened more"],"affirm":"Holding a limit calmly usually settles a child sooner and keeps them close; firm-and-kind works, and you've likely felt it.","relearn":"You've probably noticed it: a calm, held limit settles a child sooner and keeps them close.","persona":"any","source":"noticing calm works"},
  {"id":"bc-1028","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: every calm limit you hold today is a script your child may one day use with their own kids. Sit with that?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["I'll sit with it","Passing the calm on","A better script"],"affirm":"The way you discipline becomes the inner voice your child carries; each calm limit writes a gentler script for the next generation.","relearn":"How you discipline becomes your child's future script; each calm limit hands the next generation a gentler one.","persona":"Suresh","source":"future script"},
  {"id":"bc-1029","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: 'firm' and 'kind' aren't opposites you choose between. They're two hands holding the same limit. Feel that?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["I feel it","Both hands","Not either/or"],"affirm":"Firm and kind work together, not against each other; you can hold a clear limit and stay warm in the very same breath.","relearn":"Firm and kind aren't a choice between; they're two hands on the same limit, held together.","persona":"any","source":"two hands"},
  {"id":"bc-1030","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: a child who isn't afraid of you is freer to actually hear you. Does that change what you're aiming for?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["It changes it","Heard, not feared","Less fear, more listening"],"affirm":"Fear makes a child defend and hide; safety lets them actually listen and learn. Being heard beats being feared.","relearn":"A child who isn't afraid of you can actually hear you; aim to be listened to, not feared.","persona":"any","source":"heard not feared"},
  {"id":"bc-1031","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: think of one limit you hold warmly already. You've proved you can do firm-and-kind. Build from there?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["I'll build from there","I already do this","One at a time"],"affirm":"You already hold some limits warmly; that proof you can do firm-and-kind is the foundation to grow the rest from.","relearn":"You already do firm-and-kind somewhere; build the rest from the limits you already hold warmly.","persona":"Suresh","source":"build from proof"},
  {"id":"bc-1032","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: choosing not to hit, on a hard day, when it's all you were shown, is real courage. Honour that in yourself?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["I'll honour it","It's courage","Harder than it looks"],"affirm":"Choosing a calm limit over the hand you were raised with, on an exhausting day, is genuine courage worth honouring.","relearn":"Not hitting, on a hard day, when hitting is all you were shown, is real courage. Honour it in yourself.","persona":"Suresh","source":"honour the courage"},
  {"id":"bc-1033","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two red-flag 'discipline' moves that frighten instead of teach.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Naming the rule and the reason calmly","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Raising a hand to threaten a hit","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Offering a small choice within the limit","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Calling the child stupid to shame them","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Staying warm while holding the limit","trick":false}],"why":"Threatening to hit and shaming with names frighten and wound; the others teach a child without fear.","relearn":"The red flags are threatening a hit and shaming with names; calm reasons, small choices and warmth teach instead.","persona":"any","source":"spot frightening moves"},
  {"id":"bc-1034","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that are shame, not discipline.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"That wasn't okay; let's fix it together.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You're a disgrace to this family.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"I know you can make a better choice.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"No wonder no one wants to play with you.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Mistakes happen; what's our plan?\"","trick":false}],"why":"Calling a child a disgrace or unwanted attacks their worth; the others correct the behaviour without shaming the child.","relearn":"Shame attacks the child ('a disgrace', 'no one wants you'); discipline names the act and offers a way forward.","persona":"any","source":"spot the shame"},
  {"id":"bc-1035","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two beliefs that keep the old harsh cycle running.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I can learn discipline that doesn't hurt.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"Kids only understand a beating.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"A calm limit can be firm too.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Sparing the rod spoils the child.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Warmth and rules go together.\"","trick":false}],"why":"'Only a beating works' and 'spare the rod, spoil the child' justify hitting; the others open the door to discipline without harm.","relearn":"The cycle-keeping beliefs are 'only a beating works' and 'spare the rod'; learning, calm limits and warmth break it.","persona":"Suresh","source":"spot cycle beliefs"},
  {"id":"bc-1036","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two responses to a tantrum that pour fuel on it.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Getting down to their level, lowering your voice","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Shouting louder than the child","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Naming the feeling: 'you're so angry'","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Threatening a thrashing if they don't stop","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Holding the limit calmly while they cry","trick":false}],"why":"Out-shouting a child and threatening a thrashing escalate fear; a low voice, naming the feeling and a calm limit settle it.","relearn":"Shouting louder and threatening a thrashing fuel a tantrum; a low voice, named feelings and a calm limit settle it.","persona":"any","source":"spot tantrum fuel"},
  {"id":"bc-1037","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two 'consequences' that punish without teaching anything.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Spilt it → help wipe it up","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"A full day with no food","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Skipped homework → finish before play","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Locked alone in a dark room","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Threw the toy → toy rests for the day","trick":false}],"why":"Withholding food and locking a child in the dark frighten and harm; the others are related, fair consequences that teach.","relearn":"Starving or locking a child away harms, not teaches; a related, fair consequence is what actually teaches the lesson.","persona":"any","source":"spot harmful consequences"},
  {"id":"bc-1038","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that humiliate a child in front of others.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"A quiet word aside, away from the group","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Scolding them loudly in front of guests","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Letting them save face while holding the rule","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Mocking their crying for everyone to see","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Stepping out together to talk it through","trick":false}],"why":"Loud public scolding and mocking a child's tears humiliate; a quiet word, saving face and stepping out keep their dignity.","relearn":"Public scolding and mocking tears humiliate; correct a child privately so they keep their dignity.","persona":"Vikram","source":"spot humiliation"},
  {"id":"bc-1039","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"thrown plate","hook":"Your son Chintu throws his plate in anger, and the old script wants you to hit him.","setup":"Rice is scattered all over the floor.","steps":[{"prompt":"Chintu shouts: \"I hate bhindi! I'm not eating it!\"","options":[{"text":"\"Throw one more thing and you'll really get it.\"","then":"Chintu freezes, then bursts into tears. \"I'm sorry!\""},{"text":"\"Pick it all up this second, or there's no food for you tonight!\"","then":"He folds his arms. \"Fine! I don't want any!\""},{"text":"\"Fine. I'll make you something else.\"","then":"He grins. \"Maggi, please!\""},{"text":"\"You're angry. That's okay. But I won't let you throw things.\"","then":"Chintu's lip trembles. \"But I really hate it.\"","best":true}],"why":"Allowing the feeling while holding the limit teaches self-control."},{"prompt":"Chintu looks at the rice on the floor and asks: \"Do I have to clean that?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You will, or you'll go to bed without dinner.\"","then":"He cries harder. \"You're mean!\""},{"text":"\"Let's do it together. You get the rice. I'll get the plate.\"","then":"Chintu sighs, then kneels down. \"This bit's sticky.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Look at this mess. What kind of child does a thing like this?\"","then":"He hangs his head. \"A bad one,\" he whispers."},{"text":"\"No, leave it. I'll clean it. Go and play.\"","then":"\"Yay!\" He runs off, and the mess waits for you."}],"why":"Helping him make it right teaches repair, not shame."},{"prompt":"At bedtime, Chintu asks: \"Are you still angry about the plate?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Not if you promise to always eat what you're given.\"","then":"He hesitates. \"Even karela?\""},{"text":"\"Yes, very. Don't you ever do that again.\"","then":"He pulls the sheet over his head. \"Okay,\" he whispers."},{"text":"\"No. I was upset, but it's over now. Sleep well.\"","then":"He smiles sleepily. \"Night. Sorry about the plate.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's not talk about it. Go to sleep.\"","then":"\"Okay,\" he says, but he lies awake, unsure."}],"why":"Letting the moment end kindly shows him a mistake doesn't break your love."}],"relearn":"Blocking the harm, allowing the feeling and holding the limit teaches self-control far better than a threat."},
  {"id":"bc-1040","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"relative scolds","hook":"Your aunt scolds your daughter Ira harshly for spilling tea, then looks to you to pile on.","setup":"Ira's eyes are filling with tears.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your aunt says: \"Aren't you going to say something to her?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Ira, go to your room. We'll talk later.\"","then":"Ira runs off, crying. \"Good,\" your aunt says, satisfied."},{"text":"\"You heard her. Say sorry before I lose my temper too.\"","then":"Ira whispers \"sorry,\" her face crumpling."},{"text":"\"I've got this one. Come here, beta. Let's talk.\"","then":"Your aunt raises her eyebrows. \"Well, as you like.\" Ira comes to your side.","best":true},{"text":"\"Aunty, how dare you shout at my child!\"","then":"Your aunt gasps. \"I'm only trying to help!\""}],"why":"Calmly taking over protects your child without a public clash."},{"prompt":"Later, Ira says quietly: \"Everyone thinks I'm clumsy.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Don't mind Aunty. She's just old-fashioned. That's all.\"","then":"She giggles. \"She is!\" But she still looks hurt."},{"text":"\"Everyone spills things. It was an accident. It's over.\"","then":"Ira sniffles, then half-smiles. \"Even you?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Well, you do need to be more careful.\"","then":"Her shoulders slump. \"I know.\""},{"text":"\"Stop crying about it. It was only tea.\"","then":"She wipes her eyes quickly. \"Okay.\""}],"why":"Treating an accident as an accident protects her from shame."},{"prompt":"Your aunt says: \"In our time, children feared their elders. That was respect.\"","options":[{"text":"\"And look how scared we all grew up.\"","then":"She stiffens. \"We turned out fine.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe you're right. Kids today have no fear at all.\"","then":"She nods. \"See? I knew you'd understand.\""},{"text":"\"I'd rather she trusts us. That's the respect I want.\"","then":"She considers it. \"Trust. Hmm. She does come to you. I've noticed.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Sure, Aunty. Can I get you more tea?\"","then":"\"Yes, please,\" she says, and the lecture carries on."}],"why":"Fear teaches children to hide. Respect grows from trust."},{"prompt":"As she leaves, your aunt asks: \"So how will you correct her, then?\"","options":[{"text":"\"With a good scolding, when she really deserves it.\"","then":"She smiles knowingly. \"Just the way we did.\""},{"text":"\"I won't. She'll learn on her own.\"","then":"She shakes her head. \"Then she'll run wild.\""},{"text":"\"I'll correct her, firmly and calmly, and in private.\"","then":"She nods slowly. \"Firm and calm. That I can understand.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'll do it your way whenever you're around, Aunty.\"","then":"She beams. \"Good.\" Ira overhears from the doorway."}],"why":"Firm and kind isn't permissive. You correct clearly, calmly and with dignity."}],"relearn":"Calmly taking over from a harsh relative protects the child and models the firm-and-kind way without a public clash."},
  {"id":"bc-1041","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"interrupting dinner","hook":"Your son Vihaan won't sit down for dinner and keeps interrupting everyone.","setup":"You're tired, and the food is getting cold.","steps":[{"prompt":"Vihaan says: \"But I have to tell you about my cricket match first!\"","options":[{"text":"\"I want to hear it. First we eat, then it's all yours.\"","then":"Vihaan hesitates, then slides into his chair. \"Promise?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Go and tell your grandmother instead. I'm too busy.\"","then":"\"She's asleep,\" Vihaan says, and flops into his chair, sulking."},{"text":"\"Quiet! One more word and there's no dinner for you.\"","then":"He sits, silent and sulky. \"Fine,\" he mutters, pushing his food around."},{"text":"\"Nobody wants to hear about cricket right now.\"","then":"His face falls. \"You never listen to me.\""}],"why":"Acknowledging his need while holding the limit gets him to the table."},{"prompt":"Halfway through dinner, Vihaan asks: \"Can I tell you now?\"","options":[{"text":"\"After dinner. You'll forget half of it anyway.\"","then":"He goes quiet. \"No, I won't,\" he mumbles, stung."},{"text":"\"Not now. Maybe tomorrow, if there's time.\"","then":"He shrugs. \"You always say that.\""},{"text":"\"Fine, go on then. Tell us now.\"","then":"He launches in mid-bite, and nobody else gets a word in."},{"text":"\"Nearly done. Once plates are clear, you go first.\"","then":"He wriggles, but waits. As the plates clear, he bursts out: \"Two wickets!\"","best":true}],"why":"A kind 'nearly done' holds the limit, and a turn that really comes shows him waiting pays off."},{"prompt":"As you clear up, Vihaan asks: \"Can I always talk first at dinner?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Everyone gets a turn. You'll always get yours.\"","then":"He nods slowly. \"Okay. Tomorrow I'll tell you about practice.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"No. I said so, and that's the end of it.\"","then":"\"That's not a reason,\" he mutters."},{"text":"\"No. Children should be seen, not heard.\"","then":"He looks crushed. \"So I'm not allowed to talk?\""},{"text":"\"Sure. Talk whenever you like. I'll stop minding.\"","then":"\"Yes!\" he cheers, already planning tomorrow's stories."}],"why":"A fair rule, explained, is easier for a child to keep than a command."}],"relearn":"Acknowledging the need and holding the limit, with a promise you keep, works better than a threat that punishes."},
  {"id":"bc-1042","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"admitting cheating","hook":"Your daughter Sana admits she copied answers in a class test, clearly scared of your reaction.","setup":"She's standing by the door, twisting her dupatta.","steps":[{"prompt":"Sana whispers: \"Please don't be angry. I copied from Aisha.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's okay, beta. Everyone copies a little sometimes.\"","then":"\"Really?\" She looks relieved, and a little puzzled."},{"text":"\"Thank you for telling me. That took courage. Let's talk.\"","then":"Sana lets out a shaky breath. \"I didn't know the answers.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You cheated? You've shamed us. Wait till I deal with you.\"","then":"She bursts into tears. \"I'm sorry!\" She runs out."},{"text":"\"I'm very disappointed in you. Go to your room now.\"","then":"\"Sorry,\" she whispers, and goes, head down."}],"why":"Welcoming the confession keeps her telling you the truth."},{"prompt":"Later, Sana says: \"I was scared I'd fail and you'd be angry.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Well, next time just study harder, and you won't have to.\"","then":"\"I did study,\" she mumbles, and gives up explaining."},{"text":"\"Why would I be angry? I never get angry.\"","then":"She glances at you. \"You do, though.\""},{"text":"\"I'm glad you said it. Your marks matter less than you do.\"","then":"Her eyes fill. \"Really? Even in maths?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You should be scared of failing. It's serious.\"","then":"She nods, eyes down. \"I know.\""}],"why":"Easing her fear helps her see that mistakes can be faced, not hidden."},{"prompt":"Sana asks: \"What do I do now?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Nothing. Keep it quiet and just don't do it again.\"","then":"She looks relieved, then uneasy. \"What if Aisha tells?\""},{"text":"\"I'll go and tell the teacher for you.\"","then":"\"Okay,\" she says, and stays out of it completely."},{"text":"\"Tell your teacher what happened. I'll come with you.\"","then":"She gulps. \"Okay. If you're there.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You'll stand up and tell the whole class you cheated.\"","then":"She goes pale. \"I can't. Please.\""}],"why":"Making it right, with you beside her, teaches accountability without fear."},{"prompt":"A few days later, Sana asks: \"Are you going to punish me?\"","options":[{"text":"\"That depends on how your next test goes.\"","then":"\"Oh.\" She's already anxious about the next test."},{"text":"\"Yes. No phone for a whole month, starting today.\"","then":"\"A month?\" She nods, resentful."},{"text":"\"No. Let's just forget the whole thing happened.\"","then":"\"Okay!\" She relaxes, and the gaps in her learning stay."},{"text":"\"Your job is to relearn those chapters, with my help.\"","then":"Sana nods. \"Can we start with fractions?\"","best":true}],"why":"A task that fixes the gap teaches more than punishment on top of honesty."}],"relearn":"Welcoming the honest admission keeps a child telling you the truth; a furious reaction teaches them to hide it next time."},
  {"id":"bc-1043","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"homework choice","hook":"Your son Om refuses to do his homework and slams his book down. You feel the urge to grab his ear.","setup":"He's glaring at you across the table.","steps":[{"prompt":"Om shouts: \"I'm not doing it! It's boring!\"","options":[{"text":"\"Homework comes before play. Now, or after a short break?\"","then":"He scowls, then mutters, \"After a break. Ten minutes.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Fine. Do it whenever you feel like it, then.\"","then":"\"Later, then,\" he says, and runs off to play."},{"text":"\"Your cousin never complains like this, you know.\"","then":"\"Then go and live with him!\" he yells."},{"text":"\"Do it now or I'll give you something to cry about.\"","then":"He flinches and opens the book. \"Okay, okay.\""}],"why":"A fixed limit with a small real choice avoids a power struggle."},{"prompt":"Later, Om groans: \"This sum is too hard. I can't do it.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's hard. Show me where you got stuck.\"","then":"He points. \"This part. The carrying.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Here, let me just do it for you.\"","then":"\"Thanks!\" he says, and watches you finish it."},{"text":"\"You're just not trying hard enough.\"","then":"\"I AM trying!\" he shouts, and shoves the book."},{"text":"\"Everyone else manages. Try harder.\"","then":"He slumps. \"I'm just stupid, then.\""}],"why":"Getting curious about where he's stuck turns a fight into help."},{"prompt":"When it's done, Om asks: \"Why do I even have to do homework?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Because I said so, and I don't need another reason.\"","then":"He rolls his eyes. \"That's not a reason.\""},{"text":"\"Honestly, I don't know. Schools give too much.\"","then":"He grins. \"So I can skip it tomorrow?\""},{"text":"\"Because if you fail, you'll end up with nothing.\"","then":"He looks worried. \"What if I fail?\""},{"text":"\"It's practice. Each sum makes the next one easier.\"","then":"He thinks. \"Like cricket practice?\"","best":true}],"why":"A real reason helps him see the point, which fear or a shrug never can."}],"relearn":"A fixed limit plus a small real choice gives a child control without dropping the rule, and skips the power struggle."},
  {"id":"bc-1044","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"i hate you","hook":"You said no to a sleepover at Riya's, and your daughter Aanya is furious.","setup":"Her door is half open, and she's glaring through it.","steps":[{"prompt":"Aanya yells: \"I hate you! You ruin everything!\"","options":[{"text":"\"How dare you say that to me! Apologise this instant!\"","then":"\"No!\" she screams, and slams the door."},{"text":"\"Go to your room until you can speak properly.\"","then":"\"I'm already in my room!\" she yells back."},{"text":"\"Fine. Hate me all you like. See if I care.\"","then":"\"I knew it,\" she says quietly, and shuts the door."},{"text":"\"You can be angry with me. I still love you. It's still no.\"","then":"\"You don't get it!\" she shouts, but closes the door softly.","best":true}],"why":"Letting her feelings be while holding the limit shows big feelings don't break the bond."},{"prompt":"An hour later, Aanya mumbles: \"Everyone else is allowed.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Okay, maybe I'll call Riya's parents and see.\"","then":"\"So that's a yes?\" she asks, suddenly hopeful."},{"text":"\"If everyone else jumped off a cliff, would you jump too?\"","then":"She rolls her eyes. \"That's not the same.\""},{"text":"\"I don't care what everyone else's parents do.\"","then":"\"Obviously,\" she mutters, and turns away."},{"text":"\"That sounds hard. Tell me why this one matters so much.\"","then":"She sits on the bed. \"Riya's moving to Pune next month.\"","best":true}],"why":"Listening to what's underneath can open a way forward without giving in."},{"prompt":"Next morning, Aanya asks: \"So can I go to Riya's on Saturday or not?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No sleepover. Go for the day, and I'll drop you at ten.\"","then":"She hugs you, a little stiffly. \"Sorry I said I hate you.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"After the way you spoke to me last night? Absolutely not.\"","then":"\"You're so unfair!\" she cries, and the fight starts again."},{"text":"\"Fine. Go on, then. You might as well stay the night too.\"","then":"\"Yes!\" she cheers, and runs to pack her bag."},{"text":"\"We'll see. Ask me again later in the week.\"","then":"\"That always means no,\" she sighs."}],"why":"A calm no that finds a middle way keeps both the bond and the limit."}],"relearn":"Letting a child's angry words be, while holding the limit and the love, teaches that big feelings don't break the bond."},
  {"id":"bc-1045","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"market sweets","hook":"In a crowded market, your son Aarush grabs a packet of sweets from a stall, and you say no.","setup":"You can feel people watching.","steps":[{"prompt":"Aarush wails: \"I want them! Please, please, please!\"","options":[{"text":"\"Not today. I know that's hard. Let's put them back.\"","then":"Aarush sobs, but lets you take the packet. \"But I'm hungry.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Fine, just this once. Now stop that crying, please.\"","then":"\"Yay!\" He stops crying at once, then grabs a second packet too."},{"text":"\"Stop embarrassing me. Want everyone to see you get a hiding?\"","then":"He goes silent and clings to your leg. \"Sorry,\" he whispers."},{"text":"\"If you stop crying, I'll buy you a bigger treat.\"","then":"\"The big chocolate!\" he says, tears gone, still clutching the sweets."}],"why":"Naming the disappointment while holding the no settles him without fear."},{"prompt":"The shopkeeper chuckles: \"Arre, let him have them. Kids are kids.\"","options":[{"text":"\"See, Aarush? Even uncle thinks you're a crybaby.\"","then":"\"I didn't say that!\" the shopkeeper says, as Aarush howls."},{"text":"\"Okay, okay. Give me one packet, then. Just the one.\"","then":"\"Good!\" the shopkeeper says, winking at Aarush, who beams."},{"text":"\"Thanks, but not today. We'll have a treat at home.\"","then":"\"Strict parent!\" the shopkeeper laughs. Aarush sniffles, but walks on.","best":true},{"text":"\"Please don't interfere. This is between us.\"","then":"\"Only trying to help,\" he says, offended. Aarush wails louder."}],"why":"Thanking others politely while keeping your limit keeps it consistent."},{"prompt":"On the way home, Aarush asks: \"Can I have a sweet before dinner?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No. Sweets will rot all your teeth out.\"","then":"He covers his mouth. \"All of them?\""},{"text":"\"No, because I'm the parent and I decide.\"","then":"He sighs and kicks a stone. \"Not fair.\""},{"text":"\"Not before dinner. Sweets come after meals.\"","then":"He thinks. \"So I can have one after dinner?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"No. Crying like a baby doesn't get you sweets.\"","then":"He looks down. \"I'm not a baby.\""}],"why":"A simple, honest reason helps a child accept a limit."},{"prompt":"After dinner, Aarush asks: \"Can I have a sweet now?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Only if you promise never to cry in public again.\"","then":"He nods, confused. \"Is crying bad?\""},{"text":"\"Take as many as you want. You've earned it.\"","then":"\"Really?\" He grabs a fistful, grinning."},{"text":"\"No. Not after that tantrum in the market.\"","then":"His face crumples. \"But that was ages ago!\""},{"text":"\"Yes, one, now that dinner's done.\"","then":"He picks one carefully. \"The pink one.\"","best":true}],"why":"Following through calmly shows the no was about timing, not punishment."}],"relearn":"Naming the disappointment and holding the 'no' calmly settles a public scene better than threatening or shaming."},
  {"id":"bc-1046","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"clear consequence","hook":"Your daughter Diya used her tablet after bedtime, breaking a rule you'd both agreed on.","setup":"You find her under the blanket, screen glowing.","steps":[{"prompt":"Diya says: \"I was just finishing one video!\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's it. No screens for a month, and don't test me again.\"","then":"\"A MONTH?\" she wails. \"That's so unfair!\""},{"text":"\"You're a liar. I can't trust you with anything.\"","then":"She pulls the blanket over her head. \"Fine. I'm a liar.\""},{"text":"\"Okay, finish it. But just this once.\"","then":"\"Thanks!\" she says, and starts another one."},{"text":"\"We agreed, so the tablet stays with me tomorrow.\"","then":"She groans. \"Only tomorrow, right?\"","best":true}],"why":"A clear, related, short consequence teaches the limit without resentment."},{"prompt":"The next evening, Diya asks: \"Can we change the bedtime rule? I'm older now.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's talk about it. What time do you think is fair?\"","then":"She thinks. \"Maybe nine-thirty on weekends?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Sure, stay up as late as you like.\"","then":"\"Really?\" she squeals, then pauses. \"Wait. Maybe ten on school nights?\""},{"text":"\"Not after what you did last night.\"","then":"Her face falls. \"So I'm punished forever?\""},{"text":"\"No. Rules are rules, and that's the end of the story.\"","then":"\"You never listen,\" she mutters."}],"why":"Letting her have a say in the rules makes her more likely to keep them."},{"prompt":"A week later, Diya says: \"I kept the rule every night. Did you notice?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Good. Make sure you don't break it again.\"","then":"\"I wasn't going to,\" she says, hurt."},{"text":"\"That's just what you're supposed to do.\"","then":"\"Yeah, yeah,\" she mutters, deflated."},{"text":"\"Yes. Here, have an extra hour of screens.\"","then":"\"Yes!\" she says. The next week she asks for two."},{"text":"\"I did. Thank you. That's you being trustworthy.\"","then":"She beams. \"I told you I could.\"","best":true}],"why":"Noticing when she keeps a rule builds trust faster than pointing out when she breaks it."}],"relearn":"A clear, related, time-limited consequence delivered calmly teaches the limit; a huge, angry ban only breeds resentment."},
  {"id":"bc-1047","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: what firm-and-kind discipline grows in a child, or what fear-based discipline grows?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Confidence to admit mistakes"},{"id":"b","text":"Learning to lie and hide"},{"id":"c","text":"Trust that you're on their side"},{"id":"d","text":"Flinching when you raise your voice"},{"id":"e","text":"Self-control they copy from you"},{"id":"f","text":"Belief that force solves problems"}],"bins":[{"id":"firm","label":"Firm-and-kind grows","valence":"pos"},{"id":"fear","label":"Fear grows","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"firm","b":"fear","c":"firm","d":"fear","e":"firm","f":"fear"},"relearn":"Firm-and-kind grows confidence, trust and self-control; fear grows hiding, flinching and a belief that force wins.","persona":"any","source":"what it grows"},
  {"id":"bc-1048","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"match","hook":"Match each everyday slip-up to the calm, teaching response.","pairs":[{"left":"Spilled milk rushing about","right":"'Accidents happen. Grab a cloth'"},{"left":"Forgot to pack their bag","right":"A reminder routine, not a rant"},{"left":"Loud during your phone call","right":"A quiet hand-signal you agreed on"},{"left":"Won't wear shoes to leave","right":"'Shoes on, or I carry you?': a choice"},{"left":"Rough with the family pet","right":"Show gentle hands, stay close"}],"relearn":"Everyday slips meet calm teaching, like a cloth, a routine, a signal, a choice or a gentle demo, not a rant or a smack.","persona":"any","source":"everyday slips"},
  {"id":"bc-1049","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"match","hook":"Match each thing you want your child to learn to the way they actually learn it.","pairs":[{"left":"How to handle anger","right":"Watching you stay calm"},{"left":"That mistakes are safe","right":"Your gentle response to theirs"},{"left":"To treat others kindly","right":"Being treated kindly themselves"},{"left":"To respect limits","right":"Limits held warmly and consistently"},{"left":"To say sorry","right":"Hearing you say it too"}],"relearn":"Children learn anger, safety, kindness, limits and apology mostly by watching how you do them, not from punishment.","persona":"any","source":"how kids learn"},
  {"id":"bc-1050","cat":"discipline-differently","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two beliefs that secretly excuse hitting a child.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"A clear limit can be held calmly.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"Some kids only learn through pain.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"My child can learn without fear.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"It's discipline, not violence, if I love them.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Warmth and firmness work together.\"","trick":false}],"why":"'Only pain teaches' and 'love makes it not violence' both excuse hitting; the others hold discipline without harm.","relearn":"Two beliefs excuse hitting: 'only pain teaches' and 'love makes it not violence'; neither is true, and calm limits work.","persona":"Suresh","source":"spot the excuses"},
  {"id":"bc-1080","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Suresh","source":"buried not gone","hook":"\"What happened to me as a child is over and forgotten; it can't touch my kids.\"","myth":{"un":"What happened to me as a child is over and forgotten; it can't touch my kids.","re":"Pain you never tended doesn't vanish; it surfaces in your triggers and slips onto the people you love most.","why":"Buried isn't gone; untended wounds leak into the present."},"relearn":"Untended childhood pain doesn't disappear; it resurfaces in your triggers until you tend it.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1081","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"feeling it isn't weak","hook":"\"If I let myself feel the old hurt, I'll fall apart and won't cope.\"","myth":{"un":"If I let myself feel the old hurt, I'll fall apart and won't cope.","re":"Feeling it with support, a little at a time, is how it loses its grip; what you won't feel keeps running you.","why":"Felt and tended, pain softens; avoided, it stays in charge."},"relearn":"Gently feeling old hurt with support loosens its grip; avoiding it leaves it controlling your reactions.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1082","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Suresh","source":"forgiveness not required","hook":"\"To heal I must forgive my parents fully, or I'm a bad son.\"","myth":{"un":"To heal I must forgive my parents fully, or I'm a bad son.","re":"Healing is about tending your wound and your parenting now; forgiveness can come, or not, in its own time.","why":"You can stop the harm without forcing forgiveness."},"relearn":"Healing means tending your wound and your parenting; forgiving your parents isn't a precondition or a test.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1083","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"not too late","hook":"\"I'm a grown adult now; it's too late to do anything about my childhood.\"","myth":{"un":"I'm a grown adult now; it's too late to do anything about my childhood.","re":"It's never too late to tend an old wound; adults heal and change patterns at any age, and your child benefits now.","why":"Healing has no expiry; tending it still changes today."},"relearn":"It's never too late to tend a childhood wound; healing at any age changes how you parent today.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1084","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Suresh","source":"not your fault","hook":"\"I was raised with the belt and the silence, so deep down it must be what I deserved.\"","myth":{"un":"I was raised with the belt and the silence, so deep down it must be what I deserved.","re":"If you grew up with harsh discipline, it wasn't your fault and you didn't deserve it; you can choose differently now.","why":"A child is never to blame for how they were treated."},"relearn":"Growing up with harsh discipline was never your fault; you didn't deserve it, and you can choose differently now.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1085","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"healing helps kids","hook":"\"Working on my own past is selfish; my energy should all go to my kids.\"","myth":{"un":"Working on my own past is selfish; my energy should all go to my kids.","re":"Tending your wounds is for your kids: a calmer, less-triggered you is the surest gift you give them.","why":"You can't pour from a wound you keep open."},"relearn":"Tending your own wounds isn't selfish; a calmer, less-triggered parent is a direct gift to your child.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1086","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"triggers are signals","hook":"\"When my child's crying enrages me out of nowhere, that's just my bad temper.\"","myth":{"un":"When my child's crying enrages me out of nowhere, that's just my bad temper.","re":"A reaction far bigger than the moment is often an old wound speaking; naming it is the first step to easing it.","why":"Outsized reactions point to something older underneath."},"relearn":"A reaction far bigger than the moment often signals an old wound; naming it is the first step to easing it.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1087","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Suresh","source":"talking helps","hook":"\"Real men in my family don't talk about feelings; you just carry it and get on.\"","myth":{"un":"Real men in my family don't talk about feelings; you just carry it and get on.","re":"Carrying it silently is how the pain passed down to you; naming it to someone safe is what breaks that chain.","why":"Silence is how the wound was inherited; voicing it stops it."},"relearn":"Carrying pain in silence is how it gets passed down; voicing it to someone safe is what breaks the chain.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1088","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"slow not instant","hook":"\"If healing were real, I'd feel fixed by now; since I don't, it isn't working.\"","myth":{"un":"If healing were real, I'd feel fixed by now; since I don't, it isn't working.","re":"Healing isn't a switch; it's gradual, with hard days mixed in, and each step still means less leaks onto your child.","why":"Slow and uneven is what real healing looks like."},"relearn":"Healing is gradual, not a switch; hard days are normal, and each step still means less pain leaks onto your child.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1089","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"good memories too","hook":"\"There were good times too, so I've no right to call any of my childhood a wound.\"","myth":{"un":"There were good times too, so I've no right to call any of my childhood a wound.","re":"Good memories and real hurt can both be true; honouring the love doesn't mean denying what wounded you.","why":"Two things can be true; love doesn't erase the hurt."},"relearn":"Good memories and real hurt can both be true; honouring the love doesn't mean you must deny what wounded you.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1090","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"support is signposting","hook":"\"Reaching out to a helpline is only for people who are truly broken, not me.\"","myth":{"un":"Reaching out to a helpline is only for people who are truly broken, not me.","re":"Support like Tele-MANAS 14416 is for any parent tending a heavy load, not just a crisis; you don't have to wait to deserve it.","why":"Help is for tending wounds, not only emergencies."},"relearn":"A support line like Tele-MANAS 14416 is for any parent carrying a heavy load, not only those in crisis.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1091","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"the child you were","hook":"Lensy: the small child you once were deserved gentleness too, not just your own kids. Let that land?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Let it land","They did","I see them"],"affirm":"The child you once were deserved care and gentleness too; offering it to that child now is part of how you heal.","relearn":"The child you once were deserved gentleness too; offering it to that younger self is part of healing.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1092","type":"reflect","persona":"Suresh","source":"naming it aloud","hook":"Lensy: simply naming what happened to you, out loud or on paper, can loosen its hold. Worth a try?","prompt":"How does that sit?","options":["Worth a try","Naming it","I could"],"affirm":"Naming what happened to you is the first step of healing; it turns a vague ache into something you can begin to tend.","relearn":"Naming what happened to you is the first step of healing; it makes an old ache something you can tend.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1093","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"you broke pattern already","hook":"Lensy: the very fact you're here, looking at this, means you're already breaking the pattern. Feel that?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Feel it","Already started","That's me"],"affirm":"The fact that you're examining this at all means you've already begun breaking the pattern; that's not nothing, it's everything.","relearn":"Choosing to look at your own patterns at all means you've already begun to break them.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1094","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"grieve what you missed","hook":"Lensy: it's okay to feel sad for the care you didn't get as a child. Grief like that is allowed here.","prompt":"How does that sit?","options":["It's allowed","I feel it","Okay to grieve"],"affirm":"Allowing yourself to grieve the care you didn't receive isn't self-pity; it's part of processing it so less of it leaks forward.","relearn":"Grieving the care you didn't receive as a child is part of processing it, so less of it leaks onto your kids.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1095","type":"reflect","persona":"Suresh","source":"slow progress counts","hook":"Lensy: you don't have to heal it all at once; one honest look today is real progress. Sit right?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Sits right","One look","That counts"],"affirm":"You don't have to heal everything at once; each small, honest moment of tending an old wound is genuine progress.","relearn":"Healing happens in small steps; each honest moment of tending an old wound is real progress, not all-or-nothing.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1096","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"compassion for younger self","hook":"Lensy: if a small child told you what was done to you, you'd be gentle with them. Offer that to yourself?","prompt":"How does that sit?","options":["I'll try","Be gentle","To myself too"],"affirm":"The compassion you'd offer a hurt child is the same compassion your younger self has been waiting for from you.","relearn":"Offer your younger self the same compassion you'd give any hurt child; it's part of how the wound eases.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1097","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"not passing it down","hook":"Lensy: every bit of pain you tend in yourself is a bit your child and their child never inherit. Resonate?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Resonates","Stops here","For them all"],"affirm":"Tending your pain doesn't just spare your child; it can spare generations after, because the cycle stops here, with you.","relearn":"Tending your own pain can spare not just your child but generations after; the cycle can stop here, with you.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1098","type":"reflect","persona":"Divya","source":"rest is healing too","hook":"Lensy: rest, a kind word to yourself, a quiet breath; these are healing too, not luxuries. Feel that?","prompt":"How does that sit?","options":["Feel it","Not a luxury","I'll allow it"],"affirm":"Rest and self-kindness aren't indulgences you must earn; for a depleted parent, they're part of the healing itself.","relearn":"Rest and self-kindness aren't luxuries to earn; for a tired parent they're part of healing, not separate from it.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1099","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"you are not the wound","hook":"Lensy: what was done to you is something that happened; it isn't who you are. Let that breathe?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Let it breathe","Not who I am","It happened"],"affirm":"What happened to you is part of your story, not your identity; you are the one choosing, today, to tend it and change.","relearn":"What happened to you is part of your story, not who you are; you're the one choosing to tend it now.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1100","type":"reflect","persona":"Suresh","source":"asking is courage","hook":"Lensy: reaching for help with your own past takes real courage, the kind you're already showing. Sit right?","prompt":"How does that sit?","options":["Sits right","It's courage","I'm trying"],"affirm":"Reaching for support with your own past isn't a sign of weakness; it's one of the bravest, most loving things a parent does.","relearn":"Reaching for support with your own past is courage, not weakness; it's a loving act for you and your child.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1101","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"both parent and child","hook":"Lensy: you can be the parent your child needs and the hurt person who needs care, at once. Both are okay.","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Both okay","I'm both","That's me"],"affirm":"You can hold both: a parent showing up for your child, and a person who still needs care for old wounds. Both are true and allowed.","relearn":"You can be both the parent showing up and the person who still needs care; holding both is part of healing.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1102","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"heals vs reopens","hook":"Sort: does this help an old wound heal, or keep it raw?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Naming what happened, kindly"},{"id":"b","text":"Telling yourself to just get over it"},{"id":"c","text":"Talking to someone safe about it"},{"id":"d","text":"Drinking to push the feeling down"},{"id":"e","text":"Letting yourself grieve it"},{"id":"f","text":"Pretending it never affected you"}],"bins":[{"id":"heals","label":"Helps it heal","valence":"pos"},{"id":"raw","label":"Keeps it raw","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"heals","b":"raw","c":"heals","d":"raw","e":"heals","f":"raw"},"relearn":"Naming, talking and grieving help an old wound heal; dismissing, numbing and pretending keep it raw.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1103","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"steps vs detours","hook":"Sort: a real step of healing, or a detour that stalls it?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Understanding what triggers you"},{"id":"b","text":"Blaming yourself for being affected"},{"id":"c","text":"Reaching out for support"},{"id":"d","text":"Waiting until you feel 'ready enough'"},{"id":"e","text":"Processing the grief slowly"},{"id":"f","text":"Comparing your pain to others' and dismissing it"}],"bins":[{"id":"step","label":"A real step","valence":"pos"},{"id":"detour","label":"A stalling detour","valence":"uhoh"}],"key":{"a":"step","b":"detour","c":"step","d":"detour","e":"step","f":"detour"},"relearn":"Understanding triggers, reaching out and processing grief move healing forward; self-blame, waiting and dismissing stall it.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1104","type":"sort","persona":"Suresh","source":"keep vs set down","hook":"Sort: from your own upbringing, keep this, or gently set it down?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"The warmth at family meals"},{"id":"b","text":"Hitting when a child errs"},{"id":"c","text":"Showing up for relatives in need"},{"id":"d","text":"Silence instead of saying sorry"},{"id":"e","text":"Hard work and looking after your own"},{"id":"f","text":"Fear as the way to get respect"}],"bins":[{"id":"keep","label":"Keep the good","valence":"pos"},{"id":"setdown","label":"Set it down","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"keep","b":"setdown","c":"keep","d":"setdown","e":"keep","f":"setdown"},"relearn":"You can keep the warmth, loyalty and care from your upbringing and set down the hitting, silence and fear.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1105","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"self-talk heals or wounds","hook":"Sort: would you say this to a hurt friend, or only to yourself?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"That wasn't your fault.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"You should be over this by now.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"It makes sense that it still hurts.\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"You're weak for letting it get to you.\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"You deserved care back then.\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"Stop being so dramatic about it.\""}],"bins":[{"id":"kind","label":"Kind & healing","valence":"pos"},{"id":"harsh","label":"Harsh & wounding","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"kind","b":"harsh","c":"kind","d":"harsh","e":"kind","f":"harsh"},"relearn":"The kindness you'd give a hurt friend, 'not your fault', 'it makes sense it hurts', is the self-talk that heals.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1106","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"in scope vs route on","hook":"Sort: a wobble you can sit with, or a load to route to support?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A wave of sadness while reflecting"},{"id":"b","text":"Flashbacks that flood you daily"},{"id":"c","text":"Feeling tender after an honest journal"},{"id":"d","text":"Thoughts of harming yourself"},{"id":"e","text":"Naming a trigger and breathing through it"},{"id":"f","text":"Pain so heavy you can't function"}],"bins":[{"id":"sit","label":"Can sit with it","valence":"pos"},{"id":"route","label":"Route to support","valence":"tell"}],"key":{"a":"sit","b":"route","c":"sit","d":"route","e":"sit","f":"route"},"relearn":"Tender feelings while reflecting you can sit with; daily flashbacks, self-harm thoughts or pain you can't carry route to Tele-MANAS 14416.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1107","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"protects child vs passes pain","hook":"Sort: does this protect your child from your wound, or pass it to them?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Pausing when an old trigger fires"},{"id":"b","text":"Snapping the moment you're reminded"},{"id":"c","text":"Repairing after you're triggered"},{"id":"d","text":"Expecting your child to soothe you"},{"id":"e","text":"Getting support for the underlying pain"},{"id":"f","text":"Calling them names you were called"}],"bins":[{"id":"protect","label":"Protects them","valence":"pos"},{"id":"pass","label":"Passes it on","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"protect","b":"pass","c":"protect","d":"pass","e":"protect","f":"pass"},"relearn":"Pausing, repairing and getting support protect your child from your wound; snapping, leaning on them and old names pass it on.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1108","type":"sort","persona":"Suresh","source":"myth vs truth past","hook":"Sort: an old myth about your past, or the truer thing?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"It's too late to change anything.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"I can choose differently now.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"Talking about it makes me weak.\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"Naming it is the first step.\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"I must've deserved it.\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"A child is never to blame.\""}],"bins":[{"id":"myth","label":"Old myth","valence":"neg"},{"id":"truth","label":"The truer thing","valence":"pos"}],"key":{"a":"myth","b":"truth","c":"myth","d":"truth","e":"myth","f":"truth"},"relearn":"'Too late', 'talking is weak', 'I deserved it' are old myths; 'I can choose now', 'naming is the first step', 'a child is never to blame' are true.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1109","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"who can support","hook":"Sort: a steady place to take old pain, or one that can deepen it?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Tele-MANAS 14416 counsellor"},{"id":"b","text":"The relative who minimised it then"},{"id":"c","text":"A trusted, kind friend"},{"id":"d","text":"Bottling it and saying nothing"},{"id":"e","text":"A support group of other parents"},{"id":"f","text":"Anyone who shames you for feeling it"}],"bins":[{"id":"steady","label":"A steady place","valence":"pos"},{"id":"deepens","label":"Can deepen it","valence":"uhoh"}],"key":{"a":"steady","b":"deepens","c":"steady","d":"deepens","e":"steady","f":"deepens"},"relearn":"A counsellor, a kind friend or a parents' group are steady places for old pain; minimisers, silence and shamers can deepen it.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1110","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"trigger at bedtime","hook":"Your daughter Gauri's bedtime whining sends a hot rush through you, far too big for the moment.","steps":[{"prompt":"She asks for 'just one more story' for the third time. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Notice it's an old wound, breathe, and tend it later","then":"The rush passes. Gauri is just a sleepy child wanting you close.","best":true},{"text":"Grit your teeth and read another story, quietly seething","then":"Your voice is flat. Gauri can tell something's wrong."},{"text":"Let it out. That's just how you've always been","then":"Gauri shrinks under her blanket, crying."},{"text":"Tell her she's the reason you're so angry","then":"Gauri's eyes fill. 'Sorry,' she whispers."}],"why":"Seeing the size of the feeling as an old wound lets you keep the moment safe."},{"prompt":"Once Gauri is asleep, you wonder why whining hits you so hard. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Distract yourself with TV until you're too tired to think","then":"It works tonight. The question waits for tomorrow's bedtime."},{"text":"Tell yourself not to be so dramatic about it","then":"The feeling goes quiet, but doesn't go away."},{"text":"Decide she's just too whiny, and it's her problem","then":"You go to bed blaming her, and never ask why it hits you so hard."},{"text":"Think back to how your own tears were treated as a child","then":"You remember being told to stop crying, or else. It explains a lot.","best":true}],"why":"Understanding your trigger is how an old wound starts to heal."},{"prompt":"The next night, Gauri whines again at bedtime. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Hand bedtime over to your spouse from now on","then":"Bedtime is calmer, and you miss the cuddles."},{"text":"Breathe, remind yourself 'this is now,' and stay gentle","then":"The rush is smaller. Gauri gets her story and falls asleep holding your hand.","best":true},{"text":"Skip bedtime stories so the whining stops","then":"Gauri cries herself to sleep, and the closeness goes with the stories."},{"text":"Warn her that whining makes you very angry","then":"She stops whining, and starts watching your face."}],"why":"Each calm bedtime shows the old wound is losing its grip."}],"debrief":"A reaction far bigger than the moment is often an old wound; noticing it lets you tend it, not pass it on.","relearn":"When a reaction dwarfs the moment, treat it as an old wound to tend, not a fact about who you are."},
  {"id":"bc-1111","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"old memory surfaces","hook":"While you're playing with your kids, a memory of how you were punished as a child suddenly surfaces.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your son is waiting for you to take your turn at ludo. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Shove it away hard and pretend it never came up at all","then":"You play on, but you're distant. Your son notices."},{"text":"Stop the game and go sit alone with it now","then":"Your son looks puzzled as you leave mid-game."},{"text":"Note the memory kindly, to sit with later, and keep playing","then":"You roll a six. Your son cheers, and the memory waits quietly.","best":true},{"text":"Let it sour your mood for the rest of the day","then":"You snap at him over a ludo piece, and he stops playing."}],"why":"Noting a memory to tend later keeps it from taking over the moment."},{"prompt":"That night, the memory comes back when the house is quiet. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Let yourself remember it, gently, and name how it felt","then":"It hurts. But once it's named, the memory feels a little smaller.","best":true},{"text":"Turn on the TV so you don't have to think","then":"It fades for tonight and returns another day."},{"text":"Tell yourself it wasn't that bad, and you deserved it","then":"The thought feels familiar and heavy, like an old coat."},{"text":"Make yourself go over every detail tonight, to be done with it","then":"It all floods back at once, and you're awake until dawn."}],"why":"If you were punished harshly as a child, it wasn't your fault. Naming it helps it heal."},{"prompt":"Over the next weeks, more memories come up. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Confront your parents about everything at once","then":"The family erupts, and you feel worse, not better."},{"text":"Keep them all to yourself. It's in the past","then":"They pile up, and show up in your temper."},{"text":"Talk to someone you trust, or call Tele-MANAS 14416","then":"Talking helps. You feel less alone with it.","best":true},{"text":"Decide you're too damaged to parent well","then":"The thought sticks, and you pull back from the kids."}],"why":"Support helps you tend what surfaces, so it doesn't leak into your parenting."},{"prompt":"A week later, your son asks if you ever got into trouble as a child. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you were always a good child, so it never happened","then":"He looks down. 'I get in trouble all the time.'"},{"text":"Say yes, and that a few slaps did you good","then":"He goes still. 'Will you slap me too?'"},{"text":"Say yes, that it hurt, and that you chose differently","then":"He thinks, then takes your hand. 'I'm glad you did.'","best":true},{"text":"Tell him every detail of how you were punished","then":"His eyes widen, and he looks scared for days."}],"why":"A simple, honest answer reassures him without handing him your pain."}],"debrief":"You needn't process a surfacing memory on the spot; noting it to tend later keeps it from hijacking you.","relearn":"When an old memory surfaces, note it to tend later rather than shoving it away; what's buried keeps resurfacing."},
  {"id":"bc-1112","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"heavy after reflecting","hook":"Since you began looking back at your childhood, you've felt unusually heavy for days.","steps":[{"prompt":"Tonight the heaviness sits with you after the kids are asleep. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Call Tele-MANAS 14416 to talk it through","then":"A calm voice listens and says this weight after looking back is common. You sleep a little easier.","best":true},{"text":"Decide looking back was a mistake and stop for good","then":"The heaviness dulls, but old memories keep tugging at you in odd moments."},{"text":"Keep busy with work so there's no time to think","then":"The week fills up. The weight doesn't lift, and you're short with everyone."},{"text":"Sit up late going over the memories alone","then":"The memories get sharper and the night gets longer. You wake up tired and raw."}],"why":"Heaviness after looking back is normal. A steady voice helps you carry it instead of shutting it away."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your partner asks why you've seemed so far away. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say old memories have surfaced and it's been heavy","then":"They sit closer and ask what came up. Saying it aloud loosens something in your chest.","best":true},{"text":"Say it's just stress from work","then":"They nod and change the subject. You feel more alone with it."},{"text":"Say you're fine and don't want to talk","then":"They back off, a little hurt, and the evening goes quiet."},{"text":"Say it's heavy but you'd rather sort it out on your own","then":"They offer to help anyway. You wave it off, and the weight stays yours alone."}],"why":"Letting someone close know what has surfaced means you don't carry it alone."},{"prompt":"A month on, a forgotten memory returns and the heaviness comes back. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Reach out to a counsellor or Tele-MANAS 14416","then":"The counsellor helps you see why this memory came now. It feels lighter to hold.","best":true},{"text":"Decide healing clearly isn't working for you","then":"You close the door on it, and the memory keeps surfacing at bad moments."},{"text":"Push the memory down and get on with the day","then":"The day goes on. By evening you snap at small things and aren't sure why."},{"text":"Wait a few weeks to see if it settles by itself","then":"Some days are fine. On others the weight is back, and you're still holding it alone."}],"why":"Old pain can return in waves. Reaching out each time is how it gets tended, not buried."}],"debrief":"Feeling heavier after looking back is normal. That's the moment to reach for support, not slam the door.","relearn":"If your past leaves you heavy, reach for support like Tele-MANAS 14416; don't slam the door on healing."},
  {"id":"bc-1113","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"father comment","hook":"At a family function, your father jokes that a few slaps 'made you who you are'. Everyone laughs.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your cousins glance over to see how you'll react. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Smile, let it pass, and keep your own view","then":"The laughter moves on. Inside, you stay clear about how you raise your son.","best":true},{"text":"Laugh along and say he's probably right","then":"Your father looks pleased. On the way home, the doubt you'd buried feels heavier."},{"text":"Tell him in front of everyone that hitting is wrong","then":"The room goes silent. Your father's face hardens, and the function turns tense."},{"text":"Walk out of the room without a word","then":"A few relatives notice. Your mother follows to ask what's wrong, and you can't explain."}],"why":"You don't need to win the room. You can keep your own truth without a public fight."},{"prompt":"That night, the joke keeps replaying in your head. What do you tell yourself?","options":[{"text":"I love him, and I'm still choosing differently","then":"The sting eases. You feel steadier about the father you're becoming.","best":true},{"text":"Maybe he's right, and I turned out fine","then":"The old line settles back in, and your resolve goes quiet."},{"text":"He was a bad father, and that's all he'll ever be","then":"Anger keeps you awake, and the hurt feels louder than before."},{"text":"It was just a joke. I shouldn't let it bother me","then":"You push the feeling away. It surfaces again the next time he laughs about the old days."}],"why":"Holding love and your own choice together keeps you steady without buying the old line."},{"prompt":"A week later, your father watches you calm your son's tantrum and mutters, 'Too soft.' What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Keep going as you were, calmly","then":"Your son settles. Your father shakes his head but says nothing more.","best":true},{"text":"Switch to shouting so he sees you're firm","then":"Your son startles and cries harder. Your father nods, and you feel sick inside."},{"text":"Launch into why his way was harmful","then":"Your father bristles. The argument goes on long after your son has calmed."},{"text":"Let your father handle it his way this once","then":"Your father raises his voice. Your son flinches and looks at you."}],"why":"Quietly staying your course shows your choice more clearly than any argument would."},{"prompt":"On your way out, your mother quietly asks if your father's joke hurt you. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Admit it stung, and that you parent differently now","then":"She squeezes your hand. 'He means no harm,' she says, 'but I see what you're doing.'","best":true},{"text":"Say it's all in the past now","then":"She nods, relieved, and the chance to be heard slips by."},{"text":"Tell her she should have stopped him back then","then":"Her eyes fill. She goes quiet, and you part awkwardly."},{"text":"Ask her to tell your father to stop joking about it","then":"She looks torn and says she'll try. Nothing changes at the next function."}],"why":"Honest, quiet words with someone who cares let you be heard without a public fight."}],"debrief":"You can love your father and still privately hold that you're choosing differently; no need to win the room.","relearn":"You can hold your truth quietly, choosing differently, without a public fight or buying the old line."},
  {"id":"bc-1114","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"partner dismisses","hook":"You tell your partner Neha about your hard childhood, and she asks why you'd dig all that up now.","steps":[{"prompt":"She looks genuinely puzzled. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Explain it's showing up in how you react to the kids","then":"Neha pauses. 'I hadn't linked the two,' she says, though she still looks unsure.","best":true},{"text":"Say she's right and drop the subject","then":"She relaxes and changes the topic. The memories stay, unspoken."},{"text":"Say she never understands anything about you","then":"Neha folds her arms, and the talk turns into an argument about something else."},{"text":"Say you just needed to vent and it's nothing serious","then":"Neha nods and moves on. The real reason you raised it goes unsaid."}],"why":"Linking the past to your parenting now helps her see why it matters today."},{"prompt":"Next week, Neha says she'd rather keep home light and not 'go into all that'. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Accept that, and find a counsellor to talk to","then":"Neha looks relieved. You book a first appointment for the weekend.","best":true},{"text":"Agree to bury it so home stays peaceful","then":"The house stays calm. The old reactions keep slipping out with the kids."},{"text":"Keep pressing her until she listens","then":"Neha gets tense, and the evening ends with both of you hurt."},{"text":"Read articles and try to sort it out alone","then":"You read late into the night. Some of it helps, but the hardest parts stay tangled."}],"why":"Not everyone can hold your healing. A counsellor can, and you keep tending it."},{"prompt":"One night, a sharp memory surfaces after the kids are asleep and Neha is away. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Call Tele-MANAS 14416 and talk it through","then":"The counsellor listens without rushing you. By the end of the call, the knot has loosened.","best":true},{"text":"Scroll your phone until you're too tired to feel it","then":"You fall asleep late. The memory is waiting in the morning."},{"text":"Decide Neha was right and stop looking back","then":"You shut it away again, and it slips out the next time a child tests you."},{"text":"Keep it to yourself so no one worries","then":"No one worries, and you lie awake with it until nearly dawn."}],"why":"You don't have to hold it alone at night. Tele-MANAS 14416 is there for exactly this."},{"prompt":"A month later, Neha asks whether the old stuff still bothers you. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say it still comes up, and you want to tend it","then":"Neha thinks about it. 'Then tell me what would help,' she says.","best":true},{"text":"Say it's all behind you now, so she needn't worry","then":"She looks relieved. The old reflexes keep showing up with the kids."},{"text":"Tell her she has no right to ask","then":"Neha goes quiet, and the distance between you grows."},{"text":"Say you'll work on it, but stop if it bothers her","then":"She says it's fine, but you notice you now feel you need her permission."}],"why":"Your healing doesn't need her approval. Being honest about it may help her understand."}],"debrief":"Not everyone can hold your healing; you can keep tending it with support.","relearn":"If those near you can't hold your healing, keep tending it with a counsellor or Tele-MANAS 14416."},
  {"id":"bc-1115","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"naming the wound","hook":"You sense one old hurt sits behind your sharpest reactions, but you've never put it into words.","steps":[{"prompt":"One quiet evening, you have a notebook and some time alone. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Write down plainly what happened, without blaming yourself","then":"It takes three lines. Seeing it written, the ache has a shape you can look at.","best":true},{"text":"Write a long page about how angry you still are","then":"The page fills with rage. You feel drained, and the hurt itself is still unnamed."},{"text":"Close the notebook. Some things are better left vague","then":"The evening passes calmly. The next day, the same sharp reaction flares at nothing."},{"text":"Write that you had a hard childhood, and leave it there","then":"It's true but blurry. You still can't say what exactly stings."}],"why":"Naming what happened, kindly and plainly, turns a vague ache into something you can tend."},{"prompt":"Next morning, your son ignores you and the familiar sharp anger flares. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Pause and notice which old hurt this touches","then":"You breathe. Seeing the link, you answer your son more calmly than you expected.","best":true},{"text":"Snap at him and move on","then":"He goes quiet, and the old heaviness settles back in."},{"text":"Tell yourself the anger is his fault for ignoring you","then":"You stay irritated all morning, and the pattern stays hidden."},{"text":"Swallow the anger and say nothing at all","then":"He keeps ignoring you. The anger comes out later over something small."}],"why":"Linking the flare to the old hurt gives you the gap to choose a calmer response."},{"prompt":"A voice in your head says naming it is making a fuss over nothing. What do you tell yourself?","options":[{"text":"It's not a fuss. It's how I stop it steering me","then":"The voice quietens. You write one kind line in your notebook: it wasn't your fault.","best":true},{"text":"Maybe it really was nothing, and I should drop it","then":"You let it go, and the vague ache goes back to steering you."},{"text":"I should send my parents a list of what they did","then":"You draft an angry message to your parents and feel worse reading it back."},{"text":"I'll name it, but only once I've forgiven everything","then":"Forgiveness feels far off, so the naming keeps getting put off."}],"why":"Naming a hurt plainly is the first step to tending it, however small it seems."}],"debrief":"Naming what happened, without self-blame, is the first step; a vague ache you won't face keeps steering you.","relearn":"Naming an old hurt plainly and kindly is healing's first step; vague and unfelt, it keeps driving you."},
  {"id":"bc-1116","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"fear of harming child","hook":"Overwhelm from your past flares, and a sudden urge to lash out at your child frightens you.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your daughter keeps whining at your side, and your hands are shaking. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Make sure she's safe, then step into another room","then":"Behind the closed door your breath is ragged, but the urge has space to ease.","best":true},{"text":"Stay beside her and push through, hoping it passes","then":"The urge keeps building, and your daughter sees your face and goes very still."},{"text":"Shout at her to stop so it's over quickly","then":"She flinches and cries harder, and your own panic rises."},{"text":"Grip the table and count, staying right beside her","then":"You hold on, barely. Every whine makes the urge surge again."}],"why":"When an urge to lash out frightens you, stepping away, if it's safe, keeps your child safe."},{"prompt":"A few minutes later the moment has passed, but the urge still frightens you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Call Tele-MANAS 14416 and say what happened","then":"The counsellor stays calm, asks if your daughter is safe, and helps you slow your breathing.","best":true},{"text":"Tell yourself it was nothing and move on","then":"You carry on with the evening. The fear stays lodged in your chest."},{"text":"Decide you must be a terrible parent","then":"The shame swallows you, and you still have no plan for next time."},{"text":"Promise yourself it won't happen again, and keep it private","then":"The promise feels strong tonight. Nothing about the old overwhelm has changed."}],"why":"Getting help after a frightening urge is caring and brave. Tele-MANAS 14416 is there for this."},{"prompt":"Later, your daughter comes to you, quiet and unsure. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Hug her, say sorry, and tell her it wasn't her fault","then":"She leans into the hug. 'You looked scary,' she whispers, and you hold her gently.","best":true},{"text":"Act normal and hope she forgets it soon","then":"She plays quietly, still watching your face out of the corner of her eye."},{"text":"Tell her she made you very angry","then":"Her face crumples. She says sorry for something she doesn't understand."},{"text":"Buy her a chocolate to make up for how you acted","then":"She smiles at the chocolate, but the scary moment is never talked about."}],"why":"Coming back and owning it, without blaming her, repairs the scare. That is what she remembers."},{"prompt":"Next week, the same overwhelm flares when your daughter spills food everywhere. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Breathe, check she's safe, and step away as it rises","then":"You leave the room for a minute. When you come back, you clean up together.","best":true},{"text":"Hold it in and hope you manage this time","then":"You manage, just. The strain leaves you shaky for the rest of the day."},{"text":"Let it out once, since holding it in is worse","then":"Your voice goes sharp and loud, and your daughter freezes by the table."},{"text":"Clean it up in silence so you don't have to speak","then":"The mess is gone. Your daughter watches you, unsure if you're angry."}],"why":"Having a plan ready helps: pause, breathe, and step away if safe before the urge acts."},{"prompt":"A close friend asks what you'd do if the urge ever felt truly out of control. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Step away, and call Childline 1098 or 112 if she's in danger","then":"Your friend nods and saves both numbers in her own phone too.","best":true},{"text":"Say it will never get that far","then":"Your friend looks unconvinced, and you realise you've never thought it through."},{"text":"Say you'd handle it yourself, as family matters stay private","then":"Your friend nods slowly. The plan leaves you and your daughter with no backup."},{"text":"Say you'd call Tele-MANAS 14416 the next morning, once it passes","then":"Your friend asks, 'And in the moment itself?' You don't have an answer."}],"why":"In real danger, Childline 1098 or 112 gets help fast. Having it ready keeps your child safe."}],"debrief":"A frightening urge means step away to keep your child safe; that fear shows you're trying, and it isn't a verdict on you.","relearn":"If you fear harming your child, step away if safe and get help, Tele-MANAS 14416; in danger Childline 1098 or 112."},
  {"id":"bc-1117","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"healing not linear","hook":"You'd been calmer for weeks. Then a bad week of old reactions leaves you feeling back at square one.","steps":[{"prompt":"On Sunday night, you look back over the week. What do you tell yourself?","options":[{"text":"One hard week doesn't cancel the calmer ones","then":"You breathe out. Monday feels possible again.","best":true},{"text":"Healing is fake. I'm just this person","then":"The thought lands heavily, and you drop the habits that had been helping."},{"text":"I need to be perfect from now on","then":"You set a strict rule for yourself, and dread the first small slip."},{"text":"Next week will be better. No point dwelling on it","then":"You move on, but you never look at what set the week off."}],"why":"Healing isn't a straight line. A hard week after good ones is normal, not proof it's hopeless."},{"prompt":"On Monday, your daughter asks why you were so grumpy last week. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say sorry, and that it wasn't her fault","then":"She nods and leans against you, and the air between you clears.","best":true},{"text":"Say grown-ups just get grumpy sometimes","then":"She shrugs, but she still tiptoes around you that evening."},{"text":"Say it was because she kept misbehaving","then":"Her face falls, and she goes quiet for the rest of breakfast."},{"text":"Say you were tired, and promise it won't happen again","then":"She brightens. You feel the weight of a promise you may not keep."}],"why":"Owning a hard week and saying it wasn't her fault repairs it, without promising perfection."},{"prompt":"Midweek, a friend asks how your efforts to stay calmer are going. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you had a rough week, and you're back on the road","then":"Your friend smiles and shares her own bad week. You feel less alone in it.","best":true},{"text":"Say you've given up, since it clearly doesn't work","then":"Your friend looks sad for you. The calmer weeks start to feel like a fluke."},{"text":"Say everything's going perfectly","then":"She believes you. You carry the hard week alone."},{"text":"Say you'll restart properly once life calms down","then":"Your friend nods. Life doesn't calm down, and the restart keeps sliding."}],"why":"Naming the slip as part of a long road keeps you going, and lets others walk it with you."}],"debrief":"Healing isn't a straight line; a hard week after good ones is normal, not proof it's hopeless.","relearn":"Healing isn't linear; a hard week after calmer ones is part of the road, not failure. Keep going."},
  {"id":"bc-1118","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"trigger to root","hook":"Match each parenting trigger to the old wound it can point back to.","pairs":[{"left":"Rage at your child's tears","right":"Being told boys don't cry"},{"left":"Panic when they make a mess","right":"Punishment for any small slip"},{"left":"Going cold when they defy you","right":"Silence used against you as a child"},{"left":"Dread when they're disappointed in you","right":"A parent impossible to please"},{"left":"Flinching at raised voices","right":"A home that often shouted"}],"relearn":"Outsized parenting triggers often point back to specific old wounds; naming that link is where tending them begins.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1119","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"feeling to kind response","hook":"Match each hard feeling about your past to a kind way to meet it.","pairs":[{"left":"Grief for the care you missed","right":"Let yourself mourn it, with support"},{"left":"Shame that it still affects you","right":"Remind yourself it makes sense"},{"left":"Anger at how you were treated","right":"Allow it; it doesn't make you bad"},{"left":"Fear of becoming your parent","right":"Notice it as a sign you're changing"},{"left":"Numbness when you try to look","right":"Go slow; reach for a counsellor"}],"relearn":"Each hard feeling about your past has a kind response; meeting it gently, with support, is how it loosens its grip.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1120","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"myth to truth","hook":"Match each old myth about healing to the truth that replaces it.","pairs":[{"left":"\"It's too late for me\"","right":"Adults heal at any age"},{"left":"\"Asking for help is weak\"","right":"Reaching out is real courage"},{"left":"\"I must've deserved it\"","right":"A child is never to blame"},{"left":"\"I should be over it\"","right":"Healing is gradual, not a switch"},{"left":"\"My past doesn't touch them\"","right":"Untended, it leaks into parenting"}],"relearn":"Each old myth about healing has a truer replacement; learning the truer line is part of letting the wound heal.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1121","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"who helps with what","hook":"Match each support route to what it's there for.","pairs":[{"left":"Tele-MANAS 14416","right":"Your own heavy feelings & past"},{"left":"A trusted friend","right":"Being heard without judgement"},{"left":"A parents' support group","right":"Knowing you're not the only one"},{"left":"Childline 1098","right":"A child in real danger"},{"left":"Be the Safe Adult (g69)","right":"In-app child-safety routing"}],"relearn":"Different routes fit different needs: Tele-MANAS 14416 for your own load, Childline 1098 and g69 if a child is at risk.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1122","type":"match","persona":"Suresh","source":"keep good leave harm pairs","hook":"Match each part of your upbringing to keeping the good or leaving the harm.","pairs":[{"left":"Family loyalty and care","right":"Keep, and pass it on"},{"left":"Hitting to correct a child","right":"Leave; it harms, doesn't work"},{"left":"Hard work and providing","right":"Keep; it's a real strength"},{"left":"Silence instead of apology","right":"Leave; repair beats silence"},{"left":"Respect through fear","right":"Leave; respect grows from trust"}],"relearn":"You can keep loyalty, care and hard work from your upbringing and leave the hitting, silence and fear.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1123","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"action to gift to child","hook":"Match each healing action to the gift it quietly gives your child.","pairs":[{"left":"Tending your own triggers","right":"Fewer sharp reactions at them"},{"left":"Naming what happened to you","right":"A parent who models honesty"},{"left":"Getting support for old pain","right":"A calmer home to grow up in"},{"left":"Grieving what you missed","right":"Less of it leaking onto them"},{"left":"Choosing differently now","right":"A cycle that stops with you"}],"relearn":"Every step you take to heal is a quiet gift to your child: fewer sharp reactions, a calmer home, a cycle that stops with you.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1124","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"step to first move","hook":"Match each healing step to a small first move you can actually make this week.","pairs":[{"left":"Noticing your triggers","right":"Jot down what set you off"},{"left":"Naming the wound","right":"Write one honest sentence"},{"left":"Reaching for support","right":"Save the Tele-MANAS number"},{"left":"Being kinder to yourself","right":"Speak to yourself as a friend"},{"left":"Not carrying it alone","right":"Tell one safe person"}],"relearn":"Big healing breaks into small doable moves: jot a trigger, write one sentence, save a number, tell one safe person.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1125","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"catch the denials","hook":"Spot the two lines that keep an old wound running.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'll name what happened, kindly.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"It was nothing; I'm completely fine.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"I'll reach out for some support.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Real men just bury it and move on.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"It's okay that it still hurts.\"","trick":false}],"why":"'I'm completely fine' and 'just bury it' are denial; insisting you're fine and numbing keep the wound running.","relearn":"Naming the hurt, seeking support and allowing the feeling heal; insisting you're fine and burying it keep it running.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1126","type":"spot","persona":"Suresh","source":"catch self-blame","hook":"Spot the two things you'd never say to a hurt child, so don't say them to yourself.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"That wasn't your fault.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You should be over this by now.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"You deserved care back then.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"You're weak for letting it hurt.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"It makes sense that it stings.\"","trick":false}],"why":"'Should be over it' and 'you're weak' are harsh self-blame; you'd never say them to a hurt child, so retire them for yourself.","relearn":"Speak to your wounded self as you would a hurt child: 'not your fault', 'you deserved care', not 'get over it' or 'you're weak'.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1127","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"catch the stalls","hook":"Spot the two excuses that stall healing instead of moving it.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'll start with one honest sentence.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"I'll deal with it when I'm fully ready.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"I'll tell one safe person.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Others had it worse, so mine doesn't count.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"I'll save the Tele-MANAS number.\"","trick":false}],"why":"'When I'm fully ready' and 'others had it worse' are stalls; waiting for perfect readiness and dismissing your pain delay healing.","relearn":"Small steps now move healing; waiting to feel fully ready and dismissing your pain as not bad enough only stall it.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1128","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"catch leaking onto child","hook":"Spot the two responses that pass your wound straight to your child.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Pausing when an old trigger fires","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Snapping the second you're reminded","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Repairing after you're triggered","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Calling them a name you were called","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Getting support for the root pain","trick":false}],"why":"Snapping on contact and using the old names pass the wound on; pausing, repairing and getting support protect your child.","relearn":"Pausing, repairing and getting support protect your child; snapping on contact and old names pass the wound straight on.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1129","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"catch unsafe supports","hook":"Spot the two places that can deepen old pain rather than ease it.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"A Tele-MANAS 14416 counsellor","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"The relative who minimised it then","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"A kind, trusted friend","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Anyone who shames you for feeling it","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"A group of other parents healing too","trick":false}],"why":"A minimiser and a shamer can deepen the wound; take old pain to a counsellor, a kind friend or others healing too.","relearn":"Take old pain to safe places, a counsellor, a kind friend, a parents' group, not to those who minimise or shame you for it.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1130","type":"spot","persona":"Suresh","source":"catch fatalism","hook":"Spot the two fatalist lines that quietly keep the cycle going.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I can choose differently now.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"We all turn into our parents anyway.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Naming it is the first step.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"It's too late to change at my age.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Tending this protects my kids.\"","trick":false}],"why":"'We all turn into our parents' and 'too late to change' are fatalism; the inheritance isn't destiny and it's never too late.","relearn":"The inheritance isn't destiny and it's never too late; fatalist lines like 'too late' quietly keep the cycle going.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1131","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"catch not-therapy overreach","hook":"Spot the two beliefs that expect this game to do what real care should.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"This points me toward support.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"This alone will cure my trauma.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Deeper wounds need a professional.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"So I never need to talk to anyone.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Tele-MANAS 14416 is there for the heavy stuff.\"","trick":false}],"why":"Expecting the game alone to cure trauma, or to replace ever talking to anyone, overreaches; it signposts, care does the rest.","relearn":"This game offers compassion and signposting, not therapy; deeper wounds deserve real support like Tele-MANAS 14416.","cat":"heal-your-wounds"},
  {"id":"bc-1132","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"self-compassion after slip","hook":"Tonight you snapped at your son in a way that echoed your own father.","setup":"Later, alone on the terrace, the old shame starts talking.","steps":[{"prompt":"The shame says: \"See? You're exactly the father you swore you'd never be.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I slipped into an old script. I'm not that script.\"","then":"The shame wavers. \"Then what are you going to do about it?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. I'm hopeless.\"","then":"The shame grows louder. \"Hopeless. Just like him.\""},{"text":"\"My son pushed me to it. He kept testing me all evening.\"","then":"The shame shifts. \"Blame the boy, then? That's what your father did.\""},{"text":"\"It was one time. It doesn't really matter.\"","then":"The shame murmurs. \"Then why can't you sleep?\""}],"why":"Naming the slip without condemning yourself leaves room to repair it."},{"prompt":"The shame asks: \"And what about your son? He saw your face.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'll go to him tomorrow, own it, and say sorry.\"","then":"The shame loosens its grip. \"He might forgive that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"He'll forget by morning. Kids do.\"","then":"The shame whispers. \"Did you forget, when you were small?\""},{"text":"\"Better he stays out of my way for a while.\"","then":"The shame nods. \"That's how your father kept the peace too.\""},{"text":"\"I'll take him for ice cream. That will fix it.\"","then":"The shame shrugs. \"A treat, and no words? He'll still wonder.\""}],"why":"A clean apology owns what you did and shows your son it wasn't his fault."},{"prompt":"The shame asks: \"And next time? The same thing will just happen again.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then I'll tend the wound under it, with help if I need.\"","then":"The shame falls quiet. \"Maybe this time is different.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe. There's no changing who I am.\"","then":"The shame settles in. \"Finally, some honesty.\""},{"text":"\"No. I'll never lose my temper again.\"","then":"The shame laughs. \"You said that last time.\""},{"text":"\"I'll just try harder to hold myself together next time.\"","then":"The shame shrugs. \"Trying harder is what you did today.\""}],"why":"Tending the wound under the slip, with support, is what makes the next moment different."}],"relearn":"After an old-script slip, naming it and choosing to repair and tend the wound serves you far better than self-condemnation."},
  {"id":"bc-1133","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"ask for help","hook":"You've decided to get support about your past, and you're sitting across from a counsellor.","setup":"She reads through your intake form, then looks up.","steps":[{"prompt":"The counsellor asks: \"What brings you here today?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Old pain from my childhood is showing up in how I treat my kids.\"","then":"She nods slowly. \"That takes courage to say. Tell me more.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Honestly it's probably nothing. I shouldn't waste your time.\"","then":"She smiles gently. \"You came all this way. Let's see what it is.\""},{"text":"\"My partner said I should come.\"","then":"She nods. \"And what do you think?\""},{"text":"\"I just want some tips to stop shouting.\"","then":"She nods. \"We can look at that. What's underneath the shouting?\""}],"why":"Naming plainly why you've come lets support actually help."},{"prompt":"The counsellor says: \"You wrote that your temper with the kids worries you. When does it flare most?\"","options":[{"text":"\"When my son ignores me. I hear my father's voice then.\"","then":"She writes a note. \"That's a clear trigger. We can work with that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"All the time. I'm a mess, really.\"","then":"She pauses. \"Let's find one moment to start with.\""},{"text":"\"I don't know. It just happens.\"","then":"She waits. \"Take your time. There's no rush.\""},{"text":"\"Only when the kids really push me. That's normal, no?\"","then":"She tilts her head. \"Common, maybe. Is it how you want it to go?\""}],"why":"Naming a specific trigger gives you both something real to work on."},{"prompt":"The counsellor asks: \"When you were small, how did your parents handle it when you misbehaved?\"","options":[{"text":"\"My father hit us. I was scared of him a lot.\"","then":"She nods gently. \"Thank you for telling me. That wasn't your fault.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Normal. Every father was like that back then.\"","then":"She nods. \"Common, perhaps. How did it feel to you?\""},{"text":"\"I was a naughty child. I probably deserved it.\"","then":"She shakes her head softly. \"No child deserves to be hit.\""},{"text":"\"My father meant well. I don't want to blame him here.\"","then":"She nods. \"We're not here to blame. Just to see what happened to you.\""}],"why":"Saying it plainly, without minimising, is where healing starts. It wasn't your fault."},{"prompt":"At the end, the counsellor asks: \"Would you like to come back next week?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes. I want to keep working on this.\"","then":"She smiles. \"Good. Next week, we'll look at what sets you off.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I think talking once was enough.\"","then":"She nods. \"The door's open if you change your mind.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe, if I can find the time.\"","then":"She hands you a card. \"Call when you're ready.\""},{"text":"\"Yes, but I'll only come if things get worse.\"","then":"She pauses. \"We can also work on it before it gets worse.\""}],"why":"Coming back tells you your healing matters. One session rarely untangles years."}],"relearn":"Naming plainly why you've come, and that you deserve to be there, lets support actually help; minimising it shuts the door."},
  {"id":"bc-1134","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"set boundary with relative","hook":"Your aunt Sudha never misses a chance to tell you how children should be raised.","setup":"Today she starts in over tea.","steps":[{"prompt":"Sudha Aunty says: \"A little fear never hurt anyone. Look how well you turned out.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I love a lot about how I was raised. Some things I'm changing.\"","then":"She raises an eyebrow. \"Changing what, exactly?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. Maybe I'm being too soft.\"","then":"She nods, satisfied. \"Children need a firm hand.\""},{"text":"\"That 'little fear' is exactly what messed me up.\"","then":"Her cup clatters down. \"Messed up? After all we did for you?\""},{"text":"\"Times have changed, Aunty. Let's not argue about all this.\"","then":"She waves it off. \"Times change. Children don't.\""}],"why":"Honouring what was good while naming what you'll change holds your line without an attack."},{"prompt":"Sudha Aunty says: \"When your son talks back, a slap will teach him faster.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'd rather he learns from me than fears me.\"","then":"She sniffs. \"You'll learn the hard way.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe once, if he really crosses the line.\"","then":"She smiles. \"See? You know I'm right.\""},{"text":"\"Nobody asked for your advice, Aunty.\"","then":"Her face hardens. \"So now I can't even speak in this house?\""},{"text":"\"Let's see. Every child is different.\"","then":"She takes that as a yes. \"You'll see. It works.\""}],"why":"Respect grows from trust, not fear. Saying so plainly keeps your line without a fight."},{"prompt":"As she gets up to leave, Sudha Aunty says: \"Mark my words. Children need to fear someone.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I know you care, Aunty. I'll still raise him my way.\"","then":"She sighs, then softens a little. \"Of course I care. He's my family too.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Your way is exactly what went wrong with me.\"","then":"She stops at the door, stung. \"Then I'll keep my mouth shut.\""},{"text":"\"You're right. I'll think about the slap.\"","then":"She pats your hand. \"That's all I ask.\""},{"text":"\"I'll prove you wrong. Just wait and see.\"","then":"She laughs. \"We'll see who's right.\""}],"why":"You can end warmly and keep your choice. You don't need her agreement."}],"relearn":"You can honour your upbringing and still hold the boundary that you'll parent differently, without caving to the old way."},
  {"id":"bc-1135","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"reassure inner child","hook":"An old memory of being hurt as a child has surfaced, and it won't leave you alone.","setup":"You close your eyes and picture your younger self.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your younger self whispers: \"I must have been bad. That's why it happened.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You were a child. None of it was your fault.\"","then":"Your younger self looks up. \"Really? Not even a little?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe you did bring some of it on yourself.\"","then":"Your younger self shrinks. \"I knew it.\""},{"text":"\"Stop thinking about it. It's over now.\"","then":"Your younger self goes quiet. \"Okay. I'll hide it again.\""},{"text":"\"It wasn't that bad, really. Others had it worse.\"","then":"Your younger self looks down. \"Then why does it still hurt?\""}],"why":"A child is never to blame for being hurt. Saying so is how old self-blame loosens."},{"prompt":"Your younger self whispers: \"Nobody stopped it. Nobody even noticed.\"","options":[{"text":"\"They should have. You deserved to be protected.\"","then":"Your younger self breathes out. \"I did deserve that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"That's because you kept quiet and never told anyone.\"","then":"Your younger self flinches. \"So it was my fault after all.\""},{"text":"\"That's just how things were back then.\"","then":"Your younger self shrugs sadly. \"So nobody cared.\""},{"text":"\"The grown-ups didn't know better. Let's not blame them.\"","then":"Your younger self frowns. \"But I was the one who got hurt.\""}],"why":"Every child deserves protection. Keeping quiet was how you got through it, never why it happened."},{"prompt":"Your younger self asks: \"Will it ever stop hurting?\"","options":[{"text":"\"It can ease. I'll look after you now, with help.\"","then":"Your younger self leans in. \"You'll stay this time?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"No. Some things just stay broken.\"","then":"Your younger self curls up. \"Then what's the point?\""},{"text":"\"Yes, if we just forget it ever happened.\"","then":"Your younger self hesitates. \"I tried forgetting. It comes back.\""},{"text":"\"It'll stop once I'm a perfect parent to my own kids.\"","then":"Your younger self looks worried. \"What if you make a mistake?\""}],"why":"Gentleness toward your younger self, with support like Tele-MANAS 14416, eases old pain. Forgetting doesn't."}],"relearn":"Telling your younger self the truth, that it wasn't your fault and you deserved gentleness, loosens the old self-blame."},
  {"id":"bc-1136","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"invite partner to heal together","hook":"You and your wife Anjali were both raised with harshness, and you want to change it together.","setup":"Once the kids are asleep, you sit beside her.","steps":[{"prompt":"Anjali asks: \"You said you wanted to talk about how we parent?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We were both raised harshly. Can we change it together?\"","then":"Anjali is quiet, then nods. \"My mother's voice comes out of me too.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Your side of the family is the harsh one. That's the problem.\"","then":"Anjali's face tightens. \"Really? Have you heard yourself with the kids?\""},{"text":"\"I'll work on my temper. You don't need to change anything.\"","then":"Anjali looks relieved, then unsure. \"Okay. But I shout too, you know.\""},{"text":"\"Actually, never mind. It can wait.\"","then":"Anjali frowns. \"Okay. But you've got me thinking now.\""}],"why":"Naming the shared inheritance invites her in, instead of putting the blame on one of you."},{"prompt":"Next evening, Anjali says: \"I keep thinking about our parents. They did their best. I don't want to trash them.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Me neither. We can keep the good and drop the harsh bits.\"","then":"Anjali relaxes. \"Like the stories Papa told us. I'd keep those.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Honestly, your parents were worse than mine.\"","then":"Anjali stiffens. \"I'm not doing this tonight.\""},{"text":"\"Fine. Forget it, then.\"","then":"Anjali looks relieved. \"Let's just see how it goes.\""},{"text":"\"We don't have to talk about them. Let's just use a new method.\"","then":"Anjali nods slowly. \"A method? From where, a video?\""}],"why":"Changing a pattern isn't betraying your parents. You can keep the good and leave the harm."},{"prompt":"A few days later, Anjali says: \"I yelled at Riya today, just like my mother. I hate it.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I do it too. Let's both look at what sets us off.\"","then":"Anjali wipes her eyes. \"For me it's always the tired evenings.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You need to control yourself better.\"","then":"Anjali's eyes fill. \"I know. I thought you'd understand.\""},{"text":"\"It's fine. Kids forget these things.\"","then":"Anjali shakes her head. \"Riya didn't look like she'd forget.\""},{"text":"\"Don't worry. I'll stay calm enough for both of us.\"","then":"Anjali half smiles. \"That's a lot to carry alone.\""}],"why":"Owning your side too makes it shared work, not one parent fixing the other."},{"prompt":"Anjali asks: \"So what do we actually do, starting this week?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We each tend our own stuff and back each other up.\"","then":"Anjali squeezes your hand. \"Deal. And we tell each other when we slip.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You start with a counsellor. I'm mostly fine.\"","then":"Anjali pulls back. \"So it's just my problem, then?\""},{"text":"\"Nothing formal. Let's just try to be nicer.\"","then":"Anjali nods, unconvinced. \"We said that last year too.\""},{"text":"\"Let's download a parenting app and follow it.\"","then":"Anjali shrugs. \"An app won't know what sets us off.\""}],"why":"Tending your own wounds and backing each other up lets you both break the cycle as a team."}],"relearn":"Inviting your partner to tend their wounds and choose differently together does more than blaming one of you for the past."},
  {"id":"bc-1137","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"answer the should-be-over-it voice","hook":"Lately old hurts from your childhood have been stirring, and a harsh inner voice keeps picking at them.","setup":"It starts up again as you lie awake tonight.","steps":[{"prompt":"The voice says: \"You're a grown adult with kids. Why are you still upset?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Age doesn't seal a wound. Tending it now still matters.\"","then":"The voice pauses. \"Still? After all these years?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. Only weak people still feel this.\"","then":"The voice settles, pleased. \"So stop feeling it.\""},{"text":"\"I'm not upset. I'm completely over it.\"","then":"The voice laughs. \"Then why are you awake?\""},{"text":"\"I'll deal with it once the kids are older.\"","then":"The voice relaxes. \"Later, then. Much later.\""}],"why":"What hurt you as a child was never your fault. Time alone doesn't heal it, so tending it now still matters."},{"prompt":"The voice says: \"Your parents are old now. Digging this up is unfair to them.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'll talk to a counsellor. No one's on trial here.\"","then":"The voice falters. \"A counsellor? For something this old?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"True. I'll leave it for their sake.\"","then":"The voice softens. \"That's the respectful way.\""},{"text":"\"They should know exactly how much they hurt me.\"","then":"The voice sharpens. \"And then what? A fight at their age?\""},{"text":"\"I'll tend it quietly, but only once they're gone.\"","then":"The voice agrees. \"Yes. Wait. There's no hurry.\""}],"why":"Support, like a counsellor or Tele-MANAS 14416, helps you heal without putting your parents on trial."},{"prompt":"The voice asks: \"And what difference will any of this make to your kids?\"","options":[{"text":"\"What I tend now, I won't pass on to them.\"","then":"The voice falls quiet. \"Maybe that's worth it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"None. They're fine whatever I feel.\"","then":"The voice agrees. \"Exactly. So why bother?\""},{"text":"\"I'll just hide it better from them.\"","then":"The voice nods. \"Hide it, like your parents did.\""},{"text":"\"I'll read every parenting book to make up for it.\"","then":"The voice scoffs. \"Books, while the old hurt stays put?\""}],"why":"Tending an old wound now is how you stop passing it on to your children."}],"relearn":"Answering the 'too old to still hurt' voice with the truth that age doesn't seal a wound keeps you tending it."},
  {"id":"bc-1138","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"role-play","persona":"Priya","source":"encourage a scared parent","hook":"Your friend Farzana confides she's terrified her unhealed past will make her harm her son.","setup":"She says it quietly, after the kids run off to play.","steps":[{"prompt":"Farzana says: \"Some days I'm scared of what my hands might do.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That fear means you're already breaking the pattern.\"","then":"Farzana looks at you. \"Breaking it? It doesn't feel like that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Then you probably will. Once it's in you, it stays.\"","then":"Farzana goes pale. \"So I'm just like my mother. I knew it.\""},{"text":"\"Don't say that! You'd never hurt your child.\"","then":"She falls silent. \"Maybe I shouldn't have said anything.\""},{"text":"\"Everyone feels that sometimes. It's nothing to worry about.\"","then":"Farzana nods slowly. \"Okay. I'll just keep it to myself, then.\""}],"why":"Her fear shows she's already watching the pattern. Saying so gives hope instead of dread."},{"prompt":"Farzana asks: \"But what do I do when it gets really bad in the moment?\"","options":[{"text":"\"If Ayaan is safe, step into another room first.\"","then":"Farzana nods slowly. \"Step away. I've never let myself do that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Just hold it in. You're the adult.\"","then":"She sighs. \"I do. Until I can't.\""},{"text":"\"Distract yourself on your phone till it passes.\"","then":"She shakes her head. \"He keeps pulling at me. That makes it worse.\""},{"text":"\"Call your mother and let her calm you down.\"","then":"Farzana laughs bitterly. \"My mother is where this started.\""}],"why":"Stepping away, if it's safe, keeps everyone safe while the surge passes."},{"prompt":"Farzana asks: \"Is there anyone I can talk to about the past itself?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Tele-MANAS 14416 is there for exactly this.\"","then":"Farzana writes it down. \"I'll call tonight after he sleeps.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Talk to me anytime. You don't need anyone else.\"","then":"Farzana smiles weakly. \"You're kind. But you're not trained for this.\""},{"text":"\"Don't go to a stranger. People will talk.\"","then":"Farzana drops her gaze. \"I hadn't thought of that. Never mind.\""},{"text":"\"A counsellor, maybe, once things are really bad.\"","then":"She nods. \"Once it's bad. I hope I notice in time.\""}],"why":"A helpline like Tele-MANAS 14416 gives her real support for the past, not just a friend's ear."},{"prompt":"A week later, Farzana asks: \"What if it still feels unsafe one day, even after all this?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then step away, and call Childline 1098 or 112 for help.\"","then":"Farzana saves both numbers. \"Okay. Having a plan makes me less scared.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"That won't happen. You're too careful now.\"","then":"She hesitates. \"I hope you're right.\""},{"text":"\"Then maybe he's better off with someone else.\"","then":"Farzana's face crumples. \"You think I'm that bad?\""},{"text":"\"Then call me, and I'll come straight over.\"","then":"Farzana nods. \"And if you can't come?\""}],"why":"In real danger, Childline 1098 or 112 gets help fast. Knowing that keeps a child safe."}],"relearn":"Affirming that their fear itself shows change, plus pointing to step-away and support, helps a scared parent far more than dread."},
  {"id":"bc-1139","cat":"heal-your-wounds","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"name it without blaming parents","hook":"You want to admit an old hurt to yourself without it turning into a verdict on your parents.","setup":"As you journal, an old voice chimes in.","steps":[{"prompt":"The voice says: \"If it hurt that much, your parents must have been monsters.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It hurt me. They were likely passing on their own pain.\"","then":"The voice hesitates. \"Their own pain? You never thought of that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Yes. They ruined me, and there's nothing good to keep.\"","then":"The voice grows loud. \"Then you'll turn out the same.\""},{"text":"\"It didn't really hurt. They were good parents.\"","then":"The voice shrugs. \"Then why are you writing about it?\""},{"text":"\"I'd rather not decide either way. Let's leave it.\"","then":"The voice relaxes. \"Fine. Close the book.\""}],"why":"You can name a wound and still hold compassion for parents who passed on their own pain."},{"prompt":"The voice says: \"If you forgive them, you're saying it was okay.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It wasn't okay. I can still understand where it came from.\"","then":"The voice softens. \"Both at once? That's harder.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Then I can never understand them either.\"","then":"The voice nods grimly. \"Right. Keep them as villains.\""},{"text":"\"Fine. It was okay. Let's drop it.\"","then":"The voice smiles. \"Just as I thought.\""},{"text":"\"I'll forgive them fully today, and never think of it again.\"","then":"The voice narrows. \"Just like that? And the hurt?\""}],"why":"Understanding your parents doesn't mean calling the hurt okay. Both can be true."},{"prompt":"The voice asks: \"So what are you going to do with all this?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Heal it, and raise my kids differently.\"","then":"The voice quietens. \"Heal it. That could actually change something.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Nothing. It's in the past, so why bother?\"","then":"The voice nods. \"Right. Bury it again.\""},{"text":"\"Keep a list of everything they got wrong.\"","then":"The voice brightens. \"Yes. Keep score.\""},{"text":"\"Make sure my kids never face a single hard thing.\"","then":"The voice laughs. \"Wrap them in cotton wool, then?\""}],"why":"Healing your own wound is how the cycle stops with you, without condemning anyone."}],"relearn":"Naming a wound while holding compassion for parents who passed on their own pain heals more than condemning them wholesale."},
  {"id":"bc-1170","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"My father raised me with a strict hand and I turned out fine, so it must be the right way.\"","myth":{"un":"A strict hand raised me and I turned out fine, so it's the right way.","re":"Surviving harsh discipline isn't proof it helped; many carry quiet costs from it and grew despite it, not because of it.","why":"\"I turned out fine\" overlooks the fear and distance it can leave behind."},"relearn":"Turning out okay despite harsh discipline isn't proof it worked; you can choose gentler and firmer ways.","persona":"Suresh","source":"turned out fine","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1171","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"My mother never said sorry to us, and that's how a parent keeps respect.\"","myth":{"un":"A parent who apologises to a child loses their respect.","re":"Owning a mistake to your child models respect and actually strengthens your bond and your authority.","why":"Children trust a parent who can say \"I was wrong\" more, not less."},"relearn":"Apologising to your child doesn't cost you authority; it builds trust and shows respect.","persona":"Suresh","source":"apology builds it","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1172","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"In our family, children were seen and not heard, and that kept the house calm.\"","myth":{"un":"Children should be seen and not heard.","re":"A child's voice and feelings matter; letting them be heard builds trust and doesn't cost you a calm home.","why":"Silence taught fear, not calm; being heard teaches a child they're safe with you."},"relearn":"\"Seen, not heard\" silences a child; their voice and feelings matter and can be heard with warmth.","persona":"any","source":"voice matters","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1173","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"What my parents did worked for generations, so questioning any of it is just modern nonsense.\"","myth":{"un":"It worked for generations, so questioning any of it is modern nonsense.","re":"You can honour your family and still leave the parts that cause harm; keeping the good isn't all-or-nothing.","why":"Long-standing doesn't mean harmless; you get to keep some and set some down."},"relearn":"You can keep the good your family passed on and gently leave the harmful parts; it's not all-or-nothing.","persona":"Suresh","source":"not all or nothing","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1174","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"My childhood is the past and has nothing to do with how I parent today.\"","myth":{"un":"My childhood is the past and has nothing to do with how I parent.","re":"Unhealed wounds resurface in your triggers and reactions; tending them helps the cycle stop with you.","why":"The past leaks into the present in heated moments until it's tended."},"relearn":"Your childhood shapes your reactions; tending old wounds helps the harmful cycle stop with you.","persona":"any","source":"heal so it stops","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1175","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"I swore I'd parent differently, but the old way always wins in the end, so why try.\"","myth":{"un":"The way I was raised always wins in the end, so why even try.","re":"The inheritance isn't destiny; new patterns are genuinely learnable, and each time you pause you weaken the old one.","why":"Choosing differently gets easier with practice; the reflex isn't fixed."},"relearn":"How you were raised isn't destiny; new patterns are learnable and grow stronger each time you choose them.","persona":"Suresh","source":"not destiny","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1176","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"My elders shamed us when we slipped, and that shame is what made us behave.\"","myth":{"un":"Shaming a child is what makes them behave.","re":"Shame damages a child's self-worth and your bond; it discourages them rather than teaching them.","why":"A child who feels small hides and resents, instead of learning."},"relearn":"Shaming doesn't teach good behaviour; it wounds self-worth and your bond. Guide without shame.","persona":"any","source":"shame wounds","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1177","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"My parents ruled by fear, and that fear is what they called respect.\"","myth":{"un":"Fear is the same as respect.","re":"Fear teaches a child to hide and resent; real respect grows from trust, not from being afraid of you.","why":"A child who fears you obeys when watched and learns to lie when not."},"relearn":"Fear isn't respect; it breeds hiding and resentment. Respect grows from trust you build over time.","persona":"Suresh","source":"fear isn't respect","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1178","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: there may be one thing from your own childhood you'd never repeat, and one you'd happily pass on. Both can be true.","prompt":"Sit with that:","options":["Both are true","One to leave","One to keep"],"affirm":"Keeping the good and leaving the harm isn't all-or-nothing; you get to sort your inheritance with care.","relearn":"You can hold both: something from your childhood to pass on, and something to leave behind.","persona":"any","source":"keep one leave one","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1179","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the moment you notice \"I'm doing what was done to me,\" you've already stepped outside the pattern. Feel that?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["I feel it","Noticing is the step","Outside the pattern"],"affirm":"Noticing the old reflex, without blaming yourself, is the exact moment you get to choose differently.","relearn":"Noticing the inherited reflex is itself the first step to choosing a different response.","persona":"Suresh","source":"noticing is the step","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1180","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: choosing a gentler path than your parents did isn't a verdict on them. You can love them and still do it differently.","prompt":"Does that sit right?","options":["It sits right","Love and change","Not a verdict"],"affirm":"Doing some things differently honours what you learned the hard way; it isn't betrayal of your parents.","relearn":"You can love your parents and still parent differently; that's growth, not betrayal.","persona":"Suresh","source":"not a verdict","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1181","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: imagine your child, grown, describing their childhood. What one word would you most want them to use?","prompt":"What word comes up?","options":["Safe","Loved","Heard"],"affirm":"Letting that word guide your choices today is how you decide, gently, what to keep and what to leave.","relearn":"Picturing the childhood you want your child to remember helps you choose your patterns on purpose.","persona":"any","source":"one word","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1182","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the patterns you choose on purpose can stop with you, so your child never has to unlearn them. That's the gift.","prompt":"How does that land?","options":["It lands","Stops with me","A gift forward"],"affirm":"Deciding what harm ends with you spares your child the work of undoing it later; it's a quiet, lasting gift.","relearn":"What you choose to stop with you, your child never has to unlearn; that decision is a gift forward.","persona":"any","source":"stops with me","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1183","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: most parents who were raised harshly aren't cruel; they're carrying what they were handed, often unaware. Compassion fits here.","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Compassion fits","They carried it too","Without excusing harm"],"affirm":"Holding compassion for those who raised you, without excusing the harm, frees you to choose your own way.","relearn":"Most who parented harshly were carrying what they were handed; compassion for that frees you to change.","persona":"Suresh","source":"compassion for them","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1184","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: it's normal to feel grief realising some of what you needed as a child, you didn't get. That grief is allowed.","prompt":"Let it be here:","options":["It's allowed","I feel the grief","Naming it helps"],"affirm":"Letting the grief be felt, with compassion, turns it into fuel for change instead of something that leaks out.","relearn":"Grief over what you didn't get as a child is allowed; felt with care, it can fuel how you choose to parent.","persona":"Suresh","source":"grief allowed","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1185","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you don't have to throw out everything from your upbringing. Which warm thing, a food, a song, a ritual, do you keep?","prompt":"What comes to mind?","options":["A ritual","A song or food","Time together"],"affirm":"Keeping the warmth your family gave you is part of breaking the cycle; it's never all-or-nothing.","relearn":"Breaking the cycle means keeping the warmth too, the rituals and love worth passing on, not just leaving harm.","persona":"any","source":"keep the warmth","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1186","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: when your child tests you at the end of a long day, you might hear an old voice rise in your mouth. You can pause it.","prompt":"How does that feel?","options":["Familiar","I can pause it","The voice isn't me"],"affirm":"Hearing the old voice rise doesn't mean you'll repeat it; the pause between feeling and acting is yours.","relearn":"When the inherited voice rises under stress, the pause before you act is yours; the old reflex isn't you.","persona":"Suresh","source":"old voice rises","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1187","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you may be the first in your line to even ask these questions. That alone is the cycle starting to bend.","prompt":"What do you notice?","options":["I'm the first","The cycle bends","Asking is a start"],"affirm":"Being the one who stops to question the inherited way is, by itself, the start of choosing a new pattern.","relearn":"Being the first in your family to question the old way is itself the cycle beginning to change.","persona":"any","source":"first to ask","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1188","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the goal isn't a perfect childhood for your kid, just one a little gentler than the one you were given. Small is enough.","prompt":"Does that ease it?","options":["It eases it","A little gentler","Small is enough"],"affirm":"Aiming for a bit gentler than you had, not perfect, is realistic and still breaks the cycle for your child.","relearn":"You don't need a perfect childhood for your child, just one a little gentler than yours; small shifts count.","persona":"any","source":"a little gentler","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1189","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each thing you inherited: worth keeping, or worth gently leaving behind?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Bedtime stories every night"},{"id":"b","text":"A slap when you talked back"},{"id":"c","text":"Eating together as a family"},{"id":"d","text":"Silence instead of \"I love you\""},{"id":"e","text":"Respecting hard work"},{"id":"f","text":"Comparing you to other kids"}],"bins":[{"id":"keep","label":"Worth keeping","valence":"pos"},{"id":"leave","label":"Worth leaving","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"keep","b":"leave","c":"keep","d":"leave","e":"keep","f":"leave"},"relearn":"Keep the stories, shared meals and good values; leave the slaps, the silence and the comparing.","persona":"any","source":"keep vs leave","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1190","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each belief about how you were raised: a compassionate truth, or a shaming myth?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Most harsh parents were raised that way\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"If I slip once, I've ruined my child\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"I can choose differently from here\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"I'm doomed to repeat my parents\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"Noticing the pattern is real progress\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"A trying parent who errs is a monster\""}],"bins":[{"id":"truth","label":"Compassionate truth","valence":"pos"},{"id":"myth","label":"Shaming myth","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"truth","b":"myth","c":"truth","d":"myth","e":"truth","f":"myth"},"relearn":"Compassion is true: harsh parents were raised that way, you can choose now. Doom and monster talk is shaming.","persona":"any","source":"compassion vs shame","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1191","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each inherited message: pass it on to your child, or rewrite it?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Your feelings matter to me\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"Big boys don't cry\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"Mistakes are how you learn\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"You'll only learn through fear\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"You can tell me anything\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"Children should be seen, not heard\""}],"bins":[{"id":"pass","label":"Pass it on","valence":"pos"},{"id":"rewrite","label":"Rewrite it","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"pass","b":"rewrite","c":"pass","d":"rewrite","e":"pass","f":"rewrite"},"relearn":"Pass on \"your feelings matter\" and \"you can tell me anything\"; rewrite \"don't cry,\" fear and \"seen, not heard.\"","persona":"any","source":"pass or rewrite","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1192","type":"sort","hook":"Suresh notices old reflexes at day's end. Sort each one: catch it, or it's safe to keep?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Reaching to hit when angry"},{"id":"b","text":"Hugging him goodnight"},{"id":"c","text":"Mocking him when he cries"},{"id":"d","text":"Asking how his day went"},{"id":"e","text":"Threatening to scare him quiet"},{"id":"f","text":"Praising effort, not just marks"}],"bins":[{"id":"catch","label":"Catch this reflex","valence":"uhoh"},{"id":"safe","label":"Safe to keep","valence":"pos"}],"key":{"a":"catch","b":"safe","c":"catch","d":"safe","e":"catch","f":"safe"},"relearn":"Catch the hitting, mocking and threats as they rise; the hugs, the asking and praising effort are safe to keep.","persona":"Suresh","source":"catch the reflex","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1193","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each move a parent makes with their own past: tends the wound, or buries it?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Naming what hurt you as a child"},{"id":"b","text":"\"I never think about my childhood\""},{"id":"c","text":"Talking to a counsellor about it"},{"id":"d","text":"Snapping, then blaming the kids"},{"id":"e","text":"Noticing what triggers the old anger"},{"id":"f","text":"Pushing the old pain down, again"}],"bins":[{"id":"tend","label":"Tends the wound","valence":"pos"},{"id":"bury","label":"Buries it","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"tend","b":"bury","c":"tend","d":"bury","e":"tend","f":"bury"},"relearn":"Naming the hurt, getting support and knowing your triggers tends the wound; ignoring and pushing it down buries it.","persona":"any","source":"tend or bury","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1194","type":"sort","hook":"A relative pushes back on your gentler ways. Sort each reply: holds your path, or caves to the old way?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'm choosing some things differently\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"You're right, I'll be harder\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"I love what you gave me, and I'll add to it\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"Maybe fear did keep me in line\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"My child can be heard and still respectful\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"I suppose a slap never hurt anyone\""}],"bins":[{"id":"holds","label":"Holds your path","valence":"pos"},{"id":"caves","label":"Caves to old way","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"holds","b":"caves","c":"holds","d":"caves","e":"holds","f":"caves"},"relearn":"Holding your path means \"I'm choosing differently\" and \"heard and respectful\"; caving repeats fear and slaps.","persona":"Suresh","source":"hold or cave","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1195","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each childhood memory: a warm gift to pass on, or a hurt to leave behind?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Grandmother's lullabies"},{"id":"b","text":"Being beaten for poor marks"},{"id":"c","text":"Festival mornings together"},{"id":"d","text":"Being called \"useless\" when upset"},{"id":"e","text":"A parent who showed up for you"},{"id":"f","text":"Never being allowed to say no"}],"bins":[{"id":"gift","label":"Warm gift to pass on","valence":"pos"},{"id":"hurt","label":"Hurt to leave","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"gift","b":"hurt","c":"gift","d":"hurt","e":"gift","f":"hurt"},"relearn":"Pass on the lullabies, festivals and showing up; leave the beatings, the name-calling and never allowing a no.","persona":"any","source":"gift vs hurt","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1196","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each thought about questioning your upbringing: frees you to choose, or keeps you stuck?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I can keep the good and leave the rest\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"Questioning it betrays my parents\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"Loving them and changing both fit\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"This is just who I am\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"New ways are learnable\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"It worked for them, can't touch it\""}],"bins":[{"id":"frees","label":"Frees you","valence":"pos"},{"id":"stuck","label":"Keeps you stuck","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"frees","b":"stuck","c":"frees","d":"stuck","e":"frees","f":"stuck"},"relearn":"Keeping the good, loving and changing, and learnable new ways free you; betrayal-guilt and \"can't touch it\" keep you stuck.","persona":"any","source":"free or stuck","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1197","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"hand rises","hook":"Your son ignores your call to switch off the TV a third time, and your hand rises like your father's.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your raised hand hangs in the air. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Lower your hand, step back, and breathe","then":"Your heart pounds, but your hand is at your side. Your son stares, wide-eyed.","best":true},{"text":"Let the hand fall, as you were taught","then":"The old pattern passes straight to your son, and his eyes fill with fear."},{"text":"Lower it, but warn him next time you won't stop","then":"He goes very still. He's learned to fear the hand, even unused."},{"text":"Grab his arm and yank him away from the TV","then":"He stumbles, crying, and the house fills with the old fear."}],"why":"Lowering your hand and breathing breaks the reflex before it becomes your son's inheritance."},{"prompt":"Moments later, your son is watching you, frightened. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Crouch down and calmly say the TV goes off now","then":"He blinks, then switches it off, still watching your face.","best":true},{"text":"Tell him that's what happens when he doesn't listen","then":"He shrinks back, and the fear settles into his face."},{"text":"Let him go back to the TV so things calm down","then":"He turns back to the screen, and the rule quietly disappears."},{"text":"Switch the TV off yourself and walk away","then":"He sulks on the sofa, still unsure how angry you are."}],"why":"Firm and kind means holding the limit calmly, without fear or giving in."},{"prompt":"That evening, you're still shaken by how fast your hand rose. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say sorry for what you did, and that it wasn't his fault","then":"He hugs you tight. 'You scared me, Papa,' he whispers.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing. He's already forgotten it","then":"He's quieter than usual at dinner, and keeps his distance."},{"text":"Tell yourself a little fear will teach him","then":"The shaky feeling fades, and so does your resolve to change."},{"text":"Think over what triggered you, but leave him out of it","then":"You spot the trigger. Your son still goes to bed not knowing you're sorry."}],"why":"Owning what you did, and that it wasn't his fault, repairs it. If the reflex keeps rising, Tele-MANAS 14416 can help."},{"prompt":"A week later, your son ignores you again about homework. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Feel the rise, breathe, then set a clear limit","then":"Your voice stays steady. He grumbles, then pulls out his notebook.","best":true},{"text":"Raise your voice so he knows you mean it","then":"He jumps and obeys, eyes down, and the house goes tense."},{"text":"Let it go this time to avoid another scene","then":"He keeps playing, and the homework is left for later."},{"text":"Take away his cricket bat for a month","then":"He cries that it's not fair, and the homework still isn't done."}],"why":"Catching the rise and holding a calm, clear limit is how the new pattern grows."}],"debrief":"Lowering your hand and breathing interrupts the inheritance; if you ever fear harming your child, get help.","relearn":"When your hand rises like your parent's did, lower it and breathe; that pause stops the pattern."},
  {"id":"bc-1198","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"spoiling","hook":"Your mother says you're spoiling your daughter by letting her cry on your shoulder.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says it while your daughter is still sniffling in your arms. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank her, and keep holding your daughter","then":"Your mother purses her lips. Your daughter's sobs slowly ease against you.","best":true},{"text":"Put your daughter down and tell her to stop crying","then":"She tries to swallow her tears, and looks at you, confused."},{"text":"Snap at your mother to stay out of it","then":"Your mother goes stiff, and the room fills with tension."},{"text":"Agree she's too old for this, but hug her once more","then":"Your daughter hears 'too old' and pulls away sooner than she needed to."}],"why":"You can stay warm with your mother and still keep comforting your child."},{"prompt":"Later, your mother says, 'We never coddled you, and you turned out strong.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say she did her best, and you're choosing comfort","then":"Your mother sighs, but lets it rest.","best":true},{"text":"Agree, and promise to toughen her up","then":"Your mother nods, pleased, and you feel uneasy."},{"text":"Tell her she was cold, and you won't do that to her","then":"Your mother's face falls, and she leaves the room."},{"text":"Change the subject to avoid a fight","then":"The talk moves on. Next visit, she says it again."}],"why":"Honouring your mother while owning your choice holds your path without a fight."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your daughter falls and cries in front of your mother. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Comfort her, right in front of your mother","then":"She settles quickly and runs back to play. Your mother watches quietly.","best":true},{"text":"Tell her to be brave and stop crying","then":"She bites her lip and hides her face, and your mother nods."},{"text":"Wait for your mother to leave before comforting her","then":"Your daughter cries alone for a minute, looking toward you."},{"text":"Comfort her while glaring at your mother","then":"Your daughter settles, but feels the tension over her head."}],"why":"Comforting her no matter who's watching teaches her that her tears are safe with you."},{"prompt":"That night, your daughter asks if crying makes her a baby. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Crying is how feelings come out, and that's okay","then":"She thinks, then snuggles in. 'Okay. Sometimes I'm just sad.'","best":true},{"text":"Only babies cry, so try not to","then":"She nods seriously, and you see her file it away."},{"text":"Ask her who told her that","then":"She looks nervous, as if she has said something wrong."},{"text":"Say she's very brave, so she won't need to cry","then":"She beams, and decides she'll try hard not to cry."}],"why":"Telling her tears are okay helps her trust her feelings, and you."}],"debrief":"You can stay warm with your elder and keep comforting your child; her trust beats their approval.","relearn":"When an elder calls comfort \"spoiling,\" honour them but keep comforting your child; their trust matters most."},
  {"id":"bc-1199","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"rule by fear","hook":"Your father ruled your childhood home by fear. Lately you wonder if your own home runs the same way.","steps":[{"prompt":"Tonight, the chatter stops the moment you come through the door. What do you tell yourself?","options":[{"text":"I've been ruling by fear. I can start building trust","then":"It stings to admit, but something in you settles into resolve.","best":true},{"text":"Good. Quiet kids are respectful kids","then":"The house stays silent, and you eat dinner surrounded by lowered eyes."},{"text":"I'm a lost cause. It's too late to change","then":"You sink into the sofa. The quiet feels permanent."},{"text":"They're just tired. It's nothing to do with me","then":"You let it go, and the silence greets you again the next evening."}],"why":"Noticing the fear pattern, without attacking yourself, is where change begins."},{"prompt":"Next day, your daughter spills ink on her homework and looks at you, terrified. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell her mistakes happen, and help her fix it","then":"Her shoulders drop. She looks at you as if checking it's real.","best":true},{"text":"Scold her so she's more careful next time","then":"She nods fast, eyes down, and hides the next mistake from you."},{"text":"Say nothing and let her sort it out alone","then":"She scrubs at the page, still glancing at you nervously."},{"text":"Say it's fine, and redo the page for her yourself","then":"She's relieved, but still flinches when you reach for her pen."}],"why":"Meeting a mistake calmly shows her she's safe with you. That's where trust starts."},{"prompt":"At dinner, your son admits he failed a test, bracing for your anger. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank him for telling you, then ask what went wrong","then":"He looks surprised, then starts explaining the hard questions.","best":true},{"text":"Remind him what happens when he fails","then":"He goes pale, and you realise he'll hide the next result."},{"text":"Say marks don't matter at all","then":"He relaxes, but shrugs off studying for the next test."},{"text":"Stay calm, but ban his phone until results improve","then":"He nods silently and says little else all evening."}],"why":"Thanking him for honesty makes it safe to tell you hard things. That's respect, not fear."},{"prompt":"A month on, the kids still go quiet sometimes when you walk in. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Stay calm with them tonight. Trust builds slowly","then":"Slowly, the chatter lasts a little longer each evening.","best":true},{"text":"Give up. They'll always be scared of me","then":"You stop trying, and the old quiet settles back in."},{"text":"Buy them gifts so they'll warm up faster","then":"They love the gifts. The quiet when you walk in stays."},{"text":"Tell them firmly that they don't need to fear you now","then":"They nod quickly, which is its own kind of fear."}],"why":"Change is gradual. Each calm day adds to the trust that replaces fear."}],"debrief":"Noticing the fear pattern, without beating yourself up, is where change begins, not ends.","relearn":"Noticing you rule by fear isn't failure; it's the start of building respect through trust instead."},
  {"id":"bc-1200","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"children should have a say","hook":"At a Diwali lunch, relatives laugh that you asked your son's opinion. In their day, children had no say.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your son goes quiet, watching you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Smile, and turn back to hear his answer","then":"Your son brightens and answers. An uncle rolls his eyes, and lunch goes on.","best":true},{"text":"Hush him so you don't look soft","then":"Your son shrinks into his chair and stays silent for the rest of the meal."},{"text":"Tell the relatives they're stuck in the past","then":"The table goes quiet, and your aunt looks offended."},{"text":"Laugh along, and ask him later when it's quieter","then":"Your son nods, but he stops talking at the table."}],"why":"Letting him be heard even when relatives mock it shows him his voice is safe with you."},{"prompt":"After lunch, your aunt pulls you aside: 'You'll spoil him with all this asking.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say he can be heard and still respect his elders","then":"She raises an eyebrow. 'We'll see,' she says, but she doesn't argue.","best":true},{"text":"Agree to stop asking him in front of family","then":"She pats your arm, satisfied. You feel you've shrunk a little."},{"text":"Tell her she has no right to judge your parenting","then":"Her face tightens, and she avoids you for the rest of the day."},{"text":"Say he's just a child, so it's only small things","then":"She nods. The idea that his voice counts slips away."}],"why":"Being heard and being respectful go together. Saying so calmly holds your ground."},{"prompt":"On the drive home, your son asks why everyone laughed at him. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say they're used to other ways, but his voice counts","then":"He thinks about it. 'So I can still say what I think?' he asks.","best":true},{"text":"Say he should keep quiet around elders","then":"He nods and looks out of the window, silent."},{"text":"Say the relatives are silly and old-fashioned","then":"He giggles, and repeats it at the next family visit."},{"text":"Say they weren't laughing at him, so forget it","then":"He looks unsure, because it did feel like they were."}],"why":"Explaining without mocking anyone teaches him his voice matters everywhere."},{"prompt":"At the next family function, an elder asks your son a question and he looks at you first. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Nod to him to answer in his own words","then":"He answers clearly and politely, and the elder looks mildly surprised.","best":true},{"text":"Answer for him to avoid any fuss","then":"He goes quiet, and the elder turns to you instead."},{"text":"Whisper to him what to say","then":"He repeats your words, watching your face for approval."},{"text":"Tell the elder he's shy, then nudge him to speak","then":"He blushes and mumbles, feeling the label stick."}],"why":"Trusting him to speak for himself grows the voice you've been protecting."}],"debrief":"Letting your child be heard even when relatives mock it teaches him his feelings matter everywhere.","relearn":"Keep letting your child have a say even when relatives mock it; \"seen, not heard\" is the pattern to leave."},
  {"id":"bc-1201","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"comparing","hook":"You catch yourself comparing your daughter to her cousin, as you were compared, and her face falls.","steps":[{"prompt":"You're halfway through the sentence about her cousin's marks. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Stop, and tell her you love who she is","then":"She looks up, unsure, then leans into you.","best":true},{"text":"Finish it. A little push will motivate her","then":"She goes quiet, and later you find her report card hidden in a drawer."},{"text":"Stop, and quickly change the topic","then":"The words hang in the air. She stays quiet all evening."},{"text":"Say you're only saying it for her own good","then":"She nods, but her eyes stay on the floor."}],"why":"Stopping and naming your love cuts the comparison before it wounds her worth."},{"prompt":"Later, your daughter asks if you wish her cousin were your daughter instead. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Apologise for comparing, and say you'd never swap her","then":"She sniffles, then smiles. 'Promise?' she asks.","best":true},{"text":"Laugh and say she's being silly to even think that","then":"She laughs weakly, but the question stays in her eyes."},{"text":"Say her cousin just works harder","then":"Her face crumples, and she goes to her room."},{"text":"Say of course not, and buy her a treat","then":"She enjoys the treat, but she doesn't bring up her marks again."}],"why":"A clean apology owns the hurt and tells her plainly she's enough as she is."},{"prompt":"A week later, at a family get-together, an aunt starts comparing the cousins' marks. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Gently steer the talk away from comparing the kids","then":"The aunt shrugs and moves on. Your daughter relaxes beside you.","best":true},{"text":"Join in, so you don't seem touchy","then":"Your daughter stares at her plate as her marks are discussed."},{"text":"Tell the aunt off in front of everyone","then":"The room goes stiff, and your daughter wishes she could disappear."},{"text":"Stay quiet, and comfort your daughter later at home","then":"She sits through it alone, and thanks you quietly on the way home."}],"why":"Protecting her from comparison, calmly, shows her she's valued as herself."}],"debrief":"Comparing to motivate is an inherited reflex that wounds; stopping and naming your love repairs it.","relearn":"When you compare your child as you were compared, stop and affirm who they are; comparison wounds worth."},
  {"id":"bc-1202","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"harsh words form","hook":"Your old anger surges, and you hear your father's harsh words forming in your mouth at your son.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your son is right in front of you, waiting. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"If it's safe, step into another room first","then":"You shut the door and breathe. The words stay unsaid.","best":true},{"text":"Let the words out. They're already there","then":"The harsh words land on your son, as they once landed on you."},{"text":"Stay and clench your jaw, glaring at him","then":"You say nothing, but your face says it all. Your son steps back."},{"text":"Stay put and take a few breaths to push through it","then":"The breaths help a little, but the words keep pressing at your teeth."}],"why":"When old words form under anger, stepping away, if safe, keeps them from landing on your son."},{"prompt":"A few minutes later, the surge has eased. Your son is nearby, unsure where he stands. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Go to him calmly, own your part, and talk it over","then":"Your voice is steady. He edges closer, and together you sort out what happened.","best":true},{"text":"Keep your distance until he comes to apologise","then":"He hovers nearby, unsure whether he's in trouble."},{"text":"Give him the lecture you'd been holding in","then":"The anger returns with the words, and he shrinks away."},{"text":"Let it go completely, so it doesn't flare up again","then":"He's relieved, but what he did never gets talked about."}],"why":"Coming back calm, owning your part, then talking it over repairs the moment and still holds the limit."},{"prompt":"That night, you wonder why that surge felt so much like your father. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Note what set it off, so you can spot it sooner","then":"You realise being ignored does it. Next time, you'll feel it coming.","best":true},{"text":"Decide you're just like him, and that's that","then":"The thought weighs on you, and you stop trying to catch it."},{"text":"Blame your son for pushing your buttons","then":"You feel justified for a moment, and nothing changes."},{"text":"Resolve never to feel angry again","then":"It feels strong tonight. It won't last the week."}],"why":"Knowing your triggers helps you use the gap between feeling and acting next time."}],"debrief":"When old words form under anger, stepping away if safe keeps the surge from harming your child.","relearn":"When your parent's harsh words rise in your mouth, step away if safe; the gap before you act is yours."},
  {"id":"bc-1203","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"both raised with the belt","hook":"Your partner Rohit says, 'We were both raised with the belt, and look at us.'","steps":[{"prompt":"He says it while your daughter plays in the next room. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say 'We were, and let's choose differently for her'","then":"Rohit pauses. 'Differently how?' he asks, more curious than cross.","best":true},{"text":"Agree, and say a smack now and then is fine","then":"Rohit nods. The old way quietly becomes the plan for your daughter."},{"text":"Tell him he's turning into his father","then":"Rohit's face hardens, and the talk becomes a fight about his family."},{"text":"Say nothing now, and bring it up some other time","then":"Rohit takes your silence as agreement, and the moment passes."}],"why":"Naming the shared past, then inviting a new choice, makes you a team instead of opponents."},{"prompt":"That evening, your daughter ignores Rohit's third call for her bath. He asks what would even work. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Suggest calm, clear limits with a related consequence","then":"Rohit frowns, thinking. 'Like no story tonight if she won't come?'","best":true},{"text":"Admit you don't know, so maybe the belt is easier","then":"Rohit shrugs, and the old answer wins by default."},{"text":"Say she'll listen if you just love her enough","then":"Rohit looks doubtful. 'Love didn't get her to bed last night.'"},{"text":"Say you'll handle all the discipline from now on","then":"Rohit steps back. 'Fine. You deal with it, then.'"}],"why":"Firm and kind limits work better than fear. Offering one concrete idea brings him along."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your daughter throws her food and Rohit starts to raise his voice. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Calmly offer to take over so he can step away","then":"Rohit exhales and nods. You kneel and tell your daughter food stays on the plate.","best":true},{"text":"Let him handle it his way to avoid a fight","then":"Rohit shouts, and your daughter bursts into tears."},{"text":"Shout at Rohit in front of her to stop","then":"Now both parents are shouting, and your daughter covers her ears."},{"text":"Take your daughter out, and say nothing to Rohit","then":"Your daughter calms down. Rohit is left fuming alone in the kitchen."}],"why":"Backing each other up in the moment lets one parent calm down while the limit still holds."},{"prompt":"That night, Rohit admits the belt memories still sting him. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say yours do too, and suggest you both get support","then":"Rohit is quiet, then nods. 'Maybe we both need to talk to someone.'","best":true},{"text":"Tell him to stop dwelling on the past","then":"Rohit closes up, and the conversation ends there."},{"text":"Say at least his was worse than yours","then":"Rohit's jaw tightens. 'That's not the point.'"},{"text":"Hug him and say it's behind you both now","then":"He hugs you back, and the sting goes unspoken again."}],"why":"Tending your own wounds, together, is how co-parents stop the cycle for their child."}],"debrief":"Naming the shared inheritance, then choosing differently together, lets co-parents break the cycle as a team.","relearn":"When a co-parent defends the old harsh way, invite them to choose differently with you, for your child."},
  {"id":"bc-1204","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"guilt to action","hook":"A wave of guilt hits as you realise how much of your harsh upbringing you've been repeating.","steps":[{"prompt":"Lying awake, the guilt keeps growing. What do you tell yourself?","options":[{"text":"I can't undo it, but I can change one thing tomorrow","then":"The guilt doesn't vanish, but it starts to feel like fuel.","best":true},{"text":"I've failed my kids completely","then":"The thought pins you down, and you wake up exhausted and hopeless."},{"text":"Everyone parents like this. It's really not so bad","then":"The guilt quiets, and so does any reason to change."},{"text":"Tomorrow I'll change everything at once","then":"It feels hopeful now. Tomorrow will be very long."}],"why":"Guilt that turns into one small step helps. Guilt that spirals into 'I've failed' freezes you."},{"prompt":"Next morning, your son dawdles over his shoes and you're running late. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Try one new thing: a calm, clear 'shoes on now'","then":"It feels awkward, but he pulls his shoes on without tears.","best":true},{"text":"Yell as usual. Change can start another day","then":"He rushes, crying, and last night's guilt comes back doubled."},{"text":"Tie his shoes yourself to avoid any scene","then":"You're on time, but you've dodged the moment rather than changed it."},{"text":"Stay calm, then scold yourself all day for past mornings","then":"The morning goes well, but you spend the day replaying old ones."}],"why":"One small, doable change is how a new pattern actually starts."},{"prompt":"A few days later, you lose your temper and shout at him over homework. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Repair with your son, and try the calm way tomorrow","then":"He accepts your sorry, and the next morning goes a little easier.","best":true},{"text":"Decide changing is pointless for you","then":"You give up, and the mornings go back to shouting."},{"text":"Tell yourself you're a terrible father","then":"The guilt spirals, and nothing gets repaired."},{"text":"Try the calm way tomorrow, and skip the awkward sorry","then":"The mornings improve, but he still carries the shout from that day."}],"why":"Slips are part of change. Repair and keep going, instead of spiralling."}],"debrief":"Guilt that turns into one small change helps; guilt that spirals into \"I've failed\" freezes you.","relearn":"Let guilt about repeating the old way move you to one small change, not a spiral into \"I've failed.\""},
  {"id":"bc-1205","type":"match","hook":"Match each harsh inherited message to the truer one to raise your child on.","pairs":[{"left":"\"Children should be seen, not heard\"","right":"Your voice and feelings matter to me"},{"left":"\"Fear keeps kids in line\"","right":"Respect grows from trust, not fear"},{"left":"\"Big boys don't cry\"","right":"All your feelings are welcome here"},{"left":"\"A slap teaches them\"","right":"Calm limits teach far better than pain"},{"left":"\"Affection spoils a child\"","right":"Warmth makes a child stronger, not weaker"}],"relearn":"Each harsh message has a truer one: voice matters, respect from trust, feelings welcome, warmth strengthens.","persona":"any","source":"truer messages","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1206","type":"match","hook":"Match each part of your inheritance to whether you'd keep it or leave it, and why.","pairs":[{"left":"Family closeness","right":"Keep: it nourished you"},{"left":"Hitting when angry","right":"Leave: it harms and doesn't work"},{"left":"Bedtime stories","right":"Keep: warmth your child deserves too"},{"left":"Shaming when you erred","right":"Leave: it wounds self-worth"},{"left":"Valuing honesty","right":"Keep: a value worth passing on"}],"relearn":"Keep the closeness, stories and honesty that nourished you; leave the hitting and shaming that harm.","persona":"any","source":"keep-leave reasons","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1207","type":"match","hook":"Match each old reflex Suresh notices to the gentler response he's learning instead.","pairs":[{"left":"Raising a hand when angry","right":"Step back and breathe first"},{"left":"Ruling by fear","right":"Build trust over time"},{"left":"Going silent and cold","right":"Say the warm words out loud"},{"left":"Mocking tears","right":"Name the feeling with him"},{"left":"Comparing to cousins","right":"Praise his own effort"}],"relearn":"Each old reflex has a gentler swap: breathe instead of hit, trust over fear, warmth over silence, naming over mocking.","persona":"Suresh","source":"reflex to response","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1208","type":"match","hook":"Match each step of stopping the cycle to what it does.","pairs":[{"left":"Noticing the old pattern","right":"Gives you the power to choose"},{"left":"Holding compassion for your parents","right":"Frees you from blame to act"},{"left":"Keeping the good, leaving the harm","right":"Sorts your inheritance with care"},{"left":"Tending your own old wounds","right":"Stops them leaking onto your child"},{"left":"Choosing one new pattern today","right":"Bends the cycle for the next generation"}],"relearn":"Noticing, compassion, sorting keep-from-leave, tending wounds and one new choice are the steps that bend the cycle.","persona":"any","source":"steps that stop it","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1209","type":"match","hook":"Match each belief about questioning your upbringing to whether it's a freeing truth or a stuck myth.","pairs":[{"left":"\"I can love them and still change\"","right":"Freeing truth"},{"left":"\"Questioning betrays my parents\"","right":"Stuck myth"},{"left":"\"New ways are learnable\"","right":"A freeing truth, slowly"},{"left":"\"This is just who I am\"","right":"Stuck myth, and untrue"},{"left":"\"I keep the good and leave the harm\"","right":"Both at once, freely"}],"relearn":"Loving and changing, and learnable new ways, are freeing truths; betrayal-guilt and \"just who I am\" keep you stuck.","persona":"any","source":"free truth or stuck myth","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1210","type":"match","hook":"Match each warm thing from a childhood to the harsh thing it can replace.","pairs":[{"left":"A goodnight hug","right":"Replaces cold, silent nights"},{"left":"Asking about their day","right":"Replaces being ignored"},{"left":"Praising effort","right":"Replaces constant comparing"},{"left":"Letting them say no","right":"Replaces forced obedience"},{"left":"Owning your mistakes","right":"Replaces never apologising"}],"relearn":"Hugs replace cold nights, asking replaces ignoring, praise replaces comparing, an allowed-no replaces force, apology replaces silence.","persona":"any","source":"warm replaces harsh","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1211","type":"match","hook":"Match each thing a parent might say to whether it shames the parent or holds compassion.","pairs":[{"left":"\"You're a bad parent for slipping\"","right":"Shames the parent"},{"left":"\"You were raised that way; you can change\"","right":"Holds compassion"},{"left":"\"You've already ruined your child\"","right":"Shames, and isn't true"},{"left":"\"Your worry means you're trying\"","right":"Holds compassion, warmly"},{"left":"\"One hard day doesn't define you\"","right":"Holds compassion, gently"}],"relearn":"\"Bad parent\" and \"ruined\" shame; \"you can change,\" \"you're trying\" and \"one hard day\" hold compassion.","persona":"Priya","source":"shame or compassion","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1212","type":"match","hook":"Match each inherited fear-script to the trust-script that replaces it.","pairs":[{"left":"\"Wait till your father gets home\"","right":"\"Let's sort this out together now\""},{"left":"\"Because I said so\"","right":"\"Here's why this matters\""},{"left":"\"Stop crying or I'll give you a reason\"","right":"\"It's okay to cry; I'm here\""},{"left":"\"You'll shame this whole family\"","right":"\"You can always come to me\""},{"left":"\"Children don't argue with elders\"","right":"\"You can disagree respectfully\""}],"relearn":"Fear-scripts like \"wait till your father\" and \"because I said so\" have trust-scripts that keep the child close.","persona":"any","source":"fear to trust scripts","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1213","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that just repeat the harsh inheritance unquestioned.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'll keep the warmth and leave the hitting\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"A slap never hurt me, so it's fine for him\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"I can choose my own patterns now\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"This is just how we've always done it\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"I love my childhood; I'll do some things differently\"","trick":false}],"why":"\"A slap never hurt me\" and \"how we've always done it\" repeat the harm; the rest sort it with care.","relearn":"Repeating the old way unquestioned passes on harm; keep the good and choose your own patterns instead.","persona":"Suresh","source":"repeat unquestioned","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1214","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two beliefs that keep a parent stuck in the old pattern.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"New ways are learnable, slowly\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"I'm doomed to repeat my parents\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Noticing the pattern is the first step\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"This is just who I am, I can't change\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"I choose what stops with me\"","trick":false}],"why":"\"I'm doomed\" and \"I can't change\" keep the pattern running; the inheritance isn't destiny and new ways are learnable.","relearn":"\"Doomed\" and \"can't change\" keep you stuck; noticing the pattern and choosing new ways frees you.","persona":"any","source":"stuck beliefs","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1215","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two replies that cave to the old harsh way under family pressure.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'm choosing some things differently, kindly\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You're right, maybe I should be harder on him\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"My child can be heard and still respectful\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"I suppose a little fear did keep me in line\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"I'll keep what I loved and leave the rest\"","trick":false}],"why":"\"I should be harder\" and \"fear kept me in line\" cave to the old way; the rest hold the gentler path.","relearn":"Under pressure, caving repeats fear and harshness; you can honour elders and hold your gentler path.","persona":"Suresh","source":"caving replies","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1216","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two messages that pass shame, not love, to a child.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Your feelings matter to me\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You're the shame of this family\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"You can always come to me\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Why can't you be like your cousin?\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Mistakes are how you learn\"","trick":false}],"why":"\"Shame of this family\" and \"be like your cousin\" pass shame and comparison; the others build a child's worth and trust.","relearn":"Shaming and comparing wound a child's worth; messages of worth and welcome are what's worth passing on.","persona":"any","source":"shame messages","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1217","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two things a parent does that bury their own old wounds instead of tending them.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Naming what hurt them as a child","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"I never think about my childhood\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Noticing what triggers the old anger","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Snapping, then blaming the kids for it","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Reaching out to a counsellor for support","trick":false}],"why":"\"I never think about it\" and blaming the kids bury the wound; naming, noticing triggers and getting support tend it.","relearn":"Burying old pain leaks it onto your child; naming it, knowing your triggers and seeking support tends it.","persona":"any","source":"bury the wound","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1218","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two reactions that let the old anger run straight onto the child.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Stepping into another room to cool down","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Letting the harsh words out because they're there","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Taking a few slow breaths first","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Hitting now and feeling sorry later","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Naming the anger out loud, then pausing","trick":false}],"why":"Letting the words out and hitting land the surge on the child; stepping away, breathing and naming keep it safe.","relearn":"When old anger surges, the gap between feeling and acting is yours; step away, breathe, name it before you act.","persona":"Suresh","source":"anger runs onto child","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1219","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two thoughts that treat questioning your upbringing as betrayal.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I can love them and still change\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"Doing it differently insults my parents\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Keeping the good isn't all-or-nothing\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Changing means they did everything wrong\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Growth honours what I learned the hard way\"","trick":false}],"why":"\"It insults them\" and \"they did everything wrong\" frame change as betrayal; choosing differently is growth.","relearn":"Questioning a pattern isn't betrayal; you can love your parents, keep the good and still choose differently.","persona":"Suresh","source":"betrayal thoughts","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1220","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two myths hiding among truths about how you were raised.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Awareness is the first step to change","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Hitting is just normal, harmless discipline","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"You can keep the good and leave the harm","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Fear from a parent is the same as respect","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Most harsh parents were raised that way","trick":false}],"why":"\"Hitting is harmless\" and \"fear is respect\" are the myths; awareness, keep-and-leave and compassion are the truths.","relearn":"Hitting harms and fear isn't respect; awareness, sorting your inheritance and compassion are the real truths.","persona":"any","source":"myths among truths","cat":"how-you-were-raised"},
  {"id":"bc-1221","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"too soft","hook":"Your father is visiting and watches you settle your children's squabble without raising your voice.","setup":"He waits until the kids run off.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your father says: \"You're too soft. I raised you with a firm hand, and you turned out fine.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You did your best. I'm doing some things differently.\"","then":"He folds his arms. \"Differently how? Letting them run wild?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right, Baba. I'll be firmer from now on.\"","then":"He nods, satisfied. \"They need to know who's in charge.\""},{"text":"\"Fine? I was terrified of you my whole childhood.\"","then":"His face goes still. \"Terrified? Of your own father?\""},{"text":"\"Times have changed, Baba. It's not a big deal.\"","then":"He shakes his head. \"Children haven't changed.\""}],"why":"Honouring your father while owning your choice holds your path without attacking him."},{"prompt":"Your father says: \"In my day, children feared their fathers. That kept them in line.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'd rather they trust me. They still have rules.\"","then":"He grunts. \"Rules without fear? I'd like to see that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe a little fear is good for them.\"","then":"He nods firmly. \"Now you're talking sense.\""},{"text":"\"That's why none of us could ever talk to you.\"","then":"He goes quiet, stung. \"I did what I thought was right.\""},{"text":"\"Let's not get into this again, Baba.\"","then":"He shrugs. \"You never want to hear it.\""}],"why":"Respect grows from trust, not fear, and firm rules still hold. Saying so keeps both."},{"prompt":"Your father points and says: \"See? Your son is jumping on the sofa again. Go on, show him.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Off the sofa, please. Feet on the floor.\"","then":"Your son climbs down. Your father raises his eyebrows. \"Hm. He listened.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Get down now, or you'll get a slap!\"","then":"Your son freezes. Your father nods. \"That's more like it.\""},{"text":"\"Let him be, Baba. He's only playing.\"","then":"Your father throws up his hands. \"And this is why he never listens.\""},{"text":"\"Beta, why do you always make me look bad?\"","then":"Your son climbs down, confused. Your father smirks. \"Guilt works too, I see.\""}],"why":"A calm, clear limit shows firm and kind in action, not just in words."},{"prompt":"At the door, your father says: \"One day you'll see. Children respect only what they fear.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I learned love from you too, Baba. I'll pass that on.\"","then":"He pauses at the door, softer. \"Hm. Maybe you did.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe you're right. I'll think about it.\"","then":"He smiles. \"Think hard, then.\""},{"text":"\"Honestly, Baba, you're the reason I'm doing it differently.\"","then":"He stiffens. \"So I'm the villain now.\""},{"text":"\"Okay, Baba. Safe journey home.\"","then":"He nods and leaves with the last word."}],"why":"Ending warmly, while still holding your path, honours him without giving it up."}],"relearn":"Honouring your parent while owning your own gentler choice holds your path without attacking how they raised you."},
  {"id":"bc-1222","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"why don't you hit me","hook":"Your eight-year-old, Tara, has been hearing Dadi's stories about how strict her own papa was.","setup":"At bedtime, Tara looks up at you, curious.","steps":[{"prompt":"Tara asks: \"Why don't you hit me when I'm bad, like Dadi's papa did?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Because hurting you isn't how I want to teach you.\"","then":"Tara thinks. \"So how do you teach me, then?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe I should, like they did to me.\"","then":"Tara's eyes go wide. \"Would you really?\""},{"text":"\"Dadi's papa was wrong, and so was Dadi.\"","then":"Tara frowns. \"Dadi is wrong? But she's Dadi.\""},{"text":"\"Because you're a good girl who never needs it.\"","then":"Tara looks worried. \"What if I'm bad one day, though?\""}],"why":"Telling her plainly you choose teaching over hurting models the new pattern out loud."},{"prompt":"Tara asks: \"What happens to me when I do something really wrong?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We'll talk about it, and there'll be a fair consequence.\"","then":"Tara nods. \"Like no TV when I didn't do my homework?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Nothing. You're perfect just as you are.\"","then":"Tara giggles. \"Even when I broke the lamp?\""},{"text":"\"If you're really bad, we'll see about hitting.\"","then":"Tara's smile fades. \"So you might?\""},{"text":"\"I'll just shout a little, so you remember.\"","then":"Tara wrinkles her nose. \"Shouting is scary too.\""}],"why":"Firm and kind means a clear, fair consequence, explained, not fear."},{"prompt":"Tara asks: \"Did your papa hit you when you were little?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Sometimes. It made me scared, so I chose differently.\"","then":"Tara hugs you. \"I'm glad you chose differently.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"He did, and he was a very bad man.\"","then":"Tara's eyes widen. \"Your papa is bad? But I love him.\""},{"text":"\"Yes, and it made me the strong person I am.\"","then":"Tara looks puzzled. \"So hitting is good, then?\""},{"text":"\"Let's not talk about that now. It's time to sleep.\"","then":"Tara yawns. \"Okay. But I still want to know.\""}],"why":"A short, honest answer shows her the cycle can stop, without handing her the full weight."}],"relearn":"Telling your child plainly that you choose teaching over hurting models the new pattern out loud."},
  {"id":"bc-1223","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"make him weak","hook":"Your son falls off his cycle and cries openly, with relatives watching.","setup":"An aunt clicks her tongue as you hold him.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your aunt says: \"Let him cry like this and you'll make him weak.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Feeling things will make him stronger, not weaker.\"","then":"She shakes her head. \"Boys in our day didn't cry like this.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. Stop crying now, beta. Be a man.\"","then":"Your son gulps back his sobs. Your aunt nods. \"That's better.\""},{"text":"\"Nobody asked for your opinion, Aunty.\"","then":"Her face tightens. \"I'm only trying to help!\""},{"text":"\"He's just little. He'll toughen up when he's older.\"","then":"She nods, satisfied. \"The sooner, the better.\""}],"why":"Welcoming his tears leaves behind the 'don't cry' script that teaches boys to hide pain."},{"prompt":"Your aunt adds: \"Out in the world, boys who cry get teased.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Tears don't make him weak, here or anywhere.\"","then":"She pauses. \"Even at school, in front of other boys?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe. I'll teach him to hide it at school.\"","then":"She smiles. \"That's all I'm saying.\""},{"text":"\"If anyone teases him, I'll deal with them myself.\"","then":"She laughs. \"You'll follow him around forever?\""},{"text":"\"I'll let him cry at home, just not in public.\"","then":"She nods. \"At least that's something.\""}],"why":"Tears are okay wherever he is. Knowing that helps a child stay steady out in the world."},{"prompt":"Later, your son whispers: \"Are boys allowed to cry?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes. Everyone cries. It's how feelings come out.\"","then":"He sniffs, then smiles a little. \"Even grown-ups?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Only a little, and not in front of people.\"","then":"He nods seriously. \"Okay. I'll hide it next time.\""},{"text":"\"No, beta. Aunty is right.\"","then":"His lip trembles. \"But I was hurt.\""},{"text":"\"You're my brave boy. Brave boys don't need to.\"","then":"He wipes his face hard. \"I'll be brave, then.\""}],"why":"Telling him plainly that tears are okay leaves the old 'boys don't cry' script behind."}],"relearn":"Welcoming a child's tears, even when relatives call it weakness, leaves behind the \"don't cry\" script."},
  {"id":"bc-1224","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"both raised with the belt","hook":"Your partner Imran thinks you're overthinking how to discipline your daughter.","setup":"He brings it up while you fold laundry.","steps":[{"prompt":"Imran says: \"We were both raised with the belt and we're fine. Why change?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We made it, and I'm grateful. Let's choose gentler, together.\"","then":"Imran pauses. \"Gentler how? She needs discipline.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. Let's raise her the way we were raised.\"","then":"Imran nods. \"Good. No need to overthink it.\""},{"text":"\"Fine? You still flinch when your father raises his voice.\"","then":"Imran goes stiff. \"That's a low blow.\""},{"text":"\"I'll do it my way, and you do it yours.\"","then":"Imran shrugs. \"She'll be confused, but fine.\""}],"why":"Naming what you both went through, then inviting a gentler choice, makes you a team."},{"prompt":"Imran says: \"She's been hitting her cousin lately. That needs a firm hand.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Firm, yes. We stop her, calmly, every time, and explain.\"","then":"Imran thinks. \"Every time, though? That's a lot of patience.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Then one smack, so she learns hitting hurts.\"","then":"Imran nods. \"Exactly what my father would say.\""},{"text":"\"She's only little. She'll grow out of it.\"","then":"Imran rolls his eyes. \"And until then, the cousin suffers?\""},{"text":"\"Let's just keep her away from her cousin for a while.\"","then":"Imran shrugs. \"And when they meet at Eid?\""}],"why":"Firm and kind stops the behaviour and teaches, without hitting to teach not to hit."},{"prompt":"Imran admits: \"Honestly, when she screams, I hear my father and I want to shout.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Me too. Let's tag in for each other when it happens.\"","then":"Imran exhales. \"A tag team. I like that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Then you need to control yourself better.\"","then":"Imran bristles. \"Easy for you to say.\""},{"text":"\"Shouting is fine. At least it isn't the belt.\"","then":"Imran looks relieved. \"True. Shouting it is, then.\""},{"text":"\"Just count to ten when it starts. It works for me.\"","then":"Imran sighs. \"I've tried. I never get to ten.\""}],"why":"Owning your own triggers and backing each other up lets you both break the old reflex."},{"prompt":"Imran asks: \"What if my parents see us doing it differently and say something?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We thank them and hold our line together.\"","then":"Imran smiles. \"Together. Okay.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Then we do it their way when they visit.\"","then":"Imran nods. \"Less fuss that way.\""},{"text":"\"Then we tell them exactly what they did wrong.\"","then":"Imran winces. \"That'll go well at dinner.\""},{"text":"\"You handle your parents. I'll handle mine.\"","then":"Imran hesitates. \"So we're not a team there?\""}],"why":"Honouring elders while backing each other up lets co-parents hold a new pattern."}],"relearn":"Naming the shared inheritance, then inviting a gentler choice together, lets co-parents break the cycle as a team."},
  {"id":"bc-1225","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"harsh words slipped","hook":"Your father's harsh words just came out of your mouth at your daughter, and her face fell.","setup":"Once you've both calmed down, you find her on her bed.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your daughter mumbles: \"I don't want to talk to you.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's okay. I'm sorry I spoke to you like that.\"","then":"She peeks out from the pillow. \"You were really loud.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Don't sulk. It was nothing.\"","then":"She turns to the wall. \"It wasn't nothing.\""},{"text":"\"Fine. Come find me when you're ready to behave.\"","then":"She pulls the pillow over her head. \"Go away.\""},{"text":"\"Want to go get ice cream? Let's forget it.\"","then":"She hesitates, then shakes her head. \"You were really mean.\""}],"why":"Starting with an apology, and no pressure, shows her it's safe to come back to you."},{"prompt":"After a pause, your daughter says: \"You said I'm useless, like I'm always bad.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That wasn't okay, and it wasn't your fault.\"","then":"Her eyes fill. \"It felt like you meant it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I only said it because you made me angry.\"","then":"She curls up. \"So it's my fault.\""},{"text":"\"You know I didn't mean it, so let's drop it.\"","then":"She frowns. \"Then why did you say it?\""},{"text":"\"I'm sorry, but you did spill paint on everything.\"","then":"She goes quiet. \"So I am bad.\""}],"why":"A clean apology owns what you said and makes clear it wasn't her fault."},{"prompt":"Your daughter asks: \"Where do those mean words even come from?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Old words from my papa slipped out. I'm working on it.\"","then":"She thinks about this. \"Your papa said mean things too?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Grown-ups just get angry. That's life.\"","then":"She shrugs sadly. \"Grown-ups are scary sometimes.\""},{"text":"\"They come out when you don't listen to me.\"","then":"She pulls away. \"I do listen!\""},{"text":"\"It doesn't matter where. It won't ever happen again.\"","then":"She looks doubtful. \"You said that last time.\""}],"why":"A simple, honest reason, without excuses, shows her the pattern isn't about her."},{"prompt":"Your daughter asks: \"What if you say it again?\"","options":[{"text":"\"If I start to, I'll step away and come back calmer.\"","then":"She leans against you. \"And you'll say sorry after?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I promise I'll never, ever say it again.\"","then":"She holds out her little finger. \"Pinky promise?\""},{"text":"\"Then just stay out of my way when I'm angry.\"","then":"She nods slowly. \"I'll be quiet, then.\""},{"text":"\"I'll try. Now give me a hug and it's all fine.\"","then":"She hugs you stiffly. \"Okay.\""}],"why":"A real plan, not a perfect promise, is what she can trust."}],"relearn":"A clean repair owns what you said and tells your child it wasn't their fault, breaking the no-apology pattern."},
  {"id":"bc-1226","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"role-play","persona":"Priya","source":"terrified I'll repeat it","hook":"Deepak, a dad from your son's class, is terrified he'll raise his kid as harshly as he was raised.","setup":"You're both waiting at the school gate.","steps":[{"prompt":"Deepak says: \"My father's temper is in me. I can feel it.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Worrying about it means you're already breaking it.\"","then":"Deepak looks up. \"Breaking it? I feel like I'm failing.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It's in the blood. Nothing to be done, really.\"","then":"Deepak's shoulders sag. \"That's what I was afraid of.\""},{"text":"\"You seem calm to me. I'm sure you're nothing like him.\"","then":"He shakes his head. \"You haven't seen me at bedtime.\""},{"text":"\"Just never lift a hand. That's all it takes.\"","then":"Deepak frowns. \"It's not only hands. It's the words too.\""}],"why":"His worry shows he's already noticing the pattern, which is where change begins."},{"prompt":"Deepak says: \"Last week I shouted so loud my son hid under the table.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's hard. Did you get to go back and make it right?\"","then":"He's quiet for a moment. \"I didn't know how.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Well, that's not okay, Deepak.\"","then":"Deepak goes red. \"I know. I shouldn't have said anything.\""},{"text":"\"Once in a while is normal. Don't worry.\"","then":"He nods, but his face stays tight. \"It wasn't once in a while.\""},{"text":"\"Next time, just walk away before it gets that far.\"","then":"Deepak nods. \"And what about last week?\""}],"why":"Every parent slips. Pointing him to repair gives him a way back to his son."},{"prompt":"Deepak asks: \"Do you think I need help? Or is that too much?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Asking is caring. Tele-MANAS 14416 can help.\"","then":"Deepak takes out his phone. \"Tell me the number again.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Help is for people with real problems.\"","then":"Deepak looks down. \"Right. I'll manage.\""},{"text":"\"Just read a parenting book. That's enough.\"","then":"He nods slowly. \"A book. Okay.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe wait and see if it gets worse first.\"","then":"Deepak nods. \"I'll wait, then.\""}],"why":"Reaching for help to parent more gently is caring and brave, never shameful."}],"relearn":"Reminding a frightened parent that their worry itself is the cycle bending offers compassion, not condemnation."},
  {"id":"bc-1227","cat":"how-you-were-raised","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"children don't get a say","hook":"At a family lunch, your uncle laughs when you ask your son which dish he'd like.","setup":"The whole table turns to look.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your uncle says: \"Children don't get a say in our house.\"","options":[{"text":"\"In ours, his voice matters. He still respects his elders.\"","then":"Your uncle raises his eyebrows. \"Respects his elders? We'll see.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. Just eat what's served, beta.\"","then":"Your uncle nods, pleased. \"That's how it should be.\""},{"text":"\"That's why your kids never talk to you.\"","then":"The table goes silent. Your uncle glares. \"What did you say?\""},{"text":"\"It's just food, Mamaji. Let's not make it a big thing.\"","then":"He chuckles. \"Today food, tomorrow he'll run the house.\""}],"why":"Standing by his voice, calmly, shows being heard and being respectful go together."},{"prompt":"Your uncle says: \"Give children a say now, and they'll talk back later.\"","options":[{"text":"\"He can disagree politely. I'd rather he tells me things.\"","then":"He shakes his head. \"In my time, we just listened.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe. I'll keep it to small things.\"","then":"He smiles. \"Small things become big things.\""},{"text":"\"Better than being scared silent, like your kids.\"","then":"His face darkens. \"Leave my kids out of this.\""},{"text":"\"He only talks back when he's tired. It's nothing.\"","then":"Your uncle laughs. \"See? It's already started.\""}],"why":"A child who's heard at home is more likely to come to you with what matters."},{"prompt":"On the way home, your son whispers: \"Should I stop talking when Mamaji is there?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No. Speak kindly, and your voice matters.\"","then":"He sits up a little straighter. \"Okay. Even about food?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Yes, just around him, to keep the peace.\"","then":"He nods and goes quiet. \"Okay. I'll be quiet.\""},{"text":"\"Ignore Mamaji. He doesn't know anything.\"","then":"He giggles, then looks unsure. \"Can I say that to him?\""},{"text":"\"Only speak if someone asks you something.\"","then":"He deflates a little. \"So only when asked.\""}],"why":"Telling him his voice matters, even around elders, leaves 'seen, not heard' behind."}],"relearn":"Standing by your child's voice, calmly, even when elders mock it leaves \"seen, not heard\" behind for good."},
  {"id":"bc-1260","cat":"the-repair","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Divya","source":"buying gift not repair","relearn":"A toy or treat can't replace the words; a real repair owns what happened and reconnects.","hook":"\"I'll just buy him a treat and we'll be fine; I don't need to say anything.\"","myth":{"un":"I'll just buy him a treat and we'll be fine; I don't need to say anything.","re":"A gift can soften the day, but it isn't a repair; the child needs you to name it and reconnect, not to be bought off.","why":"Repair is words and warmth, not a bribe."}},
  {"id":"bc-1261","cat":"the-repair","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Suresh","source":"silent treatment not repair","relearn":"Going quiet for days isn't keeping the peace; it leaves the rupture open. Reconnecting once calm is the repair.","hook":"\"If I just go quiet for a few days, it'll blow over on its own.\"","myth":{"un":"If I just go quiet for a few days, it'll blow over on its own.","re":"Silence doesn't heal a rupture; it leaves the child anxious and guessing. Going back and reconnecting once you're calm is what repairs it.","why":"Withdrawal leaves the hurt open."}},
  {"id":"bc-1262","cat":"the-repair","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"repair shows weakness myth","relearn":"Owning a slip in front of your child is strength, not weakness; it teaches them that mistakes can be put right.","hook":"\"Admitting I was wrong in front of my child makes me look weak.\"","myth":{"un":"Admitting I was wrong in front of my child makes me look weak.","re":"Owning a mistake to your child is a sign of strength; it shows them how to be accountable and that being wrong can be put right.","why":"Accountability models strength, not weakness."}},
  {"id":"bc-1263","cat":"the-repair","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Divya","source":"baby too young for repair","relearn":"Even a baby reads your tone and touch; a soft voice and a cuddle after a hard moment are a real repair.","hook":"\"My baby is too small to understand, so there's no point repairing after I snap.\"","myth":{"un":"My baby is too small to understand, so there's no point repairing after I snap.","re":"A baby can't follow words, but they feel your tone and touch; a calm voice and a gentle cuddle after a hard moment soothe and reconnect them.","why":"Tone and touch repair before words can."}},
  {"id":"bc-1264","cat":"the-repair","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"sorry but undoes it","relearn":"A 'sorry, but...' that tacks on blame undoes the apology; a clean sorry owns your part and stops there.","hook":"\"As long as I say sorry, adding 'but you provoked me' is fine.\"","myth":{"un":"As long as I say sorry, adding 'but you provoked me' is fine.","re":"A 'sorry, but you provoked me' cancels the apology and hands the child the blame; a clean sorry owns your reaction and stops there.","why":"'Sorry, but' transfers the fault back."}},
  {"id":"bc-1265","cat":"the-repair","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Suresh","source":"father never apologised","relearn":"Your parents may never have apologised to you; choosing to repair with your own child is how the cycle changes.","hook":"\"My father never once said sorry to me, and I turned out fine; why should I?\"","myth":{"un":"My father never once said sorry to me, and I turned out fine; why should I?","re":"That your father never repaired is part of the inheritance you can set down; choosing to own a slip with your own child is exactly how the pattern changes.","why":"Repairing is how you break the cycle."}},
  {"id":"bc-1266","cat":"the-repair","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Vikram","source":"explaining is not repairing","relearn":"Explaining why you lost your temper isn't the same as repairing; the child needs ownership, not your reasons.","hook":"\"If I explain that I was stressed about work, that counts as repairing.\"","myth":{"un":"If I explain that I was stressed about work, that counts as repairing.","re":"Naming you were stressed can help, but an explanation isn't a repair until you also own the snap and tell the child it wasn't their fault.","why":"Reasons aren't ownership."}},
  {"id":"bc-1267","cat":"the-repair","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Divya","source":"guilt is the repair myth","relearn":"Drowning in guilt doesn't help your child; turning that feeling into a calm, simple repair does.","hook":"\"Feeling terribly guilty for hours afterward shows I'm making up for it.\"","myth":{"un":"Feeling terribly guilty for hours afterward shows I'm making up for it.","re":"Guilt that spirals helps no one and can pull you further from your child; what makes up for a slip is a calm, simple repair, then letting it go.","why":"Repair, not endless guilt, mends it."}},
  {"id":"bc-1268","cat":"the-repair","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"repair in front of relatives","hook":"At a family gathering, you snapped at your son, and a room full of relatives saw it.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your son stands there, eyes wet, and everyone is watching. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Kneel beside him, and quietly say you're sorry","then":"He sniffs and nods. The room's chatter slowly picks back up.","best":true},{"text":"Skip it. Saying sorry here would look weak","then":"He slips away to a corner, and carries it alone."},{"text":"Scold him more so no one thinks you're soft","then":"His tears spill over, and an aunt nods approvingly."},{"text":"Take him outside and tell him to stop crying first","then":"He tries to stop, but it only makes his chest heave."}],"why":"A repair can be quiet even in a full room. He needs the ownership, not an audience."},{"prompt":"Over tea, your cousin says he never says sorry to his kids, or they'd lose respect. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say owning a mistake is how kids learn respect","then":"Your cousin shrugs, and the talk moves on.","best":true},{"text":"Change the subject quickly to hide your embarrassment","then":"The moment passes, but you feel small."},{"text":"Snap at your cousin to mind his own business","then":"The room goes quiet again, this time for a different reason."},{"text":"Agree, and say a father's word should be final","then":"Your cousin nods, satisfied, and your son looks down."}],"why":"Standing by repair calmly shows your son, and the room, that apologising is strength."},{"prompt":"On the way home, your son quietly asks if you're still angry with him. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say no. The snapping was your mistake, not his","then":"He relaxes into his seat. 'I thought I'd ruined the party,' he says.","best":true},{"text":"Say only if he misbehaves again","then":"He nods and sits very still for the rest of the ride."},{"text":"Say let's forget it ever happened","then":"He goes quiet, still unsure where he stands."},{"text":"Say no, and buy him an ice cream on the way","then":"He enjoys the ice cream, still wondering what he did wrong."}],"why":"Making clear the mistake was yours lifts the blame he might carry."},{"prompt":"Next month, at another gathering, you feel the old irritation rise with your son. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Notice it early and take a short walk outside","then":"You come back calmer, and your son is still happily playing.","best":true},{"text":"Hold it in until you get home","then":"You're tense all evening, and it spills over on the way home."},{"text":"Tell him sharply to behave before it gets worse","then":"He goes quiet, and your mother raises an eyebrow."},{"text":"Keep him beside you all evening so nothing happens","then":"He sulks, missing his cousins, and your irritation grows."}],"why":"Catching the rise early and stepping away keeps the next moment from needing repair."}],"debrief":"A repair can be quiet and private even in a full room; your child needs the ownership, not an audience.","relearn":"You can repair quietly even with relatives nearby; don't let pride cancel it."},
  {"id":"bc-1269","cat":"the-repair","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"repair timing mid-meltdown","hook":"You've just snapped, and now your toddler is mid-meltdown, screaming on the floor.","steps":[{"prompt":"She's too upset to hear anything yet. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Stay near her, calm, and let the storm pass","then":"Her screams slowly turn to hiccupping sobs.","best":true},{"text":"Demand she calm down so you can say sorry","then":"She screams louder, and your apology gets lost in the noise."},{"text":"Walk away and let her cry it out alone","then":"Her cries go on, and she looks for you through her tears."},{"text":"Keep saying sorry over and over while she screams","then":"She can't take it in, and your voice adds to the noise."}],"why":"Repair needs a calm moment to land. Staying near and steady helps her get there."},{"prompt":"A few minutes later, she's quiet but won't look at you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Sit close, and gently say sorry you shouted","then":"She crawls into your lap and buries her face in your shoulder.","best":true},{"text":"Tell her she was being very naughty","then":"Her lip wobbles, and she turns away again."},{"text":"Hand her a biscuit and move on","then":"She takes it, but keeps her distance."},{"text":"Say sorry, then explain at length why you snapped","then":"She fidgets and wriggles away halfway through."}],"why":"Once she's calm, a short, simple sorry reaches her. That's when repair lands."},{"prompt":"That night, you wonder whether the snapping has hurt her for good. What do you tell yourself?","options":[{"text":"One slip isn't the end. Coming back is what counts","then":"The knot in your chest loosens, and you finally sleep.","best":true},{"text":"I've scarred her. I'm a bad mother","then":"You lie awake, replaying it, too drained to rest."},{"text":"Toddlers forget. It doesn't matter","then":"You brush it off, and never look at what set you off."},{"text":"I'll never raise my voice again, whatever happens","then":"The promise feels good now. It adds pressure to every hard moment ahead."}],"why":"One slip doesn't ruin anything. Repair, not perfection, is what builds a secure child."}],"debrief":"Repair needs a calm moment to land; stay present, let the meltdown pass, then go back and reconnect.","relearn":"Repair lands once you're both calm; wait for the lull, then reconnect."},
  {"id":"bc-1270","cat":"the-repair","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"child made to comfort parent","hook":"After you snap at your daughter, you start crying, and she rushes over to console you.","steps":[{"prompt":"She pats your arm and says, 'Don't cry.' What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Steady yourself, then turn the care back to her","then":"You wipe your eyes. She looks relieved when you ask how she's feeling.","best":true},{"text":"Let her comfort you and call it made up","then":"She hugs you hard. She's comforted you, but no one has comforted her."},{"text":"Tell her it's her fault you're this upset","then":"She freezes, and her little face fills with guilt."},{"text":"Keep crying and tell her how awful you feel","then":"She looks frightened, and tries harder to make you feel better."}],"why":"A child shouldn't have to manage your feelings. Steadying yourself keeps her the child."},{"prompt":"A little later, you sit with your daughter. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say sorry for snapping, and that it wasn't her fault","then":"She leans against you. 'You were scary,' she says, 'and then sad.'","best":true},{"text":"Thank her for being so grown-up about it","then":"She glows with pride, and decides looking after you is her job."},{"text":"Say you're just very tired these days","then":"She nods seriously and promises to be extra good."},{"text":"Say sorry, and ask her to promise she won't worry","then":"She promises, but watches your face closely all evening."}],"why":"The repair belongs to her. Owning it, and saying it's not her fault, gives it to her."},{"prompt":"That night, guilt about crying in front of her keeps you awake. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Talk it through with your partner or a friend","then":"Saying it aloud helps, and you sleep a little better.","best":true},{"text":"Decide you're too broken to be her parent","then":"The thought loops, and you wake up exhausted."},{"text":"Promise yourself you'll never cry near her again","then":"The promise hardens into another rule about hiding feelings."},{"text":"Wake her up to check she's really okay","then":"She blinks, sleepy and confused, and asks if you're sad again."}],"why":"Carrying your own guilt, with adult support, keeps it off your child's small shoulders."}],"debrief":"A child shouldn't manage your feelings; steady yourself, then make the repair about reconnecting with them.","relearn":"Repair shouldn't make your child comfort you; carry your own guilt, and let the apology be theirs."},
  {"id":"bc-1271","cat":"the-repair","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"broke a promise repair","hook":"You promised to take your daughter to the park on Sunday, then cancelled in a bad mood.","steps":[{"prompt":"She's sulking by the window, shoes still on. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Go to her, and name that you broke your promise","then":"She looks up, surprised. 'You said we'd go,' she says.","best":true},{"text":"Act like you never really promised","then":"She stares at you, and something in her face closes."},{"text":"Tell her to stop sulking over a small thing","then":"She kicks off her shoes and stomps to her room."},{"text":"Give her your phone to play on instead","then":"She takes it, but the park still hangs in the air."}],"why":"A broken promise is a rupture too. Naming it is where the repair starts."},{"prompt":"Your daughter asks why you cancelled. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you were in a bad mood, and that wasn't fair to her","then":"She thinks about it. 'I get in bad moods too,' she says.","best":true},{"text":"Say something urgent came up at the shop","then":"She nods, but she saw you on the sofa all afternoon."},{"text":"Say she hadn't finished her homework","then":"Her face falls. 'You didn't say that before,' she mutters."},{"text":"Say grown-ups get tired, and she'll understand one day","then":"She shrugs, not understanding at all."}],"why":"An honest reason that owns your part rebuilds trust better than an excuse."},{"prompt":"Then she asks when you'll actually go. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say sorry, and set a real time you can keep","then":"You agree on Wednesday after school. She writes it on the calendar.","best":true},{"text":"Say 'Soon', and leave it vague","then":"She nods, but doesn't look convinced."},{"text":"Promise to take her every day this week","then":"She cheers, and you quietly wonder how you'll manage the shop."},{"text":"Say you'll go whenever you're in a better mood","then":"She looks at you, trying to guess when that will be."}],"why":"A realistic new plan, kept, shows her your word can be trusted again."},{"prompt":"A few days later, a free evening comes, but you're tired after the shop. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Take her anyway, even if only for half an hour","then":"She runs to the swings. The short visit feels like a promise kept.","best":true},{"text":"Put it off again. She'll understand","then":"She waits by the door, then quietly takes her shoes off again."},{"text":"Send her to the park with her cousin instead","then":"She goes, but keeps looking back for you."},{"text":"Tell her tomorrow, and this time you really mean it","then":"She nods slowly, and you see her stop believing it."}],"why":"Keeping even a small version of the promise rebuilds trust more than waiting for a perfect day."}],"debrief":"A broken promise is a rupture too; owning it and setting a realistic new plan repairs the trust.","relearn":"When you break a promise, name it, apologise, and set a real new plan; pretending erodes trust."},
  {"id":"bc-1272","cat":"the-repair","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"slip after a sleepless night","hook":"Running on no sleep, you snapped at your baby, and now you feel like a failure.","steps":[{"prompt":"The baby is still fussing in the cot. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Breathe, then pick her up and hold her gently","then":"Her fussing slows against your chest, and your own heart settles a bit.","best":true},{"text":"Tell yourself exhaustion proves you're a bad mother","then":"The thought sinks in, and you feel too heavy to move."},{"text":"Replay the snap over and over in your head","then":"Her fussing goes on, and the replay drowns out everything else."},{"text":"Pick her up while scolding yourself out loud","then":"She's soothed, but your mind keeps hammering at you."}],"why":"Breathing first lets you reconnect gently. If you're still at your edge, it's okay to leave her safe and step away."},{"prompt":"That evening, your partner Karan notices your red eyes. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell him you're running on empty and need rest","then":"Karan takes the baby. 'Sleep. I've got her till two,' he says.","best":true},{"text":"Say you're fine. Mothers just manage","then":"He believes you, and you face another night alone."},{"text":"Snap at him for not helping more","then":"He bristles, and the evening turns into an argument."},{"text":"Admit it, but refuse his offer to take over","then":"He looks hurt. You're still wide awake and exhausted at midnight."}],"why":"Resting when you can is part of repair. Asking for help is care, not failure."},{"prompt":"Next morning, you think back to snapping at her. What do you tell yourself?","options":[{"text":"I was running on empty. I can still go back to her","then":"You feel steadier, and greet her with a smile when she wakes.","best":true},{"text":"Good mothers never snap, however tired","then":"The guilt tightens, and every cry today feels like a test."},{"text":"It was the baby's fault for not sleeping","then":"You feel resentful looking at her, and the warmth fades."},{"text":"It was just tiredness, so no need to think about it","then":"You move on, and nothing changes for the next sleepless night."}],"why":"Exhaustion is a reason, not a verdict. Being gentle with yourself helps you keep reconnecting."}],"debrief":"Exhaustion is a reason, not a verdict; be gentle with yourself, then reconnect when you can.","relearn":"Exhaustion explains a snap but doesn't cancel the repair; rest if you can, then reconnect."},
  {"id":"bc-1273","cat":"the-repair","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"co-parent undermining repair","hook":"Your wife Neha just said sorry to your son Kabir for yelling. Part of you feels that apologising is soft.","steps":[{"prompt":"Kabir looks up at you to see what you think. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell Neha later, in private, that you agree","then":"Neha thanks you when you tell her later. Kabir never saw you agree."},{"text":"Tell Kabir she didn't need to say sorry","then":"Neha's face tightens. Kabir looks from one of you to the other, confused."},{"text":"Add that Kabir should listen so no one yells","then":"Kabir's shoulders drop again. Now the sorry sounds like it was his fault."},{"text":"Nod and tell Kabir that his mum meant it","then":"Kabir relaxes and leans into Neha. She gives you a quick, grateful look.","best":true}],"why":"Backing her up in front of Kabir shows him both parents stand behind owning a slip."},{"prompt":"Later, Neha says she sensed your doubt and asks what you really think about saying sorry. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say it's fine now and then, but not as a habit","then":"Neha frowns. \"So we own it only sometimes?\" The question hangs there."},{"text":"Say children need parents who never back down","then":"Neha goes quiet. You both feel yourselves pulling in different directions."},{"text":"Admit you felt it was soft, and that you're rethinking it","then":"Neha smiles. \"My father never once said sorry. I don't want that for Kabir.\"","best":true},{"text":"Change the subject to tomorrow's plans","then":"The talk slides away, and the difference between you stays unspoken."}],"why":"Being honest about your doubt lets you both choose one shared way instead of quietly pulling apart."},{"prompt":"A few days later, you snap at Kabir yourself over spilled milk. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Clean up and act extra cheerful instead","then":"Kabir stays wary through breakfast, watching your face."},{"text":"Go back and say sorry to him, the way Neha did","then":"Kabir hugs you. \"Mumma also said sorry,\" he says, as if it's normal.","best":true},{"text":"Say the milk wouldn't spill if he were careful","then":"Kabir hangs his head and mumbles a sorry to you instead."},{"text":"Ask Neha to talk to him, since she's better at it","then":"Neha does, kindly. Kabir still glances at you, waiting for something."}],"why":"Owning your own slip shows Kabir that both his parents make things right."},{"prompt":"Your mother visits, sees Neha apologise to Kabir, and says you two are going soft. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell her calmly that you both own your slips","then":"Your mother huffs, but Kabir hears you and stands a little taller.","best":true},{"text":"Laugh it off and change the topic","then":"Your mother takes the laugh as agreement and repeats it at dinner."},{"text":"Agree with her, then back Neha later in private","then":"Neha hears what you said and feels alone in front of your family."},{"text":"Snap that she should stay out of your parenting","then":"Your mother leaves the room, hurt, and the point is lost in the fight."}],"why":"Backing each other up in front of family keeps the repair strong and your child sure of you both."}],"debrief":"When one parent repairs, the other backs it up. A united front teaches your child that owning a slip is normal and strong.","relearn":"Back up your partner's repair instead of undercutting it; a child needs both parents, not a split."},
  {"id":"bc-1274","cat":"the-repair","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"repair after harsh words on marks","hook":"Aarav came home with low maths marks and you called him useless. You saw his face fall.","steps":[{"prompt":"He goes to his room and shuts the door. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Go in and take back the word 'useless'","then":"Aarav doesn't look up, but he stops pretending to read.","best":true},{"text":"Leave him. He should feel how serious it is","then":"The door stays shut through dinner. He eats quickly and says nothing."},{"text":"Bring him a snack and don't mention it","then":"He takes the plate and thanks you quietly. The word still sits between you."},{"text":"Say you only said it because you care","then":"He nods, but his eyes say he heard 'useless' louder than 'care'."}],"why":"Taking the word back quickly stops a harsh label from sticking to how he sees himself."},{"prompt":"At breakfast, Aarav mumbles that maybe he really is bad at maths. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"He'll prove himself if he studies harder","then":"He nods, but hears that he's only okay once his marks go up."},{"text":"You were weak at maths too and turned out fine","then":"He smiles a little, but being bad at maths now sounds settled."},{"text":"He should stop feeling sorry for himself","then":"He goes silent and pushes his plate away."},{"text":"A low mark is one result, not who he is","then":"He looks up, surprised, then asks if you'll check his sums tonight.","best":true}],"why":"Separating his worth from his marks lets him face the subject without feeling like a failure."},{"prompt":"The next test is in two weeks. What do you do about his studying?","options":[{"text":"Book extra tuition for him without asking","then":"He goes, but tells the tutor he's just no good at maths."},{"text":"Warn him that another low mark means no cricket","then":"He studies, anxious, and hides a practice sheet he got wrong."},{"text":"Sit with him calmly and ask where he gets stuck","then":"He shows you the fractions chapter, and you make a small plan together.","best":true},{"text":"Leave it all to him so he feels no pressure","then":"He means to study, but fractions still confuse him the night before."}],"why":"Talking study calmly, once the bond is mended, helps him learn instead of fearing the next test."}],"debrief":"A child is never their marks. Take back the harsh word, repair the bond, then talk study calmly.","relearn":"After harsh words about marks, take the word back and separate marks from worth; then talk study calmly."},
  {"id":"bc-1275","cat":"the-repair","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"raised hand then stopped","hook":"Your son Rohan knocked a shelf of jars over in the shop. You raised your hand to hit him, then stopped.","steps":[{"prompt":"Rohan flinches and stares at your hand. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell him he's lucky you stopped, so behave","then":"Rohan nods fast. He behaves now, but only out of fear."},{"text":"Lower it, breathe, and tell him he's safe","then":"Rohan's shoulders drop a little, though he still watches you closely.","best":true},{"text":"Walk outside without a word to cool down","then":"You calm down outside. Inside, Rohan stands frozen among the broken jars."},{"text":"Laugh and say you were only joking","then":"Rohan tries to laugh too. His eyes say he doesn't believe it."}],"why":"Lowering your hand and reassuring him first helps him feel safe before anything else."},{"prompt":"That evening, Rohan is quiet and keeps away from you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Sit with him and own that raising your hand was wrong","then":"He listens, then says quietly, \"I thought you were going to hit me.\"","best":true},{"text":"Leave it. He knows you didn't actually hit him","then":"Rohan keeps his distance for days, careful around you."},{"text":"Buy him his favourite sweets to cheer him up","then":"He smiles at the sweets, but still flinches when you move quickly."},{"text":"Explain that his carelessness made you angry","then":"Rohan says sorry for the jars. Now he thinks the fear was his fault."}],"why":"Owning it tells him the fear wasn't his fault, and that you are choosing differently."},{"prompt":"Lying awake, you think about how close it was. What do you make of that moment?","options":[{"text":"You're turning into your father, whatever you do","then":"The thought keeps you up, and you wake tense and hopeless."},{"text":"You'll never let yourself feel that angry again","then":"The promise feels firm tonight, but you have no plan for the next rush."},{"text":"Maybe one slap would have taught him faster","then":"The old belief sounds convincing in the dark, and the pause you found feels smaller."},{"text":"You stopped, and that pause is progress to protect","then":"Under the shame you feel a small, steady hope. Next time you want to catch it sooner.","best":true}],"why":"Catching yourself is hard-won. Seeing it as progress helps you protect that growing gap."},{"prompt":"A week later, on a rushed shop day, the old urge feels close again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Grit your teeth and rely on willpower","then":"You hold on today, but by closing time you're drained and snapping."},{"text":"Keep Rohan out of the shop so he can't provoke you","then":"Rohan feels shut out, and the urge finds another moment at home."},{"text":"Step away, then call Tele-MANAS 14416 to talk it over","then":"On the call, you notice the urge comes when you're rushed and tired.","best":true},{"text":"Tell yourself a man should handle this alone","then":"You carry it alone, and the urge stays just as close as before."}],"why":"When the urge feels close, reaching for support is brave and helps keep your son safe."}],"debrief":"Catching yourself before hitting is hard-won. Reassure your child, own it, and protect that growing gap.","relearn":"Catching yourself before hitting is real progress; reassure the child and own it. If the urge feels close, get help: Tele-MANAS 14416."},
  {"id":"bc-1276","cat":"the-repair","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"over-apologising grovelling","hook":"You snapped at your daughter Myra this morning and have said sorry again and again since.","steps":[{"prompt":"Myra pats your arm and says, 'It's okay, Mumma. Don't cry.' What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Keep apologising until it feels truly forgiven","then":"Myra keeps patting your arm, trying harder to make you feel better."},{"text":"Tell her how guilty you've felt all morning","then":"Myra listens with a worried face, now carrying your guilt as well."},{"text":"Say one last calm sorry, then suggest you play","then":"Myra brightens and runs to fetch her blocks.","best":true},{"text":"Stop mentioning it, but stay quiet and distant","then":"The apologies stop, and so does the warmth. Myra watches you carefully."}],"why":"One steady sorry gives her the repair without making her look after your feelings."},{"prompt":"At lunch, the guilt rises again as you watch her eat. What do you do with it?","options":[{"text":"Say sorry to her once more, just in case","then":"Myra's smile fades. \"You said that already,\" she says quietly."},{"text":"Notice it, breathe, and keep chatting with her","then":"The feeling eases. Myra tells you a long story about her friend.","best":true},{"text":"Give her extra screen time to make up for it","then":"She's thrilled, and you feel lighter for a while. The guilt is back by evening."},{"text":"Tell her you're a bad mother and she deserves better","then":"Myra stops eating and hugs you, trying to fix it."}],"why":"Holding your guilt yourself, not handing it to her, lets her simply enjoy being with you."},{"prompt":"That night, you wonder why one snap hit you so hard. What helps most?","options":[{"text":"Remind yourself that repair, not perfection, is what counts","then":"You breathe out. The morning feels like one moment, not the whole story.","best":true},{"text":"Replay the moment to make sure you learn from it","then":"You lie awake going over it and wake up tired and short-tempered."},{"text":"Promise yourself you'll never raise your voice again","then":"The promise feels firm tonight. By morning you're already bracing to fail."},{"text":"Decide you've damaged her and can't undo it","then":"The thought sinks in heavily, and you feel too low to enjoy her tomorrow."}],"why":"Every parent slips. Knowing the repair is what counts stops the guilt from spiralling."}],"debrief":"Repair is one honest sorry and a return to warmth, not a guilt performance. Say it once and let it land.","relearn":"One clear, calm sorry repairs; grovelling on burdens the child. Own it simply, then return to warmth."},
  {"id":"bc-1277","cat":"the-repair","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"repair days later still counts","hook":"Three days ago you snapped at your son Ishaan over his messy room, and you never went back to it.","steps":[{"prompt":"Tonight he's colouring quietly beside you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Let it be. Too much time has passed to matter","then":"He colours on. You notice he still checks your face when he tidies up."},{"text":"Be extra fun this week to make up for it","then":"Ishaan laughs at your jokes, but the snap is never mentioned or mended."},{"text":"Wait for him to raise it if it still bothers him","then":"He doesn't, all evening. The moment stays unspoken."},{"text":"Gently bring up that day and say you're sorry","then":"Ishaan keeps colouring, then says softly, \"You were really loud.\"","best":true}],"why":"Bringing it up yourself shows him it's never too late to make things right."},{"prompt":"The next morning, Ishaan asks if you were very angry with him that day. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"That it was nothing and he should forget it","then":"He looks unsure, as if his feelings about it were wrong."},{"text":"That the room was a disaster, so of course you were","then":"Ishaan looks down. He hears that the shouting was about him."},{"text":"That you shouted too much, and it wasn't his fault","then":"Ishaan nods slowly. \"I'll try to keep my room tidy,\" he offers.","best":true},{"text":"That you'd rather not talk about old things","then":"He drops it, and asks you nothing else that morning."}],"why":"Owning the shout lets him set down blame that was never his to carry."},{"prompt":"By Sunday, his room is messy again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Let it go so you don't risk snapping again","then":"The mess grows all week, and so does your irritation."},{"text":"Calmly remind him of the rule and tidy it together","then":"He grumbles, then races you to fill the toy box first.","best":true},{"text":"Warn him you'll get really angry if it stays","then":"Ishaan tidies fast, glancing at you, worried."},{"text":"Tidy it yourself while he's at school","then":"The room is clean, and Ishaan learns nothing about keeping it that way."}],"why":"Repair doesn't drop the rule. A calm, firm reminder holds it without fear."}],"debrief":"A delayed repair still heals. Gently name the moment, own your part, and reconnect, even days on.","relearn":"Even days later a repair matters; name the old moment, own it, and reconnect."},
  {"id":"bc-1278","cat":"the-repair","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"model repair when child snaps","hook":"Your daughter Tara shouted at her little brother Yash, then went back and said sorry on her own.","steps":[{"prompt":"Tara walks past you afterwards, looking a little unsure. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell her you noticed she went back and made it right","then":"Tara grins. \"It felt weird, but good,\" she says.","best":true},{"text":"Scold her for the shout, then mention the sorry","then":"Tara's face closes at the scolding. She barely hears the part about the sorry."},{"text":"Say nothing. Saying sorry is simply expected","then":"Tara shrugs and goes back to her room. The moment passes unmarked."},{"text":"Give her a chocolate for being such a good girl","then":"Tara is pleased, and asks next time whether a sorry gets chocolate."}],"why":"Noticing her repair warmly tells her it counts, so she is likely to do it again."},{"prompt":"Later, Yash is still sulking and says Tara's sorry doesn't count. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell Yash he must accept it and move on now","then":"Yash mutters okay, but stays cold to Tara all evening."},{"text":"Agree with Yash that Tara was far too harsh","then":"Tara overhears and wonders why she bothered to apologise."},{"text":"Tell them both to stop fighting or lose TV time","then":"They go quiet, but neither of them feels heard."},{"text":"Say his hurt is real, and her sorry still matters","then":"Yash thinks about it, then lets Tara join his game.","best":true}],"why":"Honouring his hurt and her repair teaches them both that making things right is worth it."},{"prompt":"The next week, you snap at Tara for being slow to get ready. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Let it go. Parents needn't apologise to children","then":"Tara is quiet in the car. Saying sorry starts to look like a rule for kids only."},{"text":"Explain that she made you late, so you got upset","then":"Tara says sorry to you, taking on the blame for your snap."},{"text":"Go back and say sorry, the way she did with Yash","then":"Tara smiles. \"Now you did a Tara,\" she teases, and hugs you.","best":true},{"text":"Ask her mother to smooth it over for you","then":"Her mother does. Tara notices you never said anything yourself."}],"why":"Modelling repair yourself is what keeps the habit alive in her."}],"debrief":"Children learn repair by being noticed for it. When your child owns a slip, let them know it counts.","relearn":"When your child repairs on their own, notice it warmly; praising it grows the habit you've modelled."},
  {"id":"bc-1279","cat":"the-repair","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"name the feeling repair","hook":"After a long, draining day, you shouted at your son Reyansh when he wouldn't put his shoes on.","setup":"Later, he finds you sitting on the bed.","steps":[{"prompt":"Reyansh asks: \"Why did you shout so loud, Mumma?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You see how upset you make me when you don't listen to me?\"","then":"Reyansh's lip wobbles. \"I'll listen. I promise,\" he says, looking scared."},{"text":"\"I felt overwhelmed, and I shouted. That wasn't your fault.\"","then":"He studies your face, then edges a little closer.","best":true},{"text":"\"I didn't shout that loud, beta. Let's just forget it.\"","then":"He frowns, confused, because he remembers it differently."},{"text":"\"Mumma is very tired, so you must be extra good now.\"","then":"Reyansh nods seriously and tiptoes around you all evening."}],"why":"Naming your feeling while owning the shout helps him understand it without taking the blame."},{"prompt":"Reyansh asks: \"Are you sad because of me?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No, love. I was tired, and that's mine to sort out.\"","then":"He leans against you, relieved, and starts telling you about his day.","best":true},{"text":"\"A little. You did make my day harder.\"","then":"He looks down at his shoes, now carrying some of your day."},{"text":"\"Yes. So be good and help Mumma feel better.\"","then":"He brings you his teddy, anxious to fix your mood."},{"text":"\"Don't worry your head about grown-up things.\"","then":"He nods, but still isn't sure whether it was about him."}],"why":"Keeping your feelings as your job lets him stay a child, free of the blame."},{"prompt":"Your husband Aman walks in and asks, in front of Reyansh: \"What happened here?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Reyansh wouldn't listen to me, as usual.\"","then":"Reyansh shrinks behind you as Aman gives him a stern look."},{"text":"\"Nothing. Everything is fine now.\"","then":"Aman looks from you to Reyansh, unconvinced, and lets it drop."},{"text":"\"I shouted, but honestly, anyone would have after today.\"","then":"Aman shrugs. Reyansh hears that his shoes were the real problem."},{"text":"\"I got overwhelmed and shouted at him. It wasn't his fault.\"","then":"Aman nods. \"Long day. I'll do bath time tonight.\"","best":true}],"why":"Owning it again in front of him tells Reyansh the blame truly isn't his."}],"relearn":"Naming your feeling while owning the snap helps a child make sense of it without taking the blame."},
  {"id":"bc-1280","cat":"the-repair","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"repair the silence","hook":"Since your son Arjun lied about a fight at school yesterday, you haven't said a word to him.","setup":"This morning he hovers near the shop counter.","steps":[{"prompt":"Arjun asks quietly: \"Papa, can I help with the boxes?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Have you learned your lesson yet? Then we can talk.\"","then":"Arjun nods quickly, eyes down, and stacks boxes in silence."},{"text":"\"Yes, come. I've been too quiet, and that wasn't fair.\"","then":"Arjun hesitates, then picks up a box. \"I thought you were angry forever.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Stack those by the door. Carefully.\"","then":"He stacks them slowly, glancing up for a sign that things are okay."},{"text":"\"Go and ask your mother. I'm busy right now.\"","then":"Arjun's face falls, and he walks away."}],"why":"Breaking the silence yourself, warmly, tells him the cold spell is over."},{"prompt":"Later, Arjun says: \"I lied because I was scared you'd be angry.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thank you for telling me. I want the truth to feel safe.\"","then":"Arjun lets out a breath. \"The fight wasn't my idea, Papa.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"So now it's my fault that you lied?\"","then":"Arjun shakes his head fast. \"No, Papa. Sorry.\" He goes quiet again."},{"text":"\"Lying is still wrong, beta, whatever you were feeling.\"","then":"Arjun nods, and you can see him decide not to explain any more."},{"text":"\"I'd never be angry. You can tell me anything.\"","then":"Arjun looks doubtful. He remembers yesterday's silence."}],"why":"Thanking him for the truth shows honesty is safe with you, which fear never teaches."},{"prompt":"Arjun asks: \"Were you not talking to me to punish me?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You needed to feel how serious lying is.\"","then":"Arjun nods, learning that love can be switched off when he fails."},{"text":"\"I was just very busy with the shop.\"","then":"He looks unsure, because he knows it was more than that."},{"text":"\"I was upset with you. Let's not dig into all that now.\"","then":"He drops it, still unsure whether the silence could come back."},{"text":"\"Yes. It was wrong, and I won't use silence that way.\"","then":"Arjun is quiet, then asks, \"Did Dada do that to you too?\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming the silence as a punishment you're leaving behind breaks that pattern for him too."},{"prompt":"That evening, Arjun asks: \"Can we talk about the fight at school now?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No need. Just don't ever lie to me again.\"","then":"Arjun nods, and the fight at school goes unexplained."},{"text":"\"Only after you say a proper sorry for lying to me.\"","then":"He says sorry quickly, then is too guarded to tell you much."},{"text":"\"Yes. Tell me what happened. Then we'll decide.\"","then":"Arjun tells you everything, and you agree he'll apologise to his friend.","best":true},{"text":"\"First, you're off cricket for a whole week.\"","then":"Arjun goes quiet, and the story of the fight stays with him."}],"why":"Firm and kind means holding the limit with warmth, so he can tell you the truth."}],"relearn":"Breaking your own silence with warmth repairs the withdrawal; one quiet, kind opener tells the child the cold spell is over."},
  {"id":"bc-1281","cat":"the-repair","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"repair without grovelling","hook":"You snapped at your daughter Zoya while enforcing the no-phones-after-nine rule. Now she's crying.","setup":"The phone lies on the table between you.","steps":[{"prompt":"Zoya sobs: \"You're so mean! Everyone else gets their phone!\"","options":[{"text":"\"Fine, take it. Just stop crying. I can't take this.\"","then":"Zoya grabs the phone. The crying stops, and so does the rule."},{"text":"\"I'm sorry I shouted. Maybe the rule is too strict.\"","then":"Zoya's tears stop. \"So I can have it?\" she asks, hopeful."},{"text":"\"I'm sorry I shouted. That was too harsh. The rule still stands.\"","then":"Zoya wipes her eyes. \"So I still can't have it?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I only shouted because you kept arguing with me.\"","then":"Zoya cries harder. \"So it's my fault you yelled?\""}],"why":"A short sorry for the tone repairs things without undoing a fair limit."},{"prompt":"Zoya says quietly: \"You didn't have to yell at me.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm the worst. I'm so sorry. Please forgive me.\"","then":"Zoya ends up patting your hand, comforting you instead."},{"text":"\"You're right. I was tired, and I handled it badly.\"","then":"Zoya nods, a little calmer. \"You were really scary.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Well, you didn't have to argue with me either.\"","then":"Zoya folds her arms, and it turns into a new argument."},{"text":"\"Let's not go over it all again tonight.\"","then":"Zoya goes quiet, her upset left hanging."}],"why":"A plain, steady sorry owns the yelling without making her look after your guilt."},{"prompt":"The next night at nine, Zoya asks: \"Can I keep my phone five more minutes?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No, love. Phones go on the table at nine, like always.\"","then":"Zoya groans, but puts it down. \"Fine. Good night.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Okay, five minutes. I don't want another fight.\"","then":"Five minutes becomes twenty, and she asks again tomorrow."},{"text":"\"Don't start. You remember what happened last night.\"","then":"Zoya puts it down fast and goes to bed tense."},{"text":"\"Go and ask Nani. I'm not dealing with this again.\"","then":"Nani says yes, and now the rule depends on who Zoya asks."}],"why":"Holding the same limit calmly the next night shows her the rule and your love both stay."},{"prompt":"The next morning, Zoya asks: \"Why is it nine for me when you're on your phone till late?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Because I'm the parent, and that's that.\"","then":"Zoya rolls her eyes and takes the rule a little less seriously."},{"text":"\"Fine. Then keep yours till late too.\"","then":"Zoya grins, and the limit quietly disappears."},{"text":"\"I need my phone for work, so it's different.\"","then":"Zoya shrugs, unconvinced. The rule still feels like it's only for her."},{"text":"\"Fair point. Mine goes away at ten, and you can check.\"","then":"Zoya smirks. \"Oh, I will check.\"","best":true}],"why":"Taking her point seriously models fairness, which makes the limit easier to respect."}],"relearn":"A simple, steady apology that holds the limit repairs without grovelling or undoing the boundary you set."},
  {"id":"bc-1282","cat":"the-repair","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"repair a broken promise words","hook":"You promised your son Vihaan a trip to the cricket nets on Sunday, then cancelled for a work call.","setup":"He hasn't looked at you since lunch.","steps":[{"prompt":"Vihaan mutters: \"You always say you'll come, and then you don't.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Something came up. These things happen. Don't make it a big deal.\"","then":"Vihaan shrugs and heads for his room, then turns back at the door."},{"text":"\"Always? I took you to the nets just last month!\"","then":"\"Once,\" he says, and turns away."},{"text":"\"I'll buy you that new bat to make up for it.\"","then":"His eyes light up, then narrow. \"You'll still cancel again.\""},{"text":"\"You're right. I promised and let you down. That's on me.\"","then":"Vihaan looks up, surprised you agreed. \"So why did you?\"","best":true}],"why":"Owning the broken promise plainly, without excuses, starts to rebuild his trust."},{"prompt":"Vihaan asks: \"Was the work call more important than me?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Work pays for your cricket, you know.\"","then":"Vihaan goes quiet, feeling like a bill to be paid."},{"text":"\"Of course not. Don't be so dramatic.\"","then":"He rolls his eyes, sure now that you don't get it."},{"text":"\"No. I chose wrong today, and I'm sorry.\"","then":"Vihaan's shoulders drop. \"Can we go another day, then?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It was urgent. You'll understand when you're older.\"","then":"He nods, but hears that his day came second."}],"why":"Saying plainly that he matters, and owning the choice, answers the hurt under his question."},{"prompt":"Vihaan says: \"Promise you'll really come next time.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I promise, beta. I'll try my very best.\"","then":"He nods, but \"try\" sounds a lot like last time."},{"text":"\"Saturday morning, just us two. I'm blocking it off now.\"","then":"Vihaan watches you put it in your calendar. \"Okay. Saturday.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I can't promise. Work is unpredictable.\"","then":"He sighs. \"Then never mind.\""},{"text":"\"Sure. Whenever I'm free, we'll go.\"","then":"Vihaan knows that 'whenever' usually means never."}],"why":"A real, specific plan that you then keep repairs trust better than a vague promise."}],"relearn":"Owning a broken promise plainly and offering a real new plan repairs the trust better than excuses."},
  {"id":"bc-1283","cat":"the-repair","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"repair after harsh marks words","hook":"Last night you called your daughter Kavya 'hopeless' when she showed you her science marks.","setup":"She's packing her school bag in silence.","steps":[{"prompt":"Kavya says flatly: \"Don't worry. I know I'm hopeless.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Calling you hopeless was wrong. You're not your marks.\"","then":"Kavya stops packing. \"You sounded like you meant it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Well, prove me wrong next time, and I won't have to say it.\"","then":"Kavya zips the bag hard. \"Fine,\" she says, not looking at you."},{"text":"\"Don't be so sensitive, Kavya. I was just upset last night.\"","then":"She shrugs, and the word stays exactly where you left it."},{"text":"\"I only said it so you'd take science seriously.\"","then":"Kavya nods slowly. She hears that the word was on purpose."}],"why":"Taking the word back in plain words separates one result from who she is."},{"prompt":"Kavya says: \"You never say you're proud of me. Only marks.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's not true. I praise you all the time.\"","then":"Kavya rolls her eyes. \"Name one time.\""},{"text":"\"Marks matter for your future, beta. That's why I push you.\"","then":"\"I know,\" she sighs. \"It's all anyone here cares about.\""},{"text":"\"Get a good result and I'll say it every day.\"","then":"Kavya's face goes blank. Your pride now has a price."},{"text":"\"You're right. I'm proud of how kind and curious you are.\"","then":"Kavya blinks, then half-smiles. \"Really?\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming what you value in her, beyond marks, tells her your pride isn't conditional."},{"prompt":"Kavya asks: \"So what do I do about science?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Just study harder. You know what to do.\"","then":"She nods, no clearer than before, and dreads the next test."},{"text":"\"I've already booked you a tutor. You start on Monday evening.\"","then":"Kavya sighs. She goes, but doesn't say what confuses her."},{"text":"\"Let's look at the paper tonight and see where it slipped.\"","then":"She nods. \"The chemistry part. I didn't get it at all.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Don't worry about it. Marks don't really matter.\"","then":"She looks unsure. Last night they seemed to matter a lot."}],"why":"Talking study calmly, once her worth feels safe, helps her face the subject."}],"relearn":"Taking back a harsh word about marks and reaffirming the child's worth separates a result from who they are."},
  {"id":"bc-1284","cat":"the-repair","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"repair to a teenager","hook":"You yelled at your teenage son Kunal in front of his friend Farhan for coming home late.","setup":"Farhan has gone. Kunal's door is half shut.","steps":[{"prompt":"Kunal says, without turning round: \"What do you want now?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You should be used to it by now. Anyway, forget it.\"","then":"Kunal turns his music up and doesn't answer."},{"text":"\"To say sorry. I shouldn't have yelled in front of Farhan.\"","then":"Kunal turns a little. \"He's going to tell everyone.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm sorry, but you were two hours late, Kunal.\"","then":"\"Right. So it's my fault again,\" he mutters."},{"text":"\"Don't take that tone with me, or you'll hear more.\"","then":"Kunal goes silent, jaw tight, eyes on his phone."}],"why":"A blame-free sorry for the public yelling shows him the respect a teenager needs."},{"prompt":"Kunal says: \"You always do this. You embarrass me in front of people.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's fair. I'll talk to you privately from now on.\"","then":"Kunal looks at you properly for the first time. \"Okay.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm your father. I'll speak to you however I like.\"","then":"Kunal's face shuts. \"Yeah. Obviously.\""},{"text":"\"Always? That's an exaggeration, and you know it.\"","then":"\"See? You never listen,\" he says, turning away."},{"text":"\"My father did far worse to me in front of people.\"","then":"Kunal shrugs. \"So that makes it okay?\""}],"why":"Accepting his point and changing how you raise things rebuilds his trust in you."},{"prompt":"Kunal says: \"I don't really want to talk right now.\"","options":[{"text":"\"We're talking now, so sit and listen.\"","then":"He sits, arms folded, and hears nothing you say."},{"text":"\"Fine. But I don't want you sulking up here all evening.\"","then":"Kunal stays in his room all evening."},{"text":"\"Please, just tell me we're okay first.\"","then":"\"Fine, we're okay,\" he says flatly, just to end it."},{"text":"\"Okay. Take your time. I'm here when you're ready.\"","then":"He nods. Later, he comes down for dinner on his own.","best":true}],"why":"Not demanding instant forgiveness respects his feelings and keeps the door open."},{"prompt":"The next day, Kunal asks: \"So can we talk about the curfew properly?\"","options":[{"text":"\"There's nothing to discuss. Eight means eight.\"","then":"Kunal nods, and you suspect he'll stop telling you where he is."},{"text":"\"Come whenever you want. I don't want another fight.\"","then":"Kunal is pleased. You lie awake the next time he's out late."},{"text":"\"Yes. Tell me what feels fair, and I'll tell you my worry.\"","then":"Kunal suggests nine thirty. You settle on nine, with a text if he's running late.","best":true},{"text":"\"Only once you prove you can be on time.\"","then":"Kunal sighs. \"How do I prove it if we never talk?\""}],"why":"Holding a limit while hearing his side is firm and kind, and a teen respects it more."}],"relearn":"With a teen, a respectful, blame-free apology that doesn't demand instant forgiveness keeps the door open."},
  {"id":"bc-1285","cat":"the-repair","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"repair invites child voice","hook":"You snapped at your daughter Siya over a lost water bottle, then said sorry for it.","setup":"She sits quietly beside you at the bus stop.","steps":[{"prompt":"Siya says, eyes on her shoes: \"It's fine. You said sorry already.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Good. Then there's nothing more to talk about.\"","then":"Siya nods and stares at the road, her silence heavier than before."},{"text":"\"Let's get ice cream after school and forget it.\"","then":"Siya brightens a little, then looks back down at her shoes."},{"text":"\"I did. I'd also like to hear how it felt for you.\"","then":"Siya is quiet, then looks up, as if deciding whether to say more.","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm glad. I was scared you'd be upset with me.\"","then":"Siya pats your hand. \"I'm not,\" she says, but her voice wobbles."}],"why":"Inviting her side after the sorry tells her that her feelings matter too."},{"prompt":"A minute later, Siya says quietly: \"You made me feel stupid. Like I lose everything.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Well, you do lose things quite often, don't you?\"","then":"Siya's eyes fill, and she says nothing more until the bus comes."},{"text":"\"That makes sense. Thank you for telling me. I'm listening.\"","then":"Siya goes on. \"And Riya laughed, because you shouted in front of her.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're not stupid, Siya. Don't ever say that about yourself.\"","then":"Siya nods, but stops explaining what it was like."},{"text":"\"I felt stupid too, shouting like that at a bus stop.\"","then":"Siya waits, but now the talk is about you."}],"why":"Listening without correcting her feeling helps her say the rest."},{"prompt":"That evening, Siya asks: \"Will you tell me when you're getting cross, before you shout?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes. I'll say I need a minute, and take a breath.\"","then":"Siya nods. \"And I'll keep my bottle in my bag.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I shouldn't have to warn you. Just be more careful.\"","then":"Siya sighs, and the idea she offered is gone."},{"text":"\"I'll never get cross with you again. I promise.\"","then":"Siya smiles, but you both know the next rushed morning will test it."},{"text":"\"Oh, you'll know. My face goes all red.\"","then":"Siya laughs, but nothing is really agreed."}],"why":"Taking up her idea shows her that her voice can change things at home."}],"relearn":"A repair that invites the child to say how it felt, and listens, deepens the reconnection."},
  {"id":"bc-1286","cat":"the-repair","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"repair with a non-verbal baby","hook":"Worn out, you raised your voice on a phone call right beside your six-month-old, Kiaan.","setup":"He had just dozed off on his play mat.","steps":[{"prompt":"Kiaan startles awake on the mat and wails, arms flung wide.","options":[{"text":"\"Stop crying. It wasn't even that loud.\"","then":"Kiaan cries harder, arching away from your sharp tone."},{"text":"\"Oh no. I'm so sorry. I'm the worst mother!\"","then":"Kiaan hears the panic in your voice and cries on."},{"text":"\"Wait, baby. Let me just finish this call first.\"","then":"Kiaan's wails climb while you rush the call to an end."},{"text":"\"Shh, I'm here. That was too loud. I've got you.\"","then":"You hold him close. His cries slow to hiccups against your shoulder.","best":true}],"why":"A soft voice and gentle holding are the repair a baby understands, long before words."},{"prompt":"A few minutes on, Kiaan is still whimpering and watching your face.","options":[{"text":"\"Come on now. You're fine. That's enough.\"","then":"He whimpers on, reading the impatience in your voice."},{"text":"\"Let's put the TV on. That will distract you.\"","then":"The bright screen quiets him, but he stays tense in your arms."},{"text":"\"You're safe, little one. Mumma's calm now.\"","then":"He studies your face, then nuzzles in and yawns.","best":true},{"text":"\"Sorry, sorry, sorry. Please just stop crying now.\"","then":"Your voice is tight and pleading, and he keeps whimpering."}],"why":"Your calm presence tells him the world is safe again. Babies read tone before words."},{"prompt":"That evening, your husband Rahul asks: \"Kiaan seems clingy. Everything okay today?\"","options":[{"text":"\"He's just teething. Nothing happened.\"","then":"Rahul nods, and you keep the heavy day to yourself."},{"text":"\"I shouted near him and scared him. I'm running on empty.\"","then":"Rahul sits down beside you. \"Let me take him tonight. You sleep.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm a useless mother. I scared my own baby.\"","then":"Rahul looks worried and doesn't know what to say."},{"text":"\"He's clingy because you're never home to help me.\"","then":"Rahul stiffens, and the real story of your day gets lost."}],"why":"Naming what happened, and how tired you are, lets you get support instead of carrying it alone."}],"relearn":"With a baby, a soft voice, gentle holding and calm presence are the repair; they read your tone long before words."},
  {"id":"bc-1287","cat":"the-repair","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"repair phrase to what it does","relearn":"Each piece of a repair does a job: owning the act, lifting the blame, restoring safety, holding the limit, reconnecting.","hook":"Match each repair line to what it does for the child.","pairs":[{"left":"\"I shouldn't have shouted\"","right":"Owns the behaviour"},{"left":"\"It wasn't your fault\"","right":"Lifts the blame off them"},{"left":"\"You're safe with me\"","right":"Restores their security"},{"left":"\"The rule still stands\"","right":"Keeps the limit firm"},{"left":"\"Come here for a cuddle\"","right":"Reconnects the bond"}]},
  {"id":"bc-1288","cat":"the-repair","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"rupture to its repair","relearn":"Different ruptures need fitting repairs: a shout, a broken promise, a harsh label, withdrawal, and an unfair blame.","hook":"Match each rupture to the repair it needs.","pairs":[{"left":"You shouted in anger","right":"Own the shout and reassure them"},{"left":"You broke a promise","right":"Apologise and set a real new plan"},{"left":"You called them a harsh name","right":"Take the word back and affirm their worth"},{"left":"You went cold and silent","right":"Reopen with warmth and start fresh"},{"left":"You blamed them for your reaction","right":"Say clearly the reaction was yours"}]},
  {"id":"bc-1289","cat":"the-repair","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"clean vs blaming apology","relearn":"A clean apology owns your part; a blaming one shifts fault to the child. The wording is what makes the difference.","hook":"Match each opener to whether it owns or shifts the fault.","pairs":[{"left":"\"I was wrong to shout\"","right":"Owns your part"},{"left":"\"Sorry, but you pushed me to it\"","right":"Shifts fault to the child"},{"left":"\"That wasn't okay of me\"","right":"Takes responsibility"},{"left":"\"I only reacted because of you\"","right":"Blames the child"},{"left":"\"It wasn't your fault\"","right":"Frees them of blame"}]},
  {"id":"bc-1290","cat":"the-repair","type":"match","persona":"Suresh","source":"timing of repair","relearn":"Repair has a rhythm: pause when hot, reconnect when calm, keep it simple, and let it go once it's done.","hook":"Match each moment to the right repair move.","pairs":[{"left":"You're still furious","right":"Wait, don't repair yet"},{"left":"You've both calmed down","right":"Go back and reconnect"},{"left":"You've said a clear sorry","right":"Let it rest, don't over-repeat"},{"left":"Days have passed, still unsaid","right":"Bring it up gently now"},{"left":"Mid-meltdown screaming","right":"Stay near, wait for the lull"}]},
  {"id":"bc-1291","cat":"the-repair","type":"match","persona":"Divya","source":"guilt reframed to action","relearn":"Guilt can be turned into a useful step: noticing, owning, reconnecting, and self-forgiving so you can keep going.","hook":"Match each guilty thought to the kinder action underneath it.","pairs":[{"left":"\"I've ruined everything\"","right":"Repair what just happened"},{"left":"\"I'm a terrible mother\"","right":"Be as kind to yourself as to her"},{"left":"\"It's too late now\"","right":"Reconnect, it rarely is"},{"left":"\"I must punish myself\"","right":"Forgive yourself and continue"},{"left":"\"I can never slip again\"","right":"Expect slips, plan to repair"}]},
  {"id":"bc-1292","cat":"the-repair","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"what child learns from repair","relearn":"Watching a parent repair teaches a child lasting lessons: accountability, that love survives conflict, and how to apologise.","hook":"Match each repair you model to what your child takes from it.","pairs":[{"left":"You own your mistake aloud","right":"Mistakes can be admitted"},{"left":"You reconnect after a fight","right":"Love survives conflict"},{"left":"You apologise sincerely","right":"How to say a real sorry"},{"left":"You forgive yourself","right":"Self-kindness after slipping"},{"left":"You keep the limit kindly","right":"Boundaries and warmth coexist"}]},
  {"id":"bc-1293","cat":"the-repair","type":"match","persona":"Vikram","source":"fake vs real repair gestures","relearn":"Some gestures look like repair but dodge it; a real repair uses words and reconnection, not bribes or pressure.","hook":"Match each move to what it really is.","pairs":[{"left":"Buying a toy, saying nothing","right":"Looks like repair, dodges it"},{"left":"\"Say you forgive me, now\"","right":"Pressures, doesn't heal"},{"left":"Owning it and reconnecting","right":"A true repair"},{"left":"\"Let's just forget it happened\"","right":"Avoids the repair"},{"left":"A calm sorry and a hug","right":"Also a true repair"}]},
  {"id":"bc-1294","cat":"the-repair","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"repair you never got","relearn":"Maybe no one ever circled back to repair with you; choosing to repair with your child gives them what you missed.","hook":"Lensy: maybe no one ever came back to make it right with you as a child. Offering that to your own child now?","prompt":"What stirs in you?","options":["I want to give that","It matters","Something I missed"],"affirm":"Choosing to repair with your child offers them the very thing you may never have received, and that is how a cycle ends."},
  {"id":"bc-1295","cat":"the-repair","type":"reflect","persona":"Divya","source":"self-forgiveness in repair","relearn":"You can hold yourself with the same gentleness you'd want your child to feel; forgiving yourself is part of the repair.","hook":"Lensy: the kindness you offer your child after a slip, you're allowed to offer yourself too. Can you?","prompt":"How does that sit?","options":["I can try","I'm too hard on myself","Worth practising"],"affirm":"Forgiving yourself after a slip isn't letting yourself off; it frees the energy you need to go back and reconnect."},
  {"id":"bc-1296","cat":"the-repair","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"repair is courage","relearn":"Going back to own a slip takes courage, especially if you were never shown how; that courage is worth honouring.","hook":"Lensy: going back to say 'I was wrong' takes real courage, more if no one modelled it for you. Feel that?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["It does take courage","Honour that","Hard but worth it"],"affirm":"Choosing to repair when you were never shown how is quiet courage, and every time you do it, the next time gets easier."},
  {"id":"bc-1297","cat":"the-repair","type":"reflect","persona":"Suresh","source":"repair over being right","relearn":"You can value the connection over winning the moment; choosing repair over being right keeps the bond whole.","hook":"Lensy: sometimes the bond matters more than proving you were right. Can you let connection win?","prompt":"Where do you land?","options":["Connection wins","Still learning","Worth choosing"],"affirm":"Choosing to repair instead of insisting you were right tells your child the relationship matters more than winning, and that lasts."},
  {"id":"bc-1298","cat":"the-repair","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"small repairs add up","relearn":"You don't need one grand repair; small, consistent reconnections after ordinary slips are what build security.","hook":"Lensy: it's the small, everyday 'I'm sorry, let's start over' moments that build a secure child, not grand gestures. True for you?","prompt":"What do you feel?","options":["The small ones count","Reassuring","I can do small"],"affirm":"Security is built from many small repairs, not one perfect one; each ordinary 'let's start over' quietly adds up."},
  {"id":"bc-1299","cat":"the-repair","type":"reflect","persona":"Divya","source":"repair frees you from spiral","relearn":"A repair can be the off-ramp from a guilt spiral; doing the small kind thing breaks the loop better than ruminating.","hook":"Lensy: when guilt loops, a small repair can be the way out of the spiral, not deeper into it. Does that help?","prompt":"How does that land?","options":["That helps","A way out","I'll reach for it"],"affirm":"When guilt starts to spiral, reaching for a small repair gives the feeling somewhere to go, and quietly ends the loop."},
  {"id":"bc-1300","cat":"the-repair","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"repair you can start now","relearn":"There's likely a small rupture you could repair today; it's almost never too late to gently reconnect.","hook":"Lensy: is there a small moment from this week you could still circle back and repair? It's rarely too late.","prompt":"What comes to mind?","options":["There is one","I'll go back","Good to remember"],"affirm":"If a small rupture comes to mind, it's rarely too late to gently circle back; reconnection restores trust whenever it's offered."},
  {"id":"bc-1301","cat":"the-repair","type":"reflect","persona":"Suresh","source":"repair the silence pattern","relearn":"If silence was your family's way, breaking it with a few warm words is a real repair, even if it feels unfamiliar.","hook":"Lensy: if your home went silent after conflict, choosing a few warm words instead is its own kind of repair. Can you try it?","prompt":"What stirs?","options":["I can try","Unfamiliar but right","Worth breaking"],"affirm":"Breaking an inherited silence with a little warmth is a genuine repair; unfamiliar at first, it teaches your child that conflict can end in reconnection."},
  {"id":"bc-1302","cat":"the-repair","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"spot fake repairs","relearn":"A real repair owns the slip and reconnects; blaming the child or buying them off only looks like repair.","hook":"Spot the two lines that look like a repair but actually dodge it.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'm sorry I shouted, that wasn't okay\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"Sorry, but you made me lose it\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"It wasn't your fault, come here\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Here's a chocolate, now we're fine\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"You're safe with me, let's start over\"","trick":false}],"why":"'Sorry, but you made me' shifts the blame, and buying them off skips the words; neither owns the slip or truly reconnects."},
  {"id":"bc-1303","cat":"the-repair","type":"spot","persona":"Suresh","source":"spot blame-shifting repair","relearn":"A repair that owns your reaction heals; one that hands the child the fault for it does not.","hook":"Spot the two apologies that secretly blame the child.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I was wrong to snap at you\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"I only shouted because you provoked me\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"That was on me, not on you\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"If you'd behaved, I'd never have lost it\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"I'm sorry, you didn't deserve that tone\"","trick":false}],"why":"'You provoked me' and 'if you'd behaved' both hand the child the blame for your reaction, which is not repair."},
  {"id":"bc-1304","cat":"the-repair","type":"spot","persona":"Divya","source":"spot guilt-driven over-repair","relearn":"A clean, simple repair heals; guilt-driven grovelling or making the child reassure you burdens them instead.","hook":"Spot the two moves where your guilt becomes the child's burden.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"One calm, clear 'I'm sorry'","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Apologising over and over for hours","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"A warm cuddle and starting fresh","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Tell me you forgive me, please, again\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Letting the moment rest once it's said","trick":false}],"why":"Apologising for hours and pressing the child to keep forgiving you turn your guilt into something they must carry."},
  {"id":"bc-1305","cat":"the-repair","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"spot repair that keeps limit","relearn":"A good repair mends the tone without dropping the limit; caving on the rule to stop the tears isn't repair.","hook":"Spot the two moves that drop the limit instead of repairing the tone.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Sorry I was harsh; the rule still stands\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"Fine, forget the rule, just stop crying\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"I love you, and bedtime is still bedtime\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Have the sweets, I can't bear you upset\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"That tone was too sharp, I'm sorry\"","trick":false}],"why":"Dropping the rule or handing over sweets to end the tears caves the limit; repair mends the tone and holds it."},
  {"id":"bc-1306","cat":"the-repair","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"spot avoidance disguised as repair","relearn":"Avoiding the rupture isn't the same as repairing it; pretending, rushing past, or silencing the child all dodge the repair.","hook":"Spot the two lines that avoid the repair rather than make it.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Let's talk about what happened earlier\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"Nothing happened, stop bringing it up\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"I want to make this right with you\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Enough now, we're not discussing it\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"How did that feel for you? I'll listen\"","trick":false}],"why":"Denying it happened and shutting down the talk both avoid the rupture; repair means opening it, not closing it off."},
  {"id":"bc-1307","cat":"the-repair","type":"spot","persona":"Vikram","source":"spot undermining co-parent repair","relearn":"Backing your partner's repair strengthens it; undercutting or mocking it splits the front and teaches the child apologies are weak.","hook":"Spot the two reactions that undermine your partner's repair.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Mum's right to say sorry, that was fair\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You didn't need to apologise to him\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Standing quietly beside her repair","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Rolling your eyes at her apology","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"We both got it wrong; we're sorry\"","trick":false}],"why":"Telling her she didn't need to apologise and mocking her apology both undermine the repair and split the parenting front."},
  {"id":"bc-1308","cat":"the-repair","type":"spot","persona":"Suresh","source":"spot repair after near-hit","relearn":"After a near-hit, calm reassurance repairs; threats or denying the child's fear don't. If the urge feels close, get help: Tele-MANAS 14416.","hook":"You stopped your hand in time; spot the two reactions that fail the child.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I nearly lost it; I'm sorry I scared you\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You're lucky I stopped, so behave\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Kneeling and reassuring them calmly","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Don't look at me like that, nothing happened\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"You're safe; I'm working on this\"","trick":false}],"why":"A threat after the pause and denying their fear both fail the child; reassurance repairs."},
  {"id":"bc-1309","cat":"the-repair","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"true repair vs doesnt heal","relearn":"A true repair owns it, lifts the blame and reconnects; blaming, bribing, denying or pressuring only looks like repair.","hook":"Sort each into a true repair or one that doesn't heal.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'm sorry I shouted, that wasn't okay\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"Sorry, but you made me do it\""},{"id":"c","text":"A warm cuddle and 'let's start over'"},{"id":"d","text":"Buying a toy and saying nothing"},{"id":"e","text":"\"It wasn't your fault, I love you\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"Forget it happened, stop sulking\""}],"bins":[{"id":"true","label":"True repair","valence":"pos"},{"id":"false","label":"Doesn't heal","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"true","b":"false","c":"true","d":"false","e":"true","f":"false"}},
  {"id":"bc-1310","cat":"the-repair","type":"sort","persona":"Suresh","source":"repair timing right vs wrong","relearn":"Repair lands when you're both calm and stays simple; trying mid-rage, demanding instant calm, or grovelling on misfires.","hook":"Sort each into good repair timing or poor timing.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Wait until you've both cooled down"},{"id":"b","text":"Apologise mid-rage, still shouting"},{"id":"c","text":"Reconnect gently once it's calm"},{"id":"d","text":"Demand the child calm down this instant"},{"id":"e","text":"Say one clear sorry, then let it rest"},{"id":"f","text":"Keep apologising on and on for hours"}],"bins":[{"id":"good","label":"Good timing","valence":"pos"},{"id":"poor","label":"Poor timing","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"good","b":"poor","c":"good","d":"poor","e":"good","f":"poor"}},
  {"id":"bc-1311","cat":"the-repair","type":"sort","persona":"Divya","source":"guilt spiral vs repair action","relearn":"A guilt spiral keeps you stuck; turning the feeling into a small repair and self-forgiveness moves you forward.","hook":"Sort each into a guilt spiral or a forward repair step.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I've ruined everything forever\""},{"id":"b","text":"Go back and reconnect gently"},{"id":"c","text":"\"I'm a terrible, hopeless mother\""},{"id":"d","text":"Forgive yourself and keep going"},{"id":"e","text":"Replay the moment all night"},{"id":"f","text":"Say one calm sorry, then let it rest"}],"bins":[{"id":"spiral","label":"Guilt spiral","valence":"neg"},{"id":"forward","label":"Forward repair","valence":"pos"}],"key":{"a":"spiral","b":"forward","c":"spiral","d":"forward","e":"spiral","f":"forward"}},
  {"id":"bc-1312","cat":"the-repair","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"owns vs shifts blame","relearn":"A clean repair owns your reaction; a 'sorry but' or 'you made me' shifts the fault onto the child and doesn't heal.","hook":"Sort each apology by whether it owns your part or shifts the blame.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I was wrong to snap\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"I only yelled because of you\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"That tone was on me, not you\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"You provoked me, so I reacted\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"It wasn't your fault at all\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"If you'd listened, I'd not have shouted\""}],"bins":[{"id":"owns","label":"Owns your part","valence":"pos"},{"id":"shifts","label":"Shifts the blame","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"owns","b":"shifts","c":"owns","d":"shifts","e":"owns","f":"shifts"}},
  {"id":"bc-1313","cat":"the-repair","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"helps child feel safe vs on edge","relearn":"Warmth, ownership and reassurance help a child feel safe after a rupture; denial, blame and the cold shoulder keep them on edge.","hook":"Sort each into helping the child feel safe again or keeping them on edge.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A sincere apology and a cuddle"},{"id":"b","text":"Acting like nothing happened"},{"id":"c","text":"\"You're safe, I'm working on my temper\""},{"id":"d","text":"Making them apologise for your outburst"},{"id":"e","text":"Extra warmth and reassurance"},{"id":"f","text":"Days of cold silence afterward"}],"bins":[{"id":"safe","label":"Feels safe again","valence":"pos"},{"id":"edge","label":"Kept on edge","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"safe","b":"edge","c":"safe","d":"edge","e":"safe","f":"edge"}},
  {"id":"bc-1314","cat":"the-repair","type":"sort","persona":"Vikram","source":"real repair vs avoidance","relearn":"Real repair opens the rupture and reconnects; avoidance denies it, rushes past it, or buys the child off without words.","hook":"Sort each into a real repair or avoiding the repair.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Let's talk about earlier, I was wrong\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"Nothing happened, drop it\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"I want to make this right with you\""},{"id":"d","text":"Handing over a gift instead of words"},{"id":"e","text":"\"How did that feel? I'll just listen\""},{"id":"f","text":"Changing the subject to skip it"}],"bins":[{"id":"real","label":"Real repair","valence":"pos"},{"id":"avoid","label":"Avoiding it","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"real","b":"avoid","c":"real","d":"avoid","e":"real","f":"avoid"}},
  {"id":"bc-1315","cat":"the-repair","type":"match","persona":"Divya","source":"repair tone signals","relearn":"After a hard moment, your softened voice, gentle eyes, open arms and slowed pace each signal safety to a child before words do.","hook":"Match each gentle cue after a snap to what it signals the child.","pairs":[{"left":"A softened, lower voice","right":"The danger has passed"},{"left":"Kneeling to their eye level","right":"You're meeting them, not looming"},{"left":"Open arms held out","right":"You're welcome back close"},{"left":"A slow, deep breath first","right":"You've steadied yourself"},{"left":"A warm, unhurried pace","right":"There's no rush, you're here"}]},
  {"id":"bc-1316","cat":"the-repair","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"whose job in repair","relearn":"In a repair, owning the snap and reconnecting are the parent's job; a child should never have to apologise for your reaction or soothe your guilt.","hook":"Sort each into the parent's job in a repair or not the child's burden.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Owning the snap that was yours"},{"id":"b","text":"The child apologising for your outburst"},{"id":"c","text":"Reaching out to reconnect first"},{"id":"d","text":"The child soothing your guilt"},{"id":"e","text":"Saying clearly it wasn't their fault"},{"id":"f","text":"The child having to forgive on demand"}],"bins":[{"id":"parent","label":"Parent's job","valence":"pos"},{"id":"notchild","label":"Not the child's burden","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"parent","b":"notchild","c":"parent","d":"notchild","e":"parent","f":"notchild"}},
  {"id":"bc-1317","cat":"the-repair","type":"sort","persona":"Suresh","source":"repair vs makes it worse","relearn":"A calm sorry, reconnection and naming the feeling repair a rupture; revenge silence, threats and 'see what you made me do' make it worse.","hook":"Sort each into repairs the rupture or makes it worse.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A calm, simple sorry"},{"id":"b","text":"Days of punishing silence"},{"id":"c","text":"Reconnecting with warmth"},{"id":"d","text":"\"See what you made me do\""},{"id":"e","text":"Naming the feeling, no blame"},{"id":"f","text":"\"Behave or there's more next time\""}],"bins":[{"id":"repairs","label":"Repairs the rupture","valence":"pos"},{"id":"worse","label":"Makes it worse","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"repairs","b":"worse","c":"repairs","d":"worse","e":"repairs","f":"worse"}},
  {"id":"bc-1350","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"step away safely","hook":"Your two-year-old Meher has been screaming for twenty minutes, and your anger is climbing fast.","steps":[{"prompt":"You feel the old urge to shout her quiet. What's your move?","options":[{"text":"Stay and shout louder until she stops","then":"Meher screams harder, and your hand tightens on the table."},{"text":"Walk straight out of the house for some fresh air and quiet","then":"Outside you cool down, but inside, Meher is alone and unwatched."},{"text":"Hold her tight until she stops screaming","then":"She fights your arms, and both of you get more worked up."},{"text":"Put her in her cot, say you need a minute, and step out","then":"In the next room you breathe slowly. Her cries go on, but you feel steadier.","best":true}],"why":"Putting her somewhere safe before you step away keeps you both safe while the surge passes."},{"prompt":"A few minutes later, Meher is still crying. What do you do now?","options":[{"text":"Leave her to cry it out alone","then":"Her crying goes on and on, and you both end up exhausted."},{"text":"Hand her your phone to stop the crying","then":"The crying stops at once. The next tantrum starts when you take it back."},{"text":"Sit near her and name it: 'You're so upset'","then":"Meher's sobs slow. She crawls into your lap, hiccuping.","best":true},{"text":"Tell her that big girls don't cry","then":"Meher howls louder, too little to understand."}],"why":"Staying calm and naming her feeling helps a toddler settle and learn what the feeling is."},{"prompt":"That night, you think about the next time she screams like that. What do you plan?","options":[{"text":"Next time, one smack so she learns quickly","then":"The old idea feels like a shortcut, and your father's voice sounds very close."},{"text":"The same pause: keep her safe, step out, breathe, return","then":"Having a plan makes tomorrow feel a little less frightening.","best":true},{"text":"Hope she grows out of it soon","then":"You feel lighter for a moment, with nothing ready for tomorrow."},{"text":"Ask your wife to handle every one of her tantrums from now on","then":"Your wife agrees, tiredly, and the tantrums stay a mystery to you."}],"why":"A ready plan to step away safely is the caring move, not a failure, and gets easier with use."}],"debrief":"When anger climbs, put your child somewhere safe, step away and breathe before you respond.","relearn":"Stepping away to calm down, child kept safe, is the caring move, not a failure."},
  {"id":"bc-1351","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"route your past","hook":"Lately, small things your daughter Nisha does set off a rage that feels older than the moment.","steps":[{"prompt":"When she slams a door, you hear your own father yelling. What do you make of it?","options":[{"text":"Maybe old pain is surfacing and needs tending","then":"The thought is uncomfortable, but the rage starts to make a little more sense.","best":true},{"text":"It's just stress. The past has nothing to do with it","then":"You write it off, and the next slammed door sets you off the same way."},{"text":"Maybe your father was right to be so strict","then":"The old voice feels familiar for a moment, and a little frightening too."},{"text":"You're simply an angry person, and that's that","then":"The label settles on you like a verdict, and change feels impossible."}],"why":"Noticing the link, without blame, is the first step to stopping the cycle."},{"prompt":"The memories keep coming back at night. What do you do with them?","options":[{"text":"Push them down and hope they stay buried","then":"They stay quiet for a week, then surface in a sharp word to Nisha."},{"text":"Confront your father about how he raised you","then":"He gets defensive and says you turned out fine. You feel worse than before."},{"text":"Write them in a diary and tell no one","then":"Writing helps a little, but the rage still catches you by surprise."},{"text":"Call Tele-MANAS 14416 to talk through your past","then":"The person on the line listens, and for the first time you say it all aloud.","best":true}],"why":"Talking it through with support tends the old wound, so less of it leaks onto your child."},{"prompt":"Your partner notices you've been on edge and asks what's going on. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say it's just stress from work","then":"They nod, and you carry the rest alone."},{"text":"Say you'll handle it yourself, so don't worry","then":"They step back, still worried and unsure how to help."},{"text":"Tell them about the old memories that keep surfacing","then":"Your partner listens, then says, \"I've noticed. How can I help?\"","best":true},{"text":"Snap that nothing's wrong and walk off","then":"The room goes tense, and Nisha looks up from her homework."}],"why":"Letting someone close in means you don't have to carry the past alone."},{"prompt":"A month on, Nisha slams her door again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Shout that doors aren't for slamming in this house","then":"She freezes, and you hear your father's tone in your own voice."},{"text":"Notice the old feeling, breathe, and respond to her","then":"You feel it rise and pass. You ask her calmly what happened.","best":true},{"text":"Ignore it completely to avoid a scene","then":"The slam goes unaddressed, and the next one comes louder."},{"text":"Decide you've failed, since the anger still came","then":"The sense of failure makes you snappier all evening."}],"why":"Tending your wounds doesn't erase the feeling, but it gives you room to choose your response."}],"debrief":"Old pain that drives your reactions can route to support, Tele-MANAS 14416. Tending it helps the cycle stop with you.","relearn":"Heavy feelings from your past route to Tele-MANAS 14416; this is support, not therapy."},
  {"id":"bc-1352","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"real-harm route","hook":"Twice this month you came close to hitting your son Sonu, and it frightens you.","steps":[{"prompt":"You're scared your anger could seriously hurt him one day. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell yourself it's not that bad and carry it alone","then":"You push the fear aside. The next hard evening, it's right back."},{"text":"Get help now, before anything happens","then":"It feels hard to admit, but you start thinking about who to tell.","best":true},{"text":"Keep away from Sonu on evenings when you're tired","then":"Sonu wonders why you avoid him, and the anger is still waiting."},{"text":"Decide a quick slap is safer than losing control","then":"It feels like a plan, and it's exactly what your father did."}],"why":"Getting help before harm happens is the caring, protective move, never a shameful one."},{"prompt":"That evening, you have a few quiet minutes to yourself. What do you do with them?","options":[{"text":"Call Tele-MANAS 14416 and say you fear your anger","then":"The listener is calm and kind, and helps you plan for when the anger rises.","best":true},{"text":"Scroll your phone to forget about the day","then":"The minutes pass, and the fear is still there when you put the phone down."},{"text":"Ask your brother, who says every father gets angry","then":"He laughs it off as normal, and you feel silly for worrying."},{"text":"Read about anger online, but tell no one","then":"You learn a little, and still carry it all by yourself."}],"why":"Naming the fear to a trained listener gets you support and a plan, so you're not alone with it."},{"prompt":"One night the anger is so strong you fear you won't stop yourself. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Stay and try hard to hold yourself back","then":"Your fists are clenched, and Sonu can see how close you are."},{"text":"Lock yourself in the bathroom until it passes","then":"It passes this time, but you're shaken, and nobody knows."},{"text":"Tell Sonu to get out of your sight, now","then":"Sonu runs, frightened, and your anger is still at full heat."},{"text":"Leave the room and call Childline 1098 or 112","then":"You tell them what is happening, and help is on its way while Sonu stays safe inside.","best":true}],"why":"In a crisis, getting away and calling for help keeps your child safe. That is the caring act."}],"debrief":"If you fear real harm, reach for help now: Be the Safe Adult, Childline 1098 or 112.","relearn":"When anger risks real harm, get help now; carrying it alone keeps the risk in place."},
  {"id":"bc-1353","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"repair tool","hook":"You shouted at bedtime when your son Aadi asked for a third story. Now the house is calm.","steps":[{"prompt":"Aadi's light is off, but you can hear him sniffling. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say nothing and hope the morning erases it","then":"The sniffling stops eventually. In the morning he's clingy and quiet."},{"text":"Go in and read him the third story after all","then":"Aadi cheers up, and learns that tears can win a third story."},{"text":"Go in, own the shout, and say it wasn't his fault","then":"Aadi's sniffles slow. \"I just wanted one more story,\" he whispers.","best":true},{"text":"Tell him you shouted because he wouldn't settle down","then":"Aadi goes quiet. He hears that the shouting was his fault."}],"why":"Going back once you're both calm and owning it cleanly heals the rupture before sleep."},{"prompt":"At breakfast, Aadi asks whether he can have three stories tonight. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Yes, as many as he wants, so there's no fighting","then":"Aadi grins. Tonight's bedtime stretches well past ten."},{"text":"Two is still the rule, but he gets to choose both","then":"Aadi thinks it over and nods. \"Then I'm choosing the long ones.\"","best":true},{"text":"Only if he doesn't whine at all today","then":"Aadi goes stiff and quiet, trying hard to be good all day."},{"text":"You'll see tonight, and he should eat now","then":"Aadi eats slowly, unsure, and asks again at lunch."}],"why":"Repair doesn't undo the limit. A calm, clear rule with a little choice keeps bedtime firm and kind."},{"prompt":"That night, as he falls asleep, you think back to yesterday's shout. What do you tell yourself?","options":[{"text":"Slips happen. Coming back to repair is what counts","then":"The replay slows, and you let yourself rest.","best":true},{"text":"You're ruining him, one bedtime at a time","then":"The thought keeps you awake long after he's asleep."},{"text":"He's little. He won't remember it anyway","then":"It soothes you, and makes repairing feel less needed next time."},{"text":"From tomorrow on, you'll be perfectly patient with him","then":"The promise feels good, and leaves no room for the next tired night."}],"why":"Every parent slips. The repair, not perfection, is what helps a child feel secure."}],"debrief":"Once you're both calm, go back and repair the slip. Repair beats perfection.","relearn":"After a slip, the repair, owning it without blaming the child, counts most."},
  {"id":"bc-1354","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"ready a plan","hook":"School mornings with your twins Aryan and Anaya keep ending in shouting, and you're tired of it.","steps":[{"prompt":"One quiet Sunday, you plan how to handle the anger on those mornings. What goes in the plan?","options":[{"text":"Just try harder to stay calm each morning","then":"Monday starts well. By Tuesday, the rush wins again."},{"text":"Stricter punishments so the twins move faster","then":"The twins move faster, and flinch whenever you raise your voice."},{"text":"Wake up earlier so there's less of a rush","then":"Mornings ease a little, but when anger comes, you have nothing ready."},{"text":"Pause, breathe, step away if needed, repair after","then":"It fits on a sticky note. You put it on the fridge.","best":true}],"why":"A plan decided in advance gives you a path when willpower fails mid-surge."},{"prompt":"On Wednesday, Anaya can't find a shoe and Aryan spills his milk. You feel the surge. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Shout at them both to hurry up","then":"The twins scramble, Anaya starts crying, and you're late anyway."},{"text":"Mop up the milk yourself, fuming in silence","then":"The silence feels loud, and both twins tiptoe around you."},{"text":"Stop, take a slow breath, and say you need a moment","then":"The breath buys you a few seconds, and your voice comes out steady.","best":true},{"text":"Walk out and leave the mess to your partner","then":"Your partner is left with both twins and the milk, and snaps at you later."}],"why":"The pause comes first. A breath and a calm word stop the surge from taking over."},{"prompt":"That evening, you remember Aryan left for school looking upset. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Let it go. Mornings are just like that","then":"Aryan stays a little guarded all evening."},{"text":"Ask him about the morning and own your part in it","then":"Aryan shrugs, then says, \"You looked really cross.\" You talk it through.","best":true},{"text":"Say mornings would be easier if he were quicker","then":"Aryan nods, taking the whole morning onto his small shoulders."},{"text":"Plan a fun weekend outing to make it up to him","then":"He's excited, but the morning never gets talked about."}],"why":"Repair is part of the plan too. Owning your part reconnects you after a hard moment."}],"debrief":"Decide your pause-breathe-step-away-repair plan in advance so it's ready in the heat.","relearn":"A simple plan readied in advance beats willpower alone, which often fails mid-surge."},
  {"id":"bc-1355","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Suresh","source":"progress not perfection","hook":"It's been a hard week at the shop, and you snapped at your children twice.","steps":[{"prompt":"Late at night, you're ready to call yourself a failure. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Name two calmer moments from this week too","then":"You remember breathing through Tuesday's mess. It's small, but real.","best":true},{"text":"Decide you'll never change and stop trying","then":"Giving up feels like relief for a moment, then like a door closing."},{"text":"List every mistake so you never repeat them","then":"The list grows long, and you feel worse, not wiser."},{"text":"Remind yourself your father was far worse","then":"The comparison soothes you, and the two snaps go unexamined."}],"why":"Counting the calmer moments too shows you the real progress that slips can hide."},{"prompt":"Next morning, your daughter Rani still seems wary of you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Act cheerful and hope she forgets","then":"She smiles back politely, still watching your mood."},{"text":"Explain that the shop had a very stressful week","then":"Rani nods, and now worries about the shop as well."},{"text":"Leave it. Bringing it up will only upset her","then":"The wariness lingers through the day."},{"text":"Go to her and own the snap, without excuses","then":"Rani listens, then hugs you quickly before running off to school.","best":true}],"why":"Repairing the slip matters more than never slipping. It's what she'll remember."},{"prompt":"Your wife says she's noticed how hard you've been trying this month. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say it's no use, since you snapped twice this week","then":"She looks sad, and the good she noticed gets waved away."},{"text":"Promise her you'll be perfect from now on","then":"She smiles, a little unsure. The bar you've set is higher than anyone could clear."},{"text":"Thank her, and admit you still slip but are trying","then":"She squeezes your hand. \"That's all any of us can do.\"","best":true},{"text":"Change the subject. Praise makes you uneasy","then":"The moment passes, and so does a chance to feel supported."}],"why":"Accepting encouragement honestly keeps you in the change instead of all-or-nothing."},{"prompt":"The next week starts with another rushed morning, and the heat rises fast. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Brace for failure, since last week went badly","then":"You start the day tense, and it takes very little to set you off."},{"text":"Breathe as it rises, and repair if you slip","then":"You slip once and repair it by lunch. The rest of the day is calmer.","best":true},{"text":"Wait to pause until things feel really bad","then":"By the time it feels really bad, the snap has already happened."},{"text":"Skip the breathing. It feels silly in a rush","then":"Without it, the old reflex takes over faster."}],"why":"Small, steady changes with self-compassion last. Each pause and repair is real progress."}],"debrief":"Slips are part of changing. Count the calmer moments, repair, and keep going.","relearn":"Each pause, each repair, each calmer day is real progress; a slip isn't proof you can't change."},
  {"id":"bc-1356","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Priya","source":"help a parent","hook":"Over chai, your friend Sameer admits he hits his kids and feels trapped, like he can't stop.","steps":[{"prompt":"He looks down, ashamed, waiting for your reaction. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask him what is wrong with him, hitting children","then":"Sameer's face shuts. \"Forget I said anything,\" he mutters."},{"text":"Say you won't judge him, and offer to help find support","then":"Sameer lets out a long breath. \"I didn't think anyone would understand.\"","best":true},{"text":"Reassure him that every parent does it sometimes","then":"Sameer looks relieved, and the hitting stays as normal as ever."},{"text":"Tell him to just stop, since he already knows it's wrong","then":"He nods miserably. \"I've tried. I keep going back to it.\""}],"why":"Compassion without judgment makes it safe for him to keep talking and reach for help."},{"prompt":"Sameer says his father hit him too, and he turned out fine. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"He can love his father and still choose differently","then":"Sameer is quiet. \"I don't want my kids scared of me like I was.\"","best":true},{"text":"His father was cruel, and he's turning out the same","then":"Sameer bristles and starts defending his father."},{"text":"Maybe he's right, and a slap now and then is fine","then":"Sameer nods, and the trapped feeling he came with is brushed aside."},{"text":"Hitting harms children, and he should know better","then":"Sameer hears the truth, but also the judgment, and goes quiet."}],"why":"Questioning a pattern isn't betraying your parents. It opens the door to choosing differently."},{"prompt":"He asks what he can even do when the anger comes. What do you suggest?","options":[{"text":"Being stricter so the kids don't provoke him","then":"Sameer nods, and the kids get the blame for his anger."},{"text":"Waiting till the kids are older and easier","then":"He agrees, relieved, and nothing changes in the meantime."},{"text":"Reading a parenting book on anger when he finds the time","then":"He means to, but the book sits unopened by his bed."},{"text":"Calling Tele-MANAS 14416, and stepping away as it builds","then":"He saves the number in his phone. \"I'll try stepping away tonight.\"","best":true}],"why":"A real route to support, plus a simple step-away tool, gives him something to do in the moment."},{"prompt":"A week passes without a word from Sameer. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Leave it. He'll reach out if he needs to","then":"The silence stretches on."},{"text":"Message him to ask if he's sorted things out yet","then":"Sameer reads it and doesn't reply for two days."},{"text":"Send a warm message asking how he's doing","then":"He replies an hour later. \"Hard week. Can we talk?\"","best":true},{"text":"Ask a mutual friend to talk to him about it","then":"Word gets around, and Sameer is hurt that you told anyone."}],"why":"A gentle check-in keeps the door open without pushing or shaming."},{"prompt":"Then Sameer calls, shaken. He hit his son hard last night and fears it will get worse. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell him to keep it quiet so the family isn't shamed","then":"He agrees, relieved, and his son stays at risk."},{"text":"Thank him for telling you, and help him call Childline 1098","then":"Sameer agrees, and you stay on the line while he makes the call.","best":true},{"text":"Rush over to confront him in front of his family","then":"The scene gets loud, and his son hides in the next room."},{"text":"Suggest he calls Tele-MANAS 14416 sometime later this week","then":"He says he will, and the week stretches ahead with the risk unchanged."}],"why":"When a child's safety is at risk, get help right away, with warmth instead of blame."}],"debrief":"Meet a struggling parent with compassion plus a real route: Tele-MANAS 14416, or Childline 1098 when a child is at risk.","relearn":"Compassion, not condemnation, helps a trapped parent reach for help and change."},
  {"id":"bc-1357","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"adapt the tool","hook":"Driving your kids home through heavy traffic, you hear them fighting loudly in the back seat.","steps":[{"prompt":"Anger surges, and there's no room to step into. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Shout at full volume for them to be quiet","then":"The car goes silent, and you swerve a little as you turn to glare."},{"text":"Turn round and sort out the fight while driving","then":"Your eyes leave the road a second too long, and a horn blares."},{"text":"Slow your breathing and keep your voice low","then":"Your grip on the wheel loosens. The fight goes on, but you're steadier.","best":true},{"text":"Turn the music up loud to drown them out","then":"The noise just grows, and your head starts to pound."}],"why":"When you can't leave, slowing your breath is the tool that travels with you."},{"prompt":"Your daughter Ira cries that her brother Dev hit her. What do you say while still driving?","options":[{"text":"That Dev will get a slap when you reach home","then":"Dev shrinks into his seat, and Ira looks scared too."},{"text":"That you heard her, and you'll sort it out once parked","then":"Ira sniffles, and Dev goes quiet, knowing it's coming.","best":true},{"text":"That they'll both lose TV for a month if it goes on","then":"They go quiet and sulk, and nobody feels heard."},{"text":"That they must explain exactly what happened, now","then":"Both talk at once, and your attention drifts from the road."}],"why":"Saying you heard her, and saving the talk for when it's safe, keeps everyone safe."},{"prompt":"You pull up outside home, and the car is finally still. What now?","options":[{"text":"Take a breath, hear each child, and set a clear rule","then":"Dev admits the hit, and you all agree: no hitting, even when angry.","best":true},{"text":"Let it go now that they've calmed down","then":"The hit is forgotten by dinner, and so is the lesson."},{"text":"Send them both straight to their rooms","then":"The house goes quiet, but the hit is never talked about."},{"text":"Tell Dev he's a bad boy and should be ashamed","then":"Dev hangs his head and hears only that he is bad."}],"why":"Once it's safe, a calm talk and a clear limit teach more than shame or letting it slide."}],"debrief":"When you can't step away, slow your breath and lower your voice. Address it once it's safe.","relearn":"Calming tools adapt; when you can't leave the room, slow the breath first."},
  {"id":"bc-1358","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each need to the route that fits it.","pairs":[{"left":"Your own past trauma resurfacing","right":"Tele-MANAS 14416"},{"left":"A child in immediate danger","right":"Childline 1098"},{"left":"A life-threatening emergency","right":"Emergency 112"},{"left":"Help owning full parenting","right":"Positive-parenting support"},{"left":"Routing a child to safety in-app","right":"Be the Safe Adult (g69)"}],"relearn":"Each need has a clear route: Tele-MANAS for your past, Childline for danger, 112 for emergencies.","persona":"any","source":"need to route"},
  {"id":"bc-1359","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each hard moment to the tool that meets it.","pairs":[{"left":"The first rush of anger","right":"A few slow breaths"},{"left":"Close to losing it","right":"Step into another room"},{"left":"Just after you snapped","right":"A clean repair"},{"left":"A wound from your own past","right":"Reach for support"},{"left":"A heated day ahead","right":"A ready pause-plan"}],"relearn":"Slow breaths, stepping away, repair, support and a ready plan each fit a different hard moment.","persona":"Suresh","source":"moment to tool"},
  {"id":"bc-1360","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each tool to what it actually does.","pairs":[{"left":"The pause-before-you-react step","right":"Stops a harmful reaction"},{"left":"The repair routine","right":"Heals a rupture afterward"},{"left":"Knowing your triggers","right":"Catches the old reflex early"},{"left":"Tending your own wounds","right":"Stops the cycle with you"},{"left":"Self-compassion on a slip","right":"Keeps you in the change"}],"relearn":"Each tool has a job: pause stops the reaction, repair heals, triggers warn, healing ends the cycle.","persona":"any","source":"tool to job"},
  {"id":"bc-1361","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each calm-down step to when you'd use it.","pairs":[{"left":"Count five slow breaths","right":"The anger is just rising"},{"left":"Hand over to your partner","right":"You're close to your limit"},{"left":"Splash cold water","right":"You need to reset your body"},{"left":"Step onto the balcony","right":"You must leave the room safely"},{"left":"Name the feeling aloud","right":"You want to slow the surge"}],"relearn":"Breaths, a hand-over, cold water, stepping out and naming the feeling each suit a different stage.","persona":"Vikram","source":"step to moment"},
  {"id":"bc-1362","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each part of a clean repair to its words.","pairs":[{"left":"Own what you did","right":"\"I shouted and that wasn't okay\""},{"left":"Clear the child of blame","right":"\"It wasn't your fault\""},{"left":"Reconnect","right":"\"I love you and I'm here\""},{"left":"Name what you'll try","right":"\"I'll take a breath next time\""},{"left":"Invite them back","right":"\"Shall we start the evening again?\""}],"relearn":"A clean repair owns it, clears the child of blame, reconnects, and names what you'll try next.","persona":"Divya","source":"repair in words"},
  {"id":"bc-1363","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each co-parenting move to what it builds.","pairs":[{"left":"\"Let's choose differently together\"","right":"A shared plan"},{"left":"Backing each other in the moment","right":"A united front"},{"left":"A calm hand-over when you're spent","right":"A safety net"},{"left":"Agreeing no one hits, ever","right":"A clear family rule"},{"left":"Steadying a guilt-spiralling partner","right":"Room to repair"}],"relearn":"Choosing together, backing each other, calm hand-overs and a no-hitting rule build a steady home.","persona":"Vikram","source":"co-parent builds"},
  {"id":"bc-1364","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each sign to the tool it calls for.","pairs":[{"left":"Jaw clenching, voice rising","right":"Pause and breathe now"},{"left":"You just slipped","right":"Plan your repair"},{"left":"The same trigger keeps firing","right":"Tend its root in support"},{"left":"You fear real harm","right":"Reach for help today"},{"left":"You managed a calm response","right":"Notice the progress"}],"relearn":"Read the sign and reach for its tool: pause, repair, support, urgent help, or noticing progress.","persona":"Suresh","source":"sign to tool"},
  {"id":"bc-1365","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each worry a parent has to the reassurance that fits.","pairs":[{"left":"\"Needing help means I've failed\"","right":"It's the most caring thing you can do"},{"left":"\"One bad evening ruined it\"","right":"The repair is what they'll remember"},{"left":"\"My past has nothing to do with it\"","right":"Tending it protects your child"},{"left":"\"I'll never change\"","right":"Change is gradual; slips are part of it"},{"left":"\"Asking is shameful\"","right":"Reaching out is courageous, not shameful"}],"relearn":"Needing help isn't failure, repair beats one bad night, healing protects your child, change is gradual.","persona":"Priya","source":"worry to reassurance"},
  {"id":"bc-1366","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each routine to the moment it belongs in.","pairs":[{"left":"The morning check-in","right":"Spot a heavy day early"},{"left":"The bedtime repair","right":"Close any rupture before sleep"},{"left":"The weekly reflect","right":"Count the calmer wins"},{"left":"The trigger note","right":"Learn what sets you off"},{"left":"The support call","right":"Tend what's too heavy alone"}],"relearn":"Small routines, a check-in, a repair, a reflect, a trigger note, a support call, keep the change alive.","persona":"any","source":"routine to moment"},
  {"id":"bc-1367","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each escalation to the safe alternative.","pairs":[{"left":"Raising a hand","right":"Lower it and step back"},{"left":"Shouting to control","right":"Drop your volume, hold the limit"},{"left":"Shaming to motivate","right":"Name the behaviour, not the child"},{"left":"Cornering them in anger","right":"Give space, return when calm"},{"left":"Threatening to scare","right":"State a clear, kind consequence"}],"relearn":"Each harsh reflex has a firm-and-kind alternative that holds the limit without fear or harm.","persona":"Suresh","source":"reflex to alternative"},
  {"id":"bc-1368","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each tool to the cycle it helps break.","pairs":[{"left":"The pause step","right":"The hitting reflex"},{"left":"Speaking up warmly","right":"The silent-treatment habit"},{"left":"A clean apology","right":"The never-admit-fault rule"},{"left":"Tending your wounds","right":"Old pain leaking onto your child"},{"left":"Asking for support","right":"Carrying it all alone"}],"relearn":"Each tool targets an inherited cycle: the pause, speaking up, apology, healing and asking for help.","persona":"Suresh","source":"tool to cycle"},
  {"id":"bc-1369","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Asking for help to parent more gently means I'm a weak or bad parent.\"","myth":{"un":"Asking for help to parent more gently means I'm a weak or bad parent.","re":"Reaching for help to parent more gently is one of the most caring, courageous things you can do.","why":"It puts your child first, never shameful."},"relearn":"Needing help to parent gently isn't weakness; it's a caring, courageous act for your child.","persona":"any","source":"help isn't weakness"},
  {"id":"bc-1370","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If I can't fix this on my own, there's no point reaching out.\"","myth":{"un":"If I can't fix this on my own, there's no point reaching out.","re":"Heavy feelings from your past can route to support like Tele-MANAS 14416; you don't have to carry it alone.","why":"Tended pain leaks less onto your child."},"relearn":"You don't have to fix it alone; support like Tele-MANAS 14416 helps you tend what's heavy.","persona":"Suresh","source":"not alone"},
  {"id":"bc-1371","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Stepping away when I'm furious means I'm abandoning my child.\"","myth":{"un":"Stepping away when I'm furious means I'm abandoning my child.","re":"Stepping away briefly, with your child kept safe, to calm down is a protective, caring move, not abandonment.","why":"It keeps the heated moment safe."},"relearn":"Stepping away to calm down, child kept safe, protects them; it isn't abandonment.","persona":"any","source":"stepping away protects"},
  {"id":"bc-1372","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"There's no tool for the heat of the moment; you just react.\"","myth":{"un":"There's no tool for the heat of the moment; you just react.","re":"A simple plan readied in advance, pause, breathe, step away if needed, repair after, gives you a path in the heat.","why":"A ready path beats raw willpower."},"relearn":"A pause-breathe-step-away-repair plan readied ahead gives you a path when anger surges.","persona":"any","source":"there is a tool"},
  {"id":"bc-1373","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Reaching out for my child's safety will only get my family in trouble.\"","myth":{"un":"Reaching out for my child's safety will only get my family in trouble.","re":"When you fear real harm, reaching out, Be the Safe Adult, Childline 1098, protects your child and gets you support.","why":"Safety and support come first."},"relearn":"If you fear real harm, reaching for help protects your child; it's support, not punishment.","persona":"Suresh","source":"safety not trouble"},
  {"id":"bc-1374","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"I've slipped again, so all my effort to change was pointless.\"","myth":{"un":"I've slipped again, so all my effort to change was pointless.","re":"Change is gradual and slips are part of it; each pause, each repair, each calmer day is real progress.","why":"Progress isn't all-or-nothing."},"relearn":"A slip doesn't erase your progress; each pause and repair is a real step in gradual change.","persona":"Suresh","source":"effort not pointless"},
  {"id":"bc-1375","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"My partner and I were both raised with hitting, so we're stuck with it.\"","myth":{"un":"My partner and I were both raised with hitting, so we're stuck with it.","re":"Co-parents can choose differently together and back each other up; the inheritance you share isn't a sentence.","why":"You can choose new patterns together."},"relearn":"Two parents raised with harsh discipline can still choose differently together and back each other up.","persona":"Vikram","source":"choose together"},
  {"id":"bc-1376","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each move: a tool that helps break the cycle, or an old reflex to rethink?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Pausing before you react"},{"id":"b","text":"Hitting when overwhelmed"},{"id":"c","text":"Repairing after a slip"},{"id":"d","text":"Shaming to control"},{"id":"e","text":"Reaching for support"},{"id":"f","text":"Carrying it alone in shame"}],"bins":[{"id":"tool","label":"Helpful tool","valence":"pos"},{"id":"rethink","label":"Rethink it","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"tool","b":"rethink","c":"tool","d":"rethink","e":"tool","f":"rethink"},"relearn":"Pausing, repairing and reaching for support help; hitting, shaming and isolating shame need a rethink.","persona":"any","source":"tool or rethink"},
  {"id":"bc-1377","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each one: a realistic way to change, or a setup to fail?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"One small change at a time"},{"id":"b","text":"Becoming perfect overnight"},{"id":"c","text":"Self-compassion when you slip"},{"id":"d","text":"Quitting at the first relapse"},{"id":"e","text":"Counting your calmer days"},{"id":"f","text":"Shaming yourself harder"}],"bins":[{"id":"real","label":"Realistic","valence":"pos"},{"id":"fail","label":"Setup to fail","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"real","b":"fail","c":"real","d":"fail","e":"real","f":"fail"},"relearn":"Small changes, self-compassion and counting wins are realistic; perfectionism and self-shaming set you up to fail.","persona":"any","source":"realistic or fail"},
  {"id":"bc-1378","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each situation: tend it yourself, or route it to outside help?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A bedtime slip you can repair"},{"id":"b","text":"Your past trauma resurfacing hard"},{"id":"c","text":"A rising temper you can pause"},{"id":"d","text":"Fear you might really harm your child"},{"id":"e","text":"A trigger you can name and breathe through"},{"id":"f","text":"Pain too heavy to carry alone"}],"bins":[{"id":"self","label":"Tend yourself","valence":"pos"},{"id":"route","label":"Route to help","valence":"tell"}],"key":{"a":"self","b":"route","c":"self","d":"route","e":"self","f":"route"},"relearn":"You can repair slips and pause tempers yourself; deep trauma and real-harm fears route to help.","persona":"Suresh","source":"tend or route"},
  {"id":"bc-1379","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each line said to a struggling parent: supportive, or shaming?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"You're already breaking the pattern by worrying\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"What kind of parent does that?\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"Let's find you real support\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"You've scarred them for life\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"You were raised with it; you can still choose new\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"You're an abuser\""}],"bins":[{"id":"support","label":"Supportive","valence":"pos"},{"id":"shame","label":"Shaming","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"support","b":"shame","c":"support","d":"shame","e":"support","f":"shame"},"relearn":"Compassion and a real route help a struggling parent; verdicts and labels make them hide it.","persona":"Priya","source":"support or shame"},
  {"id":"bc-1380","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each helpline use: the right route, or the wrong fit?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Tele-MANAS 14416 for your own heavy past"},{"id":"b","text":"Childline 1098 for a child in danger"},{"id":"c","text":"Emergency 112 for a life-threatening crisis"},{"id":"d","text":"Tele-MANAS for a child being harmed right now"},{"id":"e","text":"Be the Safe Adult to route a child to safety"},{"id":"f","text":"112 just to vent your frustration"}],"bins":[{"id":"right","label":"Right route","valence":"pos"},{"id":"wrong","label":"Wrong fit","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"right","b":"right","c":"right","d":"wrong","e":"right","f":"wrong"},"relearn":"Match the route: Tele-MANAS for your wellbeing, Childline 1098 and 112 for a child in danger.","persona":"any","source":"right route"},
  {"id":"bc-1381","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each calm-down idea: it keeps the moment safe, or it makes it riskier?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Stepping into another room to breathe"},{"id":"b","text":"Bottling it until you explode later"},{"id":"c","text":"Slowing your breath before you speak"},{"id":"d","text":"Drinking to numb the anger"},{"id":"e","text":"Handing over to your partner"},{"id":"f","text":"Driving fast to blow off steam"}],"bins":[{"id":"safe","label":"Keeps it safe","valence":"pos"},{"id":"risky","label":"Makes it riskier","valence":"uhoh"}],"key":{"a":"safe","b":"risky","c":"safe","d":"risky","e":"safe","f":"risky"},"relearn":"Stepping away, slow breathing and handing over keep a heated moment safe; bottling, numbing and reckless venting don't.","persona":"Vikram","source":"safe or risky"},
  {"id":"bc-1382","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each thing: it counts as real progress, or it's a myth about change?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A calmer response you managed today"},{"id":"b","text":"\"If I slip once, I've failed\""},{"id":"c","text":"A slip you went back and repaired"},{"id":"d","text":"\"Real change happens overnight\""},{"id":"e","text":"Catching a trigger before it acted"},{"id":"f","text":"\"Wanting to change but slipping means it's hopeless\""}],"bins":[{"id":"progress","label":"Real progress","valence":"pos"},{"id":"myth","label":"A change-myth","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"progress","b":"myth","c":"progress","d":"myth","e":"progress","f":"myth"},"relearn":"Calmer responses, repaired slips and caught triggers are real progress; all-or-nothing thinking is a myth.","persona":"any","source":"progress or myth"},
  {"id":"bc-1383","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each co-parenting move: it builds a steady home, or it undermines one?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Let's choose differently for our kid\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"You're just like your father\""},{"id":"c","text":"Backing each other up in the moment"},{"id":"d","text":"Undercutting your partner in front of the kids"},{"id":"e","text":"A calm hand-over when one of you is spent"},{"id":"f","text":"Keeping score of each other's slips"}],"bins":[{"id":"build","label":"Builds it","valence":"pos"},{"id":"undermine","label":"Undermines it","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"build","b":"undermine","c":"build","d":"undermine","e":"build","f":"undermine"},"relearn":"Choosing together, backing each other and calm hand-overs build a steady home; blame and scorekeeping erode it.","persona":"Vikram","source":"build or undermine"},
  {"id":"bc-1384","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: reaching for help to parent more gently is brave, not shameful. Where do you feel that?","prompt":"What's alive for you?","options":["It's brave","Hard to ask","I want to try"],"affirm":"However it lands, reaching for help to parent more gently is a caring, courageous act, never shameful.","relearn":"Asking for help to parent gently is brave and caring; there's no shame in it.","persona":"any","source":"brave to ask"},
  {"id":"bc-1385","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: think of one calm-down tool you could ready before the next hard evening. Picture it.","prompt":"Which feels yours?","options":["Slow breaths","Step away","Hand over"],"affirm":"Any of these is a real tool; readying one before the heat gives you a path when anger climbs.","relearn":"Choosing a calm-down tool in advance gives you a ready path for the next hard moment.","persona":"Suresh","source":"ready your tool"},
  {"id":"bc-1386","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: change here is gradual, and slips are part of it. Sit with that for a moment.","prompt":"How does it land?","options":["A relief","Still hard","I can keep going"],"affirm":"Whatever you feel, each pause, each repair, each calmer day is real progress; you don't have to be perfect.","relearn":"Change is gradual and slips are part of it; progress, not perfection, is the goal.","persona":"any","source":"gradual change"},
  {"id":"bc-1387","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: needing support for your own old pain isn't a flaw. Notice what comes up.","prompt":"What surfaces?","options":["Some resistance","Quiet relief","I'm curious"],"affirm":"Whatever surfaces is welcome; tending your own wounds, with support like Tele-MANAS 14416, is a gift to your child.","relearn":"Tending your own old pain, with support, is caring for your child, not a personal flaw.","persona":"any","source":"support not flaw"},
  {"id":"bc-1388","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you noticed the pattern, and noticing is where the power to choose begins. Feel that?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["I feel it","Still early","I'm choosing"],"affirm":"However ready you feel, noticing the pattern is exactly where you regain the power to choose differently.","relearn":"Noticing the inherited pattern is the first step that gives you the power to choose differently.","persona":"any","source":"noticing is power"},
  {"id":"bc-1389","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the repair after a slip is what your child remembers most. Let that settle.","prompt":"What do you notice?","options":["That eases me","I want to repair","Good to know"],"affirm":"Wherever you are, going back to repair, owning it without blame, is what your child carries, not the slip.","relearn":"The repair after a slip, not the slip itself, is what your child remembers and learns from.","persona":"Divya","source":"repair remembered"},
  {"id":"bc-1390","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the cycle can end with you, one calmer choice at a time. How does that sit?","prompt":"What's stirring?","options":["It ends with me","One choice","Let's go"],"affirm":"However it stirs, noticing, repairing, healing and reaching for help are how the harmful cycle ends with you.","relearn":"Noticing, repairing, healing and asking for help are how the cycle ends with you, one choice at a time.","persona":"any","source":"ends with me"},
  {"id":"bc-1391","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you and your co-parent can choose new patterns together and back each other up. Picture it.","prompt":"What feels possible?","options":["We can try","One step","I'll start"],"affirm":"Whatever feels possible, choosing differently together and backing each other up makes the change far more likely to hold.","relearn":"Co-parents choosing new patterns together and backing each other up help the change hold.","persona":"Vikram","source":"together it holds"},
  {"id":"bc-1392","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"In this calm-down plan, spot the two steps that would make a heated moment riskier.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Pause and take a few slow breaths","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Bottle it up until you explode later","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Step into another room if you can","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Numb the anger with a drink","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Repair with your child once you're calm","trick":false}],"why":"Bottling it up and numbing with a drink don't calm anger, they make a slip more likely; the other three keep the moment safe.","relearn":"Pausing, stepping away and repairing keep a moment safe; bottling and numbing make it riskier.","persona":"any","source":"spot risky steps"},
  {"id":"bc-1393","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"A friend gives advice to a struggling parent. Spot the two lines that shame instead of help.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"You're already breaking the pattern by worrying\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"What's wrong with you as a parent?\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Let's find you real support, no judgment\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"You've scarred them for life\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"You were raised with it; you can still choose new\"","trick":false}],"why":"\"What's wrong with you\" and \"scarred for life\" shame the parent into hiding it; the other three offer compassion and a route.","relearn":"Compassion and a real route help a struggling parent; verdicts shame them.","persona":"Priya","source":"spot the shaming lines"},
  {"id":"bc-1394","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"Here's how a parent talks about help. Spot the two beliefs that keep them stuck.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Reaching out is caring, not shameful\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"If I can't fix it alone, there's no point\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Tele-MANAS 14416 can help with my past\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Asking for help means I'm a bad parent\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Slips are part of change, not the end\"","trick":false}],"why":"\"No point unless I fix it alone\" and \"asking means I'm bad\" keep a parent stuck; the other three open the door to support.","relearn":"Reaching for help is caring; the beliefs that asking is shameful or pointless keep a parent stuck.","persona":"Suresh","source":"spot stuck beliefs"},
  {"id":"bc-1395","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"A parent is about to react in anger. Spot the two responses that aren't safe or kind.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Lower your voice and slow your breathing","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Raise a hand to make them listen","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Name the feeling and hold the limit","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Corner and shame them to scare them","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Step away to calm, child kept safe","trick":false}],"why":"Raising a hand and shaming to scare are harmful reflexes; lowering your voice, naming the feeling and stepping away are firm-and-kind.","relearn":"Firm-and-kind responses hold the limit safely; raising a hand or shaming to scare are reflexes to drop.","persona":"Suresh","source":"spot unsafe responses"},
  {"id":"bc-1396","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two routing choices that send a child-safety worry to the wrong place.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Child in danger → Childline 1098","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Child being harmed now → Tele-MANAS 14416","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Life-threatening crisis → emergency 112","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Route a child to safety → Be the Safe Adult","trick":false},{"id":"e","text":"A child in danger → just wait and see","trick":true}],"why":"Tele-MANAS is for your wellbeing, not a child being harmed, and \"wait and see\" leaves a child in danger; a child at risk goes to Childline 1098 or 112.","relearn":"A child in danger routes to Childline 1098 or 112, never Tele-MANAS.","persona":"any","source":"spot wrong routing"},
  {"id":"bc-1397","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"A parent sums up their progress. Spot the two all-or-nothing thoughts dragging them down.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I managed two calmer evenings this week\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"One slip means I've ruined everything\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"I repaired the time I snapped\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"If I'm not perfect, why bother\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"I caught a trigger before it acted\"","trick":false}],"why":"\"One slip ruins everything\" and \"why bother if I'm not perfect\" are all-or-nothing traps; the other three are real progress.","relearn":"Calmer days, repairs and caught triggers are real progress; all-or-nothing thinking drags you down.","persona":"any","source":"spot all-or-nothing"},
  {"id":"bc-1398","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"Two co-parents talk. Spot the two lines that undermine choosing differently together.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Let's back each other up on no hitting\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You're useless, just like your father\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Tap me in when you're at your limit\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"I'll undercut you in front of the kids\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"We were both raised with it; let's choose new\"","trick":false}],"why":"The insult and undercutting in front of the kids break the united front; the other three build it.","relearn":"Backing each other and choosing new patterns build a united front; insults break it.","persona":"Vikram","source":"spot undermining lines"},
  {"id":"bc-1399","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"name and step away","hook":"Your son Rahul has ignored you three times about switching off the TV, and your anger is surging.","setup":"He's still on the sofa, remote in hand.","steps":[{"prompt":"Rahul says, eyes on the screen: \"Five more minutes, Papa!\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm too angry. I'll take a minute and come back.\"","then":"Rahul glances up, surprised, and turns the volume down.","best":true},{"text":"\"Get out of my sight, now, before I really lose it.\"","then":"Rahul drops the remote and runs to his room, frightened."},{"text":"\"Switch it off this second or you'll regret it.\"","then":"Rahul switches it off fast, watching your hands."},{"text":"\"Fine. Watch it. I'm not arguing with you any more.\"","then":"Rahul grins and turns the volume up. The limit is gone."}],"why":"Naming your anger and stepping away shows him self-control instead of a threat."},{"prompt":"A few minutes later, Rahul asks: \"Are you still angry with me?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes, I am, and it's all your fault for ignoring me.\"","then":"Rahul looks down, sure now that your anger is his job."},{"text":"\"No, no, I'm fine. Forget it.\"","then":"Rahul isn't sure whether to believe you, and stays wary."},{"text":"\"Don't ask. Just do what I say next time.\"","then":"Rahul nods, silent and wary, but doesn't move away."},{"text":"\"I was, but I've calmed down. Let's sort out the TV.\"","then":"Rahul nods, relieved, and waits to hear what's next.","best":true}],"why":"Saying you've calmed down shows him feelings can be handled, and then the issue sorted."},{"prompt":"Rahul asks: \"Why do you always make me stop the TV, Papa?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Because I said so. That's enough.\"","then":"Rahul stomps off, sulking, and ignores you through dinner."},{"text":"\"Never mind. Watch as long as you like.\"","then":"Rahul watches till bedtime, and tomorrow the same fight begins again."},{"text":"\"Homework comes first. You can watch again after dinner.\"","then":"Rahul sighs, but leaves the TV and takes out his books.","best":true},{"text":"\"Because you'll fail your exams if this carries on, beta.\"","then":"Rahul takes out his books, anxious, and can't focus on them."}],"why":"A clear, calm reason keeps the limit firm and kind, so he follows it without fear."}],"relearn":"Naming your anger and stepping away to breathe models self-control, far better than threatening your child."},
  {"id":"bc-1400","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Priya","source":"open the door","hook":"Your close friend Karan phones late at night, his voice thick with shame.","setup":"You step out onto the balcony to listen.","steps":[{"prompt":"Karan says: \"I hit my daughter again today. I swore I never would.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I can't believe you'd do that. What's wrong with you?\"","then":"A long silence. \"I shouldn't have called,\" he says, but he doesn't hang up."},{"text":"\"Arre, all fathers lose it sometimes. Don't worry.\"","then":"Karan laughs weakly. \"You don't get it. This isn't just losing it.\""},{"text":"\"You have to promise me it will never happen again.\"","then":"\"I've promised myself that a hundred times,\" he says quietly."},{"text":"\"Thank you for telling me. I'm listening, not judging.\"","then":"Karan's breath shakes. \"I didn't know who else to tell.\"","best":true}],"why":"Listening without judgment makes it safe for him to keep talking."},{"prompt":"Karan says: \"Maybe I'm just a monster, like my father.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Well, hitting a child is what monsters do.\"","then":"Karan goes silent, and you hear a door close behind him."},{"text":"\"Your father was far worse. Don't be so hard on yourself.\"","then":"Karan agrees a little too quickly, and the urgency fades."},{"text":"\"You're not a monster. You learned this, and you can unlearn it.\"","then":"Karan is quiet, then says, \"I want to. I just don't know how.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's not get dramatic, Karan. Just calm down first.\"","then":"Karan goes quiet. \"I thought you, of all people, would understand.\""}],"why":"Separating him from the pattern gives him hope that change is possible."},{"prompt":"Karan asks: \"But who would I even talk to about this?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Talk to your wife. She can handle the kids for now.\"","then":"Karan hesitates. \"She'll just be angry with me.\""},{"text":"\"Try Tele-MANAS 14416. I can sit with you while you call.\"","then":"Karan writes the number down. \"Tomorrow morning. Will you come?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"No one. Keep this quiet, or people will talk.\"","then":"Karan agrees, his voice flat, still carrying it all alone."},{"text":"\"I don't know. Maybe you just need to try harder.\"","then":"Karan sighs. \"I've been trying harder for years.\""}],"why":"A real route to support gives him somewhere to take the shame, instead of carrying it home."},{"prompt":"Karan asks: \"What if I feel it rising again tomorrow?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Step away while she's safe, then call me or Childline 1098.\"","then":"Karan repeats it slowly. \"Step away. Call. Okay, I can do that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Sleep on it tonight. You'll feel calmer in the morning.\"","then":"Karan agrees, but the morning rush arrives with no plan at all."},{"text":"\"Grit your teeth and hold it in. You can do it.\"","then":"Karan sighs. \"That's what I tried today.\""},{"text":"\"Keep your daughter away from you all day tomorrow.\"","then":"Karan agrees, and his daughter spends the day wondering why."}],"why":"A simple plan for the next hard moment, with a route to help, keeps his daughter safe."}],"relearn":"Meeting a struggling parent with compassion and a route to help opens the door that judgment slams shut."},
  {"id":"bc-1401","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"tap in","hook":"After a long day, you've spent an hour settling your crying toddler Veer, and your patience is gone.","setup":"Veer is still wailing in your arms.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your wife Sneha calls from the next room: \"Everything okay in there?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm at my limit. Can you take over for ten minutes?\"","then":"Sneha comes in at once. \"Go. I've got him.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"All fine. I'll just push through it.\"","then":"Sneha goes back to her work. Veer cries on, and your jaw tightens."},{"text":"\"You never help me with bedtime. You know that, right?\"","then":"Sneha comes in, stung. \"I was waiting for you to ask.\""},{"text":"\"He's impossible tonight. You deal with him.\"","then":"Sneha takes Veer, frowning at your tone, and he cries harder."}],"why":"Asking for a tap-in before you snap is a tool, not a failure."},{"prompt":"Later, Sneha says: \"You seemed really close to snapping tonight.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm fine. I can handle it.\"","then":"Sneha looks unconvinced. \"You don't have to handle it alone, you know.\""},{"text":"\"Veer was being impossible. That's all.\"","then":"Sneha frowns. \"He's two. He was tired.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe I'm just not good at this dad stuff.\"","then":"Sneha sighs. \"You're good at it. You're exhausted.\""},{"text":"\"I was. I've been running on empty lately.\"","then":"Sneha nods. \"Then let's split bedtimes this week.\"","best":true}],"why":"Being honest about how empty you are lets her help before it gets worse."},{"prompt":"Sneha asks: \"How should we handle nights like this from now on?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'll just have to be more patient.\"","then":"Sneha shrugs. \"That's what you said last week.\""},{"text":"\"You take all the bedtimes from now on. You're calmer.\"","then":"Sneha's face tightens. \"I'm tired too, you know.\""},{"text":"\"A signal. 'Tap in' means the other takes over.\"","then":"Sneha smiles. \"Deal. And no one keeps score.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"We'll figure it out as we go along.\"","then":"Sneha nods, and the next hard night arrives with no plan."}],"why":"Agreeing a simple signal in advance makes it easy to ask before either of you snaps."}],"relearn":"Asking your co-parent to tap in before you snap is a tool, not a failure; pushing through risks a slip."},
  {"id":"bc-1402","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"clean repair","hook":"You snapped at your daughter Mishti for dawdling over dinner, and she looked crushed.","setup":"The plates are cleared, and you find her in her room.","steps":[{"prompt":"Mishti says, very quietly: \"I'm sorry I was slow.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I only shouted because you wouldn't listen.\"","then":"Mishti nods, head down. \"I'll listen next time.\""},{"text":"\"No, I'm the one who's sorry. Shouting wasn't okay.\"","then":"Mishti looks up, surprised, and wipes her nose on her sleeve.","best":true},{"text":"\"It's okay, beta. Let's just forget about it now.\"","then":"Mishti nods, but her face stays sad."},{"text":"\"Thank you for saying sorry. I'm sorry too.\"","then":"Mishti looks relieved, still sure that she started it."}],"why":"Taking her sorry off her shoulders and owning your shout makes the repair clean."},{"prompt":"Mishti asks in a small voice: \"Was I naughty, being so slow?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Being slow isn't naughty. Shouting was my mistake.\"","then":"Mishti thinks about it, then leans against you.","best":true},{"text":"\"A bit. That's why Mumma got so upset.\"","then":"Mishti's face falls. She hears that your shout was earned."},{"text":"\"Let's not talk about who was naughty.\"","then":"Mishti nods, still unsure whose fault it was."},{"text":"\"You were slow, but I love you anyway.\"","then":"Mishti smiles a little, still carrying the idea that she was bad."}],"why":"Separating her behaviour from your reaction keeps the blame where it belongs."},{"prompt":"Mishti asks: \"Can we have a hug now?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Only if you promise to eat faster tomorrow.\"","then":"Mishti promises, and the hug feels like a deal."},{"text":"\"In a minute. Mumma needs to finish the dishes.\"","then":"By the time you come back, she's asleep, the hug still waiting."},{"text":"\"You're a big girl. You don't need a hug for everything.\"","then":"Mishti pulls back, embarrassed for asking."},{"text":"\"Yes, please. I love you, always.\"","then":"She squeezes you tight, and the room feels warm again.","best":true}],"why":"A warm, unconditional yes finishes the repair and tells her the bond is safe."}],"relearn":"A clean repair owns your reaction without blaming the child; blaming them transfers your fault to them."},
  {"id":"bc-1403","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"steady toward repair","hook":"At work, you get a call from your wife Meera. She snapped at your toddler Jiya and can't stop crying.","setup":"You step into a quiet corridor.","steps":[{"prompt":"Meera sobs: \"I screamed at her. She's only three. What kind of mother does that?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Great. Now you've gone and damaged her.\"","then":"Meera goes silent. \"I know,\" she whispers, barely holding on."},{"text":"\"Calm down. You're overreacting to one shout.\"","then":"Meera's voice tightens. \"You weren't here.\""},{"text":"\"A tired one. You slipped, and it happens to all of us.\"","then":"Meera's breathing slows a little. \"She looked so frightened.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"What did Jiya do to make you scream at her like that?\"","then":"Meera starts listing Jiya's tantrums, then stops. \"But I'm the grown-up.\""}],"why":"Naming the slip without blame steadies her enough to think about repair."},{"prompt":"Meera asks: \"How do I fix it? She's too little to understand sorry.\"","options":[{"text":"\"She's three. She'll have forgotten all about it by lunch.\"","then":"Meera sounds unsure, the guilt still sitting heavy."},{"text":"\"Hold her close and say sorry softly. She'll feel it.\"","then":"Meera sniffs. \"Okay. She's in her room with her bunny.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Buy her a new toy today. That will fix it.\"","then":"Meera hesitates, unsure a toy is what Jiya needs."},{"text":"\"Leave her alone for a while to calm down.\"","then":"Meera agrees, and Jiya stays alone and upset in her room."}],"why":"A soft voice and a cuddle are the repair a little one understands, and it counts."},{"prompt":"Meera says: \"I'm just so tired. I don't know how to stop this happening.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'll take bedtime this week. Let's plan for you to rest.\"","then":"Meera lets out a long breath. \"That would help so much.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Just try a little harder to stay calm, na?\"","then":"Meera goes quiet. \"I am trying,\" she says."},{"text":"\"Everyone's tired with a toddler. You'll get used to it.\"","then":"Meera sighs, feeling more alone than before."},{"text":"\"Let's talk about it this weekend, when there's time.\"","then":"Meera agrees, and faces the rest of the week just as tired."}],"why":"Sharing the load helps her rest, which makes the next slip less likely."}],"relearn":"Steadying a guilt-spiralling co-parent toward repair helps the child far more than piling on blame."},
  {"id":"bc-1404","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"own your slip","hook":"You took away your teenage daughter Priti's phone for shouting at her grandmother.","setup":"She corners you in the kitchen.","steps":[{"prompt":"Priti demands: \"Why am I punished for shouting when you yell all the time?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Because I'm the parent. Do as I say, not as I do.\"","then":"Priti rolls her eyes. \"That's so fake.\""},{"text":"\"I only yell because you children never listen to a word.\"","then":"Priti scoffs. \"So when I yell, is that your fault too?\""},{"text":"\"Let's not fight about this now. We'll talk later.\"","then":"Priti turns to go, then swings back. \"No. We're talking now.\""},{"text":"\"You're right. My yelling isn't fair either. I'm working on it.\"","then":"Priti blinks, thrown, then narrows her eyes, testing you.","best":true}],"why":"Admitting your own slip honestly earns her respect in a way demands can't."},{"prompt":"Priti asks: \"So why should I say sorry to Nani if you never say sorry?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Because she's your elder, and that's the end of it.\"","then":"Priti shrugs, and the sorry to Nani never happens."},{"text":"\"I do say sorry. You just never notice.\"","then":"\"Name one time,\" she says, arms folded."},{"text":"\"Fair. I'll own my slips, and you'll own yours with Nani.\"","then":"Priti crosses her arms, then nods. \"Deal. But I'm holding you to it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. Forget the sorry this time.\"","then":"Priti grins, and Nani is left waiting for an apology."}],"why":"Holding yourself to the same standard makes the limit fair, so she's likelier to follow it."},{"prompt":"Priti asks: \"Do I still lose my phone for the whole weekend?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No. Since I yell too, it's cancelled.\"","then":"Priti takes the phone, and the shouting at Nani is quietly forgotten."},{"text":"\"Yes, the weekend stands. And I'll own it next time I yell.\"","then":"Priti groans, but doesn't argue. \"Fine. I'll be watching.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Yes, and one more word from you and it's two whole weekends.\"","then":"Priti slams the cupboard door and goes silent."},{"text":"\"Let's cut it to one day if you stop arguing.\"","then":"Priti agrees, and learns that arguing shortens punishments."}],"why":"Owning your slip doesn't cancel her consequence. Firm and kind holds both."},{"prompt":"A few days later, you yell at Priti over her messy room. She raises an eyebrow and asks: \"Well?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You're right. I yelled, and I'm sorry. Let's try again.\"","then":"Priti grins. \"See? Not so hard.\" Then she starts on the desk.","best":true},{"text":"\"That's different. Your room is a disgrace.\"","then":"Priti shrugs. \"Knew it,\" she says, and walks off."},{"text":"\"Don't you dare use that against me.\"","then":"Priti's eyebrow drops, and so does her trust in you."},{"text":"\"I didn't yell, Priti. I only raised my voice a little.\"","then":"Priti snorts. \"Sure. A little.\""}],"why":"Owning your own slip, as you ask her to own hers, is what makes the fairness real to her."}],"relearn":"Admitting your own slips, instead of demanding obedience you don't model, earns the credibility kids actually follow."},
  {"id":"bc-1405","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"hold your line","hook":"At a family wedding, your son Aditya is wailing on the floor, and your Chacha is watching.","setup":"Chacha leans over to you.","steps":[{"prompt":"Chacha says: \"One tight slap and he'll never do this again.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I know that's how we grew up, Chacha. We're choosing another way.\"","then":"Chacha raises his eyebrows. \"Another way? Like what?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe you're right. I'll give him one, then.\"","then":"Chacha nods, satisfied. You lift your hand, then hesitate as Aditya freezes."},{"text":"\"With respect, Chacha, hitting children is cruel. You're wrong.\"","then":"Chacha stiffens, and the aunties turn to stare."},{"text":"\"Please, not now, Chacha. Everyone is watching.\"","then":"Chacha shrugs and tells the aunties the boy is spoilt."}],"why":"Honouring how you both grew up while holding your line keeps it warm and firm."},{"prompt":"Chacha asks: \"Well? What are you going to do with him, then?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Nothing. Children should express themselves freely.\"","then":"Chacha snorts, and Aditya's wailing goes on."},{"text":"\"All right, no slap. I'll give him a good shout instead.\"","then":"You shout, and Aditya's wails rise. The aunties shake their heads."},{"text":"\"You didn't do much better with your own sons.\"","then":"Chacha's face darkens, and the evening turns sour."},{"text":"\"I'll take him aside, calm him, and hold the limit.\"","then":"Chacha grunts, watching as you crouch beside Aditya.","best":true}],"why":"Firm and kind isn't permissive. Calming him and holding the limit shows what you mean."},{"prompt":"Aditya, still sobbing, cries: \"I want the balloon, Papa!\"","options":[{"text":"\"Stop this drama right now. Everyone is looking.\"","then":"Aditya wails louder, and more heads turn."},{"text":"\"Okay, okay. I'll get you ten balloons. Just stop.\"","then":"Aditya stops at once, and learns that screaming works."},{"text":"\"You're upset. No balloon now, but you can hold my hand.\"","then":"Aditya hiccups, then grips your hand tight.","best":true},{"text":"\"If you stop crying now, you'll get a big chocolate later.\"","then":"Aditya sniffles to a stop, already bargaining for two."}],"why":"Naming the feeling and holding the limit calmly is what settles a tantrum."},{"prompt":"Later, your mother says: \"Chacha thinks you've gone soft. Are you sure about all this?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Maybe he's right, Amma. Honestly, I don't know any more.\"","then":"Your mother nods, and the old way starts to feel easier."},{"text":"\"I'm sure. Firm and kind works, and I want him to trust me.\"","then":"Your mother is quiet, then says, \"Your father never tried that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You all hit us, and look how we turned out.\"","then":"Your mother flinches, hurt, and the talk ends there."},{"text":"\"Please don't bring this up again, Amma.\"","then":"She drops it, but the doubt stays in the family."}],"why":"Holding your line warmly with family keeps you choosing differently."}],"relearn":"Holding your non-violent line with warmth, while honouring how you grew up, keeps you choosing differently."},
  {"id":"bc-1406","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Suresh","source":"ask before harm","hook":"Lately your anger at your son Chintu frightens you. Tonight you phone your elder brother Ramesh.","setup":"Your hands are shaking as he picks up.","steps":[{"prompt":"Ramesh asks: \"It's late. Is everything okay at home?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I lost my temper with Chintu again. It's nothing, really.\"","then":"Ramesh chuckles. \"Nothing? You called me at eleven.\""},{"text":"\"I'm scared of how angry I get with Chintu. I need help.\"","then":"Ramesh is quiet for a moment. \"Okay. I'm listening.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Chintu is out of control. I don't know what to do with him.\"","then":"Ramesh laughs. \"Boys will be boys.\" Then he pauses. \"You sound shaken, though.\""},{"text":"\"I nearly hit Chintu again. I'm a terrible father.\"","then":"Ramesh's voice softens. \"Hey. Slow down. You're not terrible.\""}],"why":"Naming the fear and asking for help keeps him safe. The fear is not a verdict on you as a father."},{"prompt":"Ramesh says: \"Bauji was hard on us too, you know, and we turned out fine.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I don't want to pass it on. I could really hurt him.\"","then":"Ramesh's voice changes. \"Okay. Then this is serious.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe you're right. I'm overthinking it.\"","then":"Ramesh sounds relieved, and your fear goes back into hiding."},{"text":"\"I know. I just needed to say it out loud.\"","then":"Ramesh seems relieved, though he doesn't hang up yet."},{"text":"\"You don't understand anything, Bhaiya. Just forget it.\"","then":"Ramesh goes quiet. \"Then help me understand,\" he says."}],"why":"Being clear about the risk helps others take it seriously and help you."},{"prompt":"Ramesh asks: \"You called me for a reason. What do you want to do about it?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Just be much stricter with him, so he doesn't set me off.\"","then":"Ramesh agrees, and the plan puts it all on Chintu."},{"text":"\"Stay away from him until I've calmed down for good.\"","then":"Ramesh hesitates. \"For how long? He's your son.\""},{"text":"\"Nothing. Telling you is enough for now.\"","then":"Ramesh sounds unsure, and there's still no plan."},{"text":"\"I'll call Tele-MANAS 14416 tonight. Can you check on me?\"","then":"Ramesh promises to call you back later tonight to ask how it went.","best":true}],"why":"A real support line and someone checking on you turn fear into a plan."},{"prompt":"Ramesh asks: \"Do you want me to come over tonight?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No, no. I don't want to trouble you so late.\"","then":"Ramesh hesitates. \"Are you sure?\" You say you are, and you aren't."},{"text":"\"No. Bhabhi will find out, and then everyone will know.\"","then":"Ramesh agrees to keep it quiet, and you're alone with it again tonight."},{"text":"\"Yes, please. I'd feel safer with you here.\"","then":"\"I'm leaving now,\" he says. \"Twenty minutes.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Only if Chintu starts acting up again.\"","then":"Ramesh agrees to wait for your call, and the night stays uncertain."}],"why":"Accepting help is caring and courageous. It is never shameful."},{"prompt":"Ramesh asks: \"And if it gets bad tonight, what will you do?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'll just hold it in, the way I always do.\"","then":"Ramesh is quiet. \"That's what worries me.\""},{"text":"\"Keep Chintu safe, step out, and call you or Childline 1098.\"","then":"\"Say it back to me once more,\" Ramesh says, and you do.","best":true},{"text":"\"I'll lock myself in the bathroom and wait for it to pass.\"","then":"Ramesh asks who will be with Chintu. You don't have an answer."},{"text":"\"It won't get bad. I've told you about it now.\"","then":"Ramesh sighs, not reassured."}],"why":"A clear crisis plan, with your child kept safe, is the most protective thing you can do."}],"relearn":"Asking for help when you fear harming your child is the bravest, most protective thing you can do."},
  {"id":"bc-1407","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"kind to yourself","hook":"After a rough week of snapping at your kids, you've been very hard on yourself.","setup":"It's late, and the house is finally quiet.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your friend Farah calls and asks: \"You sound low. What's going on?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm just a terrible parent, Farah. I'll never change.\"","then":"Farah pauses. \"Whoa. That's a harsh thing to say about yourself.\""},{"text":"\"Nothing much. Just tired. How are you doing?\"","then":"Farah pauses. \"Just tired? You don't sound just tired.\""},{"text":"\"I snapped a lot this week, and I'm being hard on myself.\"","then":"Farah's voice softens. \"That sounds heavy. Tell me.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"The kids were impossible this week, honestly.\"","then":"Farah laughs. \"Kids, na?\" You leave out the snapping."}],"why":"Naming what happened honestly, without a verdict on yourself, opens the door to support."},{"prompt":"Farah admits: \"I shouted at Zaid twice this week. What would you tell me?\"","options":[{"text":"\"That you should just try harder, the same as I should.\"","then":"Farah goes quiet. \"That sounds exhausting.\""},{"text":"\"That you're human, and you can repair it and keep going.\"","then":"Farah laughs softly. \"Then that goes for you too, doesn't it?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"That it's fine. Everyone does it. Just forget it.\"","then":"Farah hums. \"Maybe. But wouldn't you tell me to make it right?\""},{"text":"\"That's different. You're a good parent.\"","then":"Farah sighs. \"And you're not?\""}],"why":"Speaking to yourself as you would to a friend keeps you in the change."},{"prompt":"Farah says: \"Now say something kind about your own week. Out loud.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I slipped, but I'm trying and repairing. I can keep going.\"","then":"Farah cheers. \"There. Now stick that on your mirror.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I slipped, but I'm still a terrible parent.\"","then":"Farah groans. \"Halfway there. Try again.\""},{"text":"\"This feels a bit silly. Can we talk about something else?\"","then":"Farah lets it go. The harsh voice is waiting when you hang up."},{"text":"\"I'll be kind to myself once I stop snapping.\"","then":"Farah sighs. \"And if the snapping only stops once you're kinder?\""}],"why":"Self-compassion after a slip keeps you going. Harsh self-talk only repeats the cycle inward."}],"relearn":"Self-compassion after a slip keeps you in the change; harsh self-talk only repeats the cycle inward."},
  {"id":"bc-1408","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"steady the moment","hook":"Your wife Nandini just smacked your son Arnav, then froze, ashamed. Arnav is crying.","setup":"Nandini turns to you, stricken.","steps":[{"prompt":"Nandini whispers: \"I don't know what happened. I just lost it.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You're just like your father. You'll never change.\"","then":"Nandini flinches, and Arnav cries harder at the raised voices."},{"text":"\"It's okay. He was being really naughty.\"","then":"Nandini looks relieved, and Arnav hears that he deserved it."},{"text":"\"Let's both step out and calm down first.\"","then":"You both step out, and Arnav cries alone until you hurry back in."},{"text":"\"I'll sit with Arnav. You step out and breathe.\"","then":"Nandini nods and goes to the balcony. You kneel beside Arnav.","best":true}],"why":"Comforting your son first, while she calms down, keeps everyone safe without blame."},{"prompt":"Arnav sobs: \"Why did Mumma hit me?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Because you didn't listen to her, beta.\"","then":"Arnav cries harder, sure that he caused it."},{"text":"\"Shh. Mumma didn't mean it. Stop crying now.\"","then":"Arnav quietens, confused, his hurt tucked away."},{"text":"\"Hitting is never okay, and it wasn't your fault.\"","then":"Arnav's sobs slow, and he leans into you.","best":true},{"text":"\"Mumma was very bad to do that to you.\"","then":"Arnav looks scared, now worried about his mother too."}],"why":"Hearing that hitting is never okay, and not his fault, is what he needs first."},{"prompt":"Later, Nandini asks: \"How do I face him now? He must hate me.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Maybe keep away from him for a few days.\"","then":"Nandini nods miserably, and Arnav wonders why his mother avoids him."},{"text":"\"We'll go together. You say sorry, and I'll be beside you.\"","then":"Nandini takes a shaky breath. \"Okay. Together.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Buy him something nice. He'll forget about it.\"","then":"Nandini hesitates. A gift can't tell him it was wrong."},{"text":"\"I'll explain it to him. You don't need to.\"","then":"Nandini agrees, and Arnav never hears sorry from her."}],"why":"A shared repair, led by the one who slipped, turns one bad moment into recovery."},{"prompt":"That night, Nandini says: \"What if I do it again? My father did it all the time.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's plan for it, and you could call Tele-MANAS 14416.\"","then":"Nandini wipes her eyes. \"I think I need to. I'll call tomorrow.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You won't. Just be stronger than your father.\"","then":"Nandini nods, not believing it, and the fear stays."},{"text":"\"Let's not think about that. It was a one-off.\"","then":"Nandini nods, and the old pattern goes unexamined."},{"text":"\"Everyone smacks their kids sometimes. Relax.\"","then":"Nandini's shoulders drop, and the smack starts to look normal."}],"why":"Support for old wounds helps the cycle stop with her. If Arnav is ever at risk, call Childline 1098."}],"relearn":"Meeting a co-parent's lapse with a shared repair plan, not a verdict, turns one bad moment into a teachable recovery."},
  {"id":"bc-1409","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"own it with a teen","hook":"You snapped at your teenage son Yuvraj when he asked for help with his project.","setup":"Ten minutes later, he's back in the doorway.","steps":[{"prompt":"Yuvraj says: \"That wasn't fair. I only asked a question.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You're right. I snapped, and that wasn't fair. I'm sorry.\"","then":"Yuvraj blinks, surprised. \"Oh. Okay.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Don't you dare talk back to me, Yuvraj.\"","then":"Yuvraj's jaw tightens, but he stays in the doorway."},{"text":"\"I've had a long day. You don't know the stress I'm under.\"","then":"Yuvraj shrugs. \"Everyone has long days,\" he says."},{"text":"\"Fine, fine. What do you want now?\"","then":"Yuvraj hesitates, unsure whether to ask again."}],"why":"Owning your slip when he names it models respect, and it costs you no authority."},{"prompt":"Yuvraj asks: \"So will you help me with it now?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Only if you ask more politely this time.\"","then":"Yuvraj stiffens. \"I did ask politely.\""},{"text":"\"I'm still tired. Maybe tomorrow, if there's time.\"","then":"Yuvraj nods and goes back to his room, the project still stuck."},{"text":"\"Just look it up online. You're old enough.\"","then":"Yuvraj sighs and leaves, feeling brushed off twice."},{"text":"\"Yes. Show me the project, and let's start over.\"","then":"He pulls up a chair, and you work through it together.","best":true}],"why":"Following the sorry with real help shows him the repair was meant."},{"prompt":"Later, Yuvraj asks: \"Do you think parents should ever say sorry to their kids?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Sometimes. But not so often that they lose respect.\"","then":"Yuvraj shrugs, as if a sorry is something to be rationed."},{"text":"\"No. Parents are there to be obeyed, not questioned.\"","then":"Yuvraj goes quiet and files that away for later."},{"text":"\"Yes. Owning a mistake takes strength, even for parents.\"","then":"Yuvraj nods slowly. \"Your parents never said sorry to you, did they?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"That's none of your business, Yuvraj.\"","then":"He drops it, and the question stays with him."}],"why":"Saying sorry to your child strengthens your bond and your authority, and he learns it too."}],"relearn":"Owning a slip when your child names it models respect and strengthens your bond, never weakens your authority."},
];

